VEL.register("dvt",{"id":"dvt","track":{"code":"DVT","name":"Dryer Vent Technician","kind":"Certificate prep","org":"NADCA","domains":[["Understanding Dryer Vents",20],["Technician Safety",20],["Containment & Occupants",20],["Inspections & Assessments",20],["Cleaning & Verification",20]],"exam":{"questions":50,"minutes":30,"cut":80,"cutNote":"NADCA has not published a question count or pass mark for the DVT certificate. 80% is a working line; sims run at the pace of CSIA's C-DET exam."},"facts":["NADCA's entry-level Dryer Vent Technician certificate, similar to its VMT program, going live on nadca.com in fall 2026.","Five published modules: understanding dryer vents, technician safety, containment and occupant safety, inspections and assessments, cleaning and verification.","Step 1 of 3. Next: a NADCA dryer exhaust cleaning standard (like ACR), then a dryer exhaust certification (like ASCS).","NADCA's existing dryer document is the DEDP performance standard (2014)."],"sims":[[20,"Quick check"],[50,"Full length"],[100,"Deep check"]],"sources":[["NADCA DVT certificate program handout","https://nadca.com/sites/nadca/files/images/2026/Dryer%20Vent%20Tech%20Cert%20.pdf"],["NADCA DEDP Standard (free)","https://dedpstandard.nadca.com/"],["2024 IRC Chapter 15, M1502 Clothes dryer exhaust","https://codes.iccsafe.org/content/IRC2024P1/chapter-15-exhaust-systems"],["2024 IMC Chapter 5, Section 504","https://codes.iccsafe.org/content/IMC2024P1/chapter-5-exhaust-systems"],["USFA clothes dryer fire safety","https://www.usfa.fema.gov/prevention/home-fires/prevent-fires/electrical-and-appliances/clothes-dryers/"]]},"units":[{"id":"code:type1-def","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"code-dryer-types","tag":"DEF","text":"Type 1 clothes dryer: a factory-built, mass-produced dryer used mainly in a family living environment, usually the smallest unit in physical size and output.","src":"IFGC 2024 202 (Clothes dryer)","prompt":"How does the IFGC define a Type 1 clothes dryer?","trap":"Type 1 is the household class and Type 2 the business class; the numbers do not mean gas vs electric.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:type2-def","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"code-dryer-types","tag":"DEF","text":"Type 2 clothes dryer: a factory-built, mass-produced dryer used in a business where the public deals directly with it, and not designed for an individual family's living space.","src":"IFGC 2024 202 (Clothes dryer)","prompt":"How does the IFGC define a Type 2 clothes dryer?","trap":"Type 2 is set by public-facing business use, not by physical size.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:type-listing","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"code-dryer-types","tag":"CODE","text":"Gas clothes dryers must be listed to ANSI Z21.5.1/CSA 7.1 (Volume I, Type 1 dryers) or ANSI Z21.5.2/CSA 7.2 (Volume II, Type 2 dryers) and installed per the maker's instructions.","src":"IFGC 2024 613.1; IFGC 2024 Ch. 8","prompt":"Which listing standards cover Type 1 and Type 2 gas clothes dryers under the IFGC?","trap":"The IRC version (G2438.1) names only Z21.5.1, the Type 1 standard.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0058"]},{"id":"code:type2-commercial","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"code-dryer-types","tag":"CODE","text":"IFGC 614.10, titled Commercial clothes dryers, applies to exhaust ducts serving Type 2 dryers and requires them to follow the appliance manufacturer's instructions.","src":"IFGC 2024 614.10","prompt":"Which dryer type do the IFGC commercial clothes dryer exhaust rules apply to?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:type1-not-vented","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"code-dryer-types","tag":"CODE","text":"Type 1 gas dryers are on the list of appliances not required to be vented, yet they must still be exhausted under the dryer exhaust section G2439.","src":"IRC 2024 G2425.8 item 4","prompt":"Does a Type 1 gas dryer need a vent, and does it still need an exhaust duct?","trap":"Not needing a vent or chimney is different from not needing a dryer exhaust duct.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:system-parts","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"code-dryer-types","tag":"DEF","text":"Code language splits a dryer exhaust system into the transition duct (dryer to duct system), the exhaust duct (from the transition connection to the outlet) and the exhaust terminal outside.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.3.1, M1502.4.3, M1502.4.6.1","prompt":"What three parts do the codes divide a dryer exhaust system into?","trap":"Exhaust duct limits (35 ft, smooth-wall metal) do not apply to the transition duct, which has its own 8 ft rule.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:transition-scope","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"code-dryer-types","tag":"FACT","text":"UL 2158A covers transition ducts for household and commercial clothes dryers, meant to connect the dryer to the building's permanent exhaust duct.","src":"UL 2158A scope §1.1, 1.2","prompt":"What does UL 2158A cover?","trap":"UL 2158A is the transition duct standard; UL 705 is the power ventilator standard.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:depv-scope","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"code-dryer-types","tag":"FACT","text":"UL 705 (Power Ventilators) includes dryer exhaust duct power ventilators (DEPVs) intended for a single residential dryer.","src":"UL 705 scope §1.2","prompt":"Which UL standard covers dryer exhaust duct power ventilators, and for what installations?","trap":"A UL 705 DEPV is not the same thing as the domestic booster fan the codes prohibit.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:g2439-numbering","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"code-dryer-types","tag":"DEF","text":"IRC Chapter 24 prints the matching IFGC section in parentheses, so gas dryer exhaust section G2439 is IFGC 614 and G2439.7.2 is IFGC 614.9.2.","src":"IRC 2024 G2439 (614)","prompt":"How do IRC G2439 section numbers relate to the IFGC?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0064"]},{"id":"code:m1502-general","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-termination","tag":"CODE","text":"IRC M1502.1 makes the dryer manufacturer's instructions the governing requirement for how a clothes dryer is exhausted.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.1","prompt":"What does IRC M1502.1 require a dryer's exhaust installation to follow?","trap":"Here the manufacturer's instructions are a code requirement, not a recommendation.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:independent","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-termination","tag":"CODE","text":"A dryer's exhaust system may not be shared with any other system, and it has to deliver the moisture outdoors.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.2","prompt":"Can a dryer exhaust duct share a duct with other exhaust, and where must it carry moisture?","trap":"Tying into a bath fan or range hood duct violates this even with a shared wall cap.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0000"]},{"id":"code:condensing-exception","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-termination","tag":"CODE","text":"Listed and labeled condensing (ductless) dryers are exempt from the rule that dryer exhaust be independent and discharged outdoors.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.2 Exception","prompt":"Which dryers are exempt from the IRC's independent outdoor exhaust requirement?","trap":"The exemption needs a listed and labeled condensing dryer; an indoor lint box on a vented dryer does not qualify.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0004"]},{"id":"code:outdoor-only","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-termination","tag":"CODE","text":"Dryer exhaust must terminate outside the building; the IRC bars discharging exhaust air into an attic, soffit, ridge vent or crawl space.","src":"IRC 2024 M1501.1, M1502.3","prompt":"Where may a dryer exhaust duct discharge under the IRC, and which spaces are prohibited?","trap":"Ending the duct at a soffit or ridge vent is discharging into it, which is prohibited.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0002","dvt-0146","dvt-0155"]},{"id":"code:term-3ft","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-termination","tag":"CODE","text":"Terminate where the dryer manufacturer's instructions say; if they name no location, keep the terminal at least 3 ft away, in every direction, from any opening into the building.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.3","prompt":"How far must a dryer terminal be from building openings when the dryer instructions give no location?","trap":"3 ft is the fallback; a termination location stated in the dryer instructions governs instead.","num":{"q":"With no location in the dryer instructions, the terminal must be at least __ in any direction from openings into the building.","a":"3 feet","d":["1 foot","6 feet","10 feet"]},"qs":["dvt-0007","dvt-0008","dvt-0154"]},{"id":"code:term-soffit","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-termination","tag":"CODE","text":"For the 3 ft termination clearance, openings in ventilated soffits count as openings into the building.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.3","prompt":"Do soffit vents count as building openings for the dryer termination clearance?","trap":"A terminal tucked under a vented soffit can fail even when it is well away from windows and doors.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0007","dvt-0154","dvt-0155"]},{"id":"code:backdraft","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-termination","tag":"CODE","text":"A dryer exhaust termination needs a backdraft damper.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.3","prompt":"What damper must a dryer exhaust termination have?","trap":"Required: a backdraft damper at the terminal. Prohibited: fire dampers inside the duct (G2439.2, IMC 504.2).","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0182"]},{"id":"code:no-screen","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-termination","tag":"CODE","text":"Screens are not allowed at a dryer exhaust duct termination.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.3","prompt":"Are screens allowed at a dryer exhaust termination?","trap":"General IMC exhaust openings need screens or louvers (IMC 501.3.2), but dryer terminals must not have screens.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0009","dvt-0130","dvt-0152","dvt-0182"]},{"id":"code:passageway","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-termination","tag":"CODE","text":"The terminal's airway may not be reduced in size and must offer an open area of 12.5 sq in or more.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.3.1","prompt":"What open area must a dryer exhaust terminal passageway provide?","trap":"A crushed hood or a cap that narrows the opening fails even when the duct itself is 4 in.","num":{"q":"A dryer terminal passageway must be undiminished and give at least __ of open area.","a":"12.5 square inches","d":["100 square inches","144 square inches","16 square inches"]},"qs":["dvt-0010"]},{"id":"code:material","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-duct-build","tag":"CODE","text":"The IRC dryer exhaust duct must be metal with a smooth interior, at least 0.0157 in thick (No. 28 gage).","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.1","prompt":"What material, finish and minimum thickness does the IRC require for a dryer exhaust duct?","trap":"Gas-dryer and IMC sections say 0.016 in. Plastic or foil flex is not smooth-wall metal duct.","num":{"q":"IRC M1502 dryer exhaust duct metal must be at least __ thick (No. 28 gage).","a":"0.0157 inch","d":["0.016 inch","0.0187 inch","0.062 inch"]},"qs":["dvt-0016","dvt-0018","dvt-0019"]},{"id":"code:diameter","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-duct-build","tag":"CODE","text":"The IRC dryer exhaust duct must be 4 in nominal diameter.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.1","prompt":"What nominal diameter does the IRC require for a dryer exhaust duct?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The IRC dryer exhaust duct must be __ nominal diameter.","a":"4 inches","d":["2 inches","6 inches","8 inches"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"code:supports","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-duct-build","tag":"CODE","text":"IRC dryer exhaust ducts need supports no more than 12 ft apart and must be held securely in place.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.2","prompt":"How often must an IRC M1502 dryer exhaust duct be supported?","trap":"IRC G2439, IFGC 614 and IMC 504 require supports every 4 ft.","num":{"q":"IRC M1502 dryer ducts must be supported at intervals not exceeding __.","a":"12 feet","d":["4 feet","6 feet","8 feet"]},"qs":["dvt-0025","dvt-0145"]},{"id":"code:male-end","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-duct-build","tag":"CODE","text":"At each joint, the insert (male) end of a duct section has to point the way the air flows, into the next duct or fitting.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.2","prompt":"Which way must the insert end of each dryer duct section face?","trap":"Male ends point downstream toward the terminal, not back toward the dryer.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0021","dvt-0144","dvt-0203"]},{"id":"code:joint-seal","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-duct-build","tag":"CODE","text":"Dryer exhaust duct joints must be both sealed per M1601.4.1 and mechanically fastened.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.2","prompt":"How must joints in an IRC dryer exhaust duct be finished?","trap":"Both are required: tape alone or screws alone does not comply.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0024","dvt-0145","dvt-0201"]},{"id":"code:tape-181b","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-duct-build","tag":"CODE","text":"Tape sealing metal duct joints under M1601.4.1 has to meet UL 181B and carry the 181 B-FX mark; mastic carries the 181 BM mark.","src":"IRC 2024 M1601.4.1","prompt":"What UL marking must tape or mastic carry when sealing metal dryer duct joints?","trap":"Plain cloth duct tape carries no 181B marking. UL 181A markings are for fibrous glass duct.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0023","dvt-0201"]},{"id":"code:fastener","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-duct-build","tag":"CODE","text":"Screws or similar fasteners used at joints may stick into the duct no more than 1/8 in.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.2","prompt":"How far may a fastener protrude into a dryer exhaust duct?","trap":"The IRC limits protrusion; it does not ban screws outright.","num":{"q":"Fasteners may protrude no more than __ into a dryer exhaust duct.","a":"1/8 inch","d":["1/4 inch","1/2 inch","1-1/4 inches"]},"qs":["dvt-0022"]},{"id":"code:cavity","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-duct-build","tag":"CODE","text":"When a dryer duct is enclosed in a wall or ceiling cavity, the cavity must allow the duct to be installed without deformation.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.2","prompt":"What must be true of a wall or ceiling cavity that encloses a dryer exhaust duct?","trap":"Squashing a 4 in round duct into a 3-1/2 in deep 2x4 wall is deformation.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0020"]},{"id":"code:transition-listed","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-duct-build","tag":"CODE","text":"The transition duct between dryer and exhaust duct system has to be one single length, listed and labeled to UL 2158A.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.3","prompt":"How many pieces may an IRC dryer transition duct be, and to what standard must it be listed and labeled?","trap":"Two transition ducts spliced together fail the single-length rule even if the total is under 8 ft.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0043"]},{"id":"code:transition-8ft","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-duct-build","tag":"CODE","text":"A dryer transition duct may be no more than 8 ft long.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.3","prompt":"What is the maximum length of a dryer transition duct?","trap":"8 ft is the transition limit; 35 ft is the exhaust duct limit, and the transition is not counted in it.","num":{"q":"A dryer transition duct may be no longer than __.","a":"8 feet","d":["4 feet","6 feet","12 feet"]},"qs":["dvt-0019","dvt-0033","dvt-0043"]},{"id":"code:transition-exposed","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-duct-build","tag":"CODE","text":"A transition duct may not be hidden inside construction such as walls, floors or ceilings.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.3","prompt":"May a dryer transition duct be concealed inside construction?","trap":"Only the smooth-wall metal exhaust duct may be concealed; the transition duct stays exposed.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0018","dvt-0044"]},{"id":"code:length-methods","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"Maximum dryer duct length is set by one of three methods: the 35 ft specified length with fitting deductions, the dryer maker's instructions, or the DEPV maker's instructions.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.6","prompt":"What three methods can set the maximum dryer exhaust duct length under the IRC?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:length-35","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"Under the specified-length method, the exhaust duct may be at most 35 ft, measured from the transition duct connection to the outlet terminal; the transition duct is not counted.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.1","prompt":"What is the IRC specified maximum dryer duct length, and where is it measured from and to?","trap":"Start measuring at the transition connection, not at the dryer; the transition duct (up to 8 ft) is excluded.","num":{"q":"The IRC specified maximum dryer exhaust duct length is __ from the transition connection to the terminal.","a":"35 feet","d":["30 feet","12 feet","8 feet"]},"qs":["dvt-0027","dvt-0033","dvt-0035"]},{"id":"code:length-fittings","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"When fittings are used, the 35 ft maximum is reduced by each fitting's equivalent length from Table M1502.4.6.1.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.1","prompt":"How do elbows affect the 35 ft IRC dryer duct limit?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:fit-mitered-90","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"A 4 in radius mitered 90 degree elbow counts as 5 ft of duct.","src":"IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","prompt":"What equivalent length does a 4 in radius mitered 90 degree elbow add?","trap":"Mitered 90 is 5 ft; mitered 45 is 2 ft 6 in.","num":{"q":"A 4-inch radius mitered 90-degree elbow counts as __ of duct.","a":"5 feet","d":["2 feet 6 inches","1 foot 9 inches","1 foot 6 inches"]},"qs":["dvt-0026","dvt-0027","dvt-0028","dvt-0031","dvt-0034"]},{"id":"code:fit-mitered-45","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"A 4 in radius mitered 45 degree elbow counts as 2 ft 6 in of duct.","src":"IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","prompt":"What equivalent length does a 4 in radius mitered 45 degree elbow add?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A 4-inch radius mitered 45-degree elbow counts as __ of duct.","a":"2 feet 6 inches","d":["5 feet","1 foot","9 inches"]},"qs":["dvt-0026","dvt-0034"]},{"id":"code:fit-6-90","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"A 6 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow counts as 1 ft 9 in of duct.","src":"IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","prompt":"What equivalent length does a 6 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow add?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A 6-inch radius smooth 90-degree elbow counts as __ of duct.","a":"1 foot 9 inches","d":["1 foot 7 inches","1 foot 6 inches","5 feet"]},"qs":["dvt-0028","dvt-0029"]},{"id":"code:fit-8-90","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"An 8 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow counts as 1 ft 7 in of duct.","src":"IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","prompt":"What equivalent length does an 8 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow add?","trap":"","num":{"q":"An 8-inch radius smooth 90-degree elbow counts as __ of duct.","a":"1 foot 7 inches","d":["1 foot 9 inches","1 foot 6 inches","1 foot"]},"qs":["dvt-0032"]},{"id":"code:fit-10-90","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"A 10 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow counts as 1 ft 6 in of duct.","src":"IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","prompt":"What equivalent length does a 10 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow add?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A 10-inch radius smooth 90-degree elbow counts as __ of duct.","a":"1 foot 6 inches","d":["1 foot 9 inches","1 foot 7 inches","9 inches"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"code:fit-smooth-45","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"A smooth 45 degree elbow with a 6 in or 8 in radius counts as 1 ft of duct.","src":"IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","prompt":"What equivalent length does a 6 in or 8 in radius smooth 45 degree elbow add?","trap":"Only the 10 in radius smooth 45 drops to 9 in.","num":{"q":"A 6-inch or 8-inch radius smooth 45-degree elbow counts as __ of duct.","a":"1 foot","d":["9 inches","1 foot 6 inches","2 feet 6 inches"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"code:fit-10-45","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"A 10 in radius smooth 45 degree elbow counts as 9 in of duct, the smallest value in the table.","src":"IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","prompt":"What equivalent length does a 10 in radius smooth 45 degree elbow add?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A 10-inch radius smooth 45-degree elbow counts as __ of duct.","a":"9 inches","d":["1 foot","1 foot 6 inches","2 feet 6 inches"]},"qs":["dvt-0030"]},{"id":"code:fit-pattern","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"NOTE","text":"In the fitting table, moving to a larger bend radius never increases equivalent length, and a mitered elbow costs more than any smooth elbow of the same angle.","src":"IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","prompt":"What pattern links bend radius and elbow type to equivalent length in the dryer fitting table?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:length-example","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"FACT","text":"Example: 22 ft of straight duct with three 4 in mitered 90s totals 37 ft equivalent, over 35 ft; with three 6 in radius smooth 90s instead it totals 27 ft 3 in.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.1, Table M1502.4.6.1","prompt":"How does swapping three 4 in mitered 90s for 6 in radius smooth 90s change a 22 ft straight run's equivalent length?","trap":"Add fitting values to the straight length; do not count the transition duct.","num":{"q":"22 ft of straight duct plus three 4-inch radius mitered 90s totals __ equivalent length.","a":"37 feet","d":["27 feet 3 inches","29 feet 6 inches","25 feet"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"code:length-maker","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"Under the manufacturer method, the dryer maker's installation instructions set the exhaust duct's size and maximum length.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.2","prompt":"Under the IRC manufacturer method, what sets the duct size and maximum length?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0036"]},{"id":"code:concealment-copy","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"Under the IRC manufacturer method, the code official must get a copy of the installation instructions for that dryer make and model at the concealment inspection.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.2","prompt":"When must the IRC code official receive the dryer's installation instructions?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0036"]},{"id":"code:length-maker-table","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"If the dryer maker gives no fitting equivalent length figures, Table M1502.4.6.1 must be used for the fittings.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.2","prompt":"What fitting values apply when the dryer manufacturer gives no fitting equivalent lengths?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0039"]},{"id":"code:length-depv","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"Where a dryer exhaust duct power ventilator is used, maximum duct length follows the DEPV manufacturer's instructions.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.3","prompt":"What sets the maximum dryer duct length when a DEPV is installed?","trap":"It is the DEPV maker's figure, not the 35 ft default.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0038"]},{"id":"code:label-required","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"Once the duct's equivalent length is over 35 ft, a permanent tag or label must state that equivalent length.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.7","prompt":"When must a dryer duct's equivalent length be labeled, and on what?","trap":"The trigger is equivalent length (straight duct plus fittings), not straight length alone.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0034","dvt-0037","dvt-0158"]},{"id":"code:label-6ft","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-length","tag":"CODE","text":"The length tag or label has to be placed no more than 6 ft from the exhaust duct connection.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.7","prompt":"Where must the dryer duct equivalent length label be placed?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The equivalent length label must be within __ of the exhaust duct connection.","a":"6 feet","d":["3 feet","8 feet","12 feet"]},"qs":["dvt-0037","dvt-0158"]},{"id":"code:depv-ul705","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-fans-protect","tag":"CODE","text":"A domestic dryer exhaust duct power ventilator (DEPV) has to meet UL 705 for dryer duct service and be installed following its maker's instructions.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.4","prompt":"What standard must a domestic DEPV meet, and how must it be installed?","trap":"UL 705 is for DEPVs; UL 2158A is for transition ducts.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0047"]},{"id":"code:booster-ban","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-fans-protect","tag":"CODE","text":"The IRC forbids putting a domestic booster fan anywhere in a dryer exhaust system.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.5","prompt":"Are domestic booster fans allowed in IRC dryer exhaust systems?","trap":"Added in the 2021 IRC; the 2018 M1502 had no booster fan section.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0046"]},{"id":"code:booster-vs-depv","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-fans-protect","tag":"NOTE","text":"The IRC allows a dryer exhaust duct power ventilator that conforms to UL 705 but prohibits domestic booster fans, so check a fan for UL 705 dryer duct compliance before calling it a DEPV.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.4, M1502.4.5","prompt":"How does the IRC treat a UL 705 DEPV compared with a domestic booster fan?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0046","dvt-0049"]},{"id":"code:shield-where","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-fans-protect","tag":"CODE","text":"Steel shield plates go on the finished face of framing wherever nails or screws are likely to hit the dryer duct and the duct is less than 1-1/4 in from that face.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.5","prompt":"When are shield plates required over a dryer exhaust duct?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The shield plate trigger distance, measured from the duct to the finished face of framing, is __ .","a":"1-1/4 inches","d":["1 inch","2 inches","1/8 inch"]},"qs":["dvt-0040"]},{"id":"code:shield-thick","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-fans-protect","tag":"CODE","text":"IRC shield plates must be steel at least 0.062 in thick.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.5","prompt":"What material and minimum thickness must IRC dryer duct shield plates have?","trap":"The 2024 IMC changed its shield plates to 0.0575 in (No. 16 gage); IRC and IFGC stay at 0.062 in.","num":{"q":"IRC dryer duct shield plates must be steel at least __ thick.","a":"0.062 inch","d":["0.0575 inch","0.0157 inch","0.0187 inch"]},"qs":["dvt-0040"]},{"id":"code:shield-extend","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-fans-protect","tag":"CODE","text":"Shield plates must extend at least 2 in above sole plates and below top plates.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.5","prompt":"How far must dryer duct shield plates extend past sole plates and top plates?","trap":"1-1/4 in is the clearance that triggers plates; 2 in is how far they extend.","num":{"q":"Shield plates must extend at least __ above sole plates and below top plates.","a":"2 inches","d":["1-1/4 inches","4 inches","6 inches"]},"qs":["dvt-0042"]},{"id":"code:makeup-200","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-fans-protect","tag":"CODE","text":"Any dryer installation that exhausts more than 200 cfm needs a makeup air supply.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.6","prompt":"Above what exhaust rate must a dryer installation have makeup air?","trap":"200 cfm is the dryer trigger; 400 cfm is the kitchen exhaust makeup air trigger (M1503.6).","num":{"q":"The IRC makeup air threshold for a dryer installation is an exhaust rate above __ .","a":"200 cfm","d":["100 cfm","400 cfm","50 cfm"]},"qs":["dvt-0051"]},{"id":"code:closet-100","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-fans-protect","tag":"CODE","text":"For a closet designed for a dryer, makeup air follows the dryer instructions; if they are silent, provide permanent openings totaling at least 100 sq in, or other approved makeup air.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.6.1","prompt":"How is makeup air provided for a dryer closet under the 2024 IRC?","trap":"The IRC checks the maker's instructions first; G2439, IFGC and IMC go straight to the 100 sq in opening.","num":{"q":"Absent maker specs, a dryer closet needs permanent openings totaling at least __.","a":"100 square inches","d":["12.5 square inches","144 square inches","200 square inches"]},"qs":["dvt-0050","dvt-0052"]},{"id":"code:duct-required","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-fans-protect","tag":"CODE","text":"Providing space for a clothes dryer triggers the requirement to install a dryer exhaust duct system.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.8","prompt":"When must a dryer exhaust duct system be installed in new construction?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0005"]},{"id":"code:future-use","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-fans-protect","tag":"CODE","text":"When no dryer is in place at occupancy, cap or plug the duct in the room where it starts and label it for future use.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.8","prompt":"How must an IRC dryer exhaust duct be left when no dryer is installed at occupancy?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0005"]},{"id":"code:duct-required-exception","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-irc-fans-protect","tag":"CODE","text":"The exhaust duct requirement is waived when a listed condensing clothes dryer is in place before the building is occupied.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.8 Exception","prompt":"What exception removes the requirement to install a dryer exhaust duct where dryer space is provided?","trap":"The condensing dryer must be listed and already installed before occupancy.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0006"]},{"id":"code:gas-poc","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-gas-dryers","tag":"CODE","text":"A gas dryer's exhaust system may not be shared with other systems and must deliver both moisture and any combustion products outside the building.","src":"IRC 2024 G2439.1; IFGC 2024 614.1","prompt":"What must a gas dryer exhaust system carry outdoors?","trap":"IRC M1502.2 mentions moisture only; the gas sections add products of combustion.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:gas-thickness","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-gas-dryers","tag":"CODE","text":"Under G2439, IFGC 614 and IMC 504, domestic dryer exhaust ducts must be smooth-interior metal at least 0.016 in thick and 4 in nominal diameter.","src":"IRC 2024 G2439.7.1; IFGC 2024 614.9.1; IMC 2024 504.9.1","prompt":"What minimum metal thickness do G2439, IFGC 614 and IMC 504 require for domestic dryer ducts?","trap":"IRC M1502.4.1 says 0.0157 in (No. 28 gage).","num":{"q":"Under IFGC 614 and IMC 504, domestic dryer duct metal must be at least __ thick.","a":"0.016 inch","d":["0.0157 inch","0.0187 inch","0.0575 inch"]},"qs":["dvt-0017"]},{"id":"code:gas-supports","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-gas-dryers","tag":"CODE","text":"Under G2439, IFGC 614 and IMC 504, domestic dryer exhaust ducts need a support every 4 ft and must be held securely.","src":"IRC 2024 G2439.7.2; IFGC 2024 614.9.2; IMC 2024 504.9.2","prompt":"What support spacing do G2439, IFGC 614 and IMC 504 require for domestic dryer ducts?","trap":"IRC M1502.4.2 allows up to 12 ft between supports.","num":{"q":"Under G2439, IFGC 614 and IMC 504, domestic dryer ducts are supported at __ intervals.","a":"4 feet","d":["12 feet","6 feet","8 feet"]},"qs":["dvt-0025"]},{"id":"code:gas-what-differs","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-gas-dryers","tag":"NOTE","text":"Compared with IRC M1502, the G2439 and IFGC 614 domestic rules change only two numbers: supports every 4 ft instead of 12 ft, and 0.016 in metal instead of 0.0157 in.","src":"IRC 2024 M1502, G2439; IFGC 2024 614","prompt":"Which two numbers differ between IRC M1502 and the gas dryer exhaust rules?","trap":"35 ft, the fitting table, 8 ft transition, 12.5 sq in, 6 ft label and shield plate figures all match.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:gas-no-vent","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-gas-dryers","tag":"CODE","text":"A dryer exhaust duct may never tie into a vent connector, a vent or a chimney.","src":"IRC 2024 G2439.3; IFGC 2024 614.4; IMC 2024 504.4","prompt":"May a dryer exhaust duct be connected to a vent connector, vent or chimney?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0001"]},{"id":"code:gas-no-plenum","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-gas-dryers","tag":"CODE","text":"A dryer exhaust duct may not run into or pass through another duct or a plenum.","src":"IRC 2024 G2439.3; IFGC 2024 614.4; IMC 2024 504.4","prompt":"May a dryer exhaust duct run into or through another duct or a plenum?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0003"]},{"id":"code:gas-fire-rated","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-gas-dryers","tag":"CODE","text":"Dryer ducts may not pass through or sit within fireblocking, draftstopping or fire-resistance-rated assemblies unless they are galvanized steel or aluminum of the required thickness and the rating is maintained.","src":"IRC 2024 G2439.2; IFGC 2024 614.2; IMC 2024 504.2","prompt":"When may a dryer exhaust duct penetrate fireblocking or a fire-resistance-rated assembly?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0053"]},{"id":"code:gas-fire-damper","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-gas-dryers","tag":"CODE","text":"A clothes dryer exhaust duct system may not contain a fire damper.","src":"IRC 2024 G2439.2; IFGC 2024 614.2","prompt":"Are fire dampers allowed in a dryer exhaust duct?","trap":"The terminal still needs a backdraft damper; the ban is on fire dampers inside the duct.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:gas-closet","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-gas-dryers","tag":"NOTE","text":"Under G2439, IFGC 614.7.1 and IMC 504.7, a dryer closet needs an opening of at least 100 sq in or other approved makeup air, with no step deferring to the dryer instructions.","src":"IRC 2024 G2439.5.1; IFGC 2024 614.7.1; IMC 2024 504.7","prompt":"How do the gas code and IMC closet makeup air rules differ from IRC M1502.6.1?","trap":"IRC M1502.6.1 checks the dryer maker's makeup air instructions first.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0052"]},{"id":"code:gas-future","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-gas-dryers","tag":"NOTE","text":"G2439, IFGC and IMC require a duct for a not-yet-installed dryer to be capped at the future dryer location; only IRC M1502.4.8 adds the future use marking.","src":"IRC 2024 G2439.7.6, M1502.4.8; IFGC 2024 614.9.6; IMC 2024 504.9.6","prompt":"How do the gas code and IMC rules for a capped future dryer duct differ from IRC M1502.4.8?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:gas-concealed-instr","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-gas-dryers","tag":"CODE","text":"When the dryer maker's instructions set duct length under G2439, IFGC 614 or IMC 504, the code official gets a copy, and before the concealment inspection if the duct will be concealed.","src":"IRC 2024 G2439.7.4.2; IFGC 2024 614.9.4.2; IMC 2024 504.9.4.2","prompt":"When must dryer installation instructions reach the code official under the gas code and IMC?","trap":"IRC M1502.4.6.2 says at the concealment inspection.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:ifgc-booster","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-gas-dryers","tag":"CODE","text":"IFGC 614 bans domestic booster fans (614.6) and requires domestic DEPVs to be listed and labeled to UL 705 (614.5).","src":"IFGC 2024 614.5, 614.6","prompt":"What does IFGC 614 say about booster fans and dryer exhaust duct power ventilators?","trap":"IRC G2439 has no booster fan subsection of its own; in the IRC the ban sits in M1502.4.5.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:ifgc-3ft","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-gas-dryers","tag":"CODE","text":"IFGC 614.4.1 and IMC 504.4.1 repeat the termination rule: follow the dryer instructions, or else end at least 3 ft in any direction from building openings, soffit vent openings included.","src":"IFGC 2024 614.4.1; IMC 2024 504.4.1","prompt":"What termination location rule do IFGC 614.4.1 and IMC 504.4.1 set?","trap":"IRC G2439 has no termination location subsection; in the IRC that rule sits in M1502.3.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:imc-annular","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"Where a dryer duct passes through a wall or ceiling membrane, fill the gap around it with noncombustible material, approved fire caulking or a noncombustible dryer duct wall receptacle.","src":"IMC 2024 504.2","prompt":"How must the gap around a dryer duct penetrating a wall or ceiling membrane be sealed under the IMC?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0054"]},{"id":"code:imc-dampers","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"The IMC prohibits fire dampers, combination fire/smoke dampers and any similar device that would obstruct flow in clothes dryer exhaust ducts.","src":"IMC 2024 504.2","prompt":"Which damper types does IMC 504.2 prohibit in dryer exhaust ducts?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0053"]},{"id":"code:imc-riser-cleanout","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"Under the IMC, every vertical dryer duct riser must have a means for cleanout.","src":"IMC 2024 504.3","prompt":"What does the IMC require at each vertical dryer duct riser?","trap":"IFGC 614.3 applies its riser cleanout rule only to dryers listed to ANSI Z21.5.2 (Type 2).","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0011","dvt-0059"]},{"id":"code:ifgc-type2-riser","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"IFGC 614.3 requires each vertical duct riser serving dryers listed to ANSI Z21.5.2 (Type 2) to have a cleanout or another way to clean the duct interior.","src":"IFGC 2024 614.3","prompt":"Which vertical dryer duct risers need a cleanout under IFGC 614.3?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0059"]},{"id":"code:imc-shield-16ga","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"The 2024 IMC requires dryer duct shield plates of steel at least 0.0575 in thick (No. 16 gage).","src":"IMC 2024 504.8.1","prompt":"What shield plate thickness does the 2024 IMC require for dryer ducts?","trap":"IRC M1502.5, IFGC 614.8 and earlier IMC editions say 0.062 in.","num":{"q":"2024 IMC dryer duct shield plates must be steel at least __ thick (No. 16 gage).","a":"0.0575 inch","d":["0.062 inch","0.016 inch","0.0187 inch"]},"qs":["dvt-0041"]},{"id":"code:comm-maker","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"Commercial clothes dryer exhaust ducts are installed as the appliance manufacturer's installation instructions direct.","src":"IMC 2024 504.10","prompt":"What governs installation of commercial clothes dryer exhaust ducts under the IMC?","trap":"The 35 ft default sits in 504.9, the domestic section; the commercial section defers to the appliance maker.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0057"]},{"id":"code:comm-fan","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"In commercial dryer exhaust systems, fan motors must sit outside the airstream, and with multiple dryers the fan runs continuously or is interlocked to run whenever any dryer runs.","src":"IMC 2024 504.10; IFGC 2024 614.10","prompt":"Where must a commercial dryer exhaust fan motor be, and when must a shared fan run?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0056","dvt-0288"]},{"id":"code:comm-6in","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"Commercial dryer exhaust ducts must keep at least 6 in clearance to combustible materials.","src":"IMC 2024 504.10; IFGC 2024 614.10","prompt":"What clearance to combustibles must commercial dryer exhaust ducts keep?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Commercial dryer exhaust ducts need at least __ clearance to combustible materials.","a":"6 inches","d":["2 inches","4 inches","12 inches"]},"qs":["dvt-0055"]},{"id":"code:comm-transition","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"Under the 2024 IMC, commercial dryer transition ducts must be single lengths no longer than 8 ft, listed and labeled to UL 2158A, and not concealed within construction.","src":"IMC 2024 504.10","prompt":"What rules apply to commercial dryer transition ducts under the 2024 IMC?","trap":"Same 8 ft single-length limit as domestic transition ducts.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:ms-shaft","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"A common multistory dryer exhaust duct must run in a shaft built and fire-resistance rated as the IBC requires, and dampers are prohibited in the exhaust duct.","src":"IMC 2024 504.11 items 1, 2; IFGC 2024 614.11","prompt":"What shaft and damper rules apply to a common multistory dryer exhaust duct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0060"]},{"id":"code:ms-duct","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"Inside the shaft, the common dryer duct must be rigid sheet steel at least 0.0187 in thick (No. 26 gage), built to SMACNA, with no offsets.","src":"IMC 2024 504.11 items 3, 4","prompt":"What duct material, thickness and routing are required inside a common multistory dryer shaft?","trap":"0.0187 in is No. 26 gage; 0.0157 in is No. 28 gage.","num":{"q":"Common multistory dryer ductwork must be sheet steel at least __ thick (No. 26 gage).","a":"0.0187 inch","d":["0.0157 inch","0.016 inch","0.0575 inch"]},"qs":["dvt-0061"]},{"id":"code:ms-fan","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"A common multistory dryer exhaust fan must run continuously, be connected to standby power and have its motor outside the airstream.","src":"IMC 2024 504.11 items 6, 7","prompt":"How must the exhaust fan on a common multistory dryer duct operate and be powered?","trap":"Commercial multi-dryer fans in 504.10 may be interlocked; a multistory common shaft fan must run continuously.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0062","dvt-0157"]},{"id":"code:ms-monitor","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"A common multistory dryer exhaust fan must be monitored from an approved location that gets an audible or visual alert whenever the fan stops.","src":"IMC 2024 504.11 item 8","prompt":"How must a common multistory dryer exhaust fan be monitored?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0062"]},{"id":"code:ms-cleanout","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"A cleanout opening at the base of a common multistory dryer shaft must give access for duct cleaning and inspection and be at least 12 in by 12 in finished.","src":"IMC 2024 504.11 item 10","prompt":"Where is the cleanout for a common multistory dryer shaft, and how big must it be?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The cleanout at the base of a common multistory dryer shaft must be at least __ finished.","a":"12 inches by 12 inches","d":["6 inches by 6 inches","8 inches by 8 inches","18 inches by 24 inches"]},"qs":["dvt-0063"]},{"id":"code:ms-dedicated","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-imc-commercial","tag":"CODE","text":"A common multistory dryer duct may serve only clothes dryers, independent of other exhaust; it must have makeup air, and its termination may not have a screen.","src":"IMC 2024 504.11 items 9, 11, 12","prompt":"What may a common multistory dryer duct serve, and what makeup air and termination rules apply?","trap":"The dryers-only item (12) is in the IMC list; the IFGC 614.11 list stops at 11 items.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:ed-irc-booster","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-edition-changes","tag":"NOTE","text":"The IRC booster fan prohibition first appeared in the 2021 edition as M1502.4.5; the 2018 M1502 had no booster fan section.","src":"IRC 2018 M1502.4; IRC 2021 M1502.4.5","prompt":"In which IRC edition did the dryer booster fan prohibition first appear?","trap":"In the 2018 IRC, M1502.4.5 is Duct length, not booster fans.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0012"]},{"id":"code:ed-irc-renumber","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-edition-changes","tag":"NOTE","text":"Inserting the booster fan section in 2021 pushed IRC duct length to M1502.4.6, length identification to M1502.4.7 and exhaust duct required to M1502.4.8.","src":"IRC 2018, 2021, 2024 M1502.4","prompt":"How did the 2021 IRC renumber the dryer duct length, length label and exhaust duct required sections?","trap":"The 2018 numbers are one lower: M1502.4.5, M1502.4.6 and M1502.4.7.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:ed-irc-table","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-edition-changes","tag":"NOTE","text":"The IRC dryer fitting table was Table M1502.4.5.1 in 2018 and is Table M1502.4.6.1 in 2021 and 2024; the fitting values did not change.","src":"IRC 2018 Table M1502.4.5.1; IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","prompt":"What is the IRC dryer fitting table numbered in 2018 versus 2021 and 2024?","trap":"The IMC table moved the same way: 504.8.4.1 in 2018 to 504.9.4.1 in 2021.","num":{"q":"In the 2024 IRC, the dryer fitting equivalent length table is Table __.","a":"M1502.4.6.1","d":["M1502.4.5.1","504.9.4.1","504.8.4.1"]},"qs":["dvt-0013"]},{"id":"code:ed-irc-soffit","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-edition-changes","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 IRC added openings in ventilated soffits to the 3 ft termination rule; the 2018 text named only openings into buildings.","src":"IRC 2018, 2021 M1502.3","prompt":"What did the 2021 IRC add to the dryer termination clearance rule?","trap":"The ban on discharging into an attic, soffit, ridge vent or crawl space (M1501.1) was already in 2018.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0012"]},{"id":"code:ed-irc-makeup","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-edition-changes","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2024 IRC added M1502.6 (makeup air over 200 cfm) and M1502.6.1 (dryer closets) to the dryer section; the 2021 M1502 ended at M1502.5.","src":"IRC 2021, 2024 M1502","prompt":"What dryer makeup air sections were new to IRC M1502 in 2024?","trap":"IMC 504 already had the 200 cfm and 100 sq in closet rules in 2018 and 2021.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0015"]},{"id":"code:ed-numbers-same","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-edition-changes","tag":"NOTE","text":"Across the 2018, 2021 and 2024 IRC, the core dryer numbers stayed the same: 35 ft, the fitting values, 8 ft transition, 12 ft supports, 0.0157 in metal, 12.5 sq in and the 6 ft label.","src":"IRC 2018, 2021, 2024 M1502","prompt":"Did the core IRC dryer exhaust numbers change between the 2018 and 2024 editions?","trap":"Only section numbers moved; a 35 ft question has the same answer in all three editions.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:ed-imc-renumber","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-edition-changes","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 IMC renumbered the dryer sections: domestic ducts moved from 504.8 to 504.9, commercial dryers from 504.9 to 504.10, and multistory common exhaust from 504.10 to 504.11.","src":"IMC 2018, 2021 504","prompt":"How were IMC dryer exhaust sections renumbered between 2018 and 2021?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:ed-imc-added-2021","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-edition-changes","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 IMC added 504.4.1, the 3 ft termination location rule that includes soffit openings, and 504.6, the domestic booster fan ban; the 2018 IMC 504 had neither.","src":"IMC 2018, 2021 504","prompt":"Which two dryer rules were added to IMC 504 in 2021?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0049"]},{"id":"code:ed-imc-shield","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-edition-changes","tag":"NOTE","text":"IMC shield plates were steel 0.062 in thick in 2018 (504.7) and 2021 (504.8); the 2024 IMC moved thickness to new 504.8.1 at 0.0575 in (No. 16 gage).","src":"IMC 2018 504.7; IMC 2021 504.8; IMC 2024 504.8.1","prompt":"How did IMC dryer duct shield plate thickness change in 2024?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"code:ed-imc-ul2158a","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-edition-changes","tag":"NOTE","text":"For commercial dryer transition ducts, the 2018 and 2021 IMC required listing and labeling for the application; the 2024 IMC names UL 2158A.","src":"IMC 2018 504.9; IMC 2021 504.10; IMC 2024 504.10","prompt":"How did the IMC listing requirement for commercial dryer transition ducts change in 2024?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0014"]},{"id":"code:ed-imc-independent","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"code-edition-changes","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2018 IMC 504.1 required dryer exhaust to be independent of all other systems; 2021 and 2024 dropped that from 504.1, but IMC 501.2 still requires it.","src":"IMC 2018 504.1; IMC 2024 501.2, 504.1","prompt":"Does the 2024 IMC still require dryer exhaust to be independent of other systems, and where?","trap":"Reading 504.1 alone misses it; the independence rule is in 501.2.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:fwd-clean-first","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"The DEDP test shall be run only on dryer exhaust ducts that were first checked by eye and confirmed clean.","src":"DEDP 2014, Foreword; Qualifications","prompt":"Per the Foreword's guiding principle, what must be true of a dryer exhaust duct before the DEDP test is performed on it?","trap":"It verifies a cleaned duct. It is not a before-cleaning test to show how restricted a dirty vent is.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0065","dvt-0264"]},{"id":"dedp:fwd-pressurized","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"DEF","text":"The DEDP method is a pressurized blower test that shows whether the dryer exhaust duct works correctly in its current installed condition.","src":"DEDP 2014, Foreword","prompt":"What kind of test does the DEDP method use, and what does it show about the duct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:fwd-compensates","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"DEF","text":"The test checks that the installed duct moves the minimum airflow, capturing losses from flexible transitions, leaky elbows and ducts, broken connections and friction.","src":"DEDP 2014, Foreword","prompt":"What does the DEDP test determine about an installed duct, and which losses does it capture?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0306"]},{"id":"dedp:fwd-video-may","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"MAY","text":"Dryer exhaust duct service companies may run cable-type video cameras through the duct to visually confirm the inside surfaces are clean.","src":"DEDP 2014, Foreword","prompt":"What may service companies use to visually confirm the inside of a dryer duct is clean?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0066"]},{"id":"dedp:fwd-visual-no-replace","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"Video inspection is not the DEDP's primary inspection, and users must understand that no visual inspection replaces the standard's performance criteria.","src":"DEDP 2014, Foreword","prompt":"Is video the DEDP's primary inspection, and can a visual inspection replace the performance criteria?","trap":"A spotless camera run can still fail the test from leaks, elbows or a restrictive damper.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0066"]},{"id":"dedp:fwd-video-after-fail","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"REC","text":"If a duct misses the minimum pressure and velocity values, video cameras are recommended as the next step to diagnose the cause.","src":"DEDP 2014, Foreword","prompt":"When does the DEDP recommend video cameras to diagnose a dryer duct?","trap":"Recommended, not required, and used after a failed test.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0160"]},{"id":"dedp:disc-new-and-existing","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"DEF","text":"The standard is meant to test both new-construction dryer exhaust ducts and existing ducts already checked by eye and confirmed clean.","src":"DEDP 2014, Disclaimer","prompt":"Which dryer exhaust ducts is the DEDP standard designed to test?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:disc-judgment","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"MAY","text":"Every job is different, so the tech's experience, common sense and professional judgment may justify departing from the standard's information.","src":"DEDP 2014, Disclaimer","prompt":"What may justify deviating from the information in the DEDP standard?","trap":"The Disclaimer allows judgment, but the Definitions say a shall clause allows no deviation for conformance.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:disc-no-guarantee","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"DEF","text":"NADCA does not guarantee that following the standard will save energy, fix indoor air quality concerns, or improve duct performance.","src":"DEDP 2014, Disclaimer","prompt":"What results does NADCA decline to guarantee from following the DEDP standard?","trap":"NADCA disclaims all three. Following the standard is not a promise of energy savings, IAQ fixes or better duct performance.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:disc-ahj-mold","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"An Authority Having Jurisdiction's instruction on mold assessment or remediation shall supersede any contradicting information in the standard.","src":"DEDP 2014, Disclaimer","prompt":"Which instruction shall supersede the DEDP standard on mold assessment or remediation?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:purpose","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"DEF","text":"The standard exists to set out a measurable way to verify how well a residential dryer exhaust duct performs.","src":"DEDP 2014, Purpose","prompt":"What is the stated purpose of the DEDP standard?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:scope-excluded","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"DEF","text":"The dryer appliance and the existing transition duct are not included in the DEDP standard.","src":"DEDP 2014, Scope","prompt":"Which two parts of the dryer exhaust system does the DEDP Scope leave out?","trap":"Only those two are excluded. The permanent duct and termination are what the test evaluates.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0067"]},{"id":"dedp:app-criteria","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"DEF","text":"The standard sets repeatable, consistent criteria for cleaned ducts, developed mainly to reveal other conditions that may be hurting duct efficiency.","src":"DEDP 2014, Application","prompt":"What were the DEDP performance criteria mainly developed to identify?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:app-parties","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"MAY","text":"Other parties with a material interest, such as owners, property managers, insurers and government bodies, may also use the standard when residential dryer ducts are serviced.","src":"DEDP 2014, Application","prompt":"Besides service contractors, who may the DEDP standard apply to?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:app-why-device","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"DEF","text":"Dryer blower strengths vary too widely between older and new high-efficiency models to test with the dryer's own fan, so NADCA built a separate device and protocol.","src":"DEDP 2014, Application","prompt":"Why does the DEDP use its own test device instead of the dryer's fan?","trap":"Newer dryers have stronger blowers. The test device is the same known air source on every job, so results compare across dryers.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0068"]},{"id":"dedp:app-4in","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"DEF","text":"The DEDP device and procedure were possible only because virtually all residential dryer vents are 4 in in diameter.","src":"DEDP 2014, Application","prompt":"What fact about residential dryer vents made a single DEDP test device possible?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:qual-after-service","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"DEF","text":"The DEDP confirms airflow together with back pressure once the service work is finished.","src":"DEDP 2014, Qualifications","prompt":"What does the DEDP validate, and at what point in the job?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:lim-appliance","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"DEF","text":"The standard does not assess cleanliness inside the dryer appliance.","src":"DEDP 2014, Limitations","prompt":"Does the DEDP standard assess cleanliness inside the dryer appliance?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:lim-hazards","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-purpose","tag":"DEF","text":"The standard does not address every hazard or risk of the work; it directs users to the Authority Having Jurisdiction and the manufacturer's guidelines.","src":"DEDP 2014, Limitations","prompt":"Where does the DEDP direct users for hazards and risks it does not address?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0069"]},{"id":"dedp:intro-single-dryer","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"The DEDP covers residential single-dryer exhaust ducts, one dryer on a 4 in (102 mm) rigid metal vent, and judges whether the system meets the standard's minimum operating requirements.","src":"DEDP 2014, Introduction; Definitions","prompt":"What kind of dryer exhaust system is the DEDP written for?","trap":"Residential and one dryer per duct. Commercial and multi-dryer systems are outside it.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:intro-airflow","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"Inside the dryer, room air is drawn in, heated by electric or gas elements, picks up moisture and lint in the drum, then passes the lint filter to the internal fan.","src":"DEDP 2014, Introduction","prompt":"What path does air take inside a dryer before it leaves the appliance?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:intro-typical","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"A typical system has a flexible transition duct behind the dryer, 4 in (100 mm) rigid galvanized pipe with galvanized elbows, and a termination device outside the house.","src":"DEDP 2014, Introduction","prompt":"What components make up a typical residential dryer exhaust duct system?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A typical dryer exhaust system uses __ round rigid galvanized pipe.","a":"4 in","d":["6 in","8 in","10 in"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:intro-efficiency","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"A clean, open duct helps the dryer run efficiently. As lint builds up, loads take longer to dry, the dryer may overheat, and energy use goes up.","src":"DEDP 2014, Introduction","prompt":"What happens to a dryer's performance as its exhaust duct collects lint?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:intro-fire","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"CAN","text":"At the extreme, a clogged dryer duct or termination device can start a fire inside the dryer or inside the duct system.","src":"DEDP 2014, Introduction","prompt":"What can a blocked dryer exhaust duct or termination cause in extreme cases?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:intro-mfr-service","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"Dryer manufacturers have long specified that exhaust ducts be serviced regularly to remove accumulated lint.","src":"DEDP 2014, Introduction","prompt":"What have dryer manufacturers long specified about servicing exhaust ducts?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:intro-accelerants","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"Lint builds faster with long runs, restricted ducts, bird or rodent nests at the termination, crushed or kinked transitions, restrictive screen-like terminations, condensation in uninsulated ducts through cold spaces, and wrong pitch.","src":"DEDP 2014, Introduction","prompt":"Which conditions speed up lint buildup or restriction in a dryer exhaust duct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0122","dvt-0130","dvt-0166"]},{"id":"dedp:intro-iaq","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"Dryers send moist air and lint outdoors, so their ducts cause little or no indoor air quality problem apart from pulling air from the room.","src":"DEDP 2014, Introduction","prompt":"How much of an indoor air quality issue does the DEDP say dryer exhaust ducts pose?","trap":"The standard frames restriction as a performance and fire problem, not an IAQ problem.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:intro-cementitious","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"CAN","text":"Improperly installed ducts can condense water and even hold liquid water, which with lint can form cementitious masses stuck to the inside of the duct.","src":"DEDP 2014, Introduction","prompt":"How can cementitious masses form inside a dryer exhaust duct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0122"]},{"id":"dedp:def-transition","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"Transition duct is the flexible or rigid link from the dryer's exhaust outlet to the dryer exhaust duct in the building's wall, floor or ceiling.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"How does the DEDP define transition duct?","trap":"A transition can be rigid as well as flexible.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:def-duct-connection","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"The dryer exhaust duct connection is the point where the transition duct joins the dryer exhaust duct.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"What is the dryer exhaust duct connection?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:def-permanent","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"Permanent exhaust duct is the whole network of 4 in rigid ductwork from an interior wall, floor or ceiling out through an exterior exhaust penetration.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"How does the DEDP define the permanent exhaust duct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:def-exhaust-duct","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"Dryer exhaust ducts are metal, have a smooth interior finish, and are 4 in (102 mm) nominal diameter.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"What three features define a dryer exhaust duct in the DEDP?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:def-penetration","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"An exhaust penetration is where the dryer exhaust duct goes through a wall or ceiling membrane.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"What is an exhaust penetration?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:def-termination","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"A termination device keeps outside backdrafts out and stops birds and other wildlife from nesting or sheltering in the ductwork.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"What two jobs does a termination device do?","trap":"The device blocks backdrafts and wildlife. The damper blocks backdrafts and weather.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0107"]},{"id":"dedp:def-wall-cap","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"SHOULD","text":"The wall cap should offer minimal resistance to exhaust flow and should need little maintenance to prevent clogging.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"What should a dryer wall cap offer in flow resistance and maintenance?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:def-damper","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"A termination device damper is installed on the last exhaust penetration and keeps outside backdrafts and weather out of the duct.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"Where is a termination device damper installed and what does it keep out?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0107"]},{"id":"dedp:def-secondary-trap","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"A secondary lint trap is an extra lint screen that catches most lint bound for the duct, protecting the duct and any booster fan, and serves as a required cleanout for vertical risers.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"What does a secondary lint trap do, and when is it a required cleanout?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0108"]},{"id":"dedp:def-dryer","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"The DEDP defines a dryer as an appliance that takes out moisture by forced draught, by heating, or by centrifuging.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"How does the DEDP define a dryer?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:def-class0","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"Class 0 transition ducts and air connectors have surface burning characteristics of zero.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"What defines a Class 0 flexible transition duct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0106"]},{"id":"dedp:def-class1","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dedp-system","tag":"DEF","text":"Class 1 transition ducts and air connectors have a flame-spread index not over 25 and a smoke-developed index not over 50, with no continued progressive combustion.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"What limits define a Class 1 flexible transition duct?","trap":"Flame spread 25, smoke 50. Swapping them is the classic miss.","num":{"q":"Class 1 transition ducts: flame-spread index and smoke-developed index not over __, respectively.","a":"25 and 50","d":["50 and 25","0 and 50","0 and 25"]},"qs":["dvt-0106"]},{"id":"dedp:1.1-confirm-clean","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-pretest","tag":"SHALL","text":"Pre-test verification: confirm the dryer exhaust duct is clean before running the performance test.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.1","prompt":"What is the first pre-test verification before a DEDP performance test?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0065"]},{"id":"dedp:1.2-access","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-pretest","tag":"SHALL","text":"Three things shall be accessible for the DEDP test: the existing transition duct (which means moving or removing the dryer), its connection to the exhaust duct, and the termination penetration.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.2, §1.2.1 to §1.2.3","prompt":"Which three components or areas shall be accessible to perform the DEDP test?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0070"]},{"id":"dedp:1.2.3-termination","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-pretest","tag":"DEF","text":"The termination penetration is where the duct discharges, at an exterior wall, eave or roof; access there reaches the termination device and termination barrier.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.2.3","prompt":"What is the termination penetration, and why must it be accessible?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:1.3-lint-checks","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-pretest","tag":"SHALL","text":"Before the test, visual observations shall determine whether non-adhered lint has been removed, whether adhered substances remain in the permanent duct, and whether the termination barrier is visibly lint-free.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.3, §1.3.1, §1.3.2, §1.3.5","prompt":"Before the DEDP test, which lint-related conditions shall visual observations determine?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:1.3-damage-checks","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-pretest","tag":"SHALL","text":"The visual observations shall also determine whether the duct is deformed, damaged or crushed, whether the termination damper is in place, and whether significant leakage is apparent.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.3.3, §1.3.4, §1.3.6","prompt":"Which damage, damper and leakage conditions shall the pre-test visual observations determine?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0071"]},{"id":"dedp:1.3.7-hold","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-pretest","tag":"REC","text":"If visual observation finds a Section 1.3 condition, it is recommended the DEDP test not proceed until the condition has been remedied or reported.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.3.7","prompt":"What is recommended when a pre-test visual observation finds a Section 1.3 condition?","trap":"Recommended, not shall. Reporting the condition satisfies it as well as fixing it.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0071"]},{"id":"dedp:1.4-short-duct","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-pretest","tag":"SHALL","text":"A short, straight duct under 6 ft with no turns that can be seen end to end shall still get the DEDP test, to check the termination damper's function and restriction.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.4","prompt":"Does a short, straight, fully visible dryer duct still require the DEDP test, and why?","trap":"Being short and visible does not exempt it. The damper can still restrict flow.","num":{"q":"Section 1.4 treats a straight dryer duct with no turns as short when it is less than __ long.","a":"6 ft","d":["4 ft","5 ft","10 ft"]},"qs":["dvt-0072"]},{"id":"dedp:1.5-contiguous","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-pretest","tag":"SHALL","text":"The DEDP test shall only be performed on 4 in ducts, and that 4 in diameter must be contiguous from the dryer transition point to the exterior termination device.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.5","prompt":"What duct diameter does the DEDP test require, and over what length must it be contiguous?","trap":"A run that changes size anywhere between transition point and termination does not qualify.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0073"]},{"id":"dedp:1.6-booster","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-pretest","tag":"SHALL","text":"Before the DEDP test, it shall be determined whether a dryer booster fan is present and operational.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.6","prompt":"What shall be determined about a dryer booster fan before the DEDP test?","trap":"Booster fan: present and operational. Secondary lint trap: present and clean.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0074"]},{"id":"dedp:1.7-secondary-trap","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-pretest","tag":"SHALL","text":"Before the DEDP test, it shall be determined whether a secondary lint trap is present and clean.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.7","prompt":"What shall be determined about a secondary lint trap before the DEDP test?","trap":"Lint trap: present and clean. Booster fan: present and operational.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0074","dvt-0188"]},{"id":"dedp:1.9-code-check","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dedp-pretest","tag":"SHALL","text":"During pre-test inspection, verify the transition duct complies with local, state and federal code or the Authority Having Jurisdiction.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.9","prompt":"What code check does the DEDP pre-test inspection call for on the transition duct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0076","dvt-0165","dvt-0205"]},{"id":"dedp:1.9-inform","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dedp-pretest","tag":"SHALL","text":"The consumer must be told which type of transition duct fails to comply.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.9","prompt":"What must the consumer be told when the transition duct is not code compliant?","trap":"Must, not should. The standard requires telling the consumer, not replacing the duct.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0076","dvt-0165","dvt-0205"]},{"id":"dedp:1.8-actual","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"SHALL","text":"Actual length shall be established during cleaning, using measured fiberglass cleaning rods, cable drives, or air whipping tools.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.8","prompt":"When and how shall the actual length of the dryer exhaust duct be determined?","trap":"Pre-test gives an estimate. The actual length is measured while cleaning.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0075","dvt-0159","dvt-0196"]},{"id":"dedp:1.8.2-inspect","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"SHALL","text":"Inspect the system's connections and configuration, then estimate total run length from the feet of hard pipe together with the number of hard elbows in the run.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.8.2; §4.1.1","prompt":"What two quantities go into the estimated total run length of the permanent duct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:1.8.3-5ft","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"SHALL","text":"Following IMC and IRC specifications, each hard elbow shall count as 5 ft of hard pipe when the total run length is estimated.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.8.3; §4.1.2","prompt":"How many feet of hard pipe shall each hard elbow count as when estimating run length?","trap":"5 ft is the 4 in radius mitered 90 value. Smooth long-radius elbows have much smaller equivalents.","num":{"q":"Each hard elbow shall be counted as __ of hard pipe when estimating total run length.","a":"5 ft","d":["2 ft 6 in","1 ft 9 in","10 ft"]},"qs":["dvt-0031","dvt-0032"]},{"id":"dedp:len-example","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"DEF","text":"Using the 5 ft per elbow rule, 20 ft of hard pipe with two hard elbows estimates to a 30 ft developed length.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.8.3","prompt":"What developed length do 20 ft of hard pipe and two hard elbows estimate to?","trap":"","num":{"q":"20 ft of hard pipe plus two hard elbows estimates to __ developed length.","a":"30 ft","d":["20 ft","25 ft","40 ft"]},"qs":["dvt-0090","dvt-0091","dvt-0093","dvt-0094","dvt-0095","dvt-0097","dvt-0098","dvt-0100"]},{"id":"dedp:1.8.4-location","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"SHALL","text":"Document whether the dryer vent terminates at the side wall, the roof, or the soffit of the house.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.8.4","prompt":"Which termination locations does the pre-test inspection document for the dryer vent?","trap":"Location decides which performance table applies: side wall or roof top.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:4.1-record","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"SHALL","text":"During pre-inspection, technicians must estimate and record the permanent duct's total developed (run) length.","src":"DEDP 2014 §1.8; §4.1","prompt":"What must technicians estimate and record about run length during DEDP pre-inspection?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:def-actual","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"DEF","text":"Actual length is the dryer exhaust duct's full measured length, with nothing added for fittings.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"How does the DEDP define actual length?","trap":"Actual length adds nothing for fittings. Developed length adds the fitting equivalents.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0105","dvt-0196","dvt-0197"]},{"id":"dedp:def-developed","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"DEF","text":"Developed length is the actual duct length plus the equivalent length of all installed fittings, taken from the IMC and IRC friction loss table for 90 and 45 degree turns.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"How does the DEDP define developed length?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0105","dvt-0197"]},{"id":"dedp:def-equivalent","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"DEF","text":"Equivalent length expresses the flow resistance of a transition, elbow, bend, fitting or obstruction as the feet of straight same-diameter duct with equal resistance.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"How does the DEDP define equivalent length?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:t504-m45","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"CODE","text":"In IMC 2012 Table 504.6.4.1, a 4 in radius mitered 45 degree elbow equals 2 ft 6 in of duct.","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 504.6.4.1 (IMC 2012)","prompt":"What is the equivalent length of a 4 in radius mitered 45 degree elbow?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A 4 in radius mitered 45 degree elbow has an equivalent length of __.","a":"2 ft 6 in","d":["5 ft","1 ft","1 ft 9 in"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:t504-m90","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"CODE","text":"In IMC 2012 Table 504.6.4.1, a 4 in radius mitered 90 degree elbow equals 5 ft of duct.","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 504.6.4.1 (IMC 2012)","prompt":"What is the equivalent length of a 4 in radius mitered 90 degree elbow?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A 4 in radius mitered 90 degree elbow has an equivalent length of __.","a":"5 ft","d":["2 ft 6 in","1 ft 9 in","1 ft 7 in"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:t504-s6-45","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"CODE","text":"In IMC 2012 Table 504.6.4.1, a 6 in radius smooth 45 degree elbow equals 1 ft of duct.","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 504.6.4.1 (IMC 2012)","prompt":"What is the equivalent length of a 6 in radius smooth 45 degree elbow?","trap":"6 in and 8 in smooth 45s are both 1 ft. Only the 10 in smooth 45 drops to 9 in.","num":{"q":"A 6 in radius smooth 45 degree elbow has an equivalent length of __.","a":"1 ft","d":["9 in","2 ft 6 in","1 ft 9 in"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:t504-s6-90","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"CODE","text":"In IMC 2012 Table 504.6.4.1, a 6 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow equals 1 ft 9 in of duct.","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 504.6.4.1 (IMC 2012)","prompt":"What is the equivalent length of a 6 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A 6 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow has an equivalent length of __.","a":"1 ft 9 in","d":["1 ft 7 in","1 ft 6 in","5 ft"]},"qs":["dvt-0029"]},{"id":"dedp:t504-s8-45","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"CODE","text":"In IMC 2012 Table 504.6.4.1, an 8 in radius smooth 45 degree elbow equals 1 ft of duct.","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 504.6.4.1 (IMC 2012)","prompt":"What is the equivalent length of an 8 in radius smooth 45 degree elbow?","trap":"","num":{"q":"An 8 in radius smooth 45 degree elbow has an equivalent length of __.","a":"1 ft","d":["9 in","1 ft 7 in","2 ft 6 in"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:t504-s8-90","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"CODE","text":"In IMC 2012 Table 504.6.4.1, an 8 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow equals 1 ft 7 in of duct.","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 504.6.4.1 (IMC 2012)","prompt":"What is the equivalent length of an 8 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow?","trap":"","num":{"q":"An 8 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow has an equivalent length of __.","a":"1 ft 7 in","d":["1 ft 9 in","1 ft 6 in","1 ft"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:t504-s10-45","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"CODE","text":"In IMC 2012 Table 504.6.4.1, a 10 in radius smooth 45 degree elbow equals 9 in of duct.","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 504.6.4.1 (IMC 2012)","prompt":"What is the equivalent length of a 10 in radius smooth 45 degree elbow?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A 10 in radius smooth 45 degree elbow has an equivalent length of __.","a":"9 in","d":["1 ft","1 ft 6 in","2 ft 6 in"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:t504-s10-90","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"CODE","text":"In IMC 2012 Table 504.6.4.1, a 10 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow equals 1 ft 6 in of duct.","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 504.6.4.1 (IMC 2012)","prompt":"What is the equivalent length of a 10 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A 10 in radius smooth 90 degree elbow has an equivalent length of __.","a":"1 ft 6 in","d":["1 ft 7 in","1 ft 9 in","9 in"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:imc-mfr-max","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"CODE","text":"Under IMC 2012 504.6.4.2, reprinted in the DEDP, the dryer manufacturer's installation instructions set the maximum exhaust duct length.","src":"DEDP 2014, p. 13 (IMC 2012 §504.6.4.2)","prompt":"Under IMC 2012 504.6.4.2, what sets the maximum length of a dryer exhaust duct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:imc-code-official","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"CODE","text":"The code official shall get the installation instructions for the dryer make and model; for a concealed duct, before the concealment inspection.","src":"DEDP 2014, p. 13 (IMC 2012 §504.6.4.2)","prompt":"When shall the code official receive the dryer's installation instructions if the duct will be concealed?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:imc-table-default","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-length","tag":"CODE","text":"When the dryer manufacturer gives no fitting equivalent length figures, the code requires using Table 504.6.4.1.","src":"DEDP 2014, p. 13 (IMC 2012 §504.6.4.2)","prompt":"When shall Table 504.6.4.1 be used for fitting equivalent lengths?","trap":"The manufacturer's fitting figures come first. The table is the fallback.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0039"]},{"id":"dedp:def-device","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-equipment","tag":"DEF","text":"The dryer exhaust duct testing device is a NADCA-designed unit that attaches to the duct and produces repeatable pressure and velocity from a known constant.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"What is the dryer exhaust duct testing device?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:2.1-motor","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-equipment","tag":"SHALL","text":"The test booster fan has a 113 W motor with a sealed bearing system built for continuous use, plus 4 in inlet and outlet for ducting.","src":"DEDP 2014 §2.1","prompt":"What motor rating, bearing type and port size does the DEDP booster fan have?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The DEDP booster fan has a __ motor.","a":"113 W","d":["110 W","190 W","200 W"]},"qs":["dvt-0077"]},{"id":"dedp:2.1-rpm","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-equipment","tag":"SHALL","text":"The DEDP booster fan generates 2,910 RPM.","src":"DEDP 2014 §2.1","prompt":"What speed does the DEDP booster fan generate?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The DEDP booster fan generates __.","a":"2,910 RPM","d":["1,700 RPM","2,000 RPM","1,900 RPM"]},"qs":["dvt-0077"]},{"id":"dedp:2.1-cfm","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-equipment","tag":"SHALL","text":"The DEDP booster fan has a true rated air volume of 190 CFM.","src":"DEDP 2014 §2.1","prompt":"What true rated air volume does the DEDP booster fan deliver?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The DEDP booster fan has a true rated air volume of __.","a":"190 CFM","d":["113 CFM","200 CFM","100 CFM"]},"qs":["dvt-0077"]},{"id":"dedp:2.2-gauge","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-equipment","tag":"SHALL","text":"The differential pressure gauge must be able to measure 0 to 2.0 in wc.","src":"DEDP 2014 §2.2; Definitions","prompt":"What range must the DEDP differential pressure gauge be able to measure?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The DEDP specifies __ as the differential pressure gauge's measuring range.","a":"0 to 2.0 in wc","d":["0 to 1.0 in wc","0 to 0.5 in wc","0 to 5.0 in wc"]},"qs":["dvt-0078"]},{"id":"dedp:2.2.1-digital","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-equipment","tag":"MAY","text":"A digital manometer can be used in place of the analog differential pressure gauge.","src":"DEDP 2014 §2.2.1","prompt":"Can a digital manometer be used in place of the analog differential pressure gauge?","trap":"Permission, not a requirement. Only non-digital gauges must sit vertical and upright.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0078"]},{"id":"dedp:2.3-tap","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-equipment","tag":"DEF","text":"A pressure tap fitting, permanent or temporary, penetrates the test duct so the gauge's connection tube can read the internal pressure differential.","src":"DEDP 2014 §2.3; Definitions","prompt":"What does a pressure tap fitting do in the DEDP test?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:2.4-tubing","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-equipment","tag":"SHALL","text":"The tap connection tubing is 1/8 in inside diameter and 1/4 in outside diameter, linking the gauge's pressure port to the test duct.","src":"DEDP 2014 §2.4","prompt":"What size tubing connects the pressure tap fitting to the gauge?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Pressure tap connection tubing is __.","a":"1/8 in ID and 1/4 in OD","d":["1/4 in ID and 1/8 in OD","1/8 in ID and 1/2 in OD","1/4 in ID and 1/2 in OD"]},"qs":["dvt-0079"]},{"id":"dedp:2.5-test-duct","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-equipment","tag":"SHALL","text":"The test duct links the test fan to the permanent duct at the wall; it must keep a continuous 4 in interior diameter whether compressed or stretched, and comply with the AHJ.","src":"DEDP 2014 §2.5; §2.5.1","prompt":"What does the DEDP test duct connect, and what must it maintain and comply with?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0081"]},{"id":"dedp:def-test-duct","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-equipment","tag":"DEF","text":"The test duct is 4 to 6 ft of semi-rigid duct with a continuous 4 in interior diameter whether compressed or stretched.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"How long is the DEDP test duct and what type of duct is it?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The DEDP test duct is __ of semi-rigid duct.","a":"4 to 6 ft","d":["2 to 4 ft","6 to 8 ft","8 to 10 ft"]},"qs":["dvt-0081"]},{"id":"dedp:2.5.2-length","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-equipment","tag":"SHOULD","text":"The test duct should be only as long as needed to reach the dryer exhaust duct connection, with as few turns or bends as possible.","src":"DEDP 2014 §2.5.2","prompt":"How long should the test duct be, and how many bends should it have?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:2.6-anemometer","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-equipment","tag":"SHALL","text":"The rotating vane anemometer must have a minimum velocity measurement range of 50 to 2000 fpm; the standard also lists 0.25 to 30 m/s.","src":"DEDP 2014 §2.6","prompt":"What minimum velocity range must the DEDP rotating vane anemometer cover?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Section 2.6 specifies __ as the anemometer's minimum velocity measurement range.","a":"50 to 2000 fpm","d":["100 to 2000 fpm","50 to 1700 fpm","0 to 1200 fpm"]},"qs":["dvt-0080"]},{"id":"dedp:2.6-fpm","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-equipment","tag":"SHALL","text":"The anemometer shall report velocity in feet per minute (fpm).","src":"DEDP 2014 §2.6","prompt":"In what scale shall the DEDP anemometer report velocity?","trap":"The metric range is listed, but readings shall be reported in fpm.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0080"]},{"id":"dedp:def-anemometer","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-equipment","tag":"DEF","text":"A rotating vane anemometer measures uneven or fluctuating flows; it reads velocity and temperature, calculates flow rate, averages, and can capture minimum and maximum readings.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"What can a rotating vane anemometer measure and calculate?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:3.0.2-no-dryer-blower","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHALL","text":"The DEDP test shall never be performed using the dryer's own blower.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.0.2","prompt":"Can the dryer's blower be used to perform the DEDP test?","trap":"The appendix allows an optional dryer-driven velocity check, but that is never the DEDP test.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0068"]},{"id":"dedp:3.1.1-step1","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHALL","text":"Step 1: pull the dryer from the wall and remove the existing transition duct from the 4 in port in the drywall, clearing access to the start of the permanent duct.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.1.1","prompt":"What is Step 1 of the DEDP test?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:3.1.2-step2","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHALL","text":"Step 2: replace the existing transition duct with the test duct and install a pressure tap fitting in the test duct.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.1.2","prompt":"What is Step 2 of the DEDP test?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:3.1.2-tap-spot","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHOULD","text":"The tap fitting goes halfway along the test duct, on the top center or an outside bend; on a 4 ft test duct it should sit at the 2 ft mark.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.1.2","prompt":"Where on the test duct should the pressure tap fitting be installed?","trap":"Midpoint of whatever test duct is used (2 ft on 4 ft), placed on top center or an outside bend.","num":{"q":"With a 4 ft test duct, the tap fitting should be installed at the __ mark.","a":"2 ft","d":["1 ft","3 ft","4 ft"]},"qs":["dvt-0082"]},{"id":"dedp:3.1.3-step3","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHALL","text":"Step 3: hook up the gauge by running clear tubing from the tap fitting's exposed tip to the gauge's positive pressure input.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.1.3","prompt":"What is Step 3 of the DEDP test, and which gauge port is used?","trap":"Positive input. The test device pushes air into the duct.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0083"]},{"id":"dedp:3.1.3.1-vertical","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHALL","text":"Non-digital pressure differential gauges shall sit vertical and upright to read accurately.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.1.3.1","prompt":"How shall a non-digital pressure gauge be positioned during the DEDP test?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0083"]},{"id":"dedp:3.1.3.2-tubing-length","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"DEF","text":"The gauge tubing is at least 4 to 6 ft long so the gauge can rest on a flat surface during the test.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.1.3.2","prompt":"How long is the gauge tubing, and why?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The standard gives __ as the minimum gauge tubing length, so the gauge can rest on a flat surface.","a":"4 to 6 ft","d":["2 to 3 ft","6 to 8 ft","8 to 10 ft"]},"qs":["dvt-0084"]},{"id":"dedp:3.1.4-step4","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHOULD","text":"Step 4: connect the testing device; its blower should attach to one end of the test duct that now holds the tap fitting.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.1.4","prompt":"What is Step 4 of the DEDP test?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:3.1.5-step5","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHALL","text":"Step 5: attach the test duct to the 4 in exhaust port in the drywall, using worm-drive clamps or equivalent for a secure connection to the hard pipe.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.1.5","prompt":"What is Step 5 of the DEDP test, and how is the connection secured?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0088"]},{"id":"dedp:3.1.6-step6","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHALL","text":"Step 6: energize the testing device by plugging into the 110 outlet or using its on/off switch, and let it run at least one minute.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.1.6","prompt":"What is Step 6 of the DEDP test, and how long does the device run?","trap":"","num":{"q":"In Step 6, the minimum run time for the energized testing device is __.","a":"1 minute","d":["30 seconds","2 minutes","5 minutes"]},"qs":["dvt-0085","dvt-0088"]},{"id":"dedp:3.1.6.1-booster","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHALL","text":"With an inline booster fan, allow time for its pressure switch to engage, typically 30 seconds to one minute after energizing the test device.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.1.6.1","prompt":"How long does a booster fan pressure switch typically take to engage after the test device starts?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A booster fan pressure switch typically engages __ after the test device is energized.","a":"30 seconds to 1 minute","d":["10 to 20 seconds","1 to 2 minutes","3 to 5 minutes"]},"qs":["dvt-0086"]},{"id":"dedp:3.1.7-step7","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHALL","text":"Step 7: after the test device pressure stabilizes, record the reading on the pressure differential gauge.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.1.7","prompt":"What is Step 7 of the DEDP test, and when is the reading taken?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0085"]},{"id":"dedp:3.2.1-step8","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHALL","text":"Step 8: measure velocity at the exterior termination with the rotating vane anemometer. Readings shall come from air leaving the termination device, documented in fpm.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.2.1","prompt":"What is Step 8 of the DEDP test, and where shall readings be taken?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:3.2.1.1-three","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHOULD","text":"At least three velocity readings should be taken in different positions, such as the top, bottom and side of the termination.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.2.1.1","prompt":"How many velocity readings should be taken at the termination, and where?","trap":"Should, not shall. Then record the maximum, not the average.","num":{"q":"The minimum number of velocity readings that should be taken at the termination is __.","a":"3","d":["2","4","5"]},"qs":["dvt-0087"]},{"id":"dedp:3.2.2-step9","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHALL","text":"Step 9: compare the recorded back pressure (wc) and termination velocity (fpm) with the side wall and roof top performance tables, 4.9 and 4.10.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.2.2","prompt":"What is Step 9 of the DEDP test?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:3.2.2.1-below","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-steps","tag":"SHALL","text":"If back pressure or velocity misses the minimum requirements, check the Appendix list of conditions that could be causing the performance problem.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.2.2.1","prompt":"Which part of the DEDP document does the tech reference when back pressure or velocity falls below the minimum?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:4.6-cross-ref","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"After the test, documentation cross-references three data points, estimated developed length, back pressure and maximum velocity, against Tables 4.9 and 4.10 to reach a pass or fail.","src":"DEDP 2014 §4.0; §4.6","prompt":"Which three data points are cross-referenced against the performance tables, and what do they produce?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:4.2-back-pressure","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"SHOULD","text":"Record the specific back pressure in water column inches; the reading should land between 0.0 and 2.0 in wc.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.1.7; §4.2","prompt":"In what unit is back pressure recorded, and what range should the value fall in?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:4.3-max-velocity","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"Record the highest velocity reading, in fpm, found at the termination point during the test.","src":"DEDP 2014 §3.2.1.1; §4.3","prompt":"Which velocity reading is recorded from the termination?","trap":"The maximum reading, not the average or the first one.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0087","dvt-0101"]},{"id":"dedp:4.4-transition","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"After the test the dryer is reconnected with transition duct; record the type used, whether the customer's existing duct or a new one that was offered.","src":"DEDP 2014 §4.4","prompt":"What is recorded about the transition duct after the DEDP test?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0089"]},{"id":"dedp:4.5-adhered","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"If visual observations show an adhered substance, indicate it and record what was found.","src":"DEDP 2014 §4.5","prompt":"What is documented when visual observations show an adhered substance?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0307"]},{"id":"dedp:4.6.1-table-choice","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"Use the performance table that matches the termination location: one table for side wall terminations, a separate one for roof terminations.","src":"DEDP 2014 §4.6.1","prompt":"How does the tech choose which performance table to use?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:4.7-fail","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"DEF","text":"A duct fails when back pressure, velocity, or both miss the table values for its developed length and termination location; the Appendix then lists likely causes.","src":"DEDP 2014 §4.7","prompt":"When does a dryer exhaust duct fail the DEDP test, and where does the tech look next?","trap":"One bad reading is enough to fail. Both readings must meet the row.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:4.8-offer","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"MAY","text":"Based on the DEDP results, the technician may choose to offer more solutions or services to improve the duct's performance.","src":"DEDP 2014 §4.8","prompt":"What may the technician offer the customer based on DEDP results?","trap":"May, not shall. The standard does not require selling or doing repairs.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:4.8.1-retest","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"MAY","text":"The technician may elect to run the DEDP test again after solutions or services are provided.","src":"DEDP 2014 §4.8.1","prompt":"May the DEDP test be repeated after solutions or services are provided?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:app-function","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"DEF","text":"The test's primary function is to capture airflow velocity and back pressure together to see whether the values fall within an acceptable range.","src":"DEDP 2014, Appendix","prompt":"What is the primary function of the DEDP performance test?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:app-not-obligated","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"DEF","text":"The Appendix list does not mean the contractor must perform any of those services, and many items are not the dryer exhaust service provider's direct responsibility.","src":"DEDP 2014, Appendix","prompt":"Does the Appendix conditions list obligate the contractor to perform those services?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:app-should-address","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"SHOULD","text":"The Appendix conditions should be addressed when DEDP results fall below minimum values.","src":"DEDP 2014, Appendix","prompt":"When should the Appendix conditions and areas of concern be addressed?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:app-design","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"DEF","text":"Possible design causes of a fail include a developed length too long for the manufacturer's specification, a wall cavity used as a riser, and too many elbows restricting airflow.","src":"DEDP 2014, Appendix","prompt":"Which duct layout conditions does the Appendix list as causes of poor performance?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0264"]},{"id":"dedp:app-leaks","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"DEF","text":"Possible leak causes of a fail include leaking longitudinal seams on snap-lock pipe and elbow or connection joints that leak or are not taped.","src":"DEDP 2014, Appendix","prompt":"Which leakage conditions does the Appendix list as causes of poor performance?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0264","dvt-0306"]},{"id":"dedp:app-termination","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"DEF","text":"Possible termination causes include an overly restrictive bird or rodent screen, assumed already serviced before the test, and a damper that is too small or restricted by its location.","src":"DEDP 2014, Appendix","prompt":"Which termination conditions does the Appendix list as causes of poor performance?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:app-moisture","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"DEF","text":"Possible moisture causes include water condensing and collecting inside the duct and a duct pitched incorrectly.","src":"DEDP 2014, Appendix","prompt":"Which moisture and slope conditions does the Appendix list as causes of poor performance?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0166"]},{"id":"dedp:app-cement","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"DEF","text":"Lint mixed with water can solidify into a cementitious mass that direct agitation cannot remove, requiring advanced procedures.","src":"DEDP 2014, Appendix","prompt":"What Appendix condition cannot be removed with direct agitation, and what does it need?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0193","dvt-0267"]},{"id":"dedp:app-still-blocked","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"REC","text":"If the duct may still be blocked or crushed after servicing, a video scope or physically dismantling the ductwork is recommended to find the cause.","src":"DEDP 2014, Appendix","prompt":"What is recommended when a serviced duct may still be blocked or crushed?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0102","dvt-0160","dvt-0195"]},{"id":"dedp:app-noncode","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"DEF","text":"Duct materials that do not comply with code are listed as a possible cause of poor performance.","src":"DEDP 2014, Appendix","prompt":"Which material problem does the Appendix list as a cause of poor performance?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:app-appliance-may","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"MAY","text":"Termination discharge velocity may be measured with the dryer in its final position, running empty in non-heat mode, to see whether a significant pressure differential exists versus the test device.","src":"DEDP 2014, Appendix","prompt":"How may the dryer's own discharge velocity be measured, and why?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0103"]},{"id":"dedp:app-appliance-should","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"SHOULD","text":"If a significant differential exists between the appliance and the test device, the dryer should be evaluated for proper operation by a qualified service provider.","src":"DEDP 2014, Appendix","prompt":"What should happen if the dryer's discharge differs significantly from the test device's?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0187"]},{"id":"dedp:app-appliance-optional","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-results","tag":"DEF","text":"The appliance velocity measurement is supplemental data to the DEDP test and is not a requirement of the standard.","src":"DEDP 2014, Appendix","prompt":"Is the dryer appliance velocity measurement required by the DEDP standard?","trap":"Optional and supplemental. The DEDP test itself shall never be run with the dryer blower.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0103","dvt-0204"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-rows","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"Performance Tables 4.9 (side wall) and 4.10 (roof top) set minimum requirements for total developed lengths of 10, 20, 30, 40, and 50 ft or more.","src":"DEDP 2014, Tables 4.9 and 4.10","prompt":"Which developed-length rows do the DEDP performance tables use?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-direction","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"NOTE","text":"In both tables, back pressure must read below the listed wc value, while velocity must reach at least the listed fpm.","src":"DEDP 2014, Tables 4.9 and 4.10","prompt":"How are the back pressure and velocity values in the performance tables applied?","trap":"Pressure is a ceiling, velocity is a floor. High pressure fails; low velocity fails.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-roof-offset","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"NOTE","text":"Every roof top back pressure limit is the side wall limit for the same row plus 0.20 in wc; velocity minimums are the same in both tables.","src":"DEDP 2014, Tables 4.9 and 4.10","prompt":"How do the roof top limits compare with the side wall limits row by row?","trap":"0.75 in wc appears twice: side wall 40 ft and roof 20 ft.","num":{"q":"Each roof top back pressure limit equals the side wall limit plus __.","a":"0.20 in wc","d":["0.10 in wc","0.25 in wc","0.30 in wc"]},"qs":["dvt-0131"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-sw10","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"For a side wall termination with a developed length of 10 ft, back pressure must be under 0.30 in wc (Table 4.9).","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 4.9","prompt":"What is the side wall back pressure limit at 10 ft of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Table 4.9 lists __ as the side wall back pressure limit at 10 ft developed length.","a":"0.30 in wc","d":["0.50 in wc","0.55 in wc","0.65 in wc"]},"qs":["dvt-0094"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-sw20","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"For a side wall termination with a developed length of 20 ft, back pressure must be under 0.55 in wc (Table 4.9).","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 4.9","prompt":"What is the side wall back pressure limit at 20 ft of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Table 4.9 lists __ as the side wall back pressure limit at 20 ft developed length.","a":"0.55 in wc","d":["0.30 in wc","0.65 in wc","0.75 in wc"]},"qs":["dvt-0090"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-sw30","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"For a side wall termination with a developed length of 30 ft, back pressure must be under 0.65 in wc (Table 4.9).","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 4.9","prompt":"What is the side wall back pressure limit at 30 ft of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Table 4.9 lists __ as the side wall back pressure limit at 30 ft developed length.","a":"0.65 in wc","d":["0.55 in wc","0.75 in wc","0.85 in wc"]},"qs":["dvt-0095","dvt-0096"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-sw40","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"For a side wall termination with a developed length of 40 ft, back pressure must be under 0.75 in wc (Table 4.9).","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 4.9","prompt":"What is the side wall back pressure limit at 40 ft of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Table 4.9 lists __ as the side wall back pressure limit at 40 ft developed length.","a":"0.75 in wc","d":["0.65 in wc","0.80 in wc","0.95 in wc"]},"qs":["dvt-0092"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-sw50","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"For a side wall termination with a developed length of 50 ft or more, back pressure must be under 0.80 in wc (Table 4.9).","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 4.9","prompt":"What is the side wall back pressure limit at 50 ft or more of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Table 4.9 lists __ as the side wall back pressure limit at 50 ft or more developed length.","a":"0.80 in wc","d":["0.75 in wc","0.95 in wc","1.00 in wc"]},"qs":["dvt-0098"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-rf10","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"For a roof top termination with a developed length of 10 ft, back pressure must be under 0.50 in wc (Table 4.10).","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 4.10","prompt":"What is the roof top back pressure limit at 10 ft of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Table 4.10 lists __ as the roof top back pressure limit at 10 ft developed length.","a":"0.50 in wc","d":["0.30 in wc","0.55 in wc","0.75 in wc"]},"qs":["dvt-0099"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-rf20","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"For a roof top termination with a developed length of 20 ft, back pressure must be under 0.75 in wc (Table 4.10).","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 4.10","prompt":"What is the roof top back pressure limit at 20 ft of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Table 4.10 lists __ as the roof top back pressure limit at 20 ft developed length.","a":"0.75 in wc","d":["0.50 in wc","0.55 in wc","0.85 in wc"]},"qs":["dvt-0097","dvt-0101"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-rf30","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"For a roof top termination with a developed length of 30 ft, back pressure must be under 0.85 in wc (Table 4.10).","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 4.10","prompt":"What is the roof top back pressure limit at 30 ft of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Table 4.10 lists __ as the roof top back pressure limit at 30 ft developed length.","a":"0.85 in wc","d":["0.65 in wc","0.75 in wc","0.95 in wc"]},"qs":["dvt-0091","dvt-0096"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-rf40","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"For a roof top termination with a developed length of 40 ft, back pressure must be under 0.95 in wc (Table 4.10).","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 4.10","prompt":"What is the roof top back pressure limit at 40 ft of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Table 4.10 lists __ as the roof top back pressure limit at 40 ft developed length.","a":"0.95 in wc","d":["0.75 in wc","0.85 in wc","1.00 in wc"]},"qs":["dvt-0100"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-rf50","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"For a roof top termination with a developed length of 50 ft or more, back pressure must be under 1.00 in wc (Table 4.10).","src":"DEDP 2014, Table 4.10","prompt":"What is the roof top back pressure limit at 50 ft or more of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Roof top, 50 ft or more developed length: back pressure must be under __.","a":"1.00 in wc","d":["0.80 in wc","0.85 in wc","0.95 in wc"]},"qs":["dvt-0093"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-v10","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"At a developed length of 10 ft, both the side wall and roof top tables require a termination velocity of at least 1700 fpm.","src":"DEDP 2014, Tables 4.9 and 4.10","prompt":"What minimum termination velocity is required at 10 ft of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Tables 4.9 and 4.10 list __ as the minimum termination velocity at 10 ft developed length.","a":"1700 fpm","d":["1600 fpm","1500 fpm","1300 fpm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-v20","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"At a developed length of 20 ft, both the side wall and roof top tables require a termination velocity of at least 1600 fpm.","src":"DEDP 2014, Tables 4.9 and 4.10","prompt":"What minimum termination velocity is required at 20 ft of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Tables 4.9 and 4.10 list __ as the minimum termination velocity at 20 ft developed length.","a":"1600 fpm","d":["1700 fpm","1500 fpm","1300 fpm"]},"qs":["dvt-0090","dvt-0097","dvt-0101"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-v30","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"At a developed length of 30 ft, both the side wall and roof top tables require a termination velocity of at least 1500 fpm.","src":"DEDP 2014, Tables 4.9 and 4.10","prompt":"What minimum termination velocity is required at 30 ft of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Tables 4.9 and 4.10 list __ as the minimum termination velocity at 30 ft developed length.","a":"1500 fpm","d":["1600 fpm","1700 fpm","1300 fpm"]},"qs":["dvt-0091","dvt-0095","dvt-0096"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-v40","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"At a developed length of 40 ft, both the side wall and roof top tables require a termination velocity of at least 1300 fpm.","src":"DEDP 2014, Tables 4.9 and 4.10","prompt":"What minimum termination velocity is required at 40 ft of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Tables 4.9 and 4.10 list __ as the minimum termination velocity at 40 ft developed length.","a":"1300 fpm","d":["1500 fpm","1200 fpm","1600 fpm"]},"qs":["dvt-0092","dvt-0100"]},{"id":"dedp:tbl-v50","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-tables","tag":"DEF","text":"At a developed length of 50 ft or more, both the side wall and roof top tables require a termination velocity of at least 1200 fpm.","src":"DEDP 2014, Tables 4.9 and 4.10","prompt":"What minimum termination velocity is required at 50 ft or more of developed length?","trap":"","num":{"q":"At 50 ft or more developed length, termination velocity must be at least __.","a":"1200 fpm","d":["1300 fpm","1500 fpm","1600 fpm"]},"qs":["dvt-0093","dvt-0098"]},{"id":"dedp:def-shall","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"In the DEDP, shall marks a mandatory requirement; conforming to the standard allows no deviation where shall is used.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"What does shall mean in the DEDP standard?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0109"]},{"id":"dedp:def-recommended","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"In the DEDP, recommended means a practice is advised or suggested, not required by the standard.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"What does recommended mean in the DEDP standard?","trap":"Recommended items, like holding the test for a Section 1.3 condition, are advice, not shall.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0109"]},{"id":"dedp:def-dedp-test","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"The NADCA DEDP Test is NADCA's method for measuring dryer vent performance after servicing.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"How does the DEDP define the NADCA Dryer Exhaust Duct Performance Test?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:def-inspection","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An inspection is a gathering of information used to make determinations and assessments.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"How does the DEDP define an inspection?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:def-visual","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A visual inspection is a naked-eye examination of how clean the dryer vent system is.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"How does the DEDP define a visual inspection?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:def-adhered","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An adhered substance is material that a brushing device with silicon carbide bristles cannot remove by direct contact.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"What makes a deposit an adhered substance under the DEDP?","trap":"Adhered resists a silicon carbide bristle brush. Debris is non-adhered.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0104","dvt-0193","dvt-0307"]},{"id":"dedp:def-debris","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Debris is non-adhered material that is not meant to be inside the dryer exhaust duct system.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"How does the DEDP define debris?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:def-cementitious","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dedp-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Cementitious describes building materials that form a cement-like substance when mixed with a liquid such as water.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"How does the DEDP define cementitious?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:def-imc","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dedp-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"The IMC is a model code for designing and installing mechanical systems, appliances, appliance venting, ducts and ventilation, combustion air, and hydronic and solar systems.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"What does the International Mechanical Code regulate?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:def-irc","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dedp-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"The IRC is a stand-alone residential code with minimum rules for one- and two-family homes up to three stories, combining building, plumbing, mechanical, fuel gas, energy and electrical provisions.","src":"DEDP 2014, Definitions","prompt":"Which buildings does the International Residential Code cover?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The IRC covers one- and two-family dwellings up to a maximum of __.","a":"three stories","d":["two stories","four stories","five stories"]},"qs":["dvt-0064"]},{"id":"dedp:ref-codes","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dedp-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"The DEDP references cite IFGC 2012 Section 614 and IMC 2009 Section 504 for clothes dryer exhaust, along with the 2012 IRC.","src":"DEDP 2014, Reference Documents","prompt":"Which code sections does the DEDP reference list cite for clothes dryer exhaust?","trap":"The fitting table reprinted in the DEDP is from the 2012 IMC, but the reference list cites IMC 2009.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dedp:ref-ul2158a","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dedp-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"UL 2158A, Clothes Dryer Transition Duct, is the reference the DEDP lists for dryer transition ducts.","src":"DEDP 2014, Reference Documents","prompt":"Which UL standard does the DEDP list for clothes dryer transition duct?","trap":"UL 181 covers factory-made air ducts and connectors, not dryer transitions.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"dvt:airflow-path","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-dryer-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"A vented dryer draws room air in, heats it at the element or burner, tumbles it through the drum to pick up moisture and lint, then passes it through the lint filter and blower into the exhaust duct.","src":"CPSC 1999 dryer report p.2; DEDP 2014 Gen. Intro","prompt":"In what order does air pass the main parts of a vented clothes dryer?","trap":"The blower comes after the drum and lint filter; it pulls air through the drum and pushes it into the duct.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0110"]},{"id":"dvt:blower-positive-pressure","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-dryer-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"Because the blower sits after the drum and pushes air into the duct, the transition and main duct run under positive pressure. A leak or loose joint blows lint, moisture and, on gas units, combustion gases out.","src":"CPSC 1999 dryer report p.2; Fantech DEDPV-705 manual","prompt":"Why does a leaky or loose dryer duct joint blow lint out instead of drawing air in?","trap":"A leak does not draw attic or basement air into the duct; the duct is pressurized.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0111","dvt-0146","dvt-0147"]},{"id":"dvt:electric-240v","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-dryer-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"Only an electric dryer's heating element runs on 240 volts; its motor and timer run on 120. Electric models need a 120/240 volt, 30 amp circuit; gas models plug into a 120 volt outlet.","src":"CPSC 1999 dryer report p.2; Whirlpool W10096987","prompt":"Which part of an electric dryer runs on 240 volts, and what power does each dryer type need?","trap":"A gas dryer has no 240 volt heater; its motor and controls run on 120 volts and its heat is the burner.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0112","dvt-0113"]},{"id":"dvt:heat-waits-for-motor","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-dryer-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"In an electric dryer, heater power runs through the drive motor's centrifugal switch, so the element heats only once the motor reaches normal speed. That same motor turns the drum and moves the air.","src":"CPSC 1999 dryer report p.2","prompt":"Why does an electric dryer's element stay cold when the drive motor hums but does not turn?","trap":"The thermal fuse is normally closed and the operating thermostat opens to stop heat; neither is what waits for the motor.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0291"]},{"id":"dvt:gas-exhaust-co","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-dryer-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"A gas dryer's exhaust duct is also its flue, carrying burner combustion products including carbon monoxide. A blocked, disconnected or nest-choked duct can spill that exhaust into the home, so the dryer should not run until fixed.","src":"CPSC 1999 dryer report; CPSC 2002 CO release; GE gas dryer installation 31-16756","prompt":"Why is a blocked or disconnected duct more dangerous on a gas dryer than on an electric one?","trap":"The danger is exhaust backing up, not raw gas or the burner's air supply; the burner draws its air from the room.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0112","dvt-0153","dvt-0164","dvt-0260"]},{"id":"dvt:type1-type2","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-dryer-basics","tag":"DEF","text":"In the IFGC, a Type 1 clothes dryer is the household unit made for a family living environment. A Type 2 dryer is used in a business where the public uses it directly, such as a coin laundry.","src":"IFGC 2024 §202 Clothes dryer","prompt":"What separates a Type 1 clothes dryer from a Type 2 clothes dryer?","trap":"Class turns on business use by the public, not building type, fuel or airflow; apartment household dryers are Type 1.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0286"]},{"id":"dvt:exhaust-four-parts","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-dryer-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"NADCA's DVT outline splits a dryer exhaust system into four parts, in airflow order: the dryer interior, the transition duct, the main duct (the permanent rigid duct from the wall to outdoors) and the termination cover.","src":"NADCA DVT handout, Module 1; DEDP 2014 Definitions","prompt":"What four parts of a dryer exhaust system does NADCA's DVT outline name, in airflow order?","trap":"The transition is only the connector from dryer outlet to wall; a slipped attic joint is a main duct problem.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0292","dvt-0293"]},{"id":"dvt:gas-always-vented","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-dryer-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"Because of combustion safety risks, gas dryers are always vented. Ventless dryers, condensing or heat pump, are electric.","src":"ENERGY STAR Dryer Scoping Report","prompt":"Can a gas clothes dryer be installed as a ventless unit?","trap":"A CO alarm warns occupants; it never replaces venting a gas dryer.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0114"]},{"id":"dvt:condensing-dryer","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-dryer-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"A ventless condensing dryer cools its air in a heat exchanger and condenses the water into a drain line or collection tank. Its waste heat goes into the room, but the moisture does not.","src":"ENERGY STAR Dryer Scoping Report","prompt":"Where does the water removed from the clothes go in a ventless condensing dryer?","trap":"A condensate drain hose belongs to ventless condensing and heat pump dryers, not a vented steam dryer.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0115","dvt-0283"]},{"id":"dvt:heat-pump-dryer","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-dryer-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"A heat pump dryer moves its air in a closed loop, reheating it each pass while a refrigeration dehumidification system removes the moisture, so it needs no exhaust duct.","src":"ENERGY STAR Dryer Scoping Report","prompt":"How does a ventless heat pump dryer remove water from a load without an exhaust duct?","trap":"Cooling the air with sprayed cold water is a different condenser design, used mostly in all-in-one washer-dryers.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0116"]},{"id":"dvt:dryer-cfm","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-dryer-basics","tag":"FACT","text":"Whirlpool says any of its dryers moves at least 105 CFM at maximum rated vent length and no more than 230 CFM; ENERGY STAR puts a conventional dryer fan at 100 to 150 CFM.","src":"Whirlpool W11222085 p.4; ENERGY STAR Dryer Scoping Report","prompt":"How much air does a modern full-size dryer move through its vent, per Whirlpool's venting specification?","trap":"1,200 and 1,700 are velocities in fpm, not airflow volumes in CFM.","num":{"q":"Whirlpool says any of its dryers moves at least __ at its maximum rated vent length.","a":"105 CFM","d":["190 CFM","200 CFM","400 CFM"]},"qs":["dvt-0117"]},{"id":"dvt:operating-thermostat","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"The operating (cycling) thermostat sits in the exhaust airstream at the blower housing. It switches the element or burner off at its set point and back on as the air cools, roughly 120 to 160 F in normal operation.","src":"CPSC 1999 dryer report Tab C; Repair Clinic cycling thermostat guide","prompt":"Which dryer control switches the heat off and on during a normal load, and where does it sit?","trap":"Cycling during a load is normal; the high limit acts only on overheating, and a thermal fuse opens once.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0124"]},{"id":"dvt:high-limit","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"The high-limit thermostat sits at the heater box, set above the operating thermostat, and shuts off the heat source when that box overheats, as it does when a restricted vent cuts airflow.","src":"CPSC 1999 dryer report Tab C","prompt":"Which dryer control is placed to catch heater overheating caused by a restricted vent?","trap":"The cycling thermostat misses it: exhaust air runs cooler, not hotter, as flow drops.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0125"]},{"id":"dvt:thermal-fuse","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"A thermal fuse is a one-time overheat device: it opens, has no reset and must be replaced. Jumping it out defeats the safety circuit, so record the bypass and refer the dryer to an appliance technician.","src":"Repair Clinic thermal fuse guide; CPSC 1999 dryer report Tab C","prompt":"What is a dryer thermal fuse, and what should a tech do on finding one jumped out with a wire?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0127"]},{"id":"dvt:fuse-repeat-vent","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"Repeated thermal fuse failures usually trace to a blocked or partly blocked exhaust vent that makes the dryer run hot. Replacing the fuse without clearing the vent just sets up the next failure.","src":"Repair Clinic thermal fuse guide","prompt":"What should a tech check first when a dryer's thermal fuse keeps blowing?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0126"]},{"id":"dvt:cpsc-temps","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"In CPSC's 1999 tests, as lint built up in a restricted vent, the heat source and drum got hotter while the exhaust air got cooler, and the clothes stayed damp.","src":"CPSC 1999 dryer report Tab C","prompt":"What did CPSC measure at the heater and at the exhaust as lint built up in a restricted dryer vent?","trap":"A warm hood proves little; exhaust temperature falls, not rises, as the vent clogs.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0123","dvt-0295"]},{"id":"dvt:moisture-sensor","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"Sensor-dry models judge dryness by conductivity: wet clothes bridging the two metal bars in the drum conduct more current than dry ones. Dryer-sheet residue on the bars can spoil the reading.","src":"ENERGY STAR Dryer Scoping Report; Repair Clinic moisture sensor guide","prompt":"How do the two metal sensor bars in a sensor-dry dryer tell that the load is dry?","trap":"Reading exhaust air temperature is a different auto-dry method that does not use the bars.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0128"]},{"id":"dvt:af-code","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"Whirlpool's AF (Restricted Air Flow) or Check Vent alert means a clogged lint screen or vent, or a vent that is crushed, kinked or has too many turns. The dryer keeps running while the alert shows.","src":"Whirlpool 'AF or Check Vent' guidance","prompt":"What does a Whirlpool AF or Check Vent alert mean, and does the dryer stop?","trap":"The dryer does not shut down, so the alert is easy to ignore while drying times stretch.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0141","dvt-0296"]},{"id":"dvt:ul2158-high-limit","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA's 2017 report lists UL 2158 dryer safety changes effective March 2019; the one credited to CPSC bars dryers from running on the high limit indefinitely.","src":"NFPA 'Home Fires Involving Clothes Dryers and Washing Machines' (2017)","prompt":"Which CPSC-proposed UL 2158 dryer change, effective March 2019, does NFPA's 2017 report describe?","trap":"None of those changes regulate vent length or transition ducts; another cuts the heat if the drum stops.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0138"]},{"id":"dvt:fine-lint-passes","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"Even a clean lint screen lets fine lint through to the duct and hood, so the vent needs periodic cleaning on top of cleaning the screen every load.","src":"Whirlpool W11222085 p.4; CPSC Pub. 5022","prompt":"Why does a dryer vent still load up with lint when the owner cleans the screen every load?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0119"]},{"id":"dvt:velocity-carries-lint","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"Air velocity, not CFM, keeps lint suspended in a dryer duct; Whirlpool's venting guide sets 1,200 fpm as the minimum duct velocity.","src":"Whirlpool W11222085 p.4","prompt":"What keeps lint moving through a dryer duct, and what minimum velocity does Whirlpool set?","trap":"1,700 fpm is the DEDP 10 ft table minimum, not Whirlpool's duct minimum.","num":{"q":"Whirlpool's venting guide sets a minimum dryer duct velocity of __.","a":"1,200 fpm","d":["1,300 fpm","1,700 fpm","2,000 fpm"]},"qs":["dvt-0117","dvt-0118"]},{"id":"dvt:no-upsize","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"Whirlpool explains that with the same blower, duct larger than 4 inches lowers air velocity, so lint drops out of the airstream. Whirlpool approves only 4 inch rigid or flexible metal duct for its dryers.","src":"Whirlpool W11222085 p.5 'Alternate Duct Sizes'","prompt":"Why does Whirlpool's venting guide warn against replacing a 4 inch dryer run with 6 inch duct?","trap":"Less restriction is not automatically better: the same CFM in a bigger duct moves slower.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0118"]},{"id":"dvt:long-runs-lint","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"A longer or more restricted run slows the airflow, so lint settles out along the way; Whirlpool warns that exhaust systems longer than specified accumulate lint.","src":"Whirlpool install instructions 3395325; DEDP 2014 Gen. Intro","prompt":"Why does a long dryer run with several elbows load up with lint faster than a short straight one?","trap":"Elbows do not speed the air up; the added resistance slows it.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0120"]},{"id":"dvt:whirlpool-elbows","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"Whirlpool's venting guide cuts the allowable vent length by 10 ft for each added 90 degree elbow and by 6 ft for each added 45 degree elbow.","src":"Whirlpool W11222085 p.5 'Additional Elbows'","prompt":"How much does Whirlpool's venting guide cut allowable vent length for each added elbow?","trap":"Code table values for mitered elbows are 5 ft and 2.5 ft; Whirlpool's own are 10 and 6.","num":{"q":"Whirlpool's venting guide cuts allowable vent length by __ for each added 90 degree elbow.","a":"10 ft","d":["2.5 ft","5 ft","6 ft"]},"qs":["dvt-0121"]},{"id":"dvt:clog-slows-drying","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"A clogged vent slows moist air leaving the dryer, so drying time and energy use rise. The fixed-speed blower moves less air against the added resistance rather than speeding up.","src":"Whirlpool 'AF or Check Vent' guidance; DEDP 2014 Gen. Intro","prompt":"Besides fire risk, what does lint buildup in the vent do to drying time and energy use?","trap":"The blower does not speed up to hold its airflow; it simply moves less air.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0148","dvt-0295"]},{"id":"dvt:lint-screen-residue","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"Whirlpool says detergent and softener residue can clog a lint screen; lint falling off the screen in the dryer is the sign. Scrub it with a nylon brush, hot water and liquid detergent every six months or sooner.","src":"Whirlpool lint screen care guidance","prompt":"What does Whirlpool say to do when lint falls off a lint screen that is cleaned after every load?","trap":"Do not rinse lint off the screen with water; Whirlpool says wet lint is hard to remove.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0289"]},{"id":"dvt:screen-housing","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"Without opening the cabinet, a vent tech can vacuum lint from the housing the lint screen slides into. The heater box, burner area and drum seals are appliance technician work.","src":"Whirlpool lint screen care guidance; LintEater manual p.20","prompt":"Which part of the dryer interior can a vent tech without appliance training clean?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0290"]},{"id":"dvt:insulate-slope","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-controls-lint","tag":"FACT","text":"GE calls for insulating dryer ducts that run through unheated areas and for sloping horizontal runs down toward the outdoors about 1/4 inch per foot.","src":"GE Dryer Installation Instructions 49-3000206 p.25","prompt":"What does GE say to do with dryer ducts in unheated spaces and with horizontal runs?","trap":"A booster fan moves air but does not stop condensation in a cold duct.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0194"]},{"id":"dvt:usfa-annual","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-fire-numbers","tag":"FACT","text":"USFA estimated 2,900 residential building clothes dryer fires a year in 2008-2010, causing about 5 deaths, 100 injuries and $35 million in property loss.","src":"USFA Topical Fire Report v13i7 (2008-2010)","prompt":"How many residential building clothes dryer fires a year did USFA estimate for 2008-2010, and with what losses?","trap":"NFPA's 13,820 counts home structure fires for 2014-2018; always name the source and years.","num":{"q":"USFA estimated __ residential building clothes dryer fires a year in 2008-2010.","a":"2,900","d":["13,820","14,630","15,970"]},"qs":["dvt-0136"]},{"id":"dvt:usfa-failure-to-clean","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-fire-numbers","tag":"FACT","text":"In USFA's 2008-2010 data, failure to clean was the leading factor contributing to ignition of residential dryer fires, at 34 percent.","src":"USFA Topical Fire Report v13i7 (2008-2010)","prompt":"Per USFA's 2008-2010 report, what was the leading factor contributing to ignition in residential dryer fires, and its share?","trap":"28 percent is dust, fiber and lint as item first ignited, a different category; NFPA's 2014-2018 figure is 32.","num":{"q":"USFA's 2008-2010 data put failure to clean at __ of residential dryer fires, the leading ignition factor.","a":"34 percent","d":["28 percent","31 percent","32 percent"]},"qs":["dvt-0134"]},{"id":"dvt:usfa-january","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-fire-numbers","tag":"FACT","text":"USFA found residential dryer fires ran higher in fall and winter, peaking in January at 11 percent of the year's fires (2008-2010 data).","src":"USFA Topical Fire Report v13i7 (2008-2010)","prompt":"In which month did USFA's 2008-2010 data show residential dryer fires peaking?","trap":"October falls in the higher season but is not the peak.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0135"]},{"id":"dvt:nfpa-annual","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-fire-numbers","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA estimated 13,820 U.S. home structure fires a year involving dryers in 2014-2018, with 7 civilian deaths, 344 injuries and $233 million in direct property damage.","src":"NFPA 'Home Dryer Fires' (June 2020)","prompt":"What were NFPA's 2014-2018 yearly estimates for U.S. home structure fires involving dryers?","trap":"15,970 is NFPA's 2010-2014 count for dryers and washers combined; dryers alone were 14,630.","num":{"q":"NFPA estimated __ U.S. home structure fires a year involving dryers in 2014-2018.","a":"13,820","d":["2,900","14,630","15,970"]},"qs":["dvt-0136"]},{"id":"dvt:nfpa-electric-gas","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-fire-numbers","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA found 78 percent of dryers in 2014-2018 home fires were electric and 22 percent gas, roughly matching the share of homes using each type.","src":"NFPA 'Home Dryer Fires' (June 2020) Table 3","prompt":"What does NFPA's 2020 analysis show about electric versus gas dryers in home fires?","trap":"Electric's larger share reflects usage (79 percent of household dryers in 2017), not a higher fire risk.","num":{"q":"NFPA found __ of dryers involved in 2014-2018 home fires were electric.","a":"78 percent","d":["22 percent","27 percent","32 percent"]},"qs":["dvt-0137"]},{"id":"dvt:nfpa-failure-to-clean","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-fire-numbers","tag":"FACT","text":"In NFPA's 2014-2018 estimates, failure to clean was the leading factor contributing to ignition in home dryer fires, at 32 percent.","src":"NFPA 'Home Dryer Fires' (June 2020)","prompt":"What was the leading factor contributing to ignition in NFPA's 2014-2018 home dryer fire estimates?","trap":"USFA's 2008-2010 figure for the same factor is 34 percent; keep source and years together.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0137"]},{"id":"dvt:nfpa-lint-deaths","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-fire-numbers","tag":"FACT","text":"In NFPA's 2014-2018 estimates, dust, fiber or lint was the item first ignited in 27 percent of home dryer fires and in all 7 yearly civilian deaths. Clothing was 25 percent of fires but led injuries.","src":"NFPA 'Home Dryer Fires' (June 2020) Table 2","prompt":"Which item first ignited accounted for all civilian deaths in NFPA's 2014-2018 home dryer fire estimates?","trap":"Clothing is the close second in fires and leads in injuries, so it tempts; lint caused the deaths.","num":{"q":"NFPA 2014-2018: dust, fiber or lint was the item first ignited in __ of home dryer fires.","a":"27 percent","d":["25 percent","28 percent","32 percent"]},"qs":["dvt-0240"]},{"id":"dvt:no-magnetic-hood","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-hoods-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Whirlpool says not to use exhaust hoods with magnetic latches and lists them as a fire hazard; a latch holding the flap shut resists the dryer's airflow. Gravity-closing louvered and box hoods are its recommended styles.","src":"Whirlpool install instructions 3395325; Whirlpool WED9250","prompt":"Which exhaust hood type do Whirlpool's installation instructions say not to use?","trap":"A hood that stays sealed against wind also holds back the exhaust; the gravity damper is the draft barrier.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0129"]},{"id":"dvt:spring-damper","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-hoods-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Aldes says its in-line spring-loaded backdraft dampers need 0.04 to 0.08 in w.g. just to start opening and are not recommended for clothes dryers unless regular cleaning is provided for.","src":"Aldes Backdraft Dampers spec sheet","prompt":"What does a damper maker such as Aldes say about adding an in-line spring-loaded backdraft damper to a dryer duct?","trap":"Any added damper is another lint catcher and pressure drop; the backdraft damper belongs at the termination.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0132"]},{"id":"dvt:depv-pressure-switch","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-hoods-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A listed dryer exhaust duct power ventilator, such as a Fantech DEDPV-705 or Tjernlund LB2, starts by sensing positive duct pressure when the dryer starts, and runs on its own power cord, not the dryer's circuit.","src":"Fantech DEDPV-705 manual; Tjernlund LB2 spec","prompt":"How does a listed dryer exhaust duct power ventilator know to start when the dryer starts?","trap":"It is not wired into the dryer's motor circuit; Fantech notes that wiring could void the dryer warranty.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0048","dvt-0133","dvt-0168"]},{"id":"dvt:depv-alert-panel","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-hoods-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A UL 705 power ventilator alerts the occupant to a fan failure or blockage, typically through an indicator panel; Fantech requires that panel to be permanently installed and readily visible in the dryer space.","src":"Fantech DEDPV literature and DEDPV-705 manual","prompt":"How does a UL 705 dryer power ventilator report trouble, and where must its panel be?","trap":"A continuously running fan with a monitored alarm describes the IMC multistory shaft fan, not a DEPV.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0048","dvt-0168"]},{"id":"dvt:ul2158a-scope","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-hoods-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"UL 2158A covers clothes dryer transition ducts for venting household and commercial dryers. UL 705 covers power ventilators, UL 181 factory-made air ducts, and UL 181B closures for flexible ducts.","src":"UL 2158A §1.1","prompt":"What does the UL 2158A standard cover?","trap":"UL 181 flexible air duct is an HVAC listing, not a dryer transition listing.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0045"]},{"id":"dvt:type2-dexter-duct","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-hoods-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Dexter's T-30X2 commercial stack dryer manual specifies an 8 inch exhaust per stack at about 600 CFM and recommends no more than 14 ft of 8 inch pipe with two elbows per cylinder.","src":"Dexter T-30X2 manual pp.4, 15","prompt":"What exhaust size, airflow and pipe length does Dexter's T-30X2 stack dryer manual give?","trap":"Domestic 4 inch, 35 ft and DEDP table rules do not size commercial ducts; the dryer maker's instructions do.","num":{"q":"Dexter's recommended maximum per T-30X2 cylinder is __ of 8 inch pipe with two elbows.","a":"14 ft","d":["8 ft","25 ft","35 ft"]},"qs":["dvt-0287"]},{"id":"dvt:type2-branch-45","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-hoods-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Dexter says each dryer's 8 inch duct enters the main discharge duct at a 45 degree angle pointing downstream with the airflow, from the sides or bottom, never at a right angle.","src":"Dexter T-30X2 manual §4.2.6","prompt":"How does Dexter's manual say each Type 2 dryer's branch duct must enter a common main duct?","trap":"A right-angle entry is never allowed, even from the side, and a branch never points upstream.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0297"]},{"id":"dvt:type2-makeup-backdraft","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","lesson":"dvt-hoods-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Dexter warns that dryer exhaust can depressurize a laundry room and backdraft other gas appliances drawing combustion air from it, so test those appliances with the dryers running and all doors and windows closed.","src":"Dexter T-30X2 manual §4.2.3","prompt":"Why does Dexter's manual say to test other gas appliances with the dryers running and the room closed up?","trap":"Every cubic foot the dryers push out must be replaced; exhaust depressurizes the room, it does not pressurize it.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0294"]},{"id":"dvt:osha-core-elements","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"FACT","text":"OSHA's Recommended Practices for Safety and Health Programs rest on seven core elements, beginning with management leadership, worker participation, and hazard identification and assessment.","src":"OSHA 3885 Recommended Practices (2016)","prompt":"Which core elements begin OSHA's Recommended Practices framework for a company safety and health program?","trap":"Labels, SDSs and chemical training are HazCom program content; selection, medical evaluation and fit testing are a respirator program.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0226"]},{"id":"dvt:osha-temps-equal","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"FACT","text":"Under OSHA's Recommended Practices, host employers, contractors and staffing agencies commit to giving temporary workers the same level of safety and health protection as their own employees.","src":"OSHA 3885, Communication and Coordination","prompt":"What level of protection should staffing-agency temps on a vent crew receive under OSHA's Recommended Practices?","trap":"The parties split who handles which duties, but the level of protection never drops to the agency's or owner's rules.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0227"]},{"id":"dvt:ppe-hazard-cert","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA 1910.132(d)(2) requires the PPE hazard assessment to be verified by a written certification naming the workplace evaluated, the person certifying and the date, and stating it is a hazard assessment certification.","src":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.132(d)(2)","prompt":"How must an employer verify under 1910.132(d) that a PPE hazard assessment was done?","trap":"A name, date and signature training record is the fall-training certification under 1926.503(b), not the hazard assessment.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0228"]},{"id":"dvt:resp-program-trigger","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"REG","text":"The 1910.134 written respiratory protection program applies wherever respirators are needed to protect health or whenever the employer requires them. A company rule alone triggers it; no air sampling is needed first.","src":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.134(c)(1)","prompt":"When does a vent company's policy requiring respirators trigger the full 1910.134 written program?","trap":"Fit testing comes before first use, not after an overexposure is measured.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0232"]},{"id":"dvt:resp-voluntary","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"REG","text":"When a tech voluntarily wears a filtering facepiece (dust mask) the employer allows, 1910.134(c)(2) requires giving him the Appendix D information; dust-mask-only voluntary users are exempt from the written program.","src":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.134(c)(2)","prompt":"What does 1910.134 require when an employer lets a tech wear his own N95 voluntarily?","trap":"Medical evaluation and annual fit testing attach to required use; a voluntary elastomeric half-mask adds a medical evaluation.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0231"]},{"id":"dvt:resp-particulate","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"REG","text":"Droppings dust and fungal spores are particulates, so an air-purifying respirator for that work needs a NIOSH-certified particulate filter (N, R or P series, such as an N95 or P100), not a vapor cartridge.","src":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.134(d)(3)(iv)","prompt":"What must a half-mask respirator be fitted with for scraping dry pigeon droppings under 1910.134?","trap":"Change schedules and end-of-service indicators manage gas and vapor cartridges. Dust needs a filter; vapor needs a sorbent.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0230"]},{"id":"dvt:linteater-ppe","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"FACT","text":"Gardus says eye protection and a respirator must be worn when the dryer or a blower pushes loosened lint out toward the tech brushing from the termination.","src":"Gardus LintEater manual, Additional Methods 1-2; OSHA 1910.133","prompt":"What PPE does the LintEater manual require when the dryer or a blower pushes loosened lint toward the operator?","trap":"Gloves and hearing protection are sensible extras, but eyes and lungs are what the manual names.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0217","dvt-0302"]},{"id":"dvt:epa-mold-goggles","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA's mold remediation guide accepts properly fitted goggles designed to keep out dust and small particles, or a full-face HEPA respirator. Safety glasses and goggles with open vent holes are not acceptable.","src":"EPA 402-K-01-001, Personal Protective Equipment","prompt":"Which eye protection does EPA's mold remediation guide accept for cutting out a moldy, lint-caked duct section?","trap":"Side-shield glasses meet 1910.133 for flying particles, not mold dust; a half-face respirator gives no eye protection.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0233"]},{"id":"dvt:glove-plan","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"REG","text":"Match gloves to the task: CDC says rubber or plastic gloves for rodent droppings and nests, and 1910.138 calls for hand protection chosen for the hazard, such as cut-resistant gloves for sharp duct edges.","src":"CDC rodent clean-up; OSHA 29 CFR 1910.138","prompt":"Which gloves fit clearing a mouse nest and then handling sharp rigid metal duct?","trap":"Thin nitrile is not cut protection, and leather or knit cut gloves are not the rubber or plastic gloves CDC names.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0234"]},{"id":"dvt:fungal-droppings","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"FACT","text":"Histoplasmosis (soil with bird or bat droppings) and cryptococcosis (soil, bird dung, decaying wood) are fungal infections caught by breathing spores stirred up during nest and droppings removal.","src":"CDC histoplasmosis; CDC cryptococcosis","prompt":"Which two diseases come from breathing fungal spores stirred up from bird droppings?","trap":"Psittacosis is bacterial and hantavirus is a rodent-borne virus; neither is fungal.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0237","dvt-0238"]},{"id":"dvt:psittacosis","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"FACT","text":"Psittacosis is a bacterial infection (Chlamydia psittaci) caught by breathing dust from dried bird droppings or secretions. Fever, headache, muscle aches and dry cough start 5 to 14 days later; antibiotics treat it.","src":"CDC psittacosis","prompt":"Which bird-borne disease is bacterial, and how does it show up after exposure?","trap":"Legionnaires' disease has the same flu-like signs but comes from water system mist, not birds.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0237"]},{"id":"dvt:histo-wet-down","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"FACT","text":"CDC/NIOSH Histoplasma guidance: spray dusty droppings with water before disturbing them, never sweep or shovel them dry, and wear a respirator and goggles. A surgical mask is not a respirator.","src":"CDC/NIOSH Histoplasma prevention","prompt":"How should a tech clear a nest caked with bird droppings per CDC/NIOSH Histoplasma guidance?","trap":"Blowing it out with compressed air or brushing it fast and dry puts the most spores in the air.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0216"]},{"id":"dvt:bag-disposables","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"FACT","text":"NIOSH says to take off protective clothing and shoe covers when droppings work is done and seal them in heavy-duty plastic bags for disposal, so spores do not ride to the truck, the next job or home.","src":"NIOSH Histoplasmosis PPE guidance","prompt":"What does NIOSH say to do with disposable coveralls and shoe covers after clearing bird droppings?","trap":"The 5-minute bleach soak is CDC's rodent-droppings method, not a way to reuse disposable gear.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0235"]},{"id":"dvt:rodent-heavy","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"FACT","text":"For heavy rodent infestations or long-vacant dwellings, CDC calls for coveralls, boots or shoe covers, rubber, latex or vinyl gloves, goggles, and a half-mask respirator with HEPA filters or a HEPA PAPR.","src":"CDC rodent clean-up, Special Precautions","prompt":"What respirator and PPE does CDC list for cleaning up a heavy mouse infestation?","trap":"Organic vapor cartridges target chemicals, not virus-carrying particles; CDC also says never sweep or vacuum droppings.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0234","dvt-0236"]},{"id":"dvt:co-symptoms","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"FACT","text":"Headache, dizziness and nausea near a running gas appliance point to carbon monoxide. NIOSH says turn off the equipment, get to fresh air and call 911 or seek medical help.","src":"NIOSH carbon monoxide hazards at work","prompt":"What should a tech do if he gets a headache, dizziness and nausea while running a gas dryer in a closed closet?","trap":"CO is colorless and odorless, particulate filters do not stop it, and its early signs mimic heat exhaustion.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0239"]},{"id":"dvt:co-alarms","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA says CO alarms belong in a central location outside each sleeping area and on every level of the home; CPSC does not recommend them in kitchens or above fuel-burning appliances.","src":"NFPA CO safety tips; CPSC CO Q&A","prompt":"Where should CO alarms go in a home with a gas dryer, per NFPA and CPSC?","trap":"Mounting one right above the dryer or beside the range is what CPSC advises against.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0211"]},{"id":"dvt:ladder-3ft","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ladders-roofs","tag":"REG","text":"A portable ladder used to reach an upper landing needs side rails extending at least 3 feet above it. Construction rules allow a top-secured ladder with a grab rail only if the ladder is too short.","src":"OSHA 1910.23(c)(11); 1926.1053(b)(1)","prompt":"How far must an extension ladder's side rails extend above the roof edge it is used to reach?","trap":"Four is the 4-to-1 setup ratio, not the extension above the landing.","num":{"q":"OSHA's minimum side rail extension above the upper landing a portable ladder reaches is __.","a":"3 feet","d":["1 foot","4 feet","6 feet"]},"qs":["dvt-0212"]},{"id":"dvt:ladder-4to1","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ladders-roofs","tag":"REG","text":"Set an extension ladder's base about one quarter of its working length from the wall: a 16 ft working length puts the feet 4 feet out.","src":"OSHA 1926.1053(b)(5)(i); OSHA Portable Ladder QuickCard","prompt":"How far from the wall should a tech set the feet of an extension ladder with a 16 ft working length?","trap":"The one-eighth ratio applies only to job-made wood ladders with spliced side rails.","num":{"q":"An extension ladder with a 16 ft working length has its feet set __ from the wall.","a":"4 feet","d":["2 feet","3 feet","8 feet"]},"qs":["dvt-0213","dvt-0249"]},{"id":"dvt:ladder-3point","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ladders-roofs","tag":"REG","text":"Climb facing the ladder with 3 points of contact, two hands and a foot or two feet and a hand. OSHA bars carrying any load that could make the climber lose balance, such as rods and a drill.","src":"OSHA Portable Ladder QuickCard; 1910.23(b)(13); 1926.1053(b)(22)","prompt":"What climbing rule applies when a tech needs a drill and brush rods at a roof cap reached by extension ladder?","trap":"The one-hand minimum in 1910.23(b)(12) is not enough; with tools in the other hand, 3-point contact is gone.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0248"]},{"id":"dvt:ladder-defect","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ladders-roofs","tag":"REG","text":"A ladder with a structural defect, such as a cracked side rail, is immediately tagged Dangerous: Do Not Use (or marked defective) and removed from service until repaired or replaced.","src":"OSHA 1910.23(b)(10); 1926.1053(b)(16)","prompt":"What must happen to an extension ladder found with a cracked side rail?","trap":"Splinting it or limiting it to light work is not allowed while it waits for repair.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0247"]},{"id":"dvt:ladder-slippery","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ladders-roofs","tag":"REG","text":"On ice or other slippery surfaces, a portable ladder must be secured and stabilized by lashing or holding it; slip-resistant feet are no substitute.","src":"OSHA 1910.23(c)(9); 1926.1053(b)(7)","prompt":"What does OSHA require before setting an extension ladder on an icy concrete patio?","trap":"A flatter lean or a board laid on the ice does not secure the ladder.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0249"]},{"id":"dvt:powerline-10ft","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ladders-roofs","tag":"REG","text":"Near energized overhead lines of 50 kV or less, an unqualified worker and any conductive object he handles stay at least 10 feet away, and a ladder that could contact energized parts needs nonconductive side rails.","src":"OSHA 1910.333(c)(3), (c)(7)","prompt":"What ladder and clearance rules apply to an unqualified tech working near an overhead line of 50 kV or less?","trap":"Three feet is the ladder extension figure, not a power line clearance.","num":{"q":"The minimum clearance for an unqualified worker from overhead lines of 50 kV or less is __.","a":"10 feet","d":["3 feet","4 feet","20 feet"]},"qs":["dvt-0214"]},{"id":"dvt:fall-triggers","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ladders-roofs","tag":"REG","text":"Fall protection is required at unprotected sides and edges 4 feet or more above a lower level under general industry rules (1910.28), and 6 feet or more under construction rules (1926.501).","src":"OSHA 1910.28(b)(1); 1926.501(b)(1)","prompt":"At what heights do OSHA's general industry and construction rules require fall protection at unprotected edges?","trap":"Ten feet is the construction trigger for scaffolds, not roof edges.","num":{"q":"The general industry (1910.28) fall protection trigger height at unprotected edges is __.","a":"4 feet","d":["6 feet","10 feet","15 feet"]},"qs":["dvt-0215","dvt-0241"]},{"id":"dvt:skylight-hole","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ladders-roofs","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA treats a skylight as a hole: it needs a cover, a guardrail system or personal fall arrest (general industry also allows travel restraint). Tape, cones or signs do not stop a fall through it.","src":"OSHA 1910.28(b)(3); 1926.501(b)(4)","prompt":"How do OSHA's fall rules treat a plastic skylight dome near a roof dryer cap?","trap":"Warning lines and safety monitors are low-slope roofing options for edges, not protection over a skylight.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0242"]},{"id":"dvt:warning-line","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ladders-roofs","tag":"REG","text":"A rope warning line's lowest point, sag included, must sit 34 to 39 inches above the walking-working surface.","src":"OSHA 1910.29(d)(2)(ii); 1926.502(f)(2)(ii)","prompt":"How high above the roof must the lowest point of a rope warning line be?","trap":"39 to 45 inches is the guardrail top-rail range, 42 plus or minus 3.","num":{"q":"A warning line's lowest point, including sag, sits __ above the roof surface.","a":"34 to 39 inches","d":["24 to 30 inches","30 to 38 inches","39 to 45 inches"]},"qs":["dvt-0243"]},{"id":"dvt:no-body-belt","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ladders-roofs","tag":"REG","text":"Body belts are not allowed as part of a personal fall arrest system; fall arrest needs a full-body harness. A body belt may still be used in a positioning system.","src":"OSHA 1926.502(d); 1910.140(d)(3)","prompt":"Can a tech clip his fall arrest lanyard to an old body belt on a steep roof?","trap":"A 6 ft free fall limit or a 5,000 lb anchor does not make a belt acceptable.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0244"]},{"id":"dvt:impact-gear","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ladders-roofs","tag":"REG","text":"Fall protection gear that has arrested a fall comes out of service immediately, harness and lanyard alike, and stays out until a competent person inspects it and finds it undamaged.","src":"OSHA 1910.140(c)(17); 1926.502(d)(19)","prompt":"What must happen to a harness and lanyard that just arrested a tech's slide off a roof edge?","trap":"The user's everyday pre-use check does not clear gear that has taken an impact load.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0245"]},{"id":"dvt:anchor-5000","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ladders-roofs","tag":"REG","text":"A personal fall arrest anchorage must hold at least 5,000 pounds per attached worker, unless a qualified person designs it with a safety factor of at least two.","src":"OSHA 1910.140(c)(13); 1926.502(d)(15)","prompt":"What minimum load must a fall arrest anchorage support unless a qualified person designs it?","trap":"1,800 pounds is the maximum arresting force on the body; 3,000 pounds is the positioning anchorage.","num":{"q":"Unless a qualified person designs it, a fall arrest anchorage's required minimum strength is __ per attached worker.","a":"5,000 pounds","d":["1,800 pounds","3,000 pounds","3,600 pounds"]},"qs":["dvt-0246"]},{"id":"dvt:prompt-rescue","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-ladders-roofs","tag":"REG","text":"An employer using personal fall arrest must provide for prompt rescue of a worker left suspended after a fall.","src":"OSHA 1910.140(c)(21); 1926.502(d)(20)","prompt":"Beyond harness inspection and anchor choice, what must an employer's personal fall arrest procedure provide for?","trap":"A qualified person's fall protection plan is only an alternative where conventional protection is infeasible.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0229"]},{"id":"dvt:heat-exhaustion","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-weather-power-gas","tag":"FACT","text":"Heavy sweating, headache, nausea and dizziness in a worker who is alert and talking clearly is heat exhaustion. NIOSH first aid: move him out of the heat, cool him, give fluids and have him evaluated.","src":"NIOSH heat-related illnesses","prompt":"What does NIOSH call heavy sweating, headache, nausea and dizziness in an alert worker, and what first aid applies?","trap":"Confusion, slurred speech or collapse means heat stroke and a 911 call; NIOSH says avoid salt tablets.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0250"]},{"id":"dvt:acclimatize","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-weather-power-gas","tag":"FACT","text":"NIOSH limits a new worker to no more than 20 percent of a normal day's heat work on day 1, with no more than a 20 percent increase each day after.","src":"NIOSH heat stress recommendations","prompt":"What does NIOSH's acclimatization schedule allow a new hire doing hot roof work?","trap":"50, 60, 80 and 100 percent over days 1 to 4 is the schedule for workers with prior heat experience.","num":{"q":"NIOSH limits a new worker to __ of a normal day's heat work on day 1.","a":"20 percent","d":["50 percent","60 percent","80 percent"]},"qs":["dvt-0251"]},{"id":"dvt:lightning","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-weather-power-gas","tag":"FACT","text":"If you can hear thunder you are likely within striking distance. NWS says get down and go indoors now, and wait 30 minutes after the last thunder or lightning before going back out.","src":"NWS Lightning Safety","prompt":"What should a tech on a roof do when he hears distant thunder under a sunny sky?","trap":"Crouching outdoors is a myth, and metal conducts lightning but does not attract it.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0252"]},{"id":"dvt:gfci-at-outlet","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-weather-power-gas","tag":"REG","text":"When tool power comes from a receptacle without GFCI protection, plug the portable GFCI in at the wall outlet so it protects the whole extension cord and every tool on it.","src":"OSHA 1910.304(b)(3)(ii); 1926.404(b)(1)(ii)","prompt":"Where should a portable GFCI connect when a tech powers his vac and drill from a non-GFCI garage outlet?","trap":"A GFCI at the far end or on one tool leaves the cord and the other tool unprotected.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0253"]},{"id":"dvt:unplug-dryer","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-weather-power-gas","tag":"FACT","text":"Before pulling out an electric dryer, turn it off and unplug it. The control knob or door switch still leaves 240 volts at the cord and terminal block.","src":"Gardus LintEater manual, Step 8; GE installation instructions","prompt":"What isolates a 240 volt electric dryer from power before the tech pulls it from the wall?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0207"]},{"id":"dvt:no-extension-cord","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-weather-power-gas","tag":"FACT","text":"GE says a gas dryer's 120 volt cord plugs straight into a grounded 3-prong outlet: no adapter, no extension cord, never remove the ground prong. A 2-prong outlet is for a licensed electrician to replace.","src":"GE Dryer Installation Instructions 49-3000206","prompt":"What do GE's instructions say when a gas dryer's cord will not reach the outlet and the owner offers an extension cord?","trap":"Hard-usage cords and GFCIs are OSHA rules for the tech's temporary tool power, not for extending an appliance cord.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0254"]},{"id":"dvt:gas-smell","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-weather-power-gas","tag":"FACT","text":"If you smell gas: light nothing, touch no electrical switch and use no phone in the building, get everyone out, then call the gas supplier from a neighbor's phone, or the fire department if it cannot be reached.","src":"GE owner's manual, What to Do If You Smell Gas; Whirlpool W11222085; CPSC Pub. 5135","prompt":"What should a tech do if he smells gas while sliding out a gas dryer?","trap":"Do not unplug the dryer or hunt for the leak; pulling a plug can arc, and repair is for a gas pro.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0162","dvt-0210"]},{"id":"dvt:gas-leak-test","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-weather-power-gas","tag":"FACT","text":"GE says check gas connections with a soap solution that contains no ammonia, which can damage brass fittings, and never an open flame. If bubbles appear, close the valve, retighten the joint and test again.","src":"GE Dryer Installation Instructions 49-3000206, Test for Leaks","prompt":"How does GE say to check gas dryer connections for leaks, and what if bubbles appear?","trap":"Ammonia glass cleaner is exactly the wrong solution; ammonia attacks brass fittings.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0208","dvt-0255"]},{"id":"dvt:new-gas-connector","dom":"Technician Safety","lesson":"dvt-weather-power-gas","tag":"FACT","text":"GE calls for a new CSA-certified flexible gas connector, installed by a qualified person, and says never reuse an old flexible connector.","src":"GE Dryer Installation Instructions 49-3000206, Gas Requirements","prompt":"What do GE's instructions say about screwing an old flexible gas connector back onto a dryer?","trap":"A clean soap test after reusing the old connector does not cure the reuse ban.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0209","dvt-0282"]},{"id":"dvt:brass-connector","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-weather-power-gas","tag":"FACT","text":"An old uncoated brass flexible gas connector can separate from its end pieces if the dryer is moved even slightly, causing a fire or explosion. Leave the dryer in place and have a qualified professional replace it.","src":"CPSC Pub. 5135","prompt":"What should a tech do on finding an old uncoated brass gas connector behind a dryer he needs to move?","trap":"Sliding it out gently or taping the joints still risks complete failure.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0163"]},{"id":"dvt:connector-slack","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-weather-power-gas","tag":"FACT","text":"Gas connectors wear out from too much moving and bending, so pull a gas dryer out only as far as the work needs, without kinking or straining the connector.","src":"CPSC Pub. 5135; GE gas dryer installation 31-16756","prompt":"How far should a tech pull out a gas dryer whose coated connector has little slack?","trap":"Coated connectors still fatigue when bent back and forth, and unscrewing one is no shortcut.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0282"]},{"id":"dvt:generator-20ft","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-weather-power-gas","tag":"FACT","text":"Run a portable generator outdoors only, at least 20 feet from the house with its exhaust pointed away from windows, doors and vents; never in a garage, even with the door open.","src":"CPSC generator guidance 2026; ACR 2025 §3.2 (principle)","prompt":"Where should a gasoline generator powering the collection vac sit while it runs?","trap":"An attached garage with the door up still lets carbon monoxide into the house.","num":{"q":"CPSC's minimum distance between a running portable generator and the house is __.","a":"20 feet","d":["3 feet","6 feet","10 feet"]},"qs":["dvt-0272"]},{"id":"dvt:sds-travel","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-hazcom-customers","tag":"REG","text":"When a crew travels between job sites during a shift, SDSs may stay at the shop as long as workers can get the information immediately in an emergency; the written HazCom program may stay there too.","src":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.1200(g)(9), (e)(5)","prompt":"Must the SDSs for the products on a vent crew's van ride along to each house under 1910.1200?","trap":"NADCA's ACR keeps SDSs on site for HVAC projects, so carrying them in the van is the safer habit.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0256"]},{"id":"dvt:portable-label","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-hazcom-customers","tag":"REG","text":"A spray bottle filled from a concentrate for a helper or for later jobs needs a label with the product identifier and general hazards; only a container for the filler's own immediate use is exempt.","src":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.1200(f)(6), (f)(8)","prompt":"What does 1910.1200 require on a spray bottle of degreaser filled for a helper to use at later houses?","trap":"An SDS in the van does not replace a container label, and emptying it by shift's end is not the test.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0257"]},{"id":"dvt:sds-section-6","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-hazcom-customers","tag":"REG","text":"SDS Section 6, accidental release measures, gives the precautions and methods for containing and cleaning up a spill; Section 7 covers handling and storage, Section 8 exposure controls and PPE, Section 13 disposal.","src":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.1200(g)(2), App. D","prompt":"Which SDS section tells a tech how to contain and clean up a chemical spill?","trap":"Section 13 covers disposal of waste afterward, not containing the spill.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0258"]},{"id":"dvt:product-consent","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-hazcom-customers","tag":"FACT","text":"Before using a chemical product such as a duct deodorizer in an occupied home, show the occupant its label and SDS and use it only if they agree, especially with asthma in the household.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7.1, §2.7.2 (principle); NADCA DVT Module 3","prompt":"How should a tech handle a deodorizer's hazards when the homeowner's son has asthma?","trap":"Leaving the SDS after use informs her too late, and an idle duct does not carry fog outdoors.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0261"]},{"id":"dvt:attic-stain-mold","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-hazcom-customers","tag":"FACT","text":"A vent tech should not call a stain mold. Tell the owner that lint and moist exhaust from a separated attic joint are loading the attic and must be piped outside; confirming mold is for an IEP or similar professional.","src":"IRC 2024 M1501.1, M1502.2; ACR 2025 §1.9 (principle)","prompt":"What should a tech tell a homeowner about lint and a dark stain above a separated attic dryer joint?","trap":"DEDP's little-IAQ-risk remark applies only to a dryer that actually discharges outdoors.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0259"]},{"id":"dvt:no-guarantee","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-hazcom-customers","tag":"FACT","text":"Promise benefits, not guarantees: cleaning helps cut drying time and fire risk, but NADCA's DEDP expressly does not guarantee energy savings or better performance, and no cleaning makes a dryer fire-proof.","src":"DEDP 2014 Disclaimer; NADCA DVT Module 3","prompt":"How should a sales script describe what dryer vent cleaning does for energy bills and fire risk?","trap":"Passing the DEDP test or staying under 35 ft is a benchmark, not a guarantee.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0262"]},{"id":"dvt:design-fix","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-hazcom-customers","tag":"FACT","text":"When a run is far past the dryer maker's length limit, cleaning cannot fix slow drying. Tell the owner it needs a design change, such as a shorter route, not another cleaning.","src":"DEDP 2014 Appendix; IRC 2024 M1502.4.6","prompt":"What should a tech tell an owner whose freshly cleaned run, about 70 ft developed length, still dries slowly?","trap":"A hardware-store inline duct fan is a prohibited domestic booster; only a UL 705 power ventilator is allowed.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0263"]},{"id":"dvt:price-before-work","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-hazcom-customers","tag":"FACT","text":"When a cleaning-only job turns up parts that need replacing, show the customer photos of each damaged part and get approval of the added cost before the extra work starts.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.24 (principle); DEDP 2014 §4.8","prompt":"When should a customer learn the added cost of replacing a crushed transition and broken hood found mid-job?","trap":"The invoice, a passed test or the moment before leaving all come after the work.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0265"]},{"id":"dvt:roof-scheduling","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-hazcom-customers","tag":"FACT","text":"When booking a roof termination, tell the customer that roof access is part of the job and that rain, wind or ice can force a new date.","src":"NADCA DVT Modules 2-3; DEDP 2014 §1.2.3","prompt":"What should a customer with a steep-roof dryer termination be told when the visit is scheduled?","trap":"Skipping the cap leaves a common blockage in place; the termination is part of the job.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0266"]},{"id":"dvt:nfpa-lint-filter","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-hazcom-customers","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA says clean the lint filter before or after every load and remove lint that collects around the drum; USFA says before and after each cycle.","src":"NFPA Clothes Dryer Safety tip sheet; USFA dryer flyer","prompt":"How often does NFPA tell customers to clean the dryer's lint filter?","trap":"Weekly slot cleaning and periodic screen washing are extra steps, not a substitute for every load.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0218"]},{"id":"dvt:nfpa-vent-interval","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-hazcom-customers","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA says clean lint out of the vent pipe, or have a dryer lint removal service do it, once a year, or sooner if clothes take longer than normal to dry.","src":"NFPA Clothes Dryer Safety tip sheet; GE owner's manual","prompt":"How often does NFPA's dryer safety tip sheet say the vent pipe should be cleaned?","trap":"Three to four times a year is the LintEater maker's advice, not NFPA's; attribute every interval to its source.","num":{"q":"NFPA says clean lint from the vent pipe every __, or sooner if drying slows.","a":"12 months","d":["3 months","4 months","6 months"]},"qs":["dvt-0192","dvt-0221"]},{"id":"dvt:nfpa-off-at-night","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-hazcom-customers","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA says turn the dryer off if you leave home or when you go to bed.","src":"NFPA Clothes Dryer Safety tip sheet","prompt":"What does NFPA advise about running the dryer overnight or while away from home?","trap":"Low heat with a cool-down is CPSC's advice for solvent-soiled clothes, not permission to run unattended.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0219"]},{"id":"dvt:nfpa-solvent-clothes","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-hazcom-customers","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA says clothes that touched gasoline, paint thinner or similar solvents are laid outside to dry, then washed and dried as usual. CPSC adds washing more than once and drying on low heat with a cool-down.","src":"NFPA Clothes Dryer Safety tip sheet; CPSC Pub. 5022","prompt":"What does NFPA recommend for rags soaked with gasoline or paint thinner that a customer wants to machine-dry?","trap":"One cold wash followed by high heat is the tempting half-measure.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0220"]},{"id":"dvt:nfpa-clear-area","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-hazcom-customers","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA says keep the area around the dryer clear of things that can burn, such as boxes, cleaning supplies and clothing.","src":"NFPA Clothes Dryer Safety tip sheet","prompt":"What does NFPA tell customers about storing things around the dryer?","trap":"3 feet is the code's default termination clearance from openings, not a storage distance.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0222"]},{"id":"dvt:walk-route","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"At a new house, walk the whole route from the laundry area along the duct path to the termination before bringing equipment in; it sets the protection, containment and equipment plan.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.0, §1.2.1 (principle); NADCA DVT Module 3","prompt":"What should a tech do first on arriving at a house he has never serviced?","trap":"Drop cloths come after the route is known, and the DEDP test only runs on a duct confirmed clean.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0268"]},{"id":"dvt:trace-unknown-end","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"If no one knows where the duct ends and no cap is visible, trace the run before cleaning; it may stop in the attic or a soffit, where brushing or blowing from inside would pack lint.","src":"IRC 2024 M1501.1, M1502.3; NADCA DVT Module 3","prompt":"What should a tech do before cleaning a duct whose termination nobody can find?","trap":"Watching outside for a lint plume means the lint is already in the attic if the duct ends there.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0273"]},{"id":"dvt:pets-confined","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"Before starting, have the owner confine pets away from open doors, the laundry area and the termination; protecting occupants is a priority on every job.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.13 (principle); NADCA DVT Module 3","prompt":"What should a tech arrange for roaming pets before running a collection hose through the front door?","trap":"Shutting pets in the laundry room or by the termination puts them in the work zone.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0269"]},{"id":"dvt:sensitive-occupant","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"For an occupant with severe asthma, keep the duct under vacuum while lint is dislodged and keep that person away from the work area. Running the home's HVAC fan can spread laundry-room dust.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2.1, §3.9.1 (principle)","prompt":"How should a tech plan the work when an occupant with severe asthma works next to the laundry room?","trap":"Compressed air blowing lint out the far end pushes it out of every leak along the way.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0270"]},{"id":"dvt:vac-outside","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"Leave a large non-HEPA collection vacuum outside and run only the hose in, laid over floor coverings; non-HEPA equipment must exhaust outdoors, and the hose route still needs protection.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.3, §4.1.3, §3.9.2 (principle)","prompt":"How should a tech set up a non-HEPA collection vacuum when the route crosses newly refinished floors?","trap":"A bare-floor hose drags grit across the finish; a non-HEPA vac set on a towel indoors still exhausts dust inside.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0271"]},{"id":"dvt:coverings","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"Minimum (Level 1) protection includes clean coverings in the work area that extend past it, where needed, to protect flooring, equipment and furniture, such as along the path the dryer is walked out.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.2 (principle)","prompt":"What minimum protection applies when a dryer must be walked out across a finished wood floor?","trap":"Floor-to-ceiling barriers, a two-layer 6-mil floor and 4 ACH air scrubbing are Level 2 over-answers.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0180","dvt-0271"]},{"id":"dvt:preexisting-damage","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"Photograph pre-existing damage, such as a cracked tile or a dented dryer panel, and show it to the owner before anything is moved.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.24 (principle)","prompt":"What should a tech do on noticing a cracked floor tile and a dented dryer panel before pulling the dryer out?","trap":"Photos filed without telling the owner meet only half the duty.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0280"]},{"id":"dvt:move-on-cardboard","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"Maytag says to move a dryer with two or more people, grasping the body rather than the console; Whirlpool says to slide it onto cardboard or hardboard before moving it to protect the floor covering.","src":"Maytag MEDX600XW0 installation instructions p.6; Whirlpool commercial dryer installation instructions W11297002A","prompt":"How should a dryer be moved off new vinyl plank flooring without damaging it?","trap":"Rocking it corner to corner or dragging it on two legs puts all the weight on one or two feet.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0281"]},{"id":"dvt:steam-dryer-leaks","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"GE's steam dryer hookup ends with a leak check at the Y connector, faucet and hose couplings, so recheck them after moving the dryer. GE's steam models connect only to the cold water supply.","src":"GE gas dryer installation 31-16756, inlet hoses","prompt":"What should a tech check after sliding a steam dryer that shares the washer's cold faucet back into place?","trap":"A condensate drain hose belongs to ventless dryers, not a vented steam dryer.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0283"]},{"id":"dvt:hot-shingles","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"On hot days asphalt shingles soften and scuff easily underfoot; ARMA advises soft-soled shoes, careful footing, or waiting until the roof cools. Cold weather makes shingles brittle and prone to cracking.","src":"ARMA hot- and cold-weather shingle bulletins","prompt":"What property risk does a 95 F day create for a tech crossing asphalt shingles, and how is it reduced?","trap":"Heat activates the shingle sealant rather than releasing it; cracking and snapping are cold-weather risks.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0284"]},{"id":"dvt:seal-vac-port","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"Seal the collection vacuum to the wall port so the duct stays under negative pressure; lint loosened by an air whip then moves into the vac instead of puffing into the room.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.1, §4.5 (principle)","prompt":"What stops lint from puffing back out of the wall port while an air whip is run toward the cap?","trap":"Raising compressor pressure makes blowback worse; a towel over the port catches only part of it.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0275"]},{"id":"dvt:clear-cap-first","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"If the termination is packed with a nest, clear it from outside before any air goes into the duct. Blowing into a blocked outlet pressurizes the run and pushes lint out the port and leaky joints.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.1 (principle); NADCA DVT Module 3","prompt":"What should a tech do first when the cap is packed with an old nest and he plans to blow the duct out from inside?","trap":"Sealing the port around the air hose just moves the blowback to hidden joints.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0276"]},{"id":"dvt:capture-at-cap","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"Lint pushed out the cap is captured at the source with a bag or vacuum sealed over the termination. NADCA's DVT outline makes protecting both the interior and the exterior from contaminants a key principle.","src":"NADCA DVT handout, Module 3","prompt":"How should a tech handle the lint discharge at a townhouse wall cap next to a neighbor's patio?","trap":"Hosing off the patio, timing the wind or closing windows still drops lint on someone else's property.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0274","dvt-0302"]},{"id":"dvt:clean-tools-between","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"Clean and inspect rods, brushes and hoses before they enter the next work site, and clean or seal collection equipment and tools before moving between units in the same building.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.1, §3.1.3 (principle)","prompt":"What must happen to lint- and droppings-coated tools between one house or unit and the next?","trap":"Cleaning only at day's end carries lint and droppings into every home in between.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0179","dvt-0277"]},{"id":"dvt:open-vac-outside","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"Open and empty a contaminated vacuum only in a containment area or outside the building, never in the laundry room or an attached garage.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.2.1 (principle)","prompt":"Where should a tech empty a shop vac packed with lint, twigs and droppings?","trap":"An attached garage with the door open is still inside the building.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0178"]},{"id":"dvt:bag-nest","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"Seal a droppings-caked nest in a bag where it is removed, before carrying it through the home; contaminated material is contained before it leaves the building.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.5 (principle)","prompt":"How should a tech carry a dry, droppings-caked nest out through the living room?","trap":"Carried loose in gloved hands or an open bucket, dry droppings shed dust with every step.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0279"]},{"id":"dvt:fold-drop-cloth","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"Take up a lint-covered drop cloth by folding its edges inward over the lint, then carry it out; shaking or dragging it puts the dust back in the air or along the route.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.5 (principle)","prompt":"How should a tech take up a lint-covered drop cloth at the end of the job?","trap":"Shaking it out on the lawn just moves the mess outdoors.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0278"]},{"id":"dvt:hepa-damp-wipe","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-jobsite-containment","tag":"FACT","text":"Clean fine lint dust off the washer lid and floor with a HEPA vacuum, then a damp wipe; sweeping, dry brushing or blowing launches the finest dust back into the room.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.8 (principle); NADCA DVT Module 5","prompt":"What is the best way to clean up the film of lint dust left after a dryer is reconnected?","trap":"Running the dryer to clear the air does not remove dust that has settled.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0285"]},{"id":"dvt:hot-humid-room","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-inspect-symptoms","tag":"FACT","text":"A hot, humid laundry room with lint on the floor behind the dryer most likely means a loose or torn transition at the dryer or wall, blowing warm, moist, linty exhaust into the room.","src":"Whirlpool W10096987, W11222085; CPSC Pub. 5022","prompt":"What most likely causes a hot, humid laundry room with lint on the floor behind the dryer?","trap":"A hood damper stuck shut restricts flow but does not blow lint out behind the dryer.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0147"]},{"id":"dvt:long-dry-hot-top","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-inspect-symptoms","tag":"FACT","text":"Long drying times or a hot dryer top call for inspecting the exhaust path, transition to hood, for a restriction before blaming the heating element, sensors or power supply.","src":"Whirlpool W11222085 p.7; CPSC Pub. 5022","prompt":"What should a tech check first when towels take two cycles and the dryer top feels hot?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0148"]},{"id":"dvt:burning-smell","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-inspect-symptoms","tag":"FACT","text":"A hot, burning smell with heavy lint around the cabinet means stop using the dryer until both the vent and the dryer interior are cleaned; lint blocks airflow and causes excessive heat.","src":"CPSC Pub. 5022; NFPA 2017 dryer safety information","prompt":"What should a tech recommend when a customer smells burning during loads and lint is heaped around the cabinet?","trap":"Low heat or emptying the screen mid-load does not separate the fuel from the heat.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0149"]},{"id":"dvt:flap-jammed","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-inspect-symptoms","tag":"FACT","text":"Lint collecting in the hood can keep flaps or louvers from opening or closing fully. A flap held open by lint restricts the outlet and lets in drafts, birds and rodents.","src":"Whirlpool W11222085 p.7","prompt":"What problems does a hood flap held partly open by a mat of lint create?","trap":"A jammed flap defeats the backdraft damper even if it still swings when the dryer runs.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0150"]},{"id":"dvt:flap-test-running","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-inspect-symptoms","tag":"FACT","text":"Check the termination with the dryer running: the flap should open fully and exhaust air should escape. Lifting the flap by hand only shows that it swings freely.","src":"CPSC Pub. 5022; USFA dryer flyer; NFPA tip sheet","prompt":"How should a tech confirm that a termination damper opens under the dryer's own airflow?","trap":"The 12.5 sq in open-area rule is a separate code check, not a function test.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0151","dvt-0183"]},{"id":"dvt:hood-12-inches","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-inspect-symptoms","tag":"FACT","text":"Whirlpool requires the exhaust hood to be at least 12 inches from the ground or any object that could block it, such as shrubs, rocks or snow.","src":"Whirlpool WED9250 and W10096987 installation instructions","prompt":"How much clearance do Whirlpool's instructions require below a dryer exhaust hood?","trap":"3 feet is the code's default distance from building openings, not ground clearance.","num":{"q":"Whirlpool's minimum clearance from an exhaust hood to the ground or any obstruction is __.","a":"12 inches","d":["2 inches","6 inches","36 inches"]},"qs":["dvt-0167"]},{"id":"dvt:roof-cap-lint","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-inspect-symptoms","tag":"FACT","text":"Whirlpool warns that lint can collect in an exhaust hood until the flappers or louvers cannot open or close fully. On a high roof cap, that buildup is out of sight and reach until it causes trouble.","src":"Whirlpool W11222085 p.7; DEDP 2014 Table 4.10","prompt":"Why does lint buildup at a high roof termination tend to go unnoticed until the flapper sticks?","trap":"Roof caps still need a backdraft damper; the problem is access, not legality.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0156"]},{"id":"dvt:crushed-transition","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-inspect-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"A dryer pushed tight to the wall can flatten the transition. CPSC warns kinks and crushing greatly reduce airflow, so photograph it, record it and correct it before reconnecting, on gas and electric dryers alike.","src":"CPSC Pub. 5022; Whirlpool W11222085 'AF Code'; DEDP 2014 §1.3.3","prompt":"What should a tech record when the semi-rigid transition behind a dryer has been flattened against the wall?","trap":"Semi-rigid duct does not spring back once it has been crushed.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0141"]},{"id":"dvt:plastic-transition","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-inspect-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Whirlpool and GE forbid plastic anywhere in dryer venting, including the transition, and CPSC says replace plastic or foil accordion duct with rigid or semi-rigid metal. Record a vinyl transition as noncompliant.","src":"Whirlpool W10096987; GE 49-3000206 p.23; CPSC Pub. 5022","prompt":"What should a tech record and tell the customer about a white vinyl accordion transition?","trap":"The 8 ft limit applies to a listed transition; vinyl fails no matter how short or visible.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0139","dvt-0198","dvt-0224"]},{"id":"dvt:flex-not-concealed","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-inspect-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Whirlpool's instructions say not to install flexible dryer vent inside enclosed walls, ceilings or floors, so foil or semi-rigid flex found running up a stud bay is a finding.","src":"Whirlpool W10096987 installation instructions","prompt":"What do Whirlpool's instructions say about running flexible dryer vent inside a wall, ceiling or floor?","trap":"Being under the 35 ft length limit does not make hidden flex acceptable.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0142"]},{"id":"dvt:whirlpool-flex-length","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-inspect-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Whirlpool says a flexible metal transition should not exceed 7 3/4 ft in total length, a little under the code's 8 ft single-length limit.","src":"Whirlpool W10096987 installation instructions","prompt":"What total length does Whirlpool set for a flexible metal transition duct?","trap":"6 ft is how close the code's length label sits to the duct connection, not a transition limit.","num":{"q":"Whirlpool's maximum total length for a flexible metal transition is __.","a":"7 3/4 ft","d":["6 ft","8 ft","10 ft"]},"qs":["dvt-0140"]},{"id":"dvt:clamps-not-screws","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-inspect-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Whirlpool says connect vent with clamps, not screws or other fasteners that extend into the duct, because the tips catch lint; GE likewise says no fasteners should extend inside.","src":"Whirlpool W10096987, W10848818; GE 49-3000206 p.25","prompt":"Why does a tech write up a transition held on with sheet metal screws driven into the airstream?","trap":"Taping over a long screw does nothing about the tip inside the duct.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0143","dvt-0202"]},{"id":"dvt:as-found-photos","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","lesson":"dvt-inspect-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Photograph the transition, connections and hood exactly as found before touching anything, and again after; dated before and after photos plus the transition type used are the strongest job record.","src":"NADCA DVT Module 4; ACR 2025 §5.6.3; DEDP 2014 §4.4","prompt":"Which photos best protect a tech if a customer later disputes what was found?","trap":"An after-only photo cannot show the condition that justified a replacement.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0161","dvt-0223"]},{"id":"dvt:refer-appliance","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-inspect-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"When a cleaned duct passes but the dryer still dries slowly, refer the dryer to a qualified appliance service provider; a vent tech without appliance training does not replace heating elements.","src":"GE owner's manual; DEDP 2014 Appendix","prompt":"What should a vent tech without appliance training do when asked to replace a heating element after the duct passes?","trap":"Recleaning a duct that already passed treats the wrong component.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0225"]},{"id":"dvt:cabinet-interior","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-inspect-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"CPSC advises having a qualified service person clean the inside of the dryer cabinet periodically, and GE says have a qualified technician vacuum lint from the dryer once a year.","src":"CPSC Pub. 5022; GE owner's manual","prompt":"Who should clean heavy lint from inside a dryer's cabinet, and how often does GE say it should be done?","trap":"Pulling panels without appliance training is the trap; DEDP also leaves the appliance outside its scope.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0149","dvt-0186"]},{"id":"dvt:decline-vinyl","dom":"Containment & Occupants","lesson":"dvt-inspect-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"If a customer insists the old plastic transition go back on, decline to reinstall it and document the refusal; a signed waiver does not make a prohibited material acceptable.","src":"GE 49-3000206 p.23; IRC 2024 M1502.4.3; CPSC Pub. 5022","prompt":"What should a tech do when a customer offers to sign a waiver to have the old white vinyl duct reconnected?","trap":"Stretching the vinyl or scheduling a recheck still leaves a prohibited material in place.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0224"]},{"id":"dvt:pre-clean-camera","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-inspect-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"A pre-clean camera pass on a long or concealed run shows crushed or disconnected sections, nests and protruding screws before the tools reach them, and recorded video helps prove results to the customer.","src":"Rotobrush RotoVent-Vac II FAQ; Vent-Vision camera literature","prompt":"What is the main value of a camera pass before brushing a long concealed dryer run?","trap":"Video is not DEDP's primary inspection and never replaces its performance test.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0303","dvt-0305"]},{"id":"dvt:clockwise-always","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-rotary-brush","tag":"FACT","text":"Gardus says always spin the LintEater drill clockwise, going in and coming out. Reversing the drill or turning rods counterclockwise can unthread the joints and leave rods and brush inside the vent.","src":"Gardus LintEater manual, Steps 11-12","prompt":"Which drill direction does the LintEater manual require going in and pulling out?","trap":"Backing out in reverse is the tempting move and exactly how rods get lost.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0169","dvt-0172"]},{"id":"dvt:stop-if-binds","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-rotary-brush","tag":"FACT","text":"If the rotary brush binds, stop spinning at once and never force the rods, to avoid breaking rods or damaging the vent.","src":"Gardus LintEater manual, Step 11","prompt":"What does the LintEater manual say to do first when the brush binds and the drill starts to labor?","trap":"Reversing briefly to free the brush unthreads rod joints inside the duct.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0170"]},{"id":"dvt:rods-one-at-a-time","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-rotary-brush","tag":"FACT","text":"To withdraw, keep spinning clockwise while pulling, disconnect one rod at a time without loosening the rods still inside, and reattach the drill after each.","src":"Gardus LintEater manual, Step 12","prompt":"How are LintEater rods withdrawn once the brush reaches the far end?","trap":"Any counterclockwise turn, by drill or by hand, can unthread a joint still in the duct.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0171"]},{"id":"dvt:retrieval-tool","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-rotary-brush","tag":"FACT","text":"If rods and brush separate inside the duct, Gardus's Retrieval Tool attaches to the remaining rods and is worked by hand, without the drill, to grab them.","src":"Gardus LintEater manual, rods-disconnect note","prompt":"What does Gardus provide for retrieving a brush and rods that separated inside a duct?","trap":"The Blockage Removal Tool is for nests and debris, not lost rods.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0172"]},{"id":"dvt:finish-other-end","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-rotary-brush","tag":"FACT","text":"If the vent is longer than the rods, finish cleaning from the opposite end, and count the rods used to estimate how far you reached.","src":"Gardus LintEater manual, Steps 11-12","prompt":"What does the LintEater manual say to do when the rods run out before the brush reaches the far end?","trap":"Pushing the last section in by hand or leaving the rest for next time leaves lint in the run.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0175"]},{"id":"dvt:dryer-adapter","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-rotary-brush","tag":"FACT","text":"When brushing from outside with the dryer running, fit the LintEater Dryer Adapter between dryer and duct; its built-in guard keeps the brush out of the blower wheel. Remove it afterward or it collects lint.","src":"Gardus LintEater manual, Additional Method 1","prompt":"What keeps the rotary brush out of the dryer's blower wheel when brushing from outside with the dryer running?","trap":"The lint screen sits on the drum side of the blower, so it cannot stop a brush coming up the exhaust.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0173"]},{"id":"dvt:vac-at-laundry-end","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-rotary-brush","tag":"FACT","text":"In the LintEater main method, the vent comes off the dryer and the shop vac or blower attaches at that laundry-room end; brushing then starts from the exterior, opposite the airflow source.","src":"Gardus LintEater manual, Steps 10-11","prompt":"Where is the shop vac or blower connected in the LintEater manual's main method?","trap":"A vac on the hood beside the rods is Method 3, not preferred, and needs the Dryer Adapter stop inside.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0177"]},{"id":"dvt:vent-collapses","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-rotary-brush","tag":"FACT","text":"If the vent compresses when the vacuum is switched on, turn the vacuum off immediately; it signals a blockage or restriction to find.","src":"Gardus LintEater manual, Steps 9-10","prompt":"What does it mean when the foil transition collapses flat as the shop vac starts?","trap":"Collapse under suction means a blockage; lint puffing out means the vac is being overpowered.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0174","dvt-0304"]},{"id":"dvt:hose-2-5","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-rotary-brush","tag":"FACT","text":"The LintEater vacuum adapter is sized for a 2.5 inch shop vac hose, because 1.5 inch household hoses clog with lint.","src":"Gardus LintEater manual p.15","prompt":"What shop vac hose size does the LintEater manual call for, and why?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The LintEater vacuum adapter is sized for a __ shop vac hose.","a":"2.5 inch","d":["1.5 inch","3 inch","4 inch"]},"qs":["dvt-0174"]},{"id":"dvt:louvers","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-rotary-brush","tag":"FACT","text":"Before brushing from outside, tape or clip the hood damper open, or pop the louvers out without breaking their pins, and reinstall them when done.","src":"Gardus LintEater manual, Steps 7 and 14","prompt":"What does the LintEater manual say to do with a louvered wall hood before brushing from outside?","trap":"Leaving the louvers off defeats the backdraft damper the codes require.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0181"]},{"id":"dvt:clean-behind-dryer","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-rotary-brush","tag":"FACT","text":"With the dryer pulled out, clean the area behind it and the back of the cabinet using the lint trap brush and a vacuum before hooking up to the vent.","src":"Gardus LintEater manual, Step 9","prompt":"What does the LintEater procedure have the tech do about the lint mat behind a pulled-out dryer?","trap":"The out-of-scope rule covers the dryer's interior, not loose lint on the floor and back panel.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0310"]},{"id":"dvt:agitate-and-collect","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-air-vacuum","tag":"FACT","text":"Mechanical cleaning pairs an agitation device that dislodges the lint with a vacuum collection device that captures it; airflow or negative pressure alone is not cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.3 (principle)","prompt":"What must accompany vacuum or blower airflow for a dryer duct to be mechanically cleaned?","trap":"HEPA exhaust and pressure verification are separate requirements that dislodge nothing.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0176"]},{"id":"dvt:reverse-skipper","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-air-vacuum","tag":"FACT","text":"A reverse-nozzle skipper line pulls itself through the duct because its rear jets blast air back toward the operator, sweeping lint back to the entry end. Fed from the hood, it needs collection at the hood.","src":"Nikro compressed air tools catalog; Vent-Vision air-tool literature","prompt":"What drives a reverse-nozzle skipper through a dryer duct, and where should its lint be collected?","trap":"Forward nozzles push debris away from the operator; match the collection point to the nozzle direction.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0206","dvt-0298"]},{"id":"dvt:forward-nozzle","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-air-vacuum","tag":"FACT","text":"A forward-blast nozzle fed from the hood drives lint ahead toward the laundry end; if the dryer is still attached, that lint is forced into its outlet and blower housing.","src":"Nikro compressed air tools catalog; LintEater manual Step 10","prompt":"Where does lint end up when a forward-blast nozzle is fed from the hood with the dryer still connected?","trap":"The lint screen is on the drum side of the blower, so it does not stop reverse flow.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0299"]},{"id":"dvt:positive-pressure-leaks","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-air-vacuum","tag":"FACT","text":"A leaf blower or the dryer pushing from the laundry end puts the whole duct under positive pressure, so lint escapes at any open joint; under negative pressure a gap draws air in instead.","src":"LintEater manual Method 2; ACR 2025 §3.4 (principle)","prompt":"Why does lint dust the attic insulation at a slipped joint after cleaning with a leaf blower on the laundry end?","trap":"The blower, not warm attic air, moves the lint out of the gap.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0302","dvt-0303"]},{"id":"dvt:vac-must-win","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-air-vacuum","tag":"FACT","text":"Collection works only while the vacuum removes more air than the agitation tool puts in; a high-flow air whip on a small shop vac turns the duct positive and lint puffs out near the vac.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.4, §4.5 (principle); Nikro DV15360 manual","prompt":"Why does lint puff out at the vac connection when a high-flow air whip runs toward a small shop vac?","trap":"A collapsing connector signals a blockage under suction, not an overpowered vac.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0304"]},{"id":"dvt:y-fitting","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-air-vacuum","tag":"FACT","text":"Nikro's dryer vent Y tool lets a tech brush and vacuum from the laundry end alone: vac hose on one port, duct on another, and a slit-capped third port feeds brushes or compressed air.","src":"Nikro DV15360 manual, Dryer Vent Cleaning","prompt":"Which accessory lets a tech agitate and vacuum a dryer duct at the same time from the laundry end only?","trap":"The LintEater dryer adapter keeps the brush out of the dryer; it does no vacuuming.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0300"]},{"id":"dvt:lintcatcher","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-air-vacuum","tag":"FACT","text":"When positive pressure pushes lint out the termination, Gardus's LintCatcher bag clips over the exterior hood to collect it; a zippered opening empties the bag.","src":"Gardus LintCatcher; LintEater manual Method 2","prompt":"What does the LintEater system use to keep lint blown out at the hood off the property?","trap":"Taping the louvers only holds the damper open; it catches no lint.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0302"]},{"id":"dvt:hepa-indoors","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-air-vacuum","tag":"FACT","text":"A collection vacuum that exhausts indoors must be HEPA filtered (99.97 percent at 0.3 micron) with its full filter set in place; non-HEPA units exhaust outdoors.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.3, §4.1.3 (principle); Nikro DV15360 manual","prompt":"What vacuum setup meets NADCA's rule when the collection vac will exhaust into a finished basement?","trap":"MERV 16 and pleated shop-vac cartridges are not HEPA, and an air scrubber does not make a non-HEPA vac compliant.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0308"]},{"id":"dvt:nikro-suction-loss","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-air-vacuum","tag":"FACT","text":"When a Nikro DV15360 loses suction, switch it off at once and look for a full bag or tank, a clogged hose or tube, or a loaded filter. The impaction prefilter is replaced, never cleaned.","src":"Nikro DV15360 manual, Filter Maintenance","prompt":"What does Nikro's manual say to do when suction at a DV15360 dryer vent vac drops sharply?","trap":"Never pull a filter to regain airflow; the vac never runs without its filters.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0301"]},{"id":"dvt:nikro-filter-option","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-air-vacuum","tag":"FACT","text":"For dryer vent lint, Nikro lets the paper collection bag be left out of the DV15360 so lint loads straight into the 4 mil poly bag; every other filter stays in place.","src":"Nikro DV15360 manual, Filter Options","prompt":"Which filter change does Nikro's DV15360 manual allow for dryer vent work?","trap":"The cloth bag, impaction prefilter and HEPA filter always stay in.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0309"]},{"id":"dvt:active-nest","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-finish-service","tag":"REG","text":"Destroying an active nest (eggs or chicks) of a bird protected by the Migratory Bird Treaty Act needs a federal permit; an inactive nest may be removed without one if it is not kept.","src":"USFWS depredation permit FAQ, Q4","prompt":"What may a tech do without a federal permit about an active songbird nest with eggs in a dryer hood?","trap":"Homeowner approval does not replace a federal permit, and state rules can add protection.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0184"]},{"id":"dvt:unprotected-birds","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-finish-service","tag":"REG","text":"Nonnative European starlings, house sparrows and rock (feral) pigeons are not protected by the Migratory Bird Treaty Act; native birds such as robins, mockingbirds and wrens are.","src":"USFWS depredation permit FAQ, Q2","prompt":"Which birds nesting in dryer terminations fall outside Migratory Bird Treaty Act protection?","trap":"Check state law too; a state can protect birds the federal act does not.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0185"]},{"id":"dvt:fix-sticking-flap","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-finish-service","tag":"FACT","text":"If the hood flap drags and barely lifts after cleaning, free it up or replace the hood so the flap opens fully under dryer airflow; never remove the flap or prop it open.","src":"NFPA Clothes Dryer Safety tip sheet; GE owner's manual; IRC 2024 M1502.3","prompt":"What should a tech do when the hood flap drags on its hinge and barely lifts after cleaning?","trap":"Removing the flap defeats the required backdraft damper.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0183"]},{"id":"dvt:booster-noise","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-finish-service","tag":"FACT","text":"Per Fantech's DBF4XLT instructions, excessive noise or vibration from a dryer booster fan may indicate lint buildup on its impeller.","src":"Fantech DBF4XLT instructions, Maintenance","prompt":"What likely makes a dryer booster fan noisy and vibrating, per Fantech's DBF4XLT instructions?","trap":"A pressure tube problem stops the fan from starting; it does not cause vibration.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0189"]},{"id":"dvt:booster-power","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-finish-service","tag":"FACT","text":"Before opening a booster fan to clean its impeller, disconnect the fan's own power supply at the source; the fan has its own cord, so switching off or unplugging the dryer does not isolate it.","src":"Fantech DBF4XLT instructions, Maintenance","prompt":"What do Fantech's instructions require before pulling the inlet duct off a DBF4XLT booster fan?","trap":"Waiting for the fan to coast down is not isolation; it runs on for a while after the dryer stops.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0190"]},{"id":"dvt:booster-led","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-finish-service","tag":"FACT","text":"If a DBF4XLT booster's LED stays dark with the dryer running, check the fan's 120 V power, obstructions between dryer and fan, and that the pressure tube is connected.","src":"Fantech DBF4XLT instructions, Troubleshooting","prompt":"What should a tech check when a DBF4XLT booster fan's indicator LED stays dark while the dryer runs?","trap":"The dryer's thermal fuse controls heat, not the booster's start signal.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0191"]},{"id":"dvt:booster-6-months","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-finish-service","tag":"FACT","text":"Fantech says inspect a DBF4XLT booster fan's impeller for lint at least every six months under normal conditions, and more often with heavy use.","src":"Fantech DBF4XLT instructions, Maintenance","prompt":"How often do Fantech's DBF4XLT instructions say to inspect the booster fan impeller for lint?","trap":"Twelve months is NFPA's vent cleaning interval, and five years is Fantech's warranty term.","num":{"q":"Fantech's stated inspection interval for a DBF4XLT impeller under normal conditions is every __.","a":"6 months","d":["3 months","12 months","60 months"]},"qs":["dvt-0192"]},{"id":"dvt:transition-trim","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-finish-service","tag":"FACT","text":"GE: clamp a foil-type transition to the dryer outlet, then with the dryer in its final position extend it, overlap the wall pipe about 2 inches, cut off the excess and keep it as straight as possible.","src":"GE Dryer Installation Instructions 49-3000206 p.23","prompt":"How does GE's installation guide say to size a UL-listed foil-type transition when reconnecting?","trap":"Extra length coiled behind the dryer or fully compressed flex traps lint and restricts flow.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0200"]},{"id":"dvt:periscope-box","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","lesson":"dvt-finish-service","tag":"FACT","text":"For a dryer pushed nearly flush to the wall, use a listed slim periscope transition or a recessed dryer vent box set in the wall so the transition is not crushed.","src":"Manufacturer literature (dryerbox.com; periscope vents)","prompt":"What fixes a transition that kinks every time the dryer is pushed nearly flush to the wall?","trap":"A longer foil transition looped behind the dryer is the usual cause of crushing, not the cure.","num":null,"qs":["dvt-0199"]}],"lessons":[{"id":"code-dryer-types","title":"Dryer types and system parts in the codes","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","order":100,"intro":"How the I-codes classify clothes dryers (Type 1 vs Type 2) and name the parts of an exhaust system. Every other code rule hangs on these terms.","units":["code:type1-def","code:type2-def","code:type-listing","code:type2-commercial","code:type1-not-vented","code:system-parts","code:transition-scope","code:depv-scope","code:g2439-numbering"],"mnemonic":"In the listing standards the last number is the type: Z21.5.1, CSA 7.1, Volume I for Type 1 (family home); Z21.5.2, CSA 7.2, Volume II for Type 2 (business the public uses). The system runs transition duct, exhaust duct, exhaust terminal, and the transition is UL 2158A. A Type 1 gas dryer needs no vent but still needs exhaust under G2439 (IFGC 614)."},{"id":"code-irc-termination","title":"Outdoor discharge and terminations (IRC M1502.1 to M1502.3)","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","order":120,"intro":"Where a dryer duct may discharge and what the terminal must look like. These are the first things checked outside and some of the most common field violations.","units":["code:m1502-general","code:independent","code:condensing-exception","code:outdoor-only","code:term-3ft","code:term-soffit","code:backdraft","code:no-screen","code:passageway"],"mnemonic":"Out, alone, 3 feet, flap, no screen, 12.5: outdoors on its own system (never attic, soffit, ridge vent or crawl space), at least 3 ft every way from any opening, soffit vents included, if the maker names no location, a backdraft damper, no screen, and at least 12.5 sq in open, unreduced. Listed and labeled condensing dryers are exempt from the outdoor rule."},{"id":"code-irc-duct-build","title":"Duct material, joints, supports and transition ducts (IRC M1502.4.1 to M1502.4.3)","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","order":140,"intro":"What the concealed exhaust duct and the exposed transition duct must be made of, and how they are joined and hung. A tech checks every one of these during a pre-service assessment.","units":["code:material","code:diameter","code:supports","code:male-end","code:joint-seal","code:tape-181b","code:fastener","code:cavity","code:transition-listed","code:transition-8ft","code:transition-exposed"],"mnemonic":"Duct is 4, 28, 12, 1/8: 4 in smooth metal, No. 28 gage (0.0157 in) or thicker, supports no more than 12 ft apart, screws no more than 1/8 in into the duct. Male ends point downstream; joints are sealed AND fastened (tape 181 B-FX, mastic 181 BM). The transition is one UL 2158A length, 8 ft at most, never inside walls, floors or ceilings."},{"id":"code-irc-length","title":"Duct length, fitting equivalents and the length label (IRC M1502.4.6 to M1502.4.7)","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","order":160,"intro":"How the maximum dryer duct length is set and how elbows eat into it. This is the most calculation-heavy part of the code and a favorite for number questions.","mnemonic":"Miters cost a nickel: a 4 in mitered 90 is 5 ft and the mitered 45 is half, 2 ft 6 in. Smooth 90s at radius 6, 8, 10 count down 9, 7, 6 (1 ft 9 in, 1 ft 7 in, 1 ft 6 in). Smooth 45s: a foot, a foot, then 9 in. Bigger radius, smaller penalty.","units":["code:length-methods","code:length-35","code:length-fittings","code:fit-mitered-90","code:fit-mitered-45","code:fit-6-90","code:fit-8-90","code:fit-10-90","code:fit-smooth-45","code:fit-10-45","code:fit-pattern","code:length-example","code:length-maker","code:concealment-copy","code:length-maker-table","code:length-depv","code:label-required","code:label-6ft"]},{"id":"code-irc-fans-protect","title":"Fans, shield plates, makeup air and roughed-in ducts (IRC M1502.4.4 to M1502.6)","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","order":180,"intro":"Powered dryer ducts, protecting ducts from nails, makeup air and capping a duct for a future dryer. Permitted equipment and outright prohibitions sit side by side here and are easy to confuse.","units":["code:depv-ul705","code:booster-ban","code:booster-vs-depv","code:shield-where","code:shield-thick","code:shield-extend","code:makeup-200","code:closet-100","code:duct-required","code:future-use","code:duct-required-exception"],"mnemonic":"No UL 705, no fan: a UL 705 power ventilator is allowed, a domestic booster fan never is. Shields are 1-1/4, 0.062, 2: a duct under 1-1/4 in from the face, where nails or screws could hit, gets steel at least 0.062 in thick, at least 2 in above sole plates and below top plates. Makeup air over 200 cfm; a closet gets at least 100 sq in if the instructions are silent."},{"id":"code-gas-dryers","title":"Gas dryer exhaust: IRC G2439 and IFGC 614","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","order":200,"intro":"The fuel gas versions of the dryer exhaust rules. Most numbers match IRC M1502, so exams test the few that differ and the gas-code prohibitions.","mnemonic":"Gas goes tight: 4 ft hangers and 0.016 in metal (G2439, IFGC 614.9, IMC 504.9). M1502 goes loose: 12 ft hangers and 0.0157 in (28 gage). The rest carries over: 35, 8, 6, 12.5, 200, 100.","units":["code:gas-poc","code:gas-thickness","code:gas-supports","code:gas-what-differs","code:gas-no-vent","code:gas-no-plenum","code:gas-fire-rated","code:gas-fire-damper","code:gas-closet","code:gas-future","code:gas-concealed-instr","code:ifgc-booster","code:ifgc-3ft"]},{"id":"code-imc-commercial","title":"IMC 504: penetrations, cleanouts, commercial dryers and multistory shafts","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","order":220,"intro":"The IMC rules a tech meets in apartment buildings, laundromats and other commercial work, including the checklist for common multistory dryer shafts.","mnemonic":"Multistory shaft, one job: rated shaft, no dampers, straight 26 gage steel, fan motor out of the airstream running nonstop on standby power with an alarm, makeup air, a 12 by 12 cleanout at the bottom, no screen at the top, dryers only.","units":["code:imc-annular","code:imc-dampers","code:imc-riser-cleanout","code:ifgc-type2-riser","code:imc-shield-16ga","code:comm-maker","code:comm-fan","code:comm-6in","code:comm-transition","code:ms-shaft","code:ms-duct","code:ms-fan","code:ms-monitor","code:ms-cleanout","code:ms-dedicated"]},{"id":"code-edition-changes","title":"Edition changes: 2018 and 2021 to 2024","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","order":240,"intro":"What moved or changed in the dryer exhaust sections between the 2018, 2021 and 2024 codes. Jurisdictions adopt different editions, so section numbers and a few rules depend on the book in force.","mnemonic":"2021 was the big year: booster fan ban, soffit wording, the IMC 3 ft rule, and later sections shifted up one number. 2024 added IRC makeup air and moved IMC shield plates to 16 gage. The numbers 35, 8, 12.5 and 6 never moved.","units":["code:ed-irc-booster","code:ed-irc-renumber","code:ed-irc-table","code:ed-irc-soffit","code:ed-irc-makeup","code:ed-numbers-same","code:ed-imc-renumber","code:ed-imc-added-2021","code:ed-imc-shield","code:ed-imc-ul2158a","code:ed-imc-independent"]},{"id":"dedp-purpose","title":"What the DEDP test is for","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","order":300,"intro":"Why NADCA wrote a performance test for cleaned dryer ducts, what it covers and leaves out, and where video fits. Tested because the clean-first rule and the scope exclusions trip people up.","units":["dedp:fwd-clean-first","dedp:fwd-pressurized","dedp:fwd-compensates","dedp:fwd-video-may","dedp:fwd-visual-no-replace","dedp:fwd-video-after-fail","dedp:disc-new-and-existing","dedp:disc-judgment","dedp:disc-no-guarantee","dedp:disc-ahj-mold","dedp:purpose","dedp:scope-excluded","dedp:app-criteria","dedp:app-parties","dedp:app-why-device","dedp:app-4in","dedp:qual-after-service","dedp:lim-appliance","dedp:lim-hazards"],"mnemonic":"Clean first, then test: the DEDP test runs only on a duct already checked by eye and confirmed clean (SHALL). Video MAY confirm the inside is clean and is recommended after a fail to find the cause, but no visual inspection replaces the performance criteria (SHALL). The dryer appliance and the existing transition duct are not included in the standard."},{"id":"dedp-system","title":"How a dryer exhaust system works","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","order":320,"intro":"The DEDP's picture of airflow through a dryer, what makes lint build up, and its definitions of each part of the exhaust system.","units":["dedp:intro-single-dryer","dedp:intro-airflow","dedp:intro-typical","dedp:intro-efficiency","dedp:intro-fire","dedp:intro-mfr-service","dedp:intro-accelerants","dedp:intro-iaq","dedp:intro-cementitious","dedp:def-transition","dedp:def-duct-connection","dedp:def-permanent","dedp:def-exhaust-duct","dedp:def-penetration","dedp:def-termination","dedp:def-wall-cap","dedp:def-damper","dedp:def-secondary-trap","dedp:def-dryer","dedp:def-class0","dedp:def-class1"],"mnemonic":"Follow the lint out: room air, heat, drum, lint filter, fan, then Transition, Connection, Permanent duct, Penetration, Termination. Class 0 is zero; Class 1 is flame spread 25 and smoke 50 at most, smoke doubling flame. Water plus lint can set like cement."},{"id":"dedp-pretest","title":"Pre-test inspection","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","order":340,"intro":"What must be accessible, observed and confirmed before the DEDP test runs. The shall versus recommended wording here is heavily tested.","units":["dedp:1.1-confirm-clean","dedp:1.2-access","dedp:1.2.3-termination","dedp:1.3-lint-checks","dedp:1.3-damage-checks","dedp:1.3.7-hold","dedp:1.4-short-duct","dedp:1.5-contiguous","dedp:1.6-booster","dedp:1.7-secondary-trap","dedp:1.9-code-check","dedp:1.9-inform"],"mnemonic":"Every pre-test step is SHALL except the pause: holding the test until a Section 1.3 condition is remedied or reported is only recommended. Reach Transition, Connection, Termination; check whether a booster fan works and a secondary lint trap is clean; test only a duct that stays 4 in end to end, even a straight run under 6 ft, since the damper still gets checked."},{"id":"dedp-length","title":"Developed length and fitting equivalents","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","order":360,"intro":"How to estimate developed length with the 5 ft elbow rule and the reprinted IMC 2012 fitting table. Every table value is a drill target.","units":["dedp:1.8-actual","dedp:1.8.2-inspect","dedp:1.8.3-5ft","dedp:len-example","dedp:1.8.4-location","dedp:4.1-record","dedp:def-actual","dedp:def-developed","dedp:def-equivalent","dedp:t504-m45","dedp:t504-m90","dedp:t504-s6-45","dedp:t504-s6-90","dedp:t504-s8-45","dedp:t504-s8-90","dedp:t504-s10-45","dedp:t504-s10-90","dedp:imc-mfr-max","dedp:imc-code-official","dedp:imc-table-default"],"mnemonic":"Mitered 4 in radius: 90 = 5 ft (the DEDP every-elbow number), 45 = half, 2 ft 6 in. Smooth 90s shrink as the radius grows: 6, 8, 10 in radius give 21, 19, 18 in (1 ft 9, 1 ft 7, 1 ft 6). Smooth 45s run 12, 12, 9 in."},{"id":"dedp-equipment","title":"DEDP test equipment","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","order":380,"intro":"The test fan, gauge, tap fitting, tubing, test duct and anemometer, with every spec number the exam can ask.","units":["dedp:def-device","dedp:2.1-motor","dedp:2.1-rpm","dedp:2.1-cfm","dedp:2.2-gauge","dedp:2.2.1-digital","dedp:2.3-tap","dedp:2.4-tubing","dedp:2.5-test-duct","dedp:def-test-duct","dedp:2.5.2-length","dedp:2.6-anemometer","dedp:2.6-fpm","dedp:def-anemometer"],"mnemonic":"Fan: 113 W, 2,910 RPM, 190 CFM. Then think in 2s and 4s: gauge 0 to 2.0 in wc, tubing 1/8 in ID and 1/4 in OD (inside is the smaller), test duct 4 to 6 ft with a 4 in ID, anemometer 50 to 2000 fpm."},{"id":"dedp-steps","title":"Running the DEDP test, Steps 1 to 9","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","order":400,"intro":"The nine test steps in order with their timings and positions. Order, run times and which lines say should are the usual questions.","units":["dedp:3.0.2-no-dryer-blower","dedp:3.1.1-step1","dedp:3.1.2-step2","dedp:3.1.2-tap-spot","dedp:3.1.3-step3","dedp:3.1.3.1-vertical","dedp:3.1.3.2-tubing-length","dedp:3.1.4-step4","dedp:3.1.5-step5","dedp:3.1.6-step6","dedp:3.1.6.1-booster","dedp:3.1.7-step7","dedp:3.2.1-step8","dedp:3.2.1.1-three","dedp:3.2.2-step9","dedp:3.2.2.1-below"],"mnemonic":"Pull, Tap, Tube, Blower, Clamp, Power, Pressure, Velocity, Compare: pull the dryer, tap the test duct, tube the gauge, blower on the duct, clamp to the wall, power up a full minute, read pressure, read velocity (3 spots, keep the max), compare to the tables."},{"id":"dedp-tables","title":"Performance Tables 4.9 and 4.10","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","order":420,"intro":"The pass and fail numbers for side wall and roof top terminations at each developed length. Pure memorization, so learn the pattern.","units":["dedp:tbl-rows","dedp:tbl-direction","dedp:tbl-roof-offset","dedp:tbl-sw10","dedp:tbl-sw20","dedp:tbl-sw30","dedp:tbl-sw40","dedp:tbl-sw50","dedp:tbl-rf10","dedp:tbl-rf20","dedp:tbl-rf30","dedp:tbl-rf40","dedp:tbl-rf50","dedp:tbl-v10","dedp:tbl-v20","dedp:tbl-v30","dedp:tbl-v40","dedp:tbl-v50"],"mnemonic":"Roof = side wall + 0.20 in wc at every row; velocity is identical in both tables. Side wall pressure 0.30, 0.55, 0.65, 0.75, 0.80: one jump of 0.25, then up 0.10, 0.10, then 0.05. Velocity counts down 1700, 1600, 1500, skips 1400, then 1300, 1200."},{"id":"dedp-results","title":"Recording results and diagnosing a fail","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","order":440,"intro":"What gets documented, how pass or fail is decided, what the tech may offer, and the Appendix list of fail causes including the optional dryer-driven reading.","units":["dedp:4.6-cross-ref","dedp:4.2-back-pressure","dedp:4.3-max-velocity","dedp:4.4-transition","dedp:4.5-adhered","dedp:4.6.1-table-choice","dedp:4.7-fail","dedp:4.8-offer","dedp:4.8.1-retest","dedp:app-function","dedp:app-not-obligated","dedp:app-should-address","dedp:app-design","dedp:app-leaks","dedp:app-termination","dedp:app-moisture","dedp:app-cement","dedp:app-still-blocked","dedp:app-noncode","dedp:app-appliance-may","dedp:app-appliance-should","dedp:app-appliance-optional"],"mnemonic":"Three numbers, two tables: estimated developed length, back pressure and maximum velocity go against the side wall or roof table, chosen by the termination, and missing on either value or both is a fail. Appendix causes should be addressed but do not obligate the contractor. The three MAYs: offer more services, retest, and take the dryer-driven velocity reading."},{"id":"dedp-terms","title":"DEDP key terms and references","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","order":460,"intro":"Definitions that decide questions (shall, recommended, adhered versus debris) plus the codes and UL listing the standard references.","units":["dedp:def-shall","dedp:def-recommended","dedp:def-dedp-test","dedp:def-inspection","dedp:def-visual","dedp:def-adhered","dedp:def-debris","dedp:def-cementitious","dedp:def-imc","dedp:def-irc","dedp:ref-codes","dedp:ref-ul2158a"],"mnemonic":"The brush decides: what silicon carbide bristles cannot remove by direct contact is adhered; loose material that does not belong is debris. The codes are 2012 except the IMC: IFGC 2012 Section 614, IMC 2009 Section 504, 2012 IRC."},{"id":"dvt-dryer-basics","title":"How a vented dryer works","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","order":500,"intro":"How air moves through a vented dryer, what differs between gas and electric models, Type 1 versus Type 2, and how ventless dryers avoid a duct. Every later module builds on this airflow picture.","mnemonic":"Air path: Heat, Tumble, Filter, Fan, Out. Exhaust parts in order: Inside, Transition, Main, Cap.","units":["dvt:airflow-path","dvt:blower-positive-pressure","dvt:electric-240v","dvt:heat-waits-for-motor","dvt:gas-exhaust-co","dvt:type1-type2","dvt:exhaust-four-parts","dvt:gas-always-vented","dvt:condensing-dryer","dvt:heat-pump-dryer","dvt:dryer-cfm"]},{"id":"dvt-controls-lint","title":"Safety controls, lint and airflow","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","order":510,"intro":"Which control does what when a vent restricts, and why lint settles in a duct. These explain the symptoms techs see and the numbers manufacturers publish.","mnemonic":"Cycling at the blower, limit at the heater, fuse is one-shot. Whirlpool elbows cost 10 and 6 ft (code table 5 and 2.5); 1,200 fpm keeps lint flying.","units":["dvt:operating-thermostat","dvt:high-limit","dvt:thermal-fuse","dvt:fuse-repeat-vent","dvt:cpsc-temps","dvt:moisture-sensor","dvt:af-code","dvt:ul2158-high-limit","dvt:fine-lint-passes","dvt:velocity-carries-lint","dvt:no-upsize","dvt:long-runs-lint","dvt:whirlpool-elbows","dvt:clog-slows-drying","dvt:lint-screen-residue","dvt:screen-housing","dvt:insulate-slope"]},{"id":"dvt-fire-numbers","title":"Dryer fire numbers by source","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","order":520,"intro":"USFA and NFPA dryer fire statistics, each tied to its source and data years. Test writers swap the agencies and years, so learn every number as a pair.","mnemonic":"USFA 2008-2010: 2,900 fires, failure to clean 34, peak January 11. NFPA 2014-2018: 13,820 fires, 7 deaths, failure to clean 32, lint 27, electric 78.","units":["dvt:usfa-annual","dvt:usfa-failure-to-clean","dvt:usfa-january","dvt:nfpa-annual","dvt:nfpa-electric-gas","dvt:nfpa-failure-to-clean","dvt:nfpa-lint-deaths"]},{"id":"dvt-hoods-fans","title":"Hoods, dampers, power ventilators and commercial dryers","dom":"Understanding Dryer Vents","order":530,"intro":"Manufacturer rules for termination hoods and in-line dampers, how listed dryer power ventilators start and warn, and how commercial Type 2 dryers are ducted.","units":["dvt:no-magnetic-hood","dvt:spring-damper","dvt:depv-pressure-switch","dvt:depv-alert-panel","dvt:ul2158a-scope","dvt:type2-dexter-duct","dvt:type2-branch-45","dvt:type2-makeup-backdraft"],"mnemonic":"Let gravity close it: magnetic latch hoods are a fire hazard, gravity-closing louvered or box hoods are recommended, and a spring damper, needing 0.04 to 0.08 in w.g. to start opening, is not recommended without regular cleaning. A UL 705 power ventilator starts on duct pressure and has its own cord. Dexter: 8 in, about 600 CFM, at most 14 ft and two elbows per cylinder, branches at 45 degrees downstream."},{"id":"dvt-ppe-contaminants","title":"Safety program, PPE and droppings hazards","dom":"Technician Safety","order":600,"intro":"The core of a company safety program, how OSHA's PPE and respirator rules apply to lint and droppings work, and the diseases and carbon monoxide hazards behind them.","mnemonic":"Dust needs a filter, vapor needs a sorbent. H and C are the fungi in droppings; psittacosis is the bird bacteria that antibiotics cure.","units":["dvt:osha-core-elements","dvt:osha-temps-equal","dvt:ppe-hazard-cert","dvt:resp-program-trigger","dvt:resp-voluntary","dvt:resp-particulate","dvt:linteater-ppe","dvt:epa-mold-goggles","dvt:glove-plan","dvt:fungal-droppings","dvt:psittacosis","dvt:histo-wet-down","dvt:bag-disposables","dvt:rodent-heavy","dvt:co-symptoms","dvt:co-alarms"]},{"id":"dvt-ladders-roofs","title":"Ladders, roofs and fall protection","dom":"Technician Safety","order":610,"intro":"OSHA's ladder setup, roof edge and personal fall arrest rules that apply every time a tech climbs to a roof or soffit termination.","mnemonic":"Ladder: 3 ft above, 4 up and 1 out, 3 points of contact. Edges: 4 ft in general industry, 6 ft in construction. Warning line 34 to 39; guardrail 42 plus or minus 3. Anchor 5,000 to hold, 1,800 to feel.","units":["dvt:ladder-3ft","dvt:ladder-4to1","dvt:ladder-3point","dvt:ladder-defect","dvt:ladder-slippery","dvt:powerline-10ft","dvt:fall-triggers","dvt:skylight-hole","dvt:warning-line","dvt:no-body-belt","dvt:impact-gear","dvt:anchor-5000","dvt:prompt-rescue"]},{"id":"dvt-weather-power-gas","title":"Weather, electricity and gas handling","dom":"Technician Safety","order":620,"intro":"Heat and lightning rules for roof work, electrical isolation for tools and dryers, and gas dryer handling steps from GE, Whirlpool and CPSC.","mnemonic":"Heat work: 20 percent on day one, add no more than 20 a day. When thunder roars, go indoors; wait 30 minutes.","units":["dvt:heat-exhaustion","dvt:acclimatize","dvt:lightning","dvt:gfci-at-outlet","dvt:unplug-dryer","dvt:no-extension-cord","dvt:gas-smell","dvt:gas-leak-test","dvt:new-gas-connector","dvt:brass-connector","dvt:connector-slack","dvt:generator-20ft"]},{"id":"dvt-hazcom-customers","title":"Hazard communication and customer expectations","dom":"Containment & Occupants","order":700,"intro":"OSHA HazCom basics for a traveling crew, how to talk with occupants about chemicals and hazards, what never to promise, and the NFPA safety tips customers ask about.","mnemonic":"SDS middle block: 6 spill, 7 store, 8 protect.","units":["dvt:sds-travel","dvt:portable-label","dvt:sds-section-6","dvt:product-consent","dvt:attic-stain-mold","dvt:no-guarantee","dvt:design-fix","dvt:price-before-work","dvt:roof-scheduling","dvt:nfpa-lint-filter","dvt:nfpa-vent-interval","dvt:nfpa-off-at-night","dvt:nfpa-solvent-clothes","dvt:nfpa-clear-area"]},{"id":"dvt-jobsite-containment","title":"Jobsite setup, lint containment and property care","dom":"Containment & Occupants","order":710,"intro":"Assessing the job, protecting occupants and property, and keeping lint and droppings contained from the wall port to the cap and out to the truck.","units":["dvt:walk-route","dvt:trace-unknown-end","dvt:pets-confined","dvt:sensitive-occupant","dvt:vac-outside","dvt:coverings","dvt:preexisting-damage","dvt:move-on-cardboard","dvt:steam-dryer-leaks","dvt:hot-shingles","dvt:seal-vac-port","dvt:clear-cap-first","dvt:capture-at-cap","dvt:clean-tools-between","dvt:open-vac-outside","dvt:bag-nest","dvt:fold-drop-cloth","dvt:hepa-damp-wipe"],"mnemonic":"Look first, then catch it where it starts. Before anything moves: walk the route, find where the duct ends, confine pets, photograph old damage for the owner. Then: vac sealed to the port, blocked cap cleared from outside first, bag or vac over the cap, nest bagged where it comes out, drop cloths folded inward, HEPA vacuum and damp wipe, never a broom."},{"id":"dvt-inspect-symptoms","title":"Reading the symptoms","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","order":800,"intro":"What common customer complaints and termination conditions point to, and how to check the hood the right way before and after cleaning.","units":["dvt:hot-humid-room","dvt:long-dry-hot-top","dvt:burning-smell","dvt:flap-jammed","dvt:flap-test-running","dvt:hood-12-inches","dvt:roof-cap-lint"],"mnemonic":"Hot, humid, linty room: likely a loose or torn transition; slow drying or a hot top: check the vent before the dryer; burning smell: stop until the vent and the dryer interior are both cleaned. Test the flap with the dryer running, not by hand, and keep the hood at least 12 inches clear."},{"id":"dvt-inspect-findings","title":"Transitions, fasteners, photos and referrals","dom":"Inspections & Assessments","order":810,"intro":"The manufacturer rules behind common transition and fastener findings, how to document them, and when the problem belongs to an appliance technician instead.","units":["dvt:crushed-transition","dvt:plastic-transition","dvt:flex-not-concealed","dvt:whirlpool-flex-length","dvt:clamps-not-screws","dvt:as-found-photos","dvt:refer-appliance","dvt:cabinet-interior","dvt:decline-vinyl","dvt:pre-clean-camera"],"mnemonic":"Whirlpool trims the code: a flex transition of 7 3/4 ft at most, a little under the code's 8. Clamps, not screws; no plastic anywhere, waiver or not; no flex inside walls, ceilings or floors. Photograph as found and after; if a cleaned duct passes but the dryer still dries slowly, refer it to a qualified appliance service provider."},{"id":"dvt-rotary-brush","title":"Rotary brush cleaning by the manual","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","order":900,"intro":"The Gardus LintEater manual's rules for spinning, withdrawing and retrieving rods, where the vacuum goes, and what to do at the hood and behind the dryer.","mnemonic":"Clockwise in, clockwise out, one rod at a time; if it binds, stop.","units":["dvt:clockwise-always","dvt:stop-if-binds","dvt:rods-one-at-a-time","dvt:retrieval-tool","dvt:finish-other-end","dvt:dryer-adapter","dvt:vac-at-laundry-end","dvt:vent-collapses","dvt:hose-2-5","dvt:louvers","dvt:clean-behind-dryer"]},{"id":"dvt-air-vacuum","title":"Air tools, pressure and vacuum collection","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","order":910,"intro":"How compressed-air nozzles move lint, why negative pressure keeps it contained, and the vacuum and filter rules from NADCA and the tool makers.","mnemonic":"Reverse jets come back to you; forward jets go away. The vac must pull more air than the whip pushes.","units":["dvt:agitate-and-collect","dvt:reverse-skipper","dvt:forward-nozzle","dvt:positive-pressure-leaks","dvt:vac-must-win","dvt:y-fitting","dvt:lintcatcher","dvt:hepa-indoors","dvt:nikro-suction-loss","dvt:nikro-filter-option"]},{"id":"dvt-finish-service","title":"Nests, flaps, booster fans and reconnecting","dom":"Cleaning & Verification","order":920,"intro":"Federal rules for nests, fixing the hood damper, servicing booster fans per Fantech, and reconnecting the transition without crushing it.","mnemonic":"Unprotected imports: Starling, Sparrow, Pigeon.","units":["dvt:active-nest","dvt:unprotected-birds","dvt:fix-sticking-flap","dvt:booster-noise","dvt:booster-power","dvt:booster-led","dvt:booster-6-months","dvt:transition-trim","dvt:periscope-box"]}],"sequences":[{"id":"seq-code-length-check","title":"Checking a dryer duct run against the IRC length limit","steps":["Start at the transition duct connection; leave the transition duct out.","Measure the straight exhaust duct length to the outlet terminal.","Add each fitting's equivalent length from the table or maker's data.","Compare the total to 35 ft or the dryer maker's allowed length.","If equivalent length exceeds 35 ft, confirm a permanent label or tag.","Confirm the label is within 6 ft of the exhaust duct connection."],"src":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.1, M1502.4.6.2, M1502.4.7"},{"id":"seq-code-airflow-path","title":"Dryer exhaust path from appliance to outdoors, in code terms","steps":["Dryer exhaust outlet on the appliance.","Transition duct: one UL 2158A length, 8 ft max, exposed.","Exhaust duct: 4 in smooth metal, joints sealed and fastened.","Terminal: backdraft damper, no screen, 12.5 sq in open area.","Outdoors, at least 3 ft from building openings unless the maker specifies otherwise."],"src":"IRC 2024 M1502.3, M1502.3.1, M1502.4.1, M1502.4.2, M1502.4.3"},{"id":"seq-dedp-setup","title":"DEDP test setup, Steps 1 to 5","steps":["Pull the dryer out and remove the existing transition duct","Swap in the test duct and install the tap fitting at its midpoint","Run tubing from the tap fitting to the gauge's positive input","Connect the testing device blower to one end of the test duct","Clamp the test duct to the 4 in wall port"],"src":"DEDP 2014 §3.1.1 to §3.1.5"},{"id":"seq-dedp-run","title":"DEDP test run and scoring, Steps 6 to 9","steps":["Energize the testing device and run it at least one minute","Record the gauge reading in wc once pressure stabilizes","Take at least three velocity readings at the termination, keep the maximum","Compare back pressure and velocity with Tables 4.9 and 4.10","If either misses its minimum, consult the Appendix conditions list"],"src":"DEDP 2014 §3.1.6 to §3.2.2.1"},{"id":"seq-dedp-job","title":"Where the DEDP test fits in a dryer vent job","steps":["Clean the duct","Visually confirm the duct is clean","Run the DEDP test with the NADCA testing device","Compare the readings with the matching table for pass or fail"],"src":"DEDP 2014, Foreword; §1.1; §3.0.1; §4.6"},{"id":"seq-dedp-after-fail","title":"What to do after a DEDP fail","steps":["A reading misses its table value, so the duct fails","Check the Appendix conditions list; video is recommended to diagnose","The technician may offer solutions or services to fix the cause","The technician may rerun the DEDP test after that work"],"src":"DEDP 2014 Foreword; §4.7; §4.8; §4.8.1; Appendix"},{"id":"seq-dedp-airflow","title":"Air path through a vented dryer and its exhaust","steps":["Room air is drawn into the dryer","Electric or gas components heat the air","Air passes through the drum, picking up moisture and lint","Air passes through the lint filter","The internal fan discharges the air from the dryer","Air travels through the dryer exhaust duct system","Air discharges at the exterior termination device"],"src":"DEDP 2014, Introduction"},{"id":"seq-dvt-airflow-path","title":"Air path through a vented dryer and its exhaust system","steps":["Room air is drawn into the dryer cabinet","The element or burner heats the air","Air tumbles through the drum, picking up moisture and lint","The lint filter catches most of the lint","The blower pushes the air into the transition duct","The main duct carries it through the building","The termination cover releases it outdoors"],"src":"CPSC 1999 dryer report p.2; NADCA DVT handout, Module 1"},{"id":"seq-dvt-service-call","title":"A full dryer vent service call, in order","steps":["Walk the route and photograph conditions as found","Lay coverings and confine pets before moving anything","Unplug the dryer and pull it out onto protection","Hook up the vacuum, then agitate the duct to dislodge lint","Inspect the cleaned run, with a camera where needed","Reconnect one listed transition and check the flap opens","HEPA-vacuum and damp-wipe the work area","Document before and after photos and parts used"],"src":"NADCA DVT Modules 3-5; Gardus LintEater manual Steps 7-14; ACR 2025 §4.3, §5.6 (principle)"},{"id":"seq-dvt-linteater","title":"LintEater rotary brush cleaning from the termination","steps":["Tape the hood damper open or remove the louvers","Turn off and unplug the dryer, then pull it out","Vacuum lint behind the dryer and off its back","Take the vent off the dryer and attach the shop vac","With the vac running, brush in from outside, spinning clockwise","Withdraw spinning clockwise, removing one rod at a time","Reinstall the hood louvers"],"src":"Gardus LintEater manual, Steps 7-14"},{"id":"seq-dvt-gas-dryer-move","title":"Moving and reconnecting a gas dryer","steps":["Check the gas connector; stop and refer out if uncoated brass","Unplug the dryer and pull it out only as far as needed","Service the vent and reconnect the transition","Slide the dryer back without kinking the connector or crushing the duct","Soap-test the gas joints with ammonia-free solution, never a flame","If bubbles appear, close the valve, retighten and retest"],"src":"CPSC Pub. 5135; GE Dryer Installation Instructions 49-3000206, Test for Leaks; Gardus LintEater manual Step 8"},{"id":"seq-dvt-gas-smell","title":"If you smell gas at a dryer","steps":["Light nothing; touch no switch or phone inside","Get everyone out of the building","Call the gas supplier from a neighbor's phone","If the supplier cannot be reached, call the fire department"],"src":"GE owner's manual, What to Do If You Smell Gas"}],"questions":[{"id":"dvt-0000","stem":"In a single-family house, a tech finds the dryer duct joined to the bath fan duct with a wye so both discharge through one wall cap. Which 2024 IRC requirement does this violate?","options":["A dryer exhaust system independent of all other systems","A termination 3 ft from openings into the building","Makeup air for any installation above 200 cfm","A single transition duct no more than 8 ft long"],"expl":"M1502.2 requires dryer exhaust systems to be independent of all other systems, so tying into a bath fan duct is prohibited no matter how good the shared cap is. The other three are real M1502 rules, but none of them is what this wye breaks. IRC 2024 M1502.2.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Independent exhaust","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.2","units":["code:independent"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0001","stem":"Which connection does 2024 IMC Section 504.4 expressly prohibit for a clothes dryer exhaust duct?","options":["Connection to a chimney flue or a vent connector","Connection to a UL 705 listed dryer power ventilator","Connection to a noncombustible wall receptacle","Connection to a UL 2158A listed transition duct"],"expl":"IMC 504.4 bars connecting a dryer duct to a vent connector, vent or chimney, and also bars running it into or through other ducts or plenums. A UL 705 power ventilator (504.5), a noncombustible wall receptacle (504.2) and a UL 2158A transition duct (504.9.3) are all expressly permitted. IMC 2024 504.4.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Prohibited connections","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.4","units":["code:gas-no-vent"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0002","stem":"A homeowner asks the tech to shorten her dryer run by ending it inside the vented attic just under the ridge vent. Under the 2024 IRC, where must the duct terminate?","options":["Outside the building, at a wall or roof cap","In the attic, 3 ft or more from any soffit vent","At the ridge vent, if fitted with a backdraft damper","In the attic, under the whole-house fan exception"],"expl":"M1502.3 requires dryer ducts to terminate on the outside of the building, and M1501.1 bars exhausting air into an attic, soffit, ridge vent or crawl space. The whole-house attic fan exception does not extend to dryers, and a ridge vent stays on the prohibited list even with a damper. IRC 2024 M1501.1, M1502.3.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Prohibited terminations","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1501.1; M1502.3","units":["code:outdoor-only"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0003","stem":"In a small office building under the 2024 IMC, a tech finds a dryer duct routed through the return-air plenum above a dropped ceiling on its way to an exterior wall. What is the correct finding?","options":["Not allowed; these ducts cannot extend into plenums","Allowed, with 6 inches of clearance to combustibles","Allowed, if the joints are sealed per IMC Section 603.9","Not allowed; plenum ducts need a UL 705 ventilator"],"expl":"IMC 504.4 says clothes dryer exhaust ducts shall not extend into or through ducts or plenums, so the route itself is the violation. Sealing per 603.9 is required anyway, and the 6-inch clearance to combustibles belongs to commercial dryer ducts in 504.10. IMC 2024 504.4.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Prohibited connections","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.4","units":["code:gas-no-plenum"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0004","stem":"Which clothes dryer is exempt from the independent-exhaust requirement of 2024 IRC Section M1502.2?","options":["A listed and labeled condensing (ductless) dryer","A gas dryer that is listed to ANSI Z21.5.1/CSA 7.1","An electric dryer served by a listed UL 705 ventilator","An electric dryer in a closet with a 100 sq in makeup opening"],"expl":"The only exception to M1502.2 is a listed and labeled condensing (ductless) dryer, which does not need an outdoor exhaust. A gas dryer must also vent its products of combustion, and neither a power ventilator nor a closet opening changes the independent-exhaust rule. IRC 2024 M1502.2 Exception.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Condensing dryers","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.2 Exc.","units":["code:condensing-exception"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0005","stem":"A builder provides a laundry space in a new 2024 IRC house, but the buyers will bring their own dryer after closing. What does M1502.4.8 require?","options":["Install the duct system, capped and marked 'future use'","Omit the duct, since condensing dryers need no exhaust","Omit the duct until the dryer is set, then run the duct","Install the duct with a damper and a 1/4-inch bird screen"],"expl":"Where space for a dryer is provided, M1502.4.8 requires the exhaust duct system to be installed, then capped or plugged where it originates and marked 'future use'. The only way to skip it is a listed condensing dryer actually installed before occupancy, not the possibility of one, and screens are never allowed at the termination. IRC 2024 M1502.4.8.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Exhaust duct required","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.8","units":["code:future-use","code:duct-required"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0006","stem":"Under 2024 IMC 504.9.6, when may a builder leave out the exhaust duct in a space provided for a clothes dryer?","options":["When a listed condensing unit is set before occupancy","When the owner signs a waiver for a future ductless unit","When the laundry closet has a 100 sq in makeup air opening","When a listed UL 705 DEPV is mounted in the laundry closet"],"expl":"IMC 504.9.6 requires the exhaust duct wherever a dryer space is provided, with one exception: a listed condensing dryer installed prior to occupancy. A signed waiver is the kind of deferral the code does not recognize, and makeup air or a DEPV has nothing to do with this requirement. IMC 2024 504.9.6.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Exhaust duct required","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.9.6","units":["code:duct-required-exception"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0007","stem":"The dryer's instructions give no termination location. The planned wall cap on a 2024 IRC house sits 20 inches below a ventilated soffit. What is the correct finding?","options":["Relocate; keep 3 ft in any direction from openings and soffit vents","Acceptable; soffit vents are not openings into the building","Relocate; keep 10 ft from any soffit or eave vent opening","Acceptable; being 1 ft above or below an opening suffices"],"expl":"When the manufacturer is silent, M1502.3 requires 3 ft in any direction from openings into the building, and since the 2021 edition that expressly includes openings in ventilated soffits. The 10 ft figure is the M1504.3 distance from mechanical air intakes, and the 1 ft allowance in M1504.3 applies to general exhaust located above a gravity intake. IRC 2024 M1502.3.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Termination clearance","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.3","units":["code:term-soffit","code:term-3ft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0008","stem":"Under 2024 IRC M1502.3, what is the first authority for where a dryer exhaust duct termination is located?","options":["The dryer maker's installation instructions","A 3 ft clearance from openings into the building","The M1504.3 distances for general exhaust outlets","The code official's approved construction plans"],"expl":"M1502.3 puts the dryer manufacturer's installation instructions first and applies the 3 ft clearance only when those instructions do not specify a termination location. Treating the 3 ft rule as primary is the common mistake; it is the fallback. IRC 2024 M1502.3.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Termination clearance","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.3","units":["code:term-3ft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0009","stem":"A tech finds a homeowner-added 1/4-inch bird screen behind the louvers of a dryer wall cap. The cap's damper works. Under the 2024 IRC, what should the tech recommend?","options":["Remove it; a screen is not allowed at the dryer duct termination","Keep it; 1/4 to 1/2 inch openings meet the exhaust rule","Swap it for a 1/2-inch mesh screen to cut lint loading","Keep it, and add a UL 705 fan to offset the restriction"],"expl":"M1502.3 requires a backdraft damper and prohibits screens at the dryer duct termination, because screens trap lint and choke the outlet. The 1/4 to 1/2 inch screen rule is IMC 501.3.2 for general exhaust openings and does not apply to dryers. IRC 2024 M1502.3; IMC 2024 501.3.2.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Termination screens","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.3","units":["code:no-screen"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0010","stem":"A tech replaces a crushed wall hood on a 4-inch dryer duct. What must the new hood's passageway provide under 2024 IRC M1502.3.1?","options":["Undiminished size, with at least 12.5 sq in of open area","A 1/4-inch screen, with at least 12.5 sq in of open area","Undiminished size, with at least 100 sq in of open area","Louvers and a screen, with at least 100 sq in of open area"],"expl":"M1502.3.1 requires the terminal passageway to be undiminished in size with at least 12.5 sq in of open area, which is the area of a 4-inch circle (about 12.57 sq in). 100 sq in is the closet makeup-air opening, and screens are banned outright at dryer terminations. IRC 2024 M1502.3.1.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Termination passageway","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.3.1","units":["code:passageway"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0011","stem":"In a 2024 IRC house with an electric dryer, the 4-inch smooth metal duct rises through the attic to a roof cap with a working backdraft damper and no screen. The riser has no cleanout. What is the correct finding?","options":["Compliant; the riser cleanout rule is IMC 504.3 and not M1502","Fails; each vertical riser needs a means of cleanout","Fails; dryer ducts cannot run through an attic space","Fails; a roof cap needs a 1/4-inch corrosion-proof screen"],"expl":"IRC M1502 contains no riser cleanout rule; 'each vertical riser shall be provided with a means for cleanout' is IMC 504.3, which does not govern a one-family dwelling built to the IRC. Passing through an attic is fine when the duct terminates outside, and screens are prohibited, not required. IRC 2024 M1502; IMC 2024 504.3.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Riser cleanouts","tier":1,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502; IMC 2024 §504.3","units":["code:imc-riser-cleanout"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0012","stem":"Which pair of dryer exhaust provisions first appears in the 2021 IRC and is absent from the 2018 IRC?","options":["The booster fan ban and the 3 ft rule's soffit wording","The booster fan ban and the 200 cfm makeup air rule","The UL 2158A transition rule and the 35 ft length limit","The 200 cfm makeup air rule and the soffit wording"],"expl":"Comparing the ICC texts, the 2021 IRC added M1502.4.5 (domestic booster fans prohibited) and added 'including openings in ventilated soffits' to the 3 ft rule in M1502.3. UL 2158A transition ducts and the 35 ft limit were already in the 2018 IRC, and M1502 makeup air did not arrive until 2024. IRC 2018 vs 2021 M1502.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Edition changes","tier":1,"ref":"IRC 2018/2021 M1502.3, M1502.4.5","units":["code:ed-irc-soffit","code:ed-irc-booster"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0013","stem":"Which number identifies the dryer exhaust fitting equivalent-length table in Section M1502 of the 2024 IRC?","options":["Table M1502.4.6.1","Table M1502.4.5.1","Table 504.9.4.1","Table M1505.4.3(1)"],"expl":"The 2021 and 2024 IRC number it Table M1502.4.6.1, because the booster-fan section inserted in 2021 pushed duct length to M1502.4.6. M1502.4.5.1 is the 2018 IRC number, 504.9.4.1 is the 2021/2024 IMC table (NADCA's DEDP reprints the 2012 IMC version, Table 504.6.4.1), and M1505.4.3(1) is the 2024 IRC whole-house ventilation airflow table in the same chapter, not in M1502. The fitting rows are identical in every edition of the dryer table.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Edition changes","tier":1,"ref":"IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","units":["code:ed-irc-table"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0014","stem":"What did the 2024 IMC change in the commercial clothes dryer provisions of Section 504.10?","options":["Transition ducts now need a UL 2158A listing","Transition ducts now reach 8 ft, up from 6 ft","Transition ducts are newly barred from concealment","Ducts newly need 6 inches of clearance to combustibles"],"expl":"The 2018 and 2021 IMC required commercial transition ducts to be listed and labeled 'for the application'; the 2024 IMC names UL 2158A. The 8 ft single-length limit, the concealment ban and the 6-inch clearance to combustibles were all already in the 2018 text. IMC 2021 vs 2024 504.10.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Edition changes","tier":1,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.10","units":["code:ed-imc-ul2158a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0015","stem":"An inspector enforcing the 2021 IRC asks where Section M1502 addresses makeup air for a dryer closet. What is the correct answer?","options":["Nowhere; it first appears as M1502.6.1 in 2024","In M1502.4.5, which sets a 100 sq in closet opening","In M1502.4.4, which sets a 100 sq in closet opening","In M1502.3.1, which sets a 12.5 sq in closet opening"],"expl":"The 2021 IRC M1502 ends at M1502.5 (shield plates); the 2024 IRC added M1502.6 (200 cfm makeup air) and M1502.6.1 (closet openings). In the 2021 IRC, M1502.4.5 is the booster fan ban, M1502.4.4 is power ventilators and M1502.3.1 is the 12.5 sq in terminal passageway. For gas dryers, the IRC fuel gas chapter (G2439) already required a 100 sq in closet opening, outside M1502. IRC 2021 vs 2024 M1502.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Edition changes","tier":1,"ref":"IRC 2021/2024 M1502.6","units":["code:ed-irc-makeup"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0016","stem":"What minimum metal thickness does 2024 IRC M1502.4.1 set for a dryer exhaust duct?","options":["0.0157 inch, No. 28 gage","0.0187 inch, No. 26 gage","0.0575 inch, No. 16 gage","0.013 inch, sheet steel"],"expl":"M1502.4.1 requires smooth-interior metal at least 0.0157 inch (No. 28 gage), 4 inches nominal. 0.0187 inch (No. 26 gage) is the IMC 504.11 multistory shaft duct, 0.0575 inch (No. 16 gage) is the 2024 IMC shield plate, and 0.013 inch is an IMC Table 603.4 value for small HVAC ducts. Mnemonic: 28 gage for the house duct, 26 for the shaft, 16 for the plate.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Duct material","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.1","units":["code:material"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0017","stem":"In a 12-unit apartment building under the 2024 IMC, each unit's dryer has its own duct to an exterior wall. What minimum thickness does IMC 504.9.1 require for that duct?","options":["0.016 inch (0.4 mm) metal","0.0157 inch (28 gage)","0.0187 inch (26 gage)","0.062 inch (1.6 mm) sheet steel"],"expl":"IMC 504.9.1 sets 0.016 inch (0.4 mm) for domestic dryer ducts, a hair thicker than the IRC's 0.0157 inch (No. 28 gage), so an IRC-minimum duct does not meet the IMC. 0.0187 inch is for duct inside a common multistory shaft (504.11), and 0.062 inch is a shield plate thickness. IMC 2024 504.9.1.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Duct material","tier":1,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.9.1","units":["code:gas-thickness"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0018","stem":"Which product meets 2024 IRC M1502.4.1 for the permanent dryer exhaust duct concealed in a wall?","options":["4-inch rigid metal pipe with a smooth interior","4-inch aluminum foil transition duct to UL 2158A","4-inch flexible air duct listed to UL 181","4-inch semi-rigid corrugated aluminum duct"],"expl":"M1502.4.1 requires metal with a smooth interior, 4 inches nominal, so rigid smooth pipe qualifies. A UL 2158A transition duct is limited to 8 ft and cannot be concealed (M1502.4.3), and UL 181 flex or corrugated semi-rigid duct lacks the smooth interior. IRC 2024 M1502.4.1.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Duct material","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.1","units":["code:material","code:transition-exposed"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0019","stem":"A tech finds 14 ft of 4-inch corrugated foil flex running through a joist bay from the laundry to the wall cap of a 2024 IRC house with an electric dryer. What is the correct finding?","options":["Fails; the IRC requires a metal duct with a smooth interior","Passes; at 14 ft, the run is well under the 35 ft length limit","Passes, provided the foil flex is listed and labeled to UL 2158A","Fails; flex duct runs need supports at 4-foot intervals"],"expl":"Under M1502.4.1 the permanent duct must be metal with a smooth interior, and even a UL 2158A foil duct qualifies only as a transition duct: one piece, 8 ft maximum, not concealed. The 4 ft support interval is IMC, IFGC and IRC G2439 language; M1502.4.2 allows up to 12 ft. IRC 2024 M1502.4.1, M1502.4.3.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Duct material","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.1; M1502.4.3","units":["code:material","code:transition-8ft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0020","stem":"To fit a 4-inch round dryer duct inside a 2x4 stud wall, the installer squashed it oval. Under 2024 IRC M1502.4.2, what is the finding?","options":["Fails; the cavity has to take the duct undeformed","Passes, if the oval still keeps 12.5 sq in of open area","Passes, if steel shield plates cover the stud faces","Fails; in-wall dryer ducts need 6 in of clearance to framing"],"expl":"M1502.4.2 says wall or ceiling cavities that enclose a dryer duct shall allow the duct to be installed without deformation, and a 2x4 wall (3.5 inches deep) cannot hold a true 4-inch round duct. Keeping 12.5 sq in or adding shield plates does not cure the deformation, and 6 inches of clearance is the commercial dryer rule in IMC 504.10. IRC 2024 M1502.4.2.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Concealed ducts","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.2","units":["code:cavity"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0021","stem":"A tech is reassembling a rigid 4-inch dryer duct after cleaning. How must each joint be oriented under 2024 IRC M1502.4.2?","options":["Male end inserted in the direction of airflow","Male end inserted against the direction of airflow","Either way, if joints get 181 B-FX foil tape","Either way, if screws protrude 1/8 inch or less"],"expl":"M1502.4.2 requires the insert (male) end to point in the direction of airflow so each joint laps downstream and gives lint no lip to catch on. Foil tape and the 1/8 inch screw limit are real requirements, but neither makes a backward joint acceptable. Mnemonic: the male end points to the outdoors. IRC 2024 M1502.4.2.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Joints and fasteners","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.2","units":["code:male-end"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0022","stem":"A tech finds sheet metal screws at every joint of a rigid dryer duct in a 2024 IRC house. What limit does M1502.4.2 place on those screws?","options":["They may extend no more than 1/8 inch into the interior of the duct","They may extend no more than 1/4 inch at a sealed joint","They may extend no more than 1/2 inch in No. 28 gage duct","They are banned; 181 B-FX foil tape alone may join the duct"],"expl":"M1502.4.2 allows mechanical fasteners but bars screws or similar fasteners that protrude more than 1/8 inch into the duct, because longer points snag lint. The IRC requires joints to be mechanically fastened as well as sealed, so a tape-only answer is wrong. IRC 2024 M1502.4.2.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Joints and fasteners","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.2","units":["code:fastener"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0023","stem":"Under 2024 IRC M1601.4.1, which M1502.4.2 points to for dryer duct joints, what does a '181 B-FX' marking on a closure product indicate?","options":["Pressure-sensitive closure for metal and flexible ducts","Pressure-sensitive closure for fiberglass duct board","Mechanical fastener for flexible nonmetallic air ducts","Heat-sensitive closure for fibrous glass duct board"],"expl":"M1502.4.2 sends joint sealing to M1601.4.1, which requires tapes for metallic and flexible ducts to comply with UL 181B and be marked 181 B-FX (pressure-sensitive tape) or 181 B-M (mastic). 181 A-P and 181 A-H are pressure-sensitive and heat-sensitive closures for fibrous glass, and 181 B-C marks fasteners for flexible nonmetallic duct. Mnemonic: A is for fibrous glass board, B is for metal and flex.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Joint sealing","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1601.4.1 (via M1502.4.2)","units":["code:tape-181b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0024","stem":"How does 2024 IRC M1502.4.2 require dryer exhaust duct joints to be made?","options":["Both sealed and mechanically fastened","Sealed, with fasteners prohibited","Mechanically fastened, left unsealed","Taped with 181 B-FX foil tape only"],"expl":"M1502.4.2 requires dryer duct joints to be sealed in accordance with M1601.4.1 and also mechanically fastened, with any screws protruding no more than 1/8 inch. A properly marked 181 B-FX tape covers only the sealing half; tape alone, with no mechanical fastening, fails. IRC 2024 M1502.4.2.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Joint sealing","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.2","units":["code:joint-seal"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0025","stem":"A tech notes dryer duct straps spaced 10 ft apart on a straight horizontal run. In which setting is that spacing compliant?","options":["An electric dryer in a 2024 IRC house","A gas dryer in a 2024 IRC house","A domestic dryer in a 2024 IMC apartment","A domestic gas dryer under the 2024 IFGC"],"expl":"IRC M1502.4.2 allows supports at intervals up to 12 ft, but IMC 504.9.2, IFGC 614.9.2 and IRC G2439.7.2 (gas dryers in IRC dwellings) all require 4 ft intervals. For a gas dryer in a house the IRC applies the more restrictive G2439 rule (R102.1). Mnemonic: electric under the IRC gets 12, anything gas or IMC gets 4.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Duct supports","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.2, G2439.7.2; IMC §504.9.2","units":["code:supports","code:gas-supports"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0026","stem":"A 2024 IRC run has 22 ft of straight 4-inch duct, three 4-inch-radius mitered 90-degree elbows and one 4-inch-radius mitered 45-degree elbow. What is its total equivalent length?","options":["39.5 ft, exceeding the 35 ft limit","42 ft, over the 35 ft limit","37 ft, over the 35 ft limit","28.25 ft, within the 35 ft limit"],"expl":"Table M1502.4.6.1 charges 5 ft per 4-inch-radius mitered 90 and 2 ft 6 in per mitered 45: 22 + 15 + 2.5 = 39.5 ft. 42 ft counts the 45 as a 90, 37 ft forgets the 45, and 28.25 ft uses the 6-inch smooth-radius rows. At 39.5 ft the run exceeds the 35 ft specified length, so it is allowed only where the dryer's (or a DEPV's) instructions permit it, and it needs a length label.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Fitting equivalents","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","units":["code:fit-mitered-45","code:fit-mitered-90"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0027","stem":"A 2024 IRC run will use two 4-inch-radius mitered 90-degree elbows, and the dryer manufacturer gives no length data. How much straight duct is allowed from the transition duct connection to the outlet terminal?","options":["25 ft, counting 5 ft per elbow","17 ft, counting the 8 ft transition too","31.5 ft, counting 1 ft 9 in per elbow","32 ft, counting 1 ft 6 in per elbow"],"expl":"Start with the 35 ft specified length and subtract 5 ft for each 4-inch-radius mitered 90: 35 - 10 = 25 ft. 17 ft wrongly charges an 8 ft transition duct against the limit (the IRC excludes it), and 31.5 ft and 32 ft use the 6-inch and 10-inch smooth 90 values of 1 ft 9 in and 1 ft 6 in. IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.1.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Fitting equivalents","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.1","units":["code:length-35","code:fit-mitered-90"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0028","stem":"On a 2024 IRC run, a tech replaces two 4-inch-radius mitered 90s with 6-inch-radius smooth 90-degree elbows. How much equivalent length does the swap recover?","options":["6.5 feet","8 feet","10 feet","3.25 feet"],"expl":"Each mitered 90 counts 5 ft and each 6-inch smooth 90 counts 1 ft 9 in, so each swap saves 3 ft 3 in and two elbows recover 6.5 ft. 8 ft uses the 1 ft value of a smooth 45, 10 ft treats the smooth elbows as free, and 3.25 ft is the saving for one elbow only. IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Fitting equivalents","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","units":["code:fit-mitered-90","code:fit-6-90"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0029","stem":"What equivalent length does 2024 IMC Table 504.9.4.1 assign to a 6-inch-radius smooth 90-degree elbow?","options":["1 foot 9 inches","1 foot 7 inches","1 foot","2 feet 6 inches"],"expl":"The table gives 1 ft 9 in for a 6-inch-radius smooth 90; 1 ft 7 in is the 8-inch-radius 90, 1 ft is the 6-inch-radius smooth 45, and 2-1/2 ft (2 ft 6 in) is the 4-inch-radius mitered 45. Mnemonic for smooth 90s: radius 6-8-10 steps down 9-7-6 inches over one foot. IMC 2024 Table 504.9.4.1.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Fitting equivalents","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 Table 504.9.4.1","units":["code:fit-6-90","dedp:t504-s6-90"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0030","stem":"Which fitting does 2024 IRC Table M1502.4.6.1 assign an equivalent length of just 9 inches?","options":["10-inch-radius smooth 45-degree elbow","10-inch-radius smooth 90-degree elbow","6- or 8-inch-radius smooth 45-degree elbow","4-inch-radius mitered 90-degree elbow"],"expl":"Only the 10-inch-radius smooth 45 drops below a foot, at 9 inches; the 10-inch 90 is 1 ft 6 in, the 6-inch and 8-inch smooth 45s are each 1 ft, and the 4-inch-radius mitered 90 is 5 ft. Rule of thumb: smooth 45s run about a foot or less, smooth 90s stay under 2 ft, and mitered elbows cost 2.5 and 5 ft. IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Fitting equivalents","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","units":["code:fit-10-45"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0031","stem":"NADCA's DEDP standard tells techs to count each hard elbow as 5 ft of pipe when estimating run length. Which code table row carries that 5 ft value?","options":["4-inch-radius mitered 90-degree elbow","6- or 8-inch-radius smooth 45-degree elbow","4-inch-radius mitered 45-degree elbow","10-inch-radius smooth 90-degree elbow"],"expl":"DEDP 1.8.3 and 4.1.2 count every hard elbow at 5 ft 'per IMC and IRC', which is the table value for a 4-inch-radius mitered 90. The mitered 45 is 2 ft 6 in, smooth-radius 90s are charged far less (1 ft 6 in to 1 ft 9 in), and 6- or 8-inch smooth 45s just 1 ft, so the DEDP shortcut is conservative. DEDP 2014 1.8.3; IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Fitting equivalents","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 §1.8.3; IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","units":["code:fit-mitered-90","dedp:1.8.3-5ft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0032","stem":"A DEDP estimate counts every elbow at 5 ft, but the run actually uses 8-inch-radius smooth 90-degree elbows. How does that affect the estimated equivalent length?","options":["Overstates it by 41 inches per elbow","Understates it by 41 inches for each elbow","Overstates it by 39 inches for each elbow","Understates it by 42 inches for each elbow"],"expl":"An 8-inch-radius smooth 90 is charged 1 ft 7 in (19 inches) in the code table, so counting it at 5 ft (60 inches) overstates each elbow by 41 inches. 39 and 42 inches are the gaps for 6-inch and 10-inch smooth 90s. DEDP 2014 1.8.3; IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Fitting equivalents","tier":1,"ref":"DEDP 2014 §1.8.3; IRC 2024 Table M1502.4.6.1","units":["code:fit-8-90","dedp:1.8.3-5ft"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0033","stem":"A 2024 IRC laundry has a 6 ft UL 2158A transition duct and 30 ft of straight permanent duct with no fittings. The dryer instructions give no length data. What is the finding on duct length?","options":["Compliant; the transition duct is excluded from the 35 ft","Compliant, but a length label is required at the connection","Noncompliant; the combined 36 ft exceeds the 35 ft limit","Noncompliant; a 6 ft transition duct exceeds its own limit"],"expl":"M1502.4.6.1 measures the 35 ft from the transition duct connection to the outlet terminal and states that the maximum length does not include the transition duct, so 30 ft passes. A label is needed only when equivalent length exceeds 35 ft, and a transition duct may be up to 8 ft. IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.1, M1502.4.3.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Duct length","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.1","units":["code:length-35","code:transition-8ft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0034","stem":"Which of these 2024 IRC runs needs a permanent equivalent-length label?","options":["28 ft straight plus two 4-inch-radius mitered 90s","31.5 ft straight plus two 10-inch-radius smooth 90s","32.5 ft straight plus two 10-inch-radius smooth 45s","27.5 ft straight plus three 4-inch-radius mitered 45s"],"expl":"M1502.4.7 requires the label only when equivalent length exceeds 35 ft: 28 + 5 + 5 = 38 ft does. The 10-inch smooth-elbow runs total 34.5 ft and 34 ft, and 27.5 ft plus three mitered 45s (2 ft 6 in each) lands exactly on 35 ft, which does not exceed the limit. IRC 2024 M1502.4.7, Table M1502.4.6.1.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Length label","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.7","units":["code:label-required","code:fit-mitered-90","code:fit-mitered-45"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0035","stem":"Under 2024 IMC 504.9.4.1, the 35 ft maximum exhaust duct length is measured between which two points?","options":["The transition duct joint and the outlet terminal","The dryer cabinet's exhaust outlet and the outlet terminal","The transition duct connection and the exterior wall","The dryer's exhaust outlet and the building's exterior wall"],"expl":"IMC 504.9.4.1 measures from the connection to the transition duct to the outlet terminal, so the transition duct is not counted and the outlet terminal, not the point where the duct crosses a wall, ends the run. Measuring from the dryer outlet charges the transition duct against the limit. IMC 2024 504.9.4.1.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Duct length","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.9.4.1","units":["code:length-35"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0036","stem":"A new 2024 IRC house will have a 48 ft equivalent-length dryer run concealed in walls. The chosen dryer's instructions allow 60 ft for this layout. Which statement is correct?","options":["Allowed; the official gets the instructions at concealment","Not allowed; 35 ft is a fixed cap on total equivalent length","Allowed, if a UL 705 power ventilator is also added to the run","Not allowed, unless the duct is upsized to 6-inch metal pipe"],"expl":"M1502.4.6.2 lets the dryer manufacturer's installation instructions set duct size and maximum length, and the code official must get a copy for that make and model at the concealment inspection. Because the run exceeds 35 ft it also needs the M1502.4.7 length label; a DEPV is a separate method, not a condition, and nothing requires upsizing. IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.2.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Manufacturer instructions","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.2","units":["code:length-maker","code:concealment-copy"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0037","stem":"A dryer run totaling 48 feet after fitting allowances is approved under the dryer's instructions in a 2024 IRC house. What must be posted, and where?","options":["Its equivalent length, within 6 ft of the duct connection","Its equivalent length, within 6 ft of the outlet terminal cap","Its developed length, in the DEDP test report file","The words 'future use', within 6 ft of the capped or plugged end"],"expl":"M1502.4.7 requires a permanent label or tag stating the equivalent length whenever it exceeds 35 ft, located within 6 ft of the exhaust duct connection, the point where the transition duct meets the permanent duct (DEDP definition). The label sits behind the dryer so whoever installs the next dryer can check that model's limit; 'future use' marking is the M1502.4.8 rule for a capped duct.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Length label","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.7","units":["code:label-6ft","code:label-required"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0038","stem":"Under 2024 IRC M1502.4.6.3, what sets the maximum exhaust duct length when a fan conforming to UL 705 is installed in the run?","options":["The DEPV manufacturer's installation instructions","The dryer manufacturer's installation instructions","Table M1502.4.6.1 applied against a 35 ft limit","The NADCA DEDP performance table for the run length"],"expl":"A fan conforming to UL 705 in a dryer duct is a dryer exhaust duct power ventilator (DEPV, M1502.4.4). M1502.4.6.3 makes the DEPV manufacturer's instructions the length authority when a DEPV is used, and M1502.4.4 requires the DEPV to be installed per those same instructions. The dryer's instructions and the 35 ft table method are the other two methods in M1502.4.6, and the DEDP tables are pass/fail test criteria, not a code length limit. IRC 2024 M1502.4.4, M1502.4.6.3.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Manufacturer instructions","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.6.3","units":["code:length-depv"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0039","stem":"Under 2024 IMC 504.9.4.2, the dryer manufacturer's instructions set the maximum duct length. When is Table 504.9.4.1 still used under that method?","options":["When the dryer maker does not provide equivalent lengths for fittings","When the duct will be concealed within walls or ceilings","When the finished run exceeds 35 ft of equivalent length","When a UL 705 power ventilator is installed in the run"],"expl":"IMC 504.9.4.2 says Table 504.9.4.1 shall be used when the clothes dryer manufacturer does not provide fitting equivalent-length calculations. Concealment triggers delivery of the instructions to the code official before the concealment inspection, not a switch to the table. IMC 2024 504.9.4.2.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Manufacturer instructions","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.9.4.2","units":["dedp:imc-table-default","code:length-maker-table"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0040","stem":"A dryer duct runs through a stud wall 1 inch behind the finished face of the studs in a 2024 IRC house. What protection does M1502.5 require?","options":["Steel plates at least 0.062 inch (1.6 mm) thick on the stud faces","Steel plates at least 0.0575 inch thick on the stud faces","Steel plates at least 0.0187 inch thick on the stud faces","Steel plates at least 1/8 inch (3.2 mm) thick on the stud faces"],"expl":"M1502.5 requires shield plates wherever the duct is less than 1-1/4 inches from the finished face of framing, made of steel at least 0.062 inch thick and extending at least 2 inches above sole plates and below top plates. 0.0575 inch is the 2024 IMC value (504.8.1), 0.0187 inch is shaft duct, and 1/8 inch is the M1502.4.2 screw-protrusion limit, not a plate thickness. IRC 2024 M1502.5.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Shield plates","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.5","units":["code:shield-thick","code:shield-where"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0041","stem":"In a 2024 IMC apartment building, a dryer duct passes a top plate 3/4 inch from the finished face. What shield plate does IMC 504.8.1 specify?","options":["Steel at least 0.0575 inch (No. 16 gage)","Steel at least 0.062 inch (1.6 mm)","Steel at least 0.0187 inch (No. 26 gage)","Steel at least 0.0157 inch (No. 28 gage) thick"],"expl":"The 2024 IMC moved shield plate thickness into new 504.8.1 and set it at 0.0575 inch (No. 16 gage); the 2021 IMC, the IRC and the IFGC use 0.062 inch. The trigger (less than 1-1/4 inches to the finished face) and the 2-inch extension past the plates are unchanged. IMC 2024 504.8.1.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Shield plates","tier":1,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.8.1","units":["code:imc-shield-16ga"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0042","stem":"How far must protective shield plates extend above sole plates and below top plates under 2024 IRC M1502.5?","options":["Not less than 2 inches (51 mm)","Not less than 1-1/4 inches (32 mm)","Not less than 22 inches","Not less than 6 inches"],"expl":"M1502.5 requires plates to extend not less than 2 inches above sole plates and below top plates so nails driven near the plate edges still hit steel. 1-1/4 inches is the clearance that triggers plates, 6 inches is the commercial dryer clearance to combustibles, and 22 inches is the shaft subduct length in IMC 607.5.5. IRC 2024 M1502.5.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Shield plates","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.5","units":["code:shield-extend"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0043","stem":"Behind a dryer in a 2024 IRC house, a tech finds two pieces of UL 2158A foil transition duct taped together, 9 ft in total. What does M1502.4.3 allow?","options":["A single length, 8 ft maximum","One continuous piece, 6 ft maximum","Spliced pieces allowed, 8 ft total","Spliced pieces allowed, 10 ft total"],"expl":"M1502.4.3 requires the transition duct to be a single length listed and labeled to UL 2158A, not more than 8 ft long and not concealed. Splicing two pieces fails even if the total were under 8 ft, and 6 ft is the length-label distance, not the transition limit. IRC 2024 M1502.4.3.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Transition ducts","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.3","units":["code:transition-listed","code:transition-8ft"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0044","stem":"A remodeler wants to run the UL 2158A transition duct through a wall cavity to reach the permanent duct 3 ft away. What does the 2024 IRC say?","options":["Not allowed; a transition duct cannot be concealed","Allowed; the transition stays under its 8 ft length limit","Allowed, if shield plates protect the transition in the cavity","Not allowed; flex in a wall cavity needs a UL 181 listing"],"expl":"M1502.4.3 says transition ducts shall not be concealed within construction regardless of length, so the permanent smooth metal duct must be extended into the laundry space instead. UL 181 is the listing for HVAC flexible air ducts, not a path to hiding dryer flex in a wall. IRC 2024 M1502.4.3.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Transition ducts","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.3","units":["code:transition-exposed"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0045","stem":"According to the ULSE scope statement, what does UL 2158A cover?","options":["Transition ducts for both household and commercial dryers","Power ventilators for single residential dryers","Closure tapes for flexible air ducts and connectors","Factory-made air ducts and air connectors"],"expl":"ULSE's scope for UL 2158A covers clothes dryer transition ducts for household and commercial dryers, meant to connect the dryer to the building's permanent duct. Power ventilators for single residential dryers fall under UL 705, closures for flexible ducts under UL 181B, and factory-made air ducts under UL 181. The commercial coverage matches 2024 IMC 504.10, which now requires UL 2158A for commercial transition ducts.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Transition ducts","tier":0,"ref":"UL 2158A §1.1-1.2","units":["dvt:ul2158a-scope"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0046","stem":"A 60 ft equivalent-length dryer run in a 2024 IRC house has a generic in-line duct booster fan added to help airflow. What should the tech report?","options":["Prohibited; the IRC allows a UL 705 dryer power ventilator, not a booster","Allowed; label the 60 ft equivalent length within 6 ft of the duct connection","Allowed; interlock the booster so it runs whenever the dryer runs","Prohibited; the IRC bars any powered fan from a dryer exhaust duct"],"expl":"M1502.4.5 (added in 2021) prohibits domestic booster fans in dryer exhaust systems, while M1502.4.4 permits a dryer exhaust duct power ventilator that conforms to UL 705 and is installed per its instructions. Labeling or interlocking does not make a booster legal, and the IRC does not ban every powered fan. The run could also be legal with no fan if the dryer's instructions allow 60 ft (M1502.4.6.2). IRC 2024 M1502.4.4, M1502.4.5.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"DEPVs and boosters","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.4; M1502.4.5","units":["code:booster-vs-depv","code:booster-ban"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0047","stem":"Which listing does 2024 IMC 504.5 require for a domestic dryer exhaust duct power ventilator?","options":["Listed and labeled to UL 705","Listed and labeled to UL 2158A","A UL 507 listing and label","A UL 181B listing and label"],"expl":"IMC 504.5 requires domestic DEPVs to be listed and labeled to UL 705, the power ventilator standard whose scope includes dryer exhaust duct power ventilators for single residential dryers. UL 2158A is the transition duct standard, UL 507 covers range hood fans (IRC M1503.2), and UL 181B covers flexible duct closures. IMC 2024 504.5.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"DEPVs and boosters","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.5","units":["code:depv-ul705"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0048","stem":"Manufacturer literature on UL 705 dryer exhaust duct power ventilators describes how a listed unit starts and reports trouble. Which description matches?","options":["A pressure switch starts it; an indicator alerts the occupant","It runs continuously; an alarm sounds at an approved monitored location","A wall timer starts it; the dryer's high limit then stops the drum","A manual wall switch starts it; a pilot light shows when it is on"],"expl":"Fantech's DEPV literature says a UL 705 DEPV starts automatically from an integral pressure switch that senses dryer airflow and alerts the occupant to a fan failure or blockage, typically through an indicator panel in the laundry space. Continuous running with a monitored alarm is the IMC 504.11 multistory shaft fan, not a DEPV. The code itself leaves these details to the DEPV manufacturer's instructions (IRC M1502.4.4).","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"DEPVs and boosters","tier":1,"ref":"UL 705 DEPV (mfr. literature); IRC 2024 M1502.4.4","units":["dvt:depv-pressure-switch","dvt:depv-alert-panel"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0049","stem":"Which statement about powered fans in domestic dryer exhaust matches the 2024 IMC?","options":["UL 705 DEPVs are permitted, but domestic boosters are not","UL 705 DEPVs are required on domestic dryer runs over 35 feet","Domestic booster fans are allowed if they are listed to UL 2158A","UL 705 DEPVs and domestic booster fans are both banned"],"expl":"IMC 504.5 permits domestic dryer exhaust duct power ventilators listed and labeled to UL 705, and 504.6 (new in 2021) prohibits domestic booster fans. Nothing requires a DEPV over 35 ft; the dryer manufacturer's instructions are another route to a longer run. IMC 2024 504.5, 504.6.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"DEPVs and boosters","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.5; §504.6","units":["code:booster-vs-depv","code:ed-imc-added-2021"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0050","stem":"A stacked dryer is going into a new closet in a 2024 IRC house, and its instructions say nothing about makeup air. What does M1502.6.1 require?","options":["Openings totaling 100 sq in, or other approved means","Openings totaling 12.5 sq in, or a UL 705 power ventilator","Openings totaling 144 sq in, with no other approved means","No opening, unless the dryer exhausts more than 200 cfm"],"expl":"M1502.6.1 (new in the 2024 IRC) defers to the dryer manufacturer first; if the instructions are silent, the closet needs one or more permanent openings totaling at least 100 sq in, or makeup air by other approved means. The 200 cfm trigger is the separate M1502.6 rule, and a closet dryer needs makeup air regardless of it. IRC 2024 M1502.6.1.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Makeup air","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.6.1","units":["code:closet-100"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0051","stem":"Above what exhaust rate does 2024 IMC 504.7 require a clothes dryer installation to be provided with makeup air?","options":["200 cfm, per clothes dryer installation","400 cfm, per residential unit","100 cfm, per residential unit","190 cfm, per individual dryer"],"expl":"IMC 504.7 requires makeup air for dryer installations exhausting more than 200 cfm. 400 cfm is the IRC M1503.6 kitchen hood makeup-air trigger, 190 cfm is the rating of the DEDP test fan, and 100 cfm is the IRC intermittent kitchen exhaust rate in Table M1505.5. IMC 2024 504.7.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Makeup air","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.7","units":["code:makeup-200"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0052","stem":"For a closet built to house a clothes dryer, how does 2024 IRC M1502.6.1 differ from 2024 IFGC 614.7.1?","options":["The IRC defers first to the dryer's makeup air instructions","The IFGC defers to dryer instructions before 100 square inches","The IRC sets 200 sq in of opening, twice the IFGC figure","The IFGC waives the opening where the dryer is below 200 cfm"],"expl":"2024 IRC M1502.6.1 applies the dryer manufacturer's makeup-air instructions first and uses the 100 sq in opening only when they are silent; IFGC 614.7.1 (and IRC G2439.5.1 for gas dryers) simply requires the 100 sq in opening or other approved means. Neither code ties the closet opening to the 200 cfm trigger. IRC 2024 M1502.6.1; IFGC 2024 614.7.1.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Makeup air","tier":1,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.6.1; IFGC 2024 §614.7.1","units":["code:closet-100","code:gas-closet"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0053","stem":"In a 2024 IMC apartment building, a domestic dryer duct must pass through a 1-hour fire-rated floor/ceiling assembly. What does IMC 504.2 require?","options":["Galvanized steel or aluminum duct, rating maintained","A listed fire damper, installed at the floor penetration","A UL 2158A flex duct, with the annular gap fire-caulked","A steel subduct, rising at least 22 inches above the floor"],"expl":"IMC 504.2 lets a dryer duct penetrate a fire-resistance-rated assembly only if it is galvanized steel or aluminum of the Section 603.4 thickness and the assembly's rating is maintained per the IBC. Fire dampers, combination fire/smoke dampers and any similar devices that would obstruct the exhaust flow are prohibited in dryer ducts. IMC 2024 504.2.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Fire-rated penetrations","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.2","units":["code:gas-fire-rated","code:imc-dampers"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0054","stem":"Where a dryer duct passes through a non-rated drywall membrane, how does 2024 IMC 504.2 require the annular space around it to be closed?","options":["With noncombustible material or a noncombustible dryer wall box","With 181 B-FX foil tape lapped from the duct onto the drywall face","With a No. 16 gage galvanized shield plate on the face of the stud","With expanding spray foam and a UL 2158A-listed plastic trim ring"],"expl":"IMC 504.2 requires the annular space at a wall or ceiling membrane to be sealed with noncombustible material, approved fire caulking or a noncombustible dryer exhaust duct wall receptacle (a dryer vent box). Foam and foil tape are combustible or meant for duct joints, and shield plates protect ducts from nails, not annular gaps. IMC 2024 504.2.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Wall penetrations","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.2","units":["code:imc-annular"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0055","stem":"What minimum clearance to combustible materials does 2024 IMC 504.10 require for ducts serving commercial clothes dryers?","options":["6 inches","18 inches","1-1/4 inches","3 feet"],"expl":"IMC 504.10 requires commercial dryer exhaust ducts to keep at least 6 inches of clearance to combustible materials. 18 inches is the Type I grease duct clearance (506.3.6), 1-1/4 inches is the clearance that triggers shield plates, and 3 feet is the default termination distance from building openings. IMC 2024 504.10.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Commercial dryers","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.10","units":["code:comm-6in"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0056","stem":"A laundromat governed by the 2024 IMC has six commercial dryers on one common exhaust fan. How must that fan operate under Section 504.10?","options":["Continuously, or interlocked to run when a dryer runs","Continuously, and connected to a source of standby power","Interlocked with the dryers, with its motor in the airstream","On a pressure switch, with an indicator light in the room"],"expl":"In multiple installations, IMC 504.10 requires the fan to operate continuously or be interlocked to operate whenever any individual dryer runs, with the fan motor located outside the airstream. Standby power is required for the common multistory shaft fan in 504.11, and pressure-switch starting describes a residential DEPV. IMC 2024 504.10.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Commercial dryers","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.10","units":["code:comm-fan"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0057","stem":"Which is the primary basis for installing exhaust ducts that serve commercial clothes dryers under 2024 IMC 504.10?","options":["The appliance maker's installation instructions","Table 504.9.4.1, with its 35 ft specified duct length","SMACNA construction standards at 0.0187 inch steel","The domestic duct rules in 504.9.1 through 504.9.6"],"expl":"IMC 504.10 makes the commercial dryer manufacturer's installation instructions the governing document, then adds the fan, clearance and transition duct rules. The 35 ft table and 504.9 material rules apply to domestic dryers, and 0.0187-inch SMACNA duct is the multistory shaft requirement. IMC 2024 504.10.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Commercial dryers","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.10","units":["code:comm-maker"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0058","stem":"Under the 2024 IFGC, which standard lists a Type 2 gas clothes dryer, the kind built for use in business rather than a family living environment?","options":["ANSI Z21.5.2/CSA 7.2","ANSI Z21.5.1/CSA 7.1","CSA/ANSI Z21.50/CSA 2.22","ANSI Z83.11/CSA 1.8"],"expl":"IFGC 613.1 and Chapter 8 list ANSI Z21.5.2/CSA 7.2 as Gas Clothes Dryers Volume II, Type 2; Z21.5.1/CSA 7.1 is Volume I, Type 1 (household), Z21.50/CSA 2.22 covers vented decorative gas fireplaces, and Z83.11/CSA 1.8 covers gas food service equipment. Mnemonic: the last digit of Z21.5.x matches the dryer Type. IFGC 2024 613.1, Chapter 8.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Gas dryer listings","tier":1,"ref":"IFGC 2024 §613.1; Ch. 8","units":["code:type-listing"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0059","stem":"Under 2024 IFGC 614.3, which vertical dryer exhaust risers must be provided with a cleanout or other means of cleaning?","options":["Each riser serving Type 2 (commercial) gas dryers","Risers serving Type 1 (household) gas dryers","Each riser, regardless of the dryer type served","Risers exceeding 35 ft of equivalent length"],"expl":"IFGC 614.3 limits the riser cleanout rule to dryers listed to ANSI Z21.5.2/CSA 7.2, which are Type 2 (commercial) dryers. IMC 504.3 is broader and requires a means of cleanout on each vertical riser, while IRC M1502 has no riser cleanout rule at all. IFGC 2024 614.3.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Riser cleanouts","tier":1,"ref":"IFGC 2024 §614.3","units":["code:ifgc-type2-riser","code:imc-riser-cleanout"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0060","stem":"A six-story apartment building uses a common multistory dryer exhaust duct per 2024 IMC 504.11. The engineer proposes a backdraft damper at each floor's connection. What does the code say?","options":["Dampers are prohibited in the common exhaust duct","A backdraft damper is required at each floor inlet","A listed fire damper is required at each floor inlet","Dampers are allowed if they are listed to UL 705"],"expl":"IMC 504.11 item 2 prohibits dampers in the common multistory dryer exhaust duct and protects shaft penetrations with the 607.5.5 Exception 2 steel subducts instead. Any damper would collect lint and could close off the only exhaust path for every dryer on the stack. IMC 2024 504.11.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Multistory shafts","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.11","units":["code:ms-shaft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0061","stem":"What minimum sheet steel thickness does 2024 IMC 504.11 require for the rigid duct inside a common multistory dryer exhaust shaft?","options":["0.0187 inch (No. 26 gage) steel","0.0157 inch (No. 28 gage)","0.016 inch (0.4 mm) steel","0.0575 inch (No. 16 gage) sheet steel"],"expl":"IMC 504.11 item 3 requires rigid sheet steel at least 0.0187 inch (No. 26 gage), built to SMACNA standards and installed without offsets. 0.0157 and 0.016 inch are the IRC and IMC minimums for an individual domestic dryer duct, and 0.0575 inch is the 2024 IMC shield plate. IMC 2024 504.11.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Multistory shafts","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.11","units":["code:ms-duct"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0062","stem":"Which exhaust fan arrangement does 2024 IMC 504.11 require for a common multistory clothes dryer exhaust system?","options":["Continuous, on standby power, with a failure signal","Continuous, or interlocked to run whenever a dryer runs","Pressure-switch start, with an indicator in each laundry","Continuous, with the fan motor located inside the airstream"],"expl":"IMC 504.11 requires the shaft exhaust fan to run continuously, be connected to standby power, have its motor outside the airstream, and trigger an audible or visual signal at an approved location when it stops. 'Continuous or interlocked' is the commercial dryer rule in 504.10, and a pressure switch with an indicator describes a residential DEPV. IMC 2024 504.11.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Multistory shafts","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.11","units":["code:ms-fan","code:ms-monitor"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0063","stem":"What cleanout does 2024 IMC 504.11 require at the base of a common multistory dryer exhaust shaft?","options":["A finished opening at least 12 by 12 inches","A finished opening at least 22 by 22 inches","A framed access opening at least 22 by 30 inches","A capped tee at the base of the riser"],"expl":"IMC 504.11 item 10 requires a cleanout at the base of the shaft with a finished opening of at least 12 by 12 inches for cleaning and inspection. 22 inches is the subduct length in 607.5.5, 22 by 30 inches is the attic appliance access opening (IRC M1305.1.3), and a capped tee is a common way to meet the general riser cleanout rule in 504.3, not the 504.11 shaft opening. IMC 2024 504.11.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Multistory shafts","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.11","units":["code:ms-cleanout"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0064","stem":"A tech services an electric dryer in a unit of a five-story apartment building. Which dryer exhaust provisions govern the duct?","options":["IMC Section 504","IRC Section M1502","IFGC Section 614","IRC Section G2439"],"expl":"The IRC covers only detached one- and two-family dwellings and townhouses up to three stories above grade plane (R101.2; IMC 101.2 exception), so a five-story apartment building falls under IMC Section 504. IFGC 614 and IRC G2439 are the fuel gas versions written for gas dryers. IMC 2024 101.2, 504.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Code scope","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §101.2; §504","units":["dedp:def-irc","code:g2439-numbering"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0065","stem":"A homeowner asks the tech to run the NADCA DEDP test before cleaning, to show how restricted the neglected vent is. What does the standard require?","options":["Clean it and visually confirm it is clean before running the test","Test it as found, since the test rates its installed condition","Test it as found, then clean it and repeat the test to compare results","Scan it with a camera first and test as found if nothing shows"],"expl":"The DEDP's guiding principle says the test shall be performed on ducts that have been visually inspected and confirmed clean, and Qualifications calls it the check made after service is complete. The Foreword's 'current installed condition' means as built, not as found dirty. DEDP Foreword, Qualifications, 3.0.1.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Guiding principle","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Foreword; Qualifications","units":["dedp:fwd-clean-first","dedp:1.1-confirm-clean"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0066","stem":"How does the DEDP standard treat cable-type video inspection of a dryer duct that has just been cleaned?","options":["Contractors may use it to check cleanliness, not to replace the test","Contractors shall use it as the primary check before the test","Contractors may use it in place of the test when the run is under 6 ft","Contractors shall use it after each test, whether the duct passes or fails"],"expl":"The Foreword says video is not the primary inspection procedure; companies may send cameras through to validate internal cleanliness, but visual inspection does not replace the performance criteria. Cameras are recommended after a duct fails the pressure and velocity criteria, not after every test, and 1.4 still requires testing short straight runs. DEDP Foreword.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Video inspection","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Foreword","units":["dedp:fwd-video-may","dedp:fwd-visual-no-replace"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0067","stem":"The DEDP is a residential single-dryer exhaust duct standard. Which parts of the system does its Scope leave out?","options":["The dryer appliance and the existing transition duct","The termination device and its backdraft damper","The secondary lint trap and the in-line dryer booster fan","The concealed risers and the exhaust penetrations"],"expl":"Scope says the dryer appliance and existing transition duct are not part of the standard; the test swaps in a test duct and measures the permanent 4 in duct to the termination. The termination damper is squarely in scope (1.4 even tests short straight runs to check it), and 1.6 and 1.7 cover booster fans and lint traps. DEDP Scope.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Scope exclusions","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Scope","units":["dedp:scope-excluded"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0068","stem":"To save setup time, a tech proposes running the customer's dryer on air-only and reading pressure and velocity from its own fan. How does the DEDP address this?","options":["The test shall never use the dryer blower, as fan strengths vary too widely","The test may use the dryer blower if the dryer is a newer high-efficiency unit","The test may use the dryer blower if the dryer runs empty in non-heat mode","The test shall use the dryer blower, since it rates as-used duct performance"],"expl":"3.0.2 says the DEDP test shall never be performed using the dryer blower. Application explains why: old and new high-efficiency dryer fans vary too much for one test, so NADCA built a separate device for 4 in ducts. Running the dryer empty in non-heat mode is the optional appendix measurement, which is supplemental and not the DEDP test. DEDP Application, 3.0.2.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Test blower rationale","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Application; §3.0.2","units":["dedp:3.0.2-no-dryer-blower","dedp:app-why-device"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0069","stem":"Under its Limitations section, where does the DEDP direct the user for hazards and risks the standard does not specifically address?","options":["The Authority Having Jurisdiction (AHJ) and the guidelines of the maker","The NADCA ACR Standard, which it lists in its reference documents","The CSIA C-DET reference manual, which it lists among its resources","NFPA 90B and UL 2158A, both listed among its reference documents"],"expl":"Limitations says the DEDP does not address every hazard of the work and directs the user to rely on the AHJ and the manufacturer's guidelines; it also does not assess cleanliness inside the dryer appliance. ACR, C-DET, NFPA 90B and UL 2158A appear only in the reference list. DEDP Limitations.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Limitations and hazards","tier":1,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Limitations","units":["dedp:lim-hazards"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0070","stem":"A stacked dryer sits in a tight closet, and the owner refuses to let the tech pull it out. Which DEDP pre-test requirement does that block?","options":["Access to the existing transition duct, which means moving the dryer aside","Access to the dryer's lint filter housing, to confirm it is clean","Access to the dryer's blower wheel, to compare its speed with the test device","Access to the dryer's rating plate, to confirm its exhaust duct length limit"],"expl":"1.2 lists what shall be accessible for the test: the existing transition duct (which requires moving or removing the dryer), the transition duct's connection to the dryer exhaust duct, and the termination penetration. The appliance's interior is outside the standard, and no rating-plate check is required. DEDP 1.2.1.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Pre-test accessibility","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §1.2","units":["dedp:1.2-access"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0071","stem":"During pre-test visual observations, a tech sees a crushed section of the permanent duct in the attic. What does the DEDP say about proceeding?","options":["It is recommended the test not proceed until it is remedied or reported","It is required that the test not proceed until the section is replaced","It is recommended the test proceed so the crush can be quantified first","It is required that the test proceed and the result be recorded as a fail"],"expl":"1.3.3 lists a deformed, damaged or crushed duct among the visual conditions, and 1.3.7 says it is recommended (advised, not mandatory) that the test not proceed until the condition has been remedied or reported. Replacement is not the only path, since reporting it also satisfies the clause. DEDP 1.3.3, 1.3.7.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Visual conditions","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §1.3.7","units":["dedp:1.3.7-hold","dedp:1.3-damage-checks"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0072","stem":"A dryer vents through a straight 4 ft run with no turns, and the tech can see its whole length after cleaning. Does the DEDP call for the performance test on this run?","options":["Yes, the run shall still be tested to check the termination damper","No, a short straight run is exempt once it is visually confirmed to be clean","Yes, but the test becomes optional when the whole run is visible","No, the test applies to runs with at least 10 ft of total developed length"],"expl":"1.4: short (under 6 ft), straight ducts with no turns that can be visually inspected end to end shall still get the DEDP test, to check the function and restrictive potential of the termination device damper. Seeing the whole run does not exempt it, and 10 ft is just the first table row. DEDP 1.4.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Short straight runs","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §1.4","units":["dedp:1.4-short-duct"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0073","stem":"Pre-test inspection shows a run that leaves the laundry wall as 4 in pipe and steps up to 5 in pipe in the attic. How does the DEDP handle it?","options":["Not tested; the 4 in size must stay contiguous to the termination cap","Tested; the 5 in section counts as 5 ft for each transition fitting","Tested; the roof table is used to offset the larger-diameter section","Tested; the wall port is the one point that must measure a full 4 in"],"expl":"1.5: the DEDP test shall only be performed on 4 in ducts, and the diameter must be contiguous from the dryer transition point to the exterior termination. The device and tables were built on the fact that virtually all residential vents are 4 in; the 5 ft figure is the hard-elbow equivalent, not a size-change allowance. DEDP 1.5, Application.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Contiguous 4 in diameter","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §1.5","units":["dedp:1.5-contiguous"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0074","stem":"A run has an inline booster fan and a secondary lint trap. What shall be determined about each before the DEDP test?","options":["Fan in place and operational; lint trap present and clean","Fan in place and operational; lint trap removed and set aside","Fan disconnected and bypassed; lint trap present and clean","Fan disconnected and bypassed; lint trap removed and set aside"],"expl":"1.6 says it shall be determined whether a booster fan is present and operational, and 1.7 whether a secondary lint trap is present and clean, before testing. Neither is removed or bypassed; Step 6 even allows time for the booster fan's pressure switch to engage. DEDP 1.6, 1.7.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Booster fan, lint trap","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §1.6-1.7","units":["dedp:1.6-booster","dedp:1.7-secondary-trap"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0075","stem":"The pre-test inspection produces an estimate of developed length. How does the DEDP say the actual length is established?","options":["During cleaning, with measured rods, cable drives, or air whip tools","During the test, by matching the gauge reading to a table's pressure row","Before cleaning, from the dryer maker's installation instructions","After the test, from a video scope pass that logs footage on the run"],"expl":"1.8: estimate total developed length during pre-inspection, then the actual length shall be determined during cleaning with measured fiberglass cleaning rods, cable drives, or air whipping tools. The dryer maker's instructions set the maximum allowed length (IMC 504.6.4.2, reprinted in the DEDP), not the measured length. DEDP 1.8.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Developed length","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §1.8","units":["dedp:1.8-actual"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0076","stem":"Pre-test inspection finds the dryer connected with a transition duct that the local code does not allow. What does the DEDP require?","options":["The consumer must be told what type of transition duct is not compliant","The tech must install a compliant transition duct before the test starts","The tech need not check it, since transition ducts are outside DEDP scope","The consumer must sign a waiver before the old duct is reconnected"],"expl":"1.9: verify the transition duct complies with local, state and federal code or the AHJ, and the consumer must be informed what type of transition duct is not in compliance. Scope excludes the existing transition duct from the performance measurement, not from this code check, and 4.4 treats a new section as offered, not required. DEDP 1.9.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Transition code check","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §1.9","units":["dedp:1.9-inform","dedp:1.9-code-check"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0077","stem":"Section 2.1 specifies the fan inside the DEDP testing device. Which specification set matches it?","options":["113-watt motor, 2,910 RPM, true rated 190 CFM","190-watt motor, 2,910 RPM, true rated 113 CFM","1,130-watt motor, 1,900 RPM, true rated 291 CFM","291-watt motor, 1,130 RPM, true rated 90 CFM"],"expl":"2.1: the test fan has a 113 W sealed-bearing motor built for continuous use, 4 in inlet and outlet, 2,910 RPM, and a true rated air volume of 190 CFM. Mnemonic: small watts (113), big revs (2,910), under 200 CFM (190). DEDP 2.1.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Test blower specs","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §2.1","units":["dedp:2.1-motor","dedp:2.1-rpm","dedp:2.1-cfm"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0078","stem":"A tech has a digital manometer but no analog differential pressure gauge. Can he use it for the DEDP test?","options":["Yes, digital manometers can stand in if they read the 0 to 2.0 in wc span","No, the gauge must be an analog unit that sits upright for true readings","Yes, digital manometers can stand in if they read the 0 to 1.00 in wc span","No, digital manometers are reserved for the optional appliance reading"],"expl":"2.2 requires a gauge capable of reading 0 to 2.0 in wc, and 2.2.1 says digital manometers can be used instead of the analog gauge. The upright rule (3.1.3.1) applies to non-digital gauges, and 1.00 in wc is the roof table's top pressure limit, not the instrument range. DEDP 2.2, 2.2.1.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Pressure gauge","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §2.2","units":["dedp:2.2.1-digital","dedp:2.2-gauge"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0079","stem":"What tubing does the DEDP specify to connect the pressure tap fitting to the gauge's pressure port?","options":["Clear plastic tubing, 1/8 in inside and 1/4 in outside diameter","Clear tubing, 3/16 in inside and 5/16 in outside diameter","Vinyl tubing, 1/16 in inside and 3/16 in outside diameter","Rubber tubing, 5/16 in inside and 7/16 in outside diameter"],"expl":"2.4: the connection tubing is 1/8 in ID and 1/4 in OD, running from the gauge's pressure port to the tap on the test duct, and Step 3 (3.1.3) calls it clear plastic tubing. Mnemonic: the inside is half the outside, 1/8 inside 1/4. DEDP 2.4, 3.1.3.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Tubing specs","tier":1,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §2.4","units":["dedp:2.4-tubing"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0080","stem":"Which rotating vane anemometer meets the DEDP's equipment requirement?","options":["A meter reading 50 to 2000 fpm that reports in feet per minute","A meter reading 50 to 2000 fpm that reports in cubic feet per minute","A meter reading 190 to 1700 fpm that reports in feet per minute","A meter reading 190 to 1700 fpm that reports in cubic feet per minute"],"expl":"2.6: the anemometer needs a minimum range of 50 to 2000 fpm and shall report in feet per minute, because the tables are written in fpm. 190 is the test blower's CFM rating and 1700 fpm is the tables' highest minimum, so a 190 to 1700 meter could not read a strong 10 ft run. DEDP 2.6.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Anemometer specs","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §2.6","units":["dedp:2.6-anemometer","dedp:2.6-fpm"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0081","stem":"Which description matches the DEDP test duct that temporarily replaces the transition duct?","options":["4 to 6 ft of semi-rigid duct, with a continuous 4 in ID compressed or stretched","4 to 6 ft of UL 2158A foil duct, a nominal 4 in ID when it is fully stretched","8 to 10 ft of rigid metal pipe with a smooth 4 in ID, plus two hard 90 degree elbows","2 to 4 ft of semi-rigid duct, with a continuous 4 in ID compressed or stretched"],"expl":"Definitions and 2.5: the test duct is 4 to 6 ft of semi-rigid duct with a continuous 4 in interior diameter whether compressed or stretched, compliant with the AHJ, and 2.5.2 says it should be no longer than needed, with minimal bends. Mnemonic: 4 in wide, 4 to 6 ft long. DEDP 2.5, Definitions.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Test duct","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §2.5; Definitions","units":["dedp:def-test-duct","dedp:2.5-test-duct"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0082","stem":"A tech is building the test rig with a 6 ft test duct. Where should the pressure tap fitting be installed?","options":["At the 3 ft mark, on the top center or the outside of a bend","At the 2 ft mark, on the top center or an outside bend","At the 3 ft mark, on the bottom center or the inside of a bend","Within 6 in of the blower outlet, on the top center of the duct"],"expl":"3.1.2 (Step 2): the tap goes halfway along the test duct, at the top or on an outside bend; the standard's example puts it at the 2 ft mark of a 4 ft duct, so a 6 ft duct takes it at 3 ft. The 2 ft figure only fits the 4 ft example. DEDP 3.1.2.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Step 2 tap placement","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §3.1.2","units":["dedp:3.1.2-tap-spot"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0083","stem":"In Step 3, how does the DEDP say an analog (non-digital) differential pressure gauge is to be set up?","options":["Tubing on the positive input, gauge vertical and upright","Tubing on the negative input, gauge sitting vertical and upright","Tubing on the positive input, gauge lying flat with dial facing up","Tubing on the negative input, gauge lying flat with dial facing up"],"expl":"3.1.3: one end of the clear tubing goes on the tap and the other on the gauge's positive pressure input, since the test blower pressurizes the duct; 3.1.3.1 says a non-digital gauge shall sit vertical and upright. Resting on a flat surface still means standing upright, not lying on its back. DEDP 3.1.3.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Step 3 gauge setup","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §3.1.3","units":["dedp:3.1.3-step3","dedp:3.1.3.1-vertical"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0084","stem":"Why does Step 3 call for the gauge tubing to be at least 4 to 6 ft long?","options":["So the gauge can rest on a flat surface while the test runs","So the tap can sit at the midpoint of a 4 to 6 ft test duct","So the pressure can settle before the one-minute run is timed","So one tech can read the gauge and hold the anemometer"],"expl":"3.1.3.2: the tubing is at least 4 to 6 ft long so the gauge can rest on any flat surface during the test. The same 4 to 6 ft figure describes the test duct, a different part, and the midpoint rule belongs to the tap. DEDP 3.1.3.2.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Step 3 tubing length","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §3.1.3.2","units":["dedp:3.1.3.2-tubing-length"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0085","stem":"In Step 6 on a run with no booster fan, what minimum run time does the DEDP specify for the testing device, and when is the pressure read?","options":["Run it at least one minute and read once the pressure stabilizes","Run it at least 30 seconds and read the gauge once the pressure stabilizes","Run it at least five minutes and record the peak pressure the gauge shows","Run it at least one minute and record the peak pressure the gauge shows"],"expl":"3.1.6 says let the device run at least one minute, and 3.1.7 says record the gauge after the pressure stabilizes. Recording the highest value is the Step 8 rule for velocity, and 30 seconds is the low end of the booster fan switch delay. DEDP 3.1.6, 3.1.7.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Step 6 run time","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §3.1.6-3.1.7","units":["dedp:3.1.6-step6","dedp:3.1.7-step7"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0086","stem":"The run has an inline booster fan with a pressure switch. After the testing device is energized, how long does the DEDP say that switch typically takes to engage?","options":["Typically 30 to 60 seconds after the test device starts","Typically five to ten seconds after the test device starts","Typically two to three minutes after the test device starts","Typically 90 seconds to two minutes after the test device starts"],"expl":"3.1.6.1: with an inline booster fan, allow adequate time for its pressure switch to engage, typically 30 seconds to one minute after energizing the test blower. 1.6 already required confirming the fan is present and operational. DEDP 3.1.6.1.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Booster fan delay","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §3.1.6.1","units":["dedp:3.1.6.1-booster"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0087","stem":"In Step 8 at the exterior termination, how does the DEDP direct the tech to take and record velocity readings?","options":["At least three should be taken in varied positions; record the maximum","At least three shall be taken at the cap center; record their average","At least five should be taken across a traverse grid; record their average","At least three should be taken in varied positions; record the lowest"],"expl":"3.2.1.1: a minimum of three readings should be taken at various positions (for example top, bottom and side of the termination), and the maximum is recorded; 4.3 repeats that the highest reading is documented. Note the obligation: the three readings are a should, while taking readings from the discharged air in fpm is a shall (3.2.1). DEDP 3.2.1.1, 4.3.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Step 8 velocity readings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §3.2.1.1","units":["dedp:3.2.1.1-three","dedp:4.3-max-velocity"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0088","stem":"A tech has just secured the test duct to the 4 in wall port with worm-drive clamps (Step 5). What is the next step in the DEDP procedure?","options":["Energize the testing device and let it run at least one minute","Install the pressure tap fitting halfway along the test duct","Take three anemometer readings at the exterior termination","Connect the testing device blower to the free end of the test duct"],"expl":"Order: 1 disconnect dryer, 2 tap in test duct, 3 hook up gauge, 4 blower onto test duct, 5 clamp test duct to wall port, 6 energize and run at least one minute, 7 record pressure, 8 record velocity, 9 compare to tables. Mnemonic: build the rig (2 to 4), mount it (5), run it (6), read it (7, 8), judge it (9). DEDP 3.1.1 to 3.2.2.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Procedure sequence","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §3.1.5-3.1.6","units":["dedp:3.1.6-step6","dedp:3.1.5-step5"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0089","stem":"After the DEDP test, the dryer is reconnected. What does Section 4 have the tech record about the transition duct?","options":["The type used to reconnect the dryer, whether existing or newly offered","The type used to reconnect, recorded solely if a new one is sold","Its measured length, added to the permanent duct's developed length","Its UL 2158A listing label, confirmed before it is allowed to be reinstalled"],"expl":"4.4: whether the customer's existing transition duct or a newly offered section is used, record the type used to reconnect the dryer. Developed length covers the permanent exhaust duct only, so the transition's length is not added. DEDP 4.4, 1.8.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Transition duct record","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §4.4","units":["dedp:4.4-transition"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0090","stem":"A side wall termination serves 10 ft of rigid pipe with two hard elbows. After cleaning, the DEDP test reads 0.50 in wc and a maximum of 1650 fpm. Which result and reason are correct?","options":["Pass; both readings meet the row for 20 ft of developed length","Fail; 0.50 in wc tops the 10 ft row's 0.30 limit","Fail; 1650 fpm misses the 10 ft row's 1700 minimum","Fail; 0.50 in wc falls below the 0.55 in wc minimum"],"expl":"Developed length is 10 + 2 x 5 = 20 ft (1.8.3), and Table 4.9 side wall at 20 ft needs back pressure under 0.55 in wc and at least 1600 fpm, so it passes. Forgetting the elbows lands on the 10 ft row, and back pressure is a ceiling, not a floor. DEDP 1.8.3, 4.9.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Performance tables","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Table 4.9","units":["dedp:tbl-sw20","dedp:tbl-v20","dedp:len-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0091","stem":"A roof termination serves 20 ft of rigid pipe with two hard elbows. The cleaned duct tests at 0.80 in wc and a maximum of 1520 fpm. Which result and reason are correct?","options":["Pass; 0.80 is under 0.85 in wc and 1520 meets 1500 fpm","Fail; 0.80 in wc is over the 0.65 in wc pressure limit","Fail; 0.80 in wc is over the 0.75 in wc roof pressure limit","Fail; 1520 fpm is over the 1500 fpm velocity maximum"],"expl":"Developed length is 20 + 2 x 5 = 30 ft, and Table 4.10 roof at 30 ft allows under 0.85 in wc with at least 1500 fpm, so it passes. 0.65 is the side wall value for 30 ft, 0.75 is the roof 20 ft row (elbows ignored), and velocity is a minimum. DEDP 4.10.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Performance tables","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Table 4.10","units":["dedp:tbl-rf30","dedp:tbl-v30","dedp:len-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0092","stem":"A side wall run has a developed length of 40 ft. After cleaning, it tests at 0.70 in wc and a maximum of 1250 fpm. Which result and reason are correct?","options":["Fail; 1250 fpm is below the 40 ft row's 1300 fpm minimum","Pass; 1250 fpm clears the 50 ft row's 1200 minimum","Fail; 0.70 in wc is over the 40 ft row's 0.65 limit","Pass; both readings meet the 40 ft side wall row"],"expl":"Table 4.9 side wall at 40 ft: under 0.75 in wc and at least 1300 fpm. 0.70 passes pressure, but 1250 fpm misses velocity, and failing either one fails the test (3.2.2.1, 4.7). 1200 fpm belongs to the 50 ft and over row, 0.65 to the 30 ft row. DEDP 4.9.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Performance tables","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Table 4.9","units":["dedp:tbl-v40","dedp:tbl-sw40"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0093","stem":"A roof termination serves 35 ft of rigid pipe with three hard elbows. The cleaned duct tests at 0.92 in wc and a maximum of 1180 fpm. Which result and reason are correct?","options":["Fail; 1180 fpm is below the 1200 fpm minimum","Pass; 0.92 in wc is under the 1.00 in wc limit","Fail; 0.92 in wc is over the 0.80 in wc limit","Pass; 1180 fpm is under the 1200 fpm limit"],"expl":"Developed length is 35 + 3 x 5 = 50 ft, so Table 4.10 roof 50 ft and over applies: under 1.00 in wc and at least 1200 fpm. Pressure passes but velocity fails, so the test fails. 0.8 in wc is the side wall 50 ft limit, and velocity is a floor, not a ceiling. DEDP 4.10.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Performance tables","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Table 4.10","units":["dedp:tbl-v50","dedp:tbl-rf50","dedp:len-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0094","stem":"A side wall run is 5 ft of rigid pipe with one hard elbow. The cleaned duct tests at 0.35 in wc and a maximum of 1750 fpm. Which result and reason are correct?","options":["Fail; 0.35 in wc is over the 0.30 in wc limit","Pass; 0.35 in wc is under the 0.50 in wc limit","Pass; 0.35 in wc is under the 0.55 in wc limit","Fail; 1750 fpm is over the 1700 fpm maximum"],"expl":"Developed length is 5 + 5 = 10 ft, and Table 4.9 side wall at 10 ft allows under 0.30 in wc, so 0.35 fails even though 1750 fpm clears 1700. 0.50 is the roof 10 ft limit and 0.55 the side wall 20 ft limit. DEDP 4.9.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Performance tables","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Table 4.9","units":["dedp:tbl-sw10","dedp:len-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0095","stem":"A side wall run has 10 ft of rigid pipe and four hard elbows. The cleaned duct tests at 0.60 in wc and a maximum of 1540 fpm. Which result and reason are correct?","options":["Pass; 0.60 is under 0.65 in wc and 1540 meets 1500","Fail; 0.60 in wc is over the 20 ft row's 0.55 limit","Fail; 1540 fpm is below the 20 ft row's 1600 fpm minimum","Fail; 0.60 in wc is below the 0.65 in wc minimum"],"expl":"Developed length is 10 + 4 x 5 = 30 ft, and Table 4.9 side wall at 30 ft allows under 0.65 in wc with at least 1500 fpm, so it passes. Counting elbows at 2.5 ft (the mitered 45 degree figure) lands on the 20 ft row, and back pressure is a ceiling. DEDP 1.8.3, 4.9.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Performance tables","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Table 4.9","units":["dedp:tbl-sw30","dedp:tbl-v30","dedp:len-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0096","stem":"A cleaned run with a 30 ft developed length tests at 0.70 in wc and a maximum of 1550 fpm, but the tech never documented where it terminates. Which statement is correct?","options":["It passes as a roof termination but fails as a side wall","It passes as a side wall termination but fails as a roof","It fails either way, as 1550 fpm tops the 1500 maximum","It passes either way, since both tables share one pressure limit"],"expl":"At 30 ft, Table 4.10 roof allows under 0.85 in wc (pass) while Table 4.9 side wall allows under 0.65 (fail); both need at least 1500 fpm. The tables share velocity minimums, not pressure limits, which is why 1.8.4 has the tech document a side, roof or soffit termination. DEDP 1.8.4, 4.6.1.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Performance tables","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Tables 4.9-4.10; §1.8.4","units":["dedp:tbl-rf30","dedp:tbl-sw30","dedp:tbl-v30"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0097","stem":"A roof termination serves 15 ft of rigid pipe with one hard elbow. The cleaned duct tests at 0.60 in wc and a maximum of 1580 fpm. Which result and reason are correct?","options":["Fail; 1580 fpm is short of the 20 ft row's 1600 minimum","Pass; roof runs carry a looser 1500 fpm minimum","Pass; both readings meet the 20 ft roof top row","Fail; 0.60 in wc is over the 0.55 in wc limit"],"expl":"Developed length is 15 + 5 = 20 ft, and Table 4.10 roof at 20 ft needs under 0.75 in wc and at least 1600 fpm; 1580 fpm fails. Roof and side wall tables use the same velocity minimums, and 0.55 is the side wall pressure limit. DEDP 4.10.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Performance tables","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Table 4.10","units":["dedp:tbl-v20","dedp:tbl-rf20","dedp:len-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0098","stem":"A side wall run has 40 ft of rigid pipe and four hard elbows. The cleaned duct tests at 0.78 in wc and a maximum of 1220 fpm. Which result and reason are correct?","options":["Pass; 0.78 is under 0.80 in wc and 1220 meets 1200 fpm","Fail; 0.78 in wc is over the 40 ft row's 0.75 limit","Fail; 1220 fpm is below the 40 ft row's 1300 fpm minimum","Fail; 1220 fpm is over the 1200 fpm velocity maximum"],"expl":"Developed length is 40 + 4 x 5 = 60 ft, so Table 4.9 side wall 50 ft and over applies: under 0.8 in wc and at least 1200 fpm, a pass. Ignoring the elbows puts the run on the 40 ft row. A run this long may still exceed the dryer maker's limit, an appendix condition. DEDP 4.9, Appendix.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Performance tables","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Table 4.9","units":["dedp:tbl-sw50","dedp:tbl-v50","dedp:len-example"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0099","stem":"A roof termination serves a straight 10 ft rigid run with no elbows. The cleaned duct tests at 0.55 in wc and a maximum of 1760 fpm. Which result and reason are correct?","options":["Fail; 0.55 in wc is over the 0.50 in wc limit","Pass; 0.55 in wc is under the 0.75 in wc limit","Pass; both readings meet the 10 ft roof top row","Fail; 1760 fpm is over the 1700 fpm maximum"],"expl":"Table 4.10 roof at 10 ft allows under 0.50 in wc with at least 1700 fpm, so 0.55 fails on pressure even though velocity is strong. 0.75 is the roof 20 ft limit, and velocity is a minimum, never a cap. DEDP 4.10.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Performance tables","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Table 4.10","units":["dedp:tbl-rf10"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0100","stem":"A roof termination serves 20 ft of rigid pipe with four hard elbows. The cleaned duct tests at 0.88 in wc and a maximum of 1340 fpm. Which result and reason are correct?","options":["Pass; both readings meet the 40 ft roof top row","Fail; 0.88 in wc is over the 0.75 in wc limit","Fail; 1340 fpm is below the 1500 fpm minimum","Fail; 0.88 in wc is below the 0.95 in wc minimum"],"expl":"Developed length is 20 + 4 x 5 = 40 ft, and Table 4.10 roof at 40 ft allows under 0.95 in wc with at least 1300 fpm, a pass. 0.75 is both the side wall 40 ft limit and the roof 20 ft limit (elbows ignored); 1500 fpm is the 30 ft row. DEDP 4.10.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Performance tables","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Table 4.10","units":["dedp:tbl-rf40","dedp:tbl-v40","dedp:len-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0101","stem":"A roof termination with a 20 ft developed length tests at 0.70 in wc. Anemometer readings at the cap are 1560 fpm top, 1620 fpm side, and 1540 fpm bottom. Which result and reason are correct?","options":["Pass; the 1620 fpm maximum reading meets the 1600 minimum","Fail; the 1573 fpm average is short of the 1600 minimum","Fail; the 1540 fpm low reading misses the 1600 minimum","Fail; 0.70 in wc is over the 0.55 in wc pressure limit"],"expl":"The maximum velocity is recorded (3.2.1.1, 4.3), so 1620 fpm meets Table 4.10's 1600 fpm roof minimum at 20 ft, and 0.70 is under the 0.75 in wc limit: pass. Averaging or taking the low reading is not the rule, and 0.55 is the side wall limit. DEDP 4.3, 4.10.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Performance tables","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §4.3; Table 4.10","units":["dedp:4.3-max-velocity","dedp:tbl-v20","dedp:tbl-rf20"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0102","stem":"A cleaned duct fails the DEDP, and the tech suspects it is still blocked or crushed somewhere after servicing. What does the appendix recommend?","options":["Run a video scope or take the duct apart to identify the cause","Rerun the test with the dryer's own blower to confirm the failure","Switch to the roof table, since it allows more back pressure for each row","Record a pass once the duct is visually clean from both ends"],"expl":"The appendix lists a duct still blocked or crushed after servicing and recommends a video scope or physical dismantling to identify the cause; the Foreword likewise recommends cameras after a fail. The list does not oblige the contractor to perform those services, and the roof table is used for roof terminations, not as a fallback. DEDP Appendix.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"After a fail","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Appendix","units":["dedp:app-still-blocked"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0103","stem":"A tech also wants to measure the dryer's own discharge velocity at the termination. How does the DEDP appendix treat that measurement?","options":["Optional extra data, taken with the dryer empty in the non-heat mode","A required second test, taken with the dryer empty in the non-heat mode","Optional extra data, taken with a full load on the high-heat cycle","A required second test, taken with a full load on the high-heat cycle"],"expl":"Appendix, Appliance Exhaust Performance: velocity may be measured with the dryer in its final installed position, running empty in non-heat mode, to spot a significant pressure differential between appliance and test device; it is supplemental and not a requirement. A significant differential means the dryer should be evaluated by a qualified service provider. DEDP Appendix.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Appliance exhaust check","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Appendix","units":["dedp:app-appliance-optional","dedp:app-appliance-may"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0104","stem":"Under the DEDP definitions, what makes a deposit in the duct an adhered substance rather than debris?","options":["It cannot be removed by direct contact with a silicon carbide bristle brush","It cannot be removed by a compressed-air whip at full tank pressure","It cannot be removed without dismantling the duct at each of its joints","It cannot be seen without a cable video camera passed through the run"],"expl":"Definitions: an adhered substance is material not removable by direct contact with a brushing device using silicon carbide bristle; debris is non-adhered material. 4.5 then has the tech record what adhered substance was found. An air whip failing is not the test, because the definition names the brush. DEDP Definitions, 4.5.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Adhered substance","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Definitions","units":["dedp:def-adhered"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0105","stem":"A run has 22 ft of measured pipe and two hard elbows, and the tech records 32 ft. In DEDP terms, what are the 22 ft and the 32 ft?","options":["22 ft is the actual length; 32 ft is the developed length","22 ft is the developed length; 32 ft is the equivalent length","Both are actual lengths, before and after the elbows","22 ft is the equivalent length; 32 ft is the developed length"],"expl":"Definitions: actual length is the measured duct; equivalent length expresses a fitting's resistance as feet of straight duct (5 ft per hard elbow per 1.8.3); developed length is actual plus all fitting equivalents, so 22 + 2 x 5 = 32 ft. DEDP Definitions, 1.8.3.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Length definitions","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Definitions","units":["dedp:def-actual","dedp:def-developed"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0106","stem":"The DEDP defines two classes of flexible transition duct. Which limits define a Class 1 transition duct?","options":["Flame spread index up to 25; smoke developed index up to 50","Flame spread index of zero; smoke developed index of zero","Flame spread index up to 50; smoke developed index up to 25","Flame spread index up to 50; smoke developed index up to 100"],"expl":"Definitions: Class 0 transition ducts have surface burning characteristics of zero; Class 1 have a flame-spread index not over 25, no continued progressive combustion, and a smoke-developed index not over 50. Mnemonic: Class 1 is 25/50, Class 0 is 0/0. DEDP Definitions.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Transition duct classes","tier":1,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Definitions","units":["dedp:def-class1","dedp:def-class0"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0107","stem":"Both the termination device and the termination device damper resist outside backdrafts. What else sets each apart in the DEDP definitions?","options":["The device also bars nesting birds and wildlife; the damper also bars weather","The device also bars weather; the damper also bars nesting wildlife","The device mounts indoors at the dryer's wall port; the damper mounts outdoors","The device is the bird screen by itself; the damper is the whole cap"],"expl":"Definitions: the termination device prevents backdrafts and keeps birds and wildlife from nesting, and should offer minimal resistance and little maintenance; the damper, on the last exhaust penetration, keeps backdrafts and weather elements out. The termination sits outside the home. DEDP Definitions.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Termination definitions","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Definitions","units":["dedp:def-termination","dedp:def-damper"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0108","stem":"Besides catching lint that would load the duct and any booster fan, what role does the DEDP definition give a secondary lint trap?","options":["A required cleanout for duct systems with vertical risers","A required cleanout for any run longer than the 50 ft table row","A recommended cleanout for runs that have more than four hard elbows","A recommended stand-in for the dryer's own internal lint filter"],"expl":"Definitions: a secondary lint trap catches most lint headed into the duct, protecting the duct and any booster fan, and also acts as a required cleanout for systems with vertical risers. 1.7 has the tech confirm it is present and clean before testing. DEDP Definitions, 1.7.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Secondary lint trap","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Definitions","units":["dedp:def-secondary-trap"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0109","stem":"Under the DEDP definitions, what is the difference between a shall clause and a recommended practice?","options":["Shall is mandatory with no deviation; recommended is advised but optional","Shall is mandatory unless the AHJ waives it; recommended is required by code","Shall covers new construction; recommended covers existing duct systems","Shall is advised for best results; recommended is mandatory when practical"],"expl":"Definitions: shall denotes a mandatory requirement, and conformance allows no deviation; recommended means advised or suggested but not a requirement of the standard. Example: 1.3.7 only recommends holding the test when a visual condition is found, while 3.0.2's ban on the dryer blower is a shall. DEDP Definitions.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Shall vs recommended","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Definitions","units":["dedp:def-shall","dedp:def-recommended"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0110","stem":"A new tech is explaining to a customer how air moves through a standard vented dryer. After the air enters the cabinet, in what order does it pass the main components?","options":["Heat source, drum, lint filter, blower, exhaust duct","Lint filter, heat source, drum, fan, exhaust duct","Heater, blower, drum, lint filter, exhaust duct","Drum, heat source, fan, lint filter, exhaust duct"],"expl":"NADCA's DEDP General Introduction traces it: ambient air is heated, passes through the drum where it picks up moisture and lint, then the lint filter, then the internal fan, and only then leaves through the exhaust duct. The trap is putting the blower ahead of the drum; it pulls air through the drum and pushes it into the duct. Mnemonic: Heat, Tumble, Filter, Fan, Out.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Airflow path","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014, General Introduction","units":["dvt:airflow-path"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0111","stem":"A tech explains why a leaky joint in an attic dryer run blows lint out instead of drawing attic air in. Which feature of the dryer explains this?","options":["The blower sits after the drum and pushes air into the duct","The blower sits ahead of the heater and pulls air from the duct","The drum rotation creates suction that draws on the duct","The hood damper traps pressure that backs up into the run"],"expl":"The blower is downstream of the drum and lint filter, so the transition and permanent duct after it run under positive pressure. Leaks therefore push lint, moisture and, on gas units, combustion products out of the duct. Listed duct power ventilators rely on the same fact: their switch senses positive duct pressure when the dryer starts.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Airflow path","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 Gen. Intro; CPSC 1999 dryer report","units":["dvt:blower-positive-pressure"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0112","stem":"A customer with a gas dryer asks why her vent matters more than her neighbor's, whose dryer is electric. What is the best answer?","options":["The burner exhaust, carbon monoxide included, leaves the house by that duct","The burner draws its combustion air back in through that duct","Gas units run hotter, so that duct needs 6-inch galvanized pipe","Gas units need 240 volts, so a blocked duct overheats the wiring"],"expl":"The IMC requires dryer exhaust systems to convey moisture and any products of combustion outdoors, so a gas dryer's duct is also its flue; a blockage or disconnect can put carbon monoxide in the house. The 240-volt trap belongs to the electric dryer's heating element; a gas dryer's motor and controls run on 120 volts, and every residential dryer duct is 4-inch nominal.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Gas vs electric","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.1; CPSC 1999 dryer report","units":["dvt:electric-240v","dvt:gas-exhaust-co"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0113","stem":"On a typical electric dryer, which part is powered at 240 volts while the rest of the appliance runs at 120 volts?","options":["The heating element inside the heater housing","The drive motor that turns drum and blower","The timer or electronic control board","The door switch and drum light circuit"],"expl":"CPSC staff describe the electric dryer's heating element as the 240-volt load; the motor that turns the drum and moves the air, and the timer, run at 120 volts, the same as in a gas dryer. That is why a gas dryer plugs into an ordinary 120-volt outlet while an electric model needs a 120/240-volt, 30-amp circuit.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Gas vs electric","tier":0,"ref":"CPSC 1999 dryer report; Whirlpool install instructions","units":["dvt:electric-240v"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0114","stem":"A condo owner wants a ventless dryer in a closet with no outside wall and asks whether a gas model will work. What is accurate?","options":["Gas dryers are always vented; ventless models are electric","Gas condensing units drain their combustion water to a floor pan","Gas heat-pump units recycle burner exhaust through the drum","Gas units go ventless only when a CO alarm is installed"],"expl":"ENERGY STAR's dryer scoping report states that, because of the safety risks of combustion, gas dryers are always vented while electric dryers can be vented or ventless. The IRC's independent-exhaust rule exempts only listed and labeled condensing (ductless) dryers. The CO-alarm option is the trap: an alarm warns, it does not replace venting.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Ventless dryers","tier":0,"ref":"ENERGY STAR Dryer Scoping Report; IRC 2024 §M1502.2 exc.","units":["dvt:gas-always-vented"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0115","stem":"A tech is booked to clean the vent on a ventless condensing dryer in an apartment. Where does the water removed from the clothes actually go?","options":["Into a drain line or collection tank","Out a small plastic vent to the outdoors","Into the room air as warm, humid exhaust","Into the lint filter, caught there as damp lint"],"expl":"ENERGY STAR describes ventless dryers as using a heat exchanger to cool the air and condense the water vapor into a drainpipe or a collection tank. Their waste heat goes into the room but the moisture does not, which is why no exhaust duct is needed. 'Humid exhaust' describes a vented dryer dumped indoors, which Whirlpool warns causes moisture damage.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Ventless dryers","tier":0,"ref":"ENERGY STAR Dryer Scoping Report","units":["dvt:condensing-dryer"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0116","stem":"How does a ventless heat-pump dryer remove water from a load without an exhaust duct?","options":["It recirculates the air and pulls out water on a refrigerant coil","It sprays cold water into the air stream to condense the water vapor","It heats room air with an electric element and discharges it indoors","It dries the air with a desiccant wheel regenerated by the room"],"expl":"ENERGY STAR describes a heat-pump dryer's air moving in a closed loop, reheated each pass while a refrigeration-dehumidification system removes the moisture. Cooling with cold water is a different condenser design, used mostly in all-in-one washer-dryers. Either way the water ends up in a drain or tank, not a duct.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Ventless dryers","tier":1,"ref":"ENERGY STAR Dryer Scoping Report","units":["dvt:heat-pump-dryer"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0117","stem":"A property manager asks how much air a modern full-size dryer pushes through its vent. Which range matches Whirlpool's published venting specification?","options":["About 105 to 230 CFM","About 1,200 to 1,700 CFM","About 400 to 600 CFM","About 25 to 75 CFM"],"expl":"Whirlpool's venting guide says any of its dryers moves at least 105 CFM at its maximum rated vent length and at most 230 CFM (ENERGY STAR gives 100 to 150 CFM for a conventional dryer fan). The 1,200 and 1,700 figures are velocities in feet per minute from Whirlpool's lint-carrying minimum and the DEDP table, not volumes; 400 CFM is the IMC kitchen-hood makeup air trigger.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Dryer airflow","tier":0,"ref":"Whirlpool W11222085 Dryer Venting Specs","units":["dvt:dryer-cfm","dvt:velocity-carries-lint"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0118","stem":"A homeowner replaced his 4-inch dryer run with 6-inch duct 'for better flow.' Why does Whirlpool's venting guide advise against this?","options":["Air velocity drops, so lint falls out of the airstream","Static pressure rises, so the blower motor overheats","The larger hood damper is heavy, so the dryer cannot open it","The bigger duct cools the exhaust and trips the high limit"],"expl":"Whirlpool explains that with the same blower, a duct larger than 4 inches lowers air speed, and velocity, not CFM, keeps lint suspended; its guide sets 1,200 FPM as the minimum duct velocity. The trap is assuming less restriction always helps. The IRC also fixes the exhaust duct at 4 inches nominal.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Lint buildup","tier":0,"ref":"Whirlpool W11222085; IRC 2024 §M1502.4.1","units":["dvt:velocity-carries-lint","dvt:no-upsize"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0119","stem":"A customer cleans the lint screen before every load and asks why the vent still needs periodic cleaning. What is the best explanation?","options":["Fine lint passes through the screen and settles in the duct","The screen traps lint on high-heat settings but not on low","Lint forms inside the duct as the fabric softener cools there","The screen releases its lint when the dryer door is opened"],"expl":"Whirlpool's venting guide notes that even with a lint screen, fine particles of lint pass through and are exhausted, which is why the duct and hood load up over time. CPSC likewise tells owners to clean the screen with every load and the vent and duct periodically. The screen is the first filter, not the last.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Lint buildup","tier":0,"ref":"Whirlpool W11222085; CPSC Pub. 5022","units":["dvt:fine-lint-passes"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0120","stem":"Two identical dryers get the same use. One vents 5 feet straight through the wall; the other runs 32 feet with four elbows to the roof. Why does the long run load up with lint faster?","options":["More resistance slows the air, so the lint settles out","More elbows trap heat, which bakes lint onto the duct wall","Longer runs cool the exhaust, so the lint filter passes more","Elbows speed the air up, which packs lint tight at each turn"],"expl":"DEDP lists extended duct lengths and restrictions among the factors that accelerate lint buildup, and its tables accept lower minimum velocities as developed length grows (1,700 FPM at 10 feet down to 1,200 FPM at 50 feet or more). Slower air drops lint. Whirlpool likewise warns that systems longer than specified accumulate lint.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Lint buildup","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 Gen. Intro, Tables 4.9-4.10; Whirlpool","units":["dvt:long-runs-lint"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0121","stem":"A Whirlpool dryer's instructions do not list the number of 90-degree elbows a job needs. By how much does Whirlpool's venting guide say to reduce the allowable vent length for each added 90-degree elbow?","options":["Reduce it by 10 feet","Reduce it by 5 feet","Reduce it by 1 3/4 feet","Reduce it by 2 1/2 feet"],"expl":"Whirlpool's guide cuts the allowable length by 10 feet per extra 90-degree elbow and 6 feet per extra 45. The 5-foot and 2 1/2-foot values are the IRC and IMC table equivalents for 4-inch-radius mitered 90 and 45 elbows, used when the dryer maker gives no fitting data. Mnemonic: Whirlpool 10 and 6, code 5 and 2.5.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Run length","tier":1,"ref":"Whirlpool W11222085 'Additional Elbows'","units":["dvt:whirlpool-elbows"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0122","stem":"A dryer duct runs uninsulated through a cold crawlspace in a northern winter. What deposit should the tech expect at the low spots of that run?","options":["Hardened lint cemented into a solid mass by condensed water","Loose dry lint packed into a light, felt-like plug","Black soot left behind by incomplete gas combustion","White mineral scale left behind by evaporated rinse water"],"expl":"DEDP's introduction says non-insulated ducts through cold spaces such as crawlspaces and attics condense water, and water combined with lint can form cementitious masses that adhere inside the duct. DEDP defines an adhered substance as one a silicon carbide bristle brush cannot remove, so these deposits need advanced procedures and should be documented.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Duct condensation","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Gen. Intro; Definitions","units":["dedp:intro-cementitious","dedp:intro-accelerants"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0123","stem":"With a partly blocked vent, CPSC staff measured what happened inside test dryers as lint built up. Which result did they report?","options":["Heater box temperature rose while the exhaust temperature fell","Heater box and exhaust temperatures both climbed together","Exhaust temperature rose while the drum temperature fell","Heater box and exhaust temperatures both fell together"],"expl":"CPSC's 1999 dryer tests found that as lint accumulated, the heat source and drum got hotter while the exhaust air got cooler, and clothes stayed damp. That is why a warm hood proves little, and why the control that protects against restriction sits at the heater rather than in the exhaust stream.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Safety controls","tier":0,"ref":"CPSC 1999 Report on Electric and Gas Clothes Dryers","units":["dvt:cpsc-temps"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0124","stem":"A tech watching a dryer with a clean vent sees the element switch off and back on every few minutes during a load. Which control is doing that?","options":["The operating thermostat at the blower housing","The high-limit thermostat at the heater housing","The thermal cutoff fuse beside the heater housing","The moisture sensor bars near the lint filter"],"expl":"The operating (cycling) thermostat sits in the exhaust airstream at the blower housing and switches the element or burner off at its set temperature and back on as the air cools, holding roughly 120 to 160 degrees F in normal operation. The high-limit acts only on overheating at the heater, and a thermal fuse opens once and must be replaced.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Safety controls","tier":0,"ref":"CPSC 1999 dryer report; Repair Clinic","units":["dvt:operating-thermostat"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0125","stem":"With a restricted vent, the heat source overheats while the air leaving the drum runs cooler than normal. Which control is positioned to catch that condition first?","options":["The high-limit thermostat at the heater","The cycling thermostat on the blower housing","The thermal cutoff on the heater housing wall","The moisture sensor bars at the lint filter"],"expl":"CPSC describes the high-limit thermostat as located in the heater box, set above the operating thermostat, and shutting off the heat source when that box overheats. The cycling thermostat sits in the exhaust stream, which runs cooler as flow drops, so it misses the problem. The thermal cutoff on the heater box is set higher still and backs up the high-limit.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Safety controls","tier":0,"ref":"CPSC 1999 dryer report","units":["dvt:high-limit"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0126","stem":"A customer says her dryer's thermal fuse has been replaced twice this year. What underlying cause should the tech check first?","options":["A restricted vent that makes the dryer run hot","A worn drum belt that slips under heavy wet loads","A failed moisture sensor that ends cycles too early","A weak supply voltage that stalls the drive motor"],"expl":"Repair Clinic names a blocked or partly blocked exhaust vent as the leading reason for repeated thermal fuse failure, since even a partial restriction pushes internal temperatures past the fuse's limit. The fuse is one-shot, so replacing it without clearing the vent just sets up the next failure.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Safety controls","tier":0,"ref":"Repair Clinic thermal fuse guide","units":["dvt:fuse-repeat-vent"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0127","stem":"A homeowner got a no-heat dryer running again by jumping out its blown thermal fuse with a piece of wire. Why is that a serious finding?","options":["It removes a one-time overheat shutoff meant to stop a fire","It overrides the cycling thermostat so the heat stays on","It disables the moisture sensor so the cycles run too long","It sends 240 volts through the drive motor and burns the windings"],"expl":"A thermal fuse is a single-use device that opens when cabinet or exhaust temperature passes a safe limit; Repair Clinic warns there is no reset and bypassing it defeats the safety circuit. The tech should record the bypass and refer the dryer to an appliance technician, and remember that blown fuses usually point back to a restricted vent.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Safety controls","tier":0,"ref":"Repair Clinic thermal fuse guide","units":["dvt:thermal-fuse"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0128","stem":"How do the two metal sensor bars inside the drum of a sensor-dry model tell the control that the load is dry?","options":["Wet clothes touching the bars conduct more current than dry ones","The bars read the exhaust air temperature at the blower","The bars weigh the load as it tumbles past the lint grille","The bars sense the drum slowing as the load loses water"],"expl":"ENERGY STAR notes that moisture sensing works by assessing conductivity, and Repair Clinic explains that wet clothes bridging the two bars conduct better than dry ones. Reading exhaust air temperature is a different auto-dry method that does not use the bars. Dryer-sheet residue on the bars can keep them from reading moisture accurately.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Moisture sensors","tier":0,"ref":"ENERGY STAR Dryer Scoping Report; Repair Clinic","units":["dvt:moisture-sensor"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0129","stem":"A homeowner wants a replacement wall hood that 'stays sealed' against wind. Which hood type do Whirlpool's installation instructions say not to use?","options":["A hood whose flap is held closed by a magnetic latch","A louvered hood whose slats close by gravity alone","A box hood with one gravity-closing flapper door","An angled hood with a hinged flap at its outlet"],"expl":"Whirlpool's instructions list exhaust hoods with magnetic latches as a fire hazard, alongside non-metal flexible duct; a latch that holds the flap shut resists the dryer's airflow. Gravity-closing louvered and box hoods are Whirlpool's recommended styles and angled hoods are acceptable, and the IRC requires a backdraft damper at every termination.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Termination hoods","tier":0,"ref":"Whirlpool dryer install instructions; IRC 2024 §M1502.3","units":["dvt:no-magnetic-hood"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0130","stem":"A customer asks why a screen is not allowed behind the hood even though it would keep birds out. What is the reason?","options":["Lint catches on the mesh and chokes off the exhaust outlet","Mesh holds the flap open and lets in cold outside air","Mesh speeds the exhaust and blows lint onto the siding","Mesh sheds rust flakes that collect in the elbows"],"expl":"The IRC and IMC both require a backdraft damper at the termination and bar screens there; lint collecting on mesh is what blocks the outlet. DEDP's appendix likewise lists a too-restrictive bird or rodent guard as a cause of poor performance. The damper, not a screen, is the pest barrier.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Termination hoods","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 §M1502.3; IMC 2024 §504.4; DEDP Appendix","units":["dedp:intro-accelerants","code:no-screen"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0131","stem":"A two-story house vents its dryer through a roof cap. Compared with a sidewall hood on a run of the same developed length, what should the tech expect?","options":["More back pressure for the blower to push against","Less back pressure because the warm exhaust rises","Higher airflow velocity leaving the roof termination","Equal back pressure but less lint at the cap"],"expl":"DEDP uses a separate roof table that accepts more back pressure at each developed length (under 0.50 inch w.c. at 10 feet versus under 0.30 for sidewall), which reflects the extra resistance typical of roof caps and vertical risers; its minimum velocities are the same for both. The 'warm air rises' trap ignores that the blower, not buoyancy, moves the exhaust.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Termination hoods","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Tables 4.9 and 4.10","units":["dedp:tbl-roof-offset"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0132","stem":"An installer wants to add an in-line spring-loaded backdraft damper behind a dryer's wall hood to stop drafts. What does a damper maker such as Aldes state about that use?","options":["Not recommended for dryers unless cleaned regularly","Acceptable for dryers if it can sit within 5 feet of the hood","Required on dryers in place of the damper built into the hood","Recommended for dryers if it is mounted with airflow down"],"expl":"Aldes' spec sheet says its spring-loaded backdraft dampers need 0.04 to 0.08 inch w.g. just to begin opening and are not recommended for use with a clothes dryer unless provisions are made for regular cleaning. Any added damper is another lint catcher and pressure drop; the code already requires the backdraft damper at the termination.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Termination hoods","tier":1,"ref":"Aldes Backdraft Dampers spec sheet","units":["dvt:spring-damper"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0133","stem":"A long run has a listed dryer exhaust duct power ventilator, such as a Fantech DEDPV-705 or Tjernlund LB2. How does the unit know to start when the dryer starts?","options":["A pressure switch senses positive duct pressure","A relay wired into the dryer's own motor circuit closes","A timer the user sets at the wall control panel runs it","A thermostat senses the laundry room air as it warms up"],"expl":"Both makers use pressure sensing: when the dryer starts, positive pressure in the duct closes the switch and runs the fan on its own circuit, with a panel near the dryer that shows status and faults. Fantech notes this avoids wiring through the dryer's circuit, which could void the dryer warranty. Code accepts only power ventilators listed to UL 705 for dryer exhaust.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Booster fans","tier":0,"ref":"Fantech DEDPV-705 manual; Tjernlund LB2 spec","units":["dvt:depv-pressure-switch"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0134","stem":"According to USFA's report on 2008-2010 clothes dryer fires in residential buildings, what was the leading factor contributing to ignition?","options":["Failure to clean, at 34 percent","Mechanical failure, at 27 percent","Electrical failure, at 16 percent","Dust, fiber and lint, at 28 percent"],"expl":"USFA found failure to clean (34 percent) was the leading factor contributing to ignition in 2008-2010. Dust, fiber and lint (28 percent) is USFA's leading item first ignited, a different category. Electrical failure (16 percent) was only USFA's third-ranked factor category, behind mechanical failure (29 percent); NFPA's 2014-2018 shares for those two were 27 and 16 percent. USFA's website puts failure to clean at 31 percent for 2018-2020.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Fire statistics","tier":0,"ref":"USFA Topical Fire Report v13i7 (2008-2010)","units":["dvt:usfa-failure-to-clean"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0135","stem":"A dryer vent company plans a seasonal marketing push. When did USFA's 2008-2010 data show residential clothes dryer fires peaking?","options":["In January, within a fall and winter high period","In July, within a summer and early fall high","In April, within a late winter and early spring high","In October, within the fall and winter high"],"expl":"USFA reported that residential dryer fire incidence was higher in the fall and winter months, peaking in January at 11 percent. October falls inside that higher period but is not the peak. Mnemonic: heavy clothes, holidays, January.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Fire statistics","tier":0,"ref":"USFA Topical Fire Report v13i7 (2008-2010)","units":["dvt:usfa-january"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0136","stem":"Which figures match NFPA's June 2020 estimate of U.S. home structure fires involving dryers per year in 2014-2018?","options":["13,820 fires, 7 civilian deaths, 344 injuries","2,900 fires, 5 deaths, 100 injuries","15,970 fires, 13 civilian deaths, 440 injuries","14,630 fires, 9 civilian deaths, 420 injuries"],"expl":"NFPA estimated 13,820 home structure fires a year in 2014-2018 involving dryers, with 7 civilian deaths, 344 injuries and $233 million in direct damage. The 2,900 set is USFA's 2008-2010 residential building estimate; 15,970 is NFPA's 2010-2014 count for dryers and washers combined, of which dryers alone were 14,630. Always name the source and years.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Fire statistics","tier":1,"ref":"NFPA 'Home Dryer Fires' (June 2020)","units":["dvt:usfa-annual","dvt:nfpa-annual"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0137","stem":"A customer read that most home dryer fires involve electric dryers and asks whether switching to gas would be safer. What does NFPA's 2020 analysis show?","options":["Electric's fire share roughly matches its share of homes","Electric models ignite lint at lower drum temperatures than gas","Gas models account for about half of dryer fire deaths","Gas models are left out of the national fire estimates"],"expl":"NFPA found 78 percent of dryers in 2014-2018 home fires were electric and 22 percent gas, which it called generally consistent with usage (79 and 21 percent of household dryers in 2017). Gas-dryer fires averaged no civilian deaths in that period, so 'half the deaths' is false. Maintenance matters more than fuel: failure to clean led at 32 percent.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Fire statistics","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA 'Home Dryer Fires' (June 2020) Table 3","units":["dvt:nfpa-electric-gas","dvt:nfpa-failure-to-clean"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0138","stem":"Which dryer safety change, proposed by CPSC for UL 2158 with a March 2019 effective date, does NFPA's 2017 report describe?","options":["Dryers are not allowed to run on the high limit for an indefinite period","Dryers are required to shut down once the vent exceeds 35 feet","Dryers are required to sound an alarm if the lint screen is out","Dryers cannot be sold unless boxed with a listed UL 2158A transition duct"],"expl":"NFPA's 2017 washer and dryer report lists recent dryer safety proposals with March 2019 effective dates. The one it credits to CPSC is the high-limit rule, which bars dryers from operating on the high limit indefinitely. The others include a drum-drive rule that cuts the heat if the drum stops and a mandatory cool-down before the end-of-cycle signal. None of them regulates vent length or transition ducts.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Safety controls","tier":1,"ref":"NFPA 'Home Fires Involving Clothes Dryers and Washing Machines' (2017)","units":["dvt:ul2158-high-limit"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0139","stem":"Behind a dryer, a tech finds a white vinyl accordion transition duct. What should he record and tell the customer?","options":["It is noncompliant and has to be replaced with a listed metal transition","It is acceptable if it stays under 8 feet and remains in view","It is acceptable if its joints are sealed with metal foil tape","It is acceptable on an electric dryer but not on a gas dryer"],"expl":"Whirlpool's instructions forbid plastic vent, CPSC tells owners to replace plastic or foil accordion duct with rigid or semi-rigid metal, and the IRC accepts only a single transition length listed and labeled to UL 2158A. DEDP adds that the consumer must be informed which transition is noncompliant. The 8-foot limit applies to a listed transition, not to vinyl.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Transition duct","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 §M1502.4.3; CPSC Pub. 5022; DEDP §1.9","units":["dvt:plastic-transition"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0140","stem":"Behind a stacked washer and dryer, the transition is two pieces of semi-rigid metal duct joined to reach 11 feet. Under the 2024 IRC, what is the finding?","options":["Noncompliant: a single listed piece of 8 feet maximum","Compliant: metal transition has no length limit","Noncompliant: one listed piece of 6 feet maximum","Compliant: the 35-foot limit covers the whole run"],"expl":"The 2024 IRC requires the transition to be a single length, listed and labeled to UL 2158A, not more than 8 feet long and not concealed; two joined pieces fail on count and length (Whirlpool's own limit for flexible metal is 7 3/4 feet). The 35-foot limit is for the permanent duct and expressly excludes the transition; 6 feet is how close the length label must sit to the duct connection.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Transition duct","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 §M1502.4.3","units":["dvt:whirlpool-flex-length"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0141","stem":"A dryer has been pushed tight to the wall, flattening the semi-rigid metal transition behind it. What should the tech record?","options":["A crushed transition that restricts flow and needs fixing","A normal condition, since semi-rigid duct springs back when run","A cosmetic dent that matters if the hood flap also stays shut","A code violation for gas dryers but not for an electric model"],"expl":"DEDP's pre-test inspection asks whether the duct is deformed, damaged or crushed, CPSC warns that kinks and crushing greatly reduce airflow, and Whirlpool's airflow-fault guidance starts with checking whether the run from dryer to wall is crushed. Photograph it and correct it before reconnecting, on gas and electric dryers alike.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Transition duct","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 §1.3.3; CPSC Pub. 5022; Whirlpool W11222085","units":["dvt:af-code","dvt:crushed-transition"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0142","stem":"Opening a laundry wall, a tech finds foil flex duct running 10 feet up the stud bay to the exterior hood. What is the correct finding?","options":["Noncompliant, because a transition duct cannot be concealed in the wall","Compliant, because the run is well under the 35 foot limit","Compliant, because foil flex is allowed in walls up to 25 feet","Noncompliant, because concealed duct requires No. 26 gage steel pipe"],"expl":"The 2024 IRC says transition ducts shall not be concealed within construction, and Whirlpool says not to install flexible vent in enclosed walls, ceilings or floors. The concealed run must be smooth-wall 4-inch metal exhaust duct, at least 0.0157 inch (No. 28 gage) under the IRC. No. 26 gage is the IMC requirement for duct inside a common multistory shaft.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Transition duct","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 §M1502.4.3, §M1502.4.1","units":["dvt:flex-not-concealed"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0143","stem":"At the dryer outlet, the transition is held on with three sheet-metal screws driven into the airstream. Why does the tech write this up?","options":["Screw tips inside the duct snag lint and start a clog","Screws void the dryer's UL listing at the outlet collar","Screws ground the duct and create a shock hazard","Screws let dryer heat conduct into the wall framing"],"expl":"Whirlpool says to use clamps and not to secure vent with screws or other fasteners that extend into the duct and catch lint, and the IMC bars fasteners that obstruct exhaust flow; the IRC limits screw protrusion to 1/8 inch. Record the protruding screws and reconnect with clamps.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Joints & fasteners","tier":0,"ref":"Whirlpool install instructions; IMC 2024 §504.4; IRC 2024 §M1502.4.2","units":["dvt:clamps-not-screws"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0144","stem":"In a basement run, every joint has the upstream section slipped over the outside of the downstream section. Why is this a finding?","options":["Each seam lip faces into the airflow and catches lint","Each seam leaks and draws basement air into the duct","Each joint requires welding when it runs horizontally","Each joint needs a drain weep to empty trapped condensate"],"expl":"The IRC and IMC require the insert end of each duct to extend into the adjoining duct or fitting in the direction of airflow, so seams trail the air instead of facing it. Reversed joints put a lip into the airstream where lint builds. Because the duct is under positive pressure, leaks push air out rather than drawing basement air in.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Joints & fasteners","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 §M1502.4.2; IMC 2024 §504.9.2","units":["code:male-end"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0145","stem":"In an attic, a 20-foot rigid dryer run hangs from a single strap near one end and sags open at two joints. What should the tech report?","options":["Inadequate support that lets the seams pull apart","Normal thermal movement that closes up once the duct heats up","Excess slope that the next cleaning will level back out","A sealing issue that foil tape at each joint fully corrects"],"expl":"The 2024 IRC requires dryer exhaust ducts to be supported at intervals not exceeding 12 feet and secured in place, with joints sealed and mechanically fastened (the IMC is stricter at 4-foot intervals). Sagging joints leak lint and moisture into the attic and create low spots where condensate collects. Tape does not replace support.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Supports & joints","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 §M1502.4.2; IMC 2024 §504.9.2","units":["code:supports","code:joint-seal"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0146","stem":"In the attic, a tech finds lint heaped on the insulation around a dryer duct joint that has slipped apart. What does this tell him?","options":["The run is discharging exhaust into the attic at that joint","Attic intake vents are drawing lint in from the exterior hood","The lint is normal carryover through an intact, sealed run","The insulation is shedding fibers that look like dryer lint"],"expl":"A separated joint dumps lint, moisture and heat into the attic, which the IRC forbids: dryer exhaust must terminate outside and no exhaust may discharge into an attic. Photograph it, reconnect and fasten the joint, and report the attic lint as a fire and moisture concern. On a gas dryer the leak also carries combustion gases.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Attic findings","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 §M1501.1, §M1502.3","units":["dvt:blower-positive-pressure","code:outdoor-only"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0147","stem":"A customer says the laundry room gets hot and humid on drying days, and the tech sees lint coating the floor behind the dryer. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A loose or torn transition at the dryer or the wall","A hood damper stuck closed at the outside wall","A cycling thermostat on the blower that has failed open","A moisture sensor coated with dryer-sheet film"],"expl":"Whirlpool warns that improper venting lets moisture and lint collect indoors, and that venting outdoors is how excess heat leaves the laundry area. Because the duct is under positive pressure, a loose or torn connection behind the dryer blows warm, moist, linty air into the room. CPSC also tells owners to clean behind the dryer, where lint builds up.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Customer symptoms","tier":0,"ref":"Whirlpool install instructions & W11222085; CPSC Pub. 5022","units":["dvt:blower-positive-pressure","dvt:hot-humid-room"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0148","stem":"A customer says towels now take two full cycles and the top of the dryer feels hot. What should the tech check first?","options":["The exhaust path from transition to hood for a restriction","The heating element, which is probably losing its output","The moisture sensor bars, which likely end cycles early","The power supply, which may be delivering low voltage"],"expl":"Whirlpool's venting guide says complaints of long drying times or a hot dryer top indicate the exhaust system needs inspection, and CPSC notes that damp clothes or longer-than-normal drying can mean the lint screen or duct is blocked. Check the common, cheap cause, the vent, before blaming components.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Customer symptoms","tier":0,"ref":"Whirlpool W11222085; CPSC Pub. 5022","units":["dvt:clog-slows-drying","dvt:long-dry-hot-top"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0149","stem":"A customer reports a hot, burning smell near the dryer during loads, and the tech finds heavy lint around the base of the cabinet. What is the best recommendation?","options":["Stop using it until the vent and the dryer interior are cleaned","Keep using it on low heat until the next scheduled vent cleaning","Keep using it but empty the lint screen twice during each load","Stop using it until the vent hood is replaced with a louvered type"],"expl":"CPSC explains that lint buildup in the dryer or duct blocks airflow, causes excessive heat and can start a fire, and advises having a qualified service person clean the dryer chassis interior. A burning smell with lint around the cabinet means fuel and heat are already together, so use should stop until both the vent and the appliance interior are cleaned.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Customer symptoms","tier":0,"ref":"CPSC Pub. 5022; NFPA 2017 dryer safety tips","units":["dvt:burning-smell","dvt:cabinet-interior"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0150","stem":"Outside, the hood flap is held partly open by a mat of lint even with the dryer off. What problems does this create?","options":["It restricts airflow and lets pests and drafts into the duct","It raises exhaust velocity and blows lint across the siding","It pulls the flap shut harder and blocks rain from entering","It meets code as long as the flap swings open when the dryer runs"],"expl":"Whirlpool's cleaning guidance warns that lint collecting in the hood keeps flappers or louvers from opening or closing completely, and says to confirm they move freely after cleaning. A jammed flap both restricts the outlet and defeats the backdraft damper the code requires, letting in cold air, birds and rodents.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Termination condition","tier":0,"ref":"Whirlpool W11222085; IRC 2024 §M1502.3","units":["dvt:flap-jammed"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0151","stem":"How should a tech confirm that a termination damper actually opens under the dryer's own airflow?","options":["Run the dryer and watch the flap open fully with air exiting","Lift the flap by hand and confirm it drops back shut freely","Shine a light up the duct from outside to check for daylight","Measure the hood's open area against the 12.5-square-inch rule"],"expl":"CPSC and USFA both say to check the outdoor vent while the dryer runs, making sure the covering opens and exhaust air escapes. A hand test shows the flap swings freely, but not that the dryer's own airflow opens it; the 12.5-square-inch figure is the code's minimum open area, a separate check.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Termination condition","tier":0,"ref":"CPSC Pub. 5022; USFA dryer safety flyer","units":["dvt:flap-test-running"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0152","stem":"The owner added a 1/4-inch wire mesh screen behind the hood flap to keep birds out. What does the tech report?","options":["Screens are not permitted at the end of the exhaust duct","Screens are allowed if the wire mesh is 1/4 inch or coarser","Screens are allowed on electric dryers but not on gas","Screens are required once birds have nested in a dryer duct"],"expl":"Both the 2024 IRC and the IMC state that screens shall not be installed at the dryer duct termination, whatever the mesh size, because lint collects on them; the backdraft damper is the required pest barrier. DEDP's appendix also lists a too-restrictive bird or rodent guard as a cause of low performance.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Termination condition","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 §M1502.3; IMC 2024 §504.4","units":["code:no-screen"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0153","stem":"A tech hears chirping and finds a bird nest packed behind the hood of a gas dryer's vent. Why is this more serious than on an electric dryer?","options":["Blocked exhaust forces burner gases back into the home","Nesting material can reach the burner and feed its flame","Gas dryers have no high limit, so heat builds unchecked","The nest blocks the burner's combustion air intake at the hood"],"expl":"CPSC's CO guidance notes that birds and other animals nest in vents and block exhaust gases, causing them to enter the home, and the IMC requires dryer exhaust to carry products of combustion outdoors. A gas dryer draws its air from the room, not through the hood, and has high-limit protection like an electric one, so the danger is exhaust backing up.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Nests & gas dryers","tier":0,"ref":"CPSC 2002 CO release; IMC 2024 §504.1","units":["dvt:gas-exhaust-co"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0154","stem":"A sidewall hood sits 18 inches below a vented soffit, and the dryer's instructions give no termination location. Under the 2024 IRC, what is the finding?","options":["Too close: the rule is 3 feet from openings, soffit vents included","Acceptable: soffit vents do not count as openings into the building","Too close: the minimum is 10 feet from any attic ventilation opening","Acceptable: the 3-foot rule applies to doors and windows alone"],"expl":"When the manufacturer is silent, the 2024 IRC and IMC require the termination to be at least 3 feet in any direction from openings into the building, and the text expressly includes openings in ventilated soffits. Exhaust released under a soffit intake is pulled back into the attic with its moisture and lint.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Termination location","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 §M1502.3; IMC 2024 §504.4.1","units":["code:term-soffit","code:term-3ft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0155","stem":"A dryer duct ends at a louvered soffit panel under the eave, and the attic insulation near that eave is damp and dusted with lint. What explains this?","options":["Soffit intakes pull the exhaust back into the attic","Warm exhaust rises through the roof vents and dries out","The duct is too short, so velocity is too high at the eave","Lint blows down onto the insulation from the ridge vent"],"expl":"Soffit vents are attic air intakes, so exhaust discharged there is drawn back into the attic. The IRC says exhaust air shall not be discharged into an attic, soffit, ridge vent or crawlspace and, absent manufacturer instructions, requires 3 feet of separation from ventilated soffit openings. Report it and recommend relocating the termination to an exterior wall or a roof cap.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Termination location","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 §M1501.1, §M1502.3","units":["code:outdoor-only","code:term-soffit"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0156","stem":"A townhouse dryer vents through a roof cap 25 feet above grade. Why are roof terminations like this a recurring problem for owners?","options":["Lint builds up at the cap where no one sees or reaches it","Roof caps cannot legally be fitted with a backdraft damper","Roof caps lower back pressure, so lint blows straight out","Roof caps are permitted for gas dryers but not electric"],"expl":"DEDP gives roof terminations their own table with higher allowable back pressure, reflecting the extra resistance typical of caps and vertical risers, and Whirlpool notes lint collects in hoods until flappers cannot open or close; on a roof that happens out of sight and reach. The code still requires a backdraft damper at every termination, and the IMC requires a cleanout on each vertical riser.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Roof terminations","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 Table 4.10; IMC 2024 §504.3; Whirlpool W11222085","units":["dvt:roof-cap-lint"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0157","stem":"In a mid-rise apartment building, each unit's dryer ties into a common vertical shaft duct with a roof exhaust fan. Which feature must that IMC common system have?","options":["A continuously running fan on standby power","A fire damper at each unit's connection to the shaft","A screen at the roof termination to keep birds out","A booster fan at each dryer outlet to balance the airflow"],"expl":"IMC 504.11 requires a common multistory dryer duct to have a fire-rated shaft, no dampers in the exhaust duct, No. 26 gage rigid duct without offsets, a continuously running fan on standby power with monitored operation, makeup air, a 12 by 12 inch cleanout at the base, no screen at the termination, and service to dryers only. Mnemonic: fan always on, nothing in the way.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Multi-family buildings","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.11","units":["code:ms-fan"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0158","stem":"In a townhouse, the run from an interior laundry closet works out to 48 feet once its elbows are counted. Under the 2024 IRC, what should the tech look for near the dryer connection?","options":["A permanent label stating the equivalent length","A cleanout tee located within 6 feet of the dryer outlet","A secondary lint trap mounted on the wall behind the dryer","A second transition duct spliced on for the extra length"],"expl":"When equivalent length exceeds 35 feet, the 2024 IRC requires it to be identified on a permanent label or tag within 6 feet of the exhaust duct connection. A run that long is allowed only under the dryer maker's instructions or a listed power ventilator's instructions, so a missing label is worth documenting. A second transition is not allowed.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Multi-family buildings","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 §M1502.4.7, §M1502.4.6","units":["code:label-required","code:label-6ft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0159","stem":"The duct disappears into a finished ceiling and the tech can only estimate its route. How does DEDP say the actual length should be established?","options":["By measuring it while cleaning with marked rods or cables","By counting elbows and adding 5 feet of length for each one","By measuring the straight line from dryer to exterior hood","By reading the maximum vent length on the dryer's data plate"],"expl":"DEDP section 1.8 has the tech estimate developed length during inspection, then determine the actual length during cleaning using measured fiberglass cleaning rods, cable drives or air-whip tools. The 5-feet-per-elbow figure converts fittings into developed length; it does not measure the duct. The data plate describes the dryer, not the installed run.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Measuring length","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §1.8","units":["dedp:1.8-actual"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0160","stem":"After cleaning a long concealed run, the tech still feels weak airflow at the hood. What is the appropriate next diagnostic step?","options":["Send a video camera through to find the blockage or crush","Brush it again with a larger head until the flow improves","Install a booster fan to overcome the hidden restriction","Recommend replacing the dryer, since the duct is now clean"],"expl":"DEDP's foreword says video is not the primary inspection method, but when a duct fails minimum pressure and velocity, cameras are recommended to diagnose it; the appendix adds that a duct still blocked or crushed after service should be video-scoped or dismantled to find the cause. A booster fan hides a defect rather than finding it.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Video inspection","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Foreword; Appendix","units":["dedp:app-still-blocked","dedp:fwd-video-after-fail"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0161","stem":"Before touching anything, a tech photographs the job. Which photo best protects him if the customer later disputes what was found?","options":["The transition and hood exactly as they were on arrival","The finished hood after cleaning with no before shot","The dryer's rating plate after it has been reconnected","The emptied lint screen after the final airflow test run"],"expl":"Before-and-after photographs are the strongest record of what was delivered, and DEDP has the tech document the termination location and tell the consumer about a noncompliant transition. An as-found photo of the transition, connections and hood is the evidence of conditions the tech did not create.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Photo documentation","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 §1.8.4, §1.9","units":["dvt:as-found-photos"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0162","stem":"While sliding out a gas dryer, the tech smells gas. What should he do?","options":["Get everyone out and call the gas supplier from outside","Close the dryer's shutoff valve and then finish the cleaning","Open the windows and run the dryer to clear the gas","Tighten the connector and check the fittings with soapy water"],"expl":"Whirlpool's gas dryer warning says not to touch electrical switches or use a phone in the building, to clear everyone out, and to call the gas supplier from a neighbor's phone, or the fire department if the supplier cannot be reached; CPSC gives the same steps. Leak repair belongs to a qualified gas professional, not the vent tech.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Stop & report","tier":0,"ref":"Whirlpool W11222085 gas warning; CPSC Pub. 5135","units":["dvt:gas-smell"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0163","stem":"Before moving a gas dryer, the tech spots an old, uncoated brass flexible gas connector. What should he do?","options":["Leave the dryer in place and have a qualified pro replace it","Slide the dryer out gently and check the flare nuts for leaks","Swap in a coated connector himself, then start the cleaning","Wrap the connector joints with tape, then proceed with the work"],"expl":"CPSC warns that older uncoated brass connectors can separate from their end pieces, that moving the appliance even slightly to clean behind it can cause complete failure and a fire or explosion, and that only a qualified professional should check and replace one. Do not move the dryer; document it and refer it out.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Gas connectors","tier":0,"ref":"CPSC Pub. 5135","units":["dvt:brass-connector"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0164","stem":"Behind a gas dryer in a small closet, the tech finds the transition pulled off the wall collar. Beyond lint, what hazard should he report?","options":["Burner exhaust, with its carbon monoxide, spilling into the home","Raw natural gas escaping through the open wall collar","Electrical arcing from the dryer's 240-volt heater circuit","Water from the burner's condensate trap pooling on the closet floor"],"expl":"The IMC requires dryer exhaust to convey moisture and any products of combustion outdoors, and Whirlpool lists expelling exhaust gases from gas dryers as a core reason for outdoor venting. A disconnected duct in a small closet discharges those gases, including carbon monoxide, into the home. A gas dryer has no 240-volt heater; its heat source is the burner.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Gas dryer hazards","tier":0,"ref":"IMC 2024 §504.1; Whirlpool W11222085","units":["dvt:gas-exhaust-co"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0165","stem":"Under NADCA's DEDP standard, what must happen when the tech finds that the existing transition duct does not comply with code?","options":["The consumer must be told which type of transition is noncompliant","The tech must replace it himself before reconnecting the dryer","The tech is free to omit it from the report if the customer declines","The AHJ must be notified before the dryer is used again"],"expl":"DEDP section 1.9 has the tech verify that the transition complies with local, state and federal code or the AHJ, and states that the consumer must be informed what type of transition is not in compliance. It does not require the tech to replace it or to notify the AHJ, and it does not allow the finding to go unmentioned.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Stop & report","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §1.9","units":["dedp:1.9-inform","dedp:1.9-code-check"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0166","stem":"An uninsulated rigid dryer run crosses a vented attic in a cold climate. Below one low joint the tech sees rust streaks and a water stain on the ceiling. What is happening?","options":["Condensation in the cold duct, so report insulation and pitch","Normal attic sweating, so no action beyond routine cleaning","Attic air drawn in at the seams, so seal them with tape","A failed hood damper, so replace it and the stain will stop"],"expl":"DEDP's introduction names non-insulated ducts through cold spaces such as attics and crawlspaces, and incorrect pitch, as causes of condensation and liquid water in the duct, which also builds cementitious lint. Water runs to the low joints and leaks out. Report the routing, the missing insulation and the sag; the duct is under positive pressure, so it is not drawing attic air in.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Unconditioned space","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 Gen. Intro; Appendix","units":["dedp:intro-accelerants","dedp:app-moisture"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0167","stem":"Outside, a wall hood sits 4 inches above grade behind dense shrubs. How much clearance do Whirlpool's instructions require below the hood outlet?","options":["At least 12 inches from the ground or any object","At least 3 feet from the ground or any object","At least 18 inches from the ground or any obstruction","At least 24 inches from the ground or any object"],"expl":"Whirlpool's installation instructions require the exhaust hood to be at least 12 inches from the ground or any object that may obstruct the exhaust, such as flowers, rocks, bushes or snow. A buried or shrub-blocked hood restricts flow and invites nesting. The 3-foot (36-inch) figure is the code's separation from building openings, not ground clearance.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Termination condition","tier":0,"ref":"Whirlpool dryer install instructions","units":["dvt:hood-12-inches"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0168","stem":"During inspection, the tech finds a dryer exhaust duct power ventilator in the attic of a house with a long run. What should he confirm before relying on the vent?","options":["That it runs with the dryer and its warning panel is visible","That it is wired into the dryer's own power terminal block","That a screen guards its outlet from lint-carried debris","That its fan motor sits right in the airstream for cooling"],"expl":"DEDP section 1.6 requires determining whether a booster fan is present and operational, and Fantech requires the fan's notification panel to be permanently installed and readily visible in the dryer space, because the dryer depends on the fan for safe operation. Listed units start from a duct pressure switch, not the dryer's wiring, and Tjernlund keeps the motor outside the airstream.","domain":"Inspections & Assessments","sub":"Booster fans","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 §1.6; Fantech DEDPV-705 manual","units":["dvt:depv-pressure-switch","dvt:depv-alert-panel"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0169","stem":"A tech is rotary-brushing a dryer duct from the termination with threaded flexible rods on a cordless drill. Per the Gardus LintEater manual, which drill direction is correct?","options":["Clockwise going in, and still clockwise while pulling out","Clockwise going in, counterclockwise to back it out","Counterclockwise going in, clockwise to back it out","Either direction, reversing whenever the brush binds"],"expl":"The LintEater manual says to always spin the drill clockwise, including while withdrawing, because reversing the drill or turning the rods counterclockwise can unthread the joints and leave rods and brush inside the vent. Backing out in reverse is the tempting trap and is exactly how rods get lost.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Rotary brush rods","tier":0,"ref":"Gardus LintEater manual, Steps 11-12","units":["dvt:clockwise-always"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0170","stem":"Halfway through a run with two elbows, the rotary brush binds and the drill starts to labor. What does the tool manufacturer say to do first?","options":["Stop spinning at once and do not force the rods","Raise the drill speed to cut through the jam","Reverse the drill briefly to free the brush","Push the rods harder to punch past the elbow"],"expl":"Gardus says that if the brush binds up, stop immediately and never force the rods, to avoid breaking rods or damaging the vent. Reversing to free it is the tempting move, but reverse rotation is what unthreads rod joints inside the duct.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Rotary brush rods","tier":0,"ref":"Gardus LintEater manual, Step 11","units":["dvt:stop-if-binds"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0171","stem":"The brush has reached the laundry-room end and the tech is ready to pull the rods back out. Per the LintEater manual, how are they withdrawn?","options":["Keep spinning clockwise and uncouple one rod at a time","Stop the drill and draw every rod out without turning","Spin counterclockwise so that each joint releases cleanly","Pull by hand, turning each rod the opposite direction"],"expl":"Gardus says to continue spinning clockwise while pulling the rods out, disconnecting one rod at a time without loosening the rods still inside, and reattaching the drill after each one. Any counterclockwise turn, by drill or by hand, can unthread a joint that is still in the duct.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Rotary brush rods","tier":0,"ref":"Gardus LintEater manual, Step 12","units":["dvt:rods-one-at-a-time"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0172","stem":"A helper accidentally ran the drill in reverse, and the brush plus three rods separated inside the duct. What does the tool maker provide for this situation?","options":["A retrieval tool attached to the remaining rods, used by hand","A blockage removal tool driven hard by the cordless drill","A reverse spin on the remaining rods to thread them back together","A dryer run on high heat to push them out through the hood flap"],"expl":"Gardus makes a Retrieval Tool that attaches to the remaining rods and is used by hand, without the drill, to grab the lost brush. The Blockage Removal Tool is a real LintEater accessory, but it is for nests and debris, and spinning backward is what caused the separation.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Rotary brush rods","tier":1,"ref":"Gardus LintEater manual, rods-disconnect note","units":["dvt:clockwise-always","dvt:retrieval-tool"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0173","stem":"A tech brushes from the termination while the customer's dryer runs to blow the loosened lint outdoors. What keeps the brush from reaching the dryer's blower wheel?","options":["A guarded adapter fitted between dryer and duct","The lint screen seated firmly in its housing slot","The clutch on the drill slipping at the dryer outlet","The transition duct's corrugations stopping the brush"],"expl":"The LintEater Dryer Adapter has a built-in guard that keeps the rotating brush out of the dryer and away from the blower wheel, and it must be removed after cleaning or it will collect lint. The lint screen sits on the drum side of the blower, so it cannot stop a brush coming up the exhaust side.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Rotary brush rods","tier":0,"ref":"Gardus LintEater manual, Additional Method 1","units":["dvt:dryer-adapter"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0174","stem":"With the shop vac connected at the laundry-room end and the hood taped open, the tech switches the vac on and the foil transition collapses flat. What does the LintEater manual say this means?","options":["A blockage or restriction; shut the vac off at once","Normal suction; keep it running and begin brushing","Weak suction; switch to a 1.5-inch household hose","An open joint; tape the foil seam and keep the vac running"],"expl":"Gardus warns that if the vent compresses when the vacuum is turned on, turn it off immediately, because it indicates a blockage or restriction to be checked. The small-hose option reverses the manual's own warning: its adapter is sized for a 2.5-inch shop vac hose because 1.5-inch hoses clog with lint.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Vacuum collection","tier":0,"ref":"Gardus LintEater manual, Steps 9-10","units":["dvt:vent-collapses","dvt:hose-2-5"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0175","stem":"Brushing from outside, the tech runs out of rods before the brush reaches the laundry-room end of a long run. What does the tool manual recommend?","options":["Finish from the inside and count the sections used","Push the last section in by hand without spinning it","Switch to a smaller brush so it travels farther along","Leave the far end for the next scheduled service visit"],"expl":"Gardus says that if the vent is longer than the included rods, finish cleaning from the opposite end, and to count the rods used so you can estimate how far you reached. That rod count is also how DEDP 1.8 expects the actual length to be found during cleaning.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Access and direction","tier":0,"ref":"Gardus LintEater manual, Steps 11-12","units":["dvt:finish-other-end"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0176","stem":"Under NADCA's general cleaning principle, what must accompany the airflow from a vacuum or blower for a dryer duct to be mechanically cleaned?","options":["An agitation device that dislodges lint from the duct","A second vacuum drawing from the opposite end","A HEPA filter on the blower or vacuum exhaust","A manometer confirming negative duct pressure"],"expl":"ACR 2025 4.3 pairs an agitation device that dislodges non-adhered material with a vacuum collection device that captures it; airflow or negative pressure alone is not cleaning. HEPA exhaust filtration and pressure verification are separate ACR requirements that do not dislodge anything.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Agitation and collection","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA ACR 2025 §4.3","units":["dvt:agitate-and-collect"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0177","stem":"In the LintEater manual's main method for brushing from the termination, where is the shop vac or leaf blower connected?","options":["To the duct at the laundry-room end","To the hood, right beside the rods","To a tee cut into the middle of the run","To the lint screen slot on the dryer"],"expl":"In Steps 10 and 11 the vent is removed from the dryer, the shop vac or blower is attached at that inside end, and brushing starts from the exterior, so the airflow source sits opposite the brush. Hooking the vac to the hood beside the rods is the manual's Method 3, which it calls not preferred and allows only with the Dryer Adapter stop installed inside.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Vacuum collection","tier":0,"ref":"Gardus LintEater manual, Steps 10-11","units":["dvt:vac-at-laundry-end"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0178","stem":"After clearing a nest, the tech's shop vac is packed with lint, twigs, and droppings and must be emptied before he continues. Following NADCA's equipment hygiene principle, where should he open it?","options":["Outside of the building, out in the open air","In the laundry room over a drop cloth","In the attached garage by the open door","At the laundry sink, rinsing it out as he goes"],"expl":"ACR 2025 3.1.2.1 allows on-site opening of contaminated vacuum equipment only in a containment area or outside the building, and bird droppings add a histoplasmosis reason to keep it outdoors. An attached garage by an open door is still inside the building, which is the tempting trap.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Equipment hygiene","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA ACR 2025 §3.1.2.1","units":["dvt:open-vac-outside"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0179","stem":"A crew is cleaning dryer vents unit by unit in a 40-unit apartment building. Following NADCA's equipment hygiene rules, what must happen to the vacuum, rods, and brushes between one unit and the next?","options":["They are cleaned or sealed before every move","They move on as-is until the day's last unit is done","They are cleaned after every third unit is finished","They are cleaned only when bird debris turns up"],"expl":"ACR 2025 3.1.3 requires collection devices, vacuums, and tools to be cleaned or sealed before relocation within the building and again before they leave it. Waiting until the last unit meets only the leaving-the-building clause and carries lint and droppings from unit to unit.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Equipment hygiene","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA ACR 2025 §3.1.3","units":["dvt:clean-tools-between"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0180","stem":"The dryer must be walked out across a finished wood floor into the hallway to reach the wall connection. What does NADCA's minimum (Level 1) containment principle call for?","options":["Clean coverings that extend past the work area","Plastic barriers sealed from floor to ceiling","A two-layer 6-mil poly floor sealed to walls","An air scrubber running at four air changes per hour"],"expl":"ACR 2025 3.9.2, part of Level 1 (the minimum on every project), requires clean protective coverings that extend beyond the work area to protect flooring, equipment, and furniture whenever necessary. Floor-to-ceiling barriers, the two-layer 6-mil floor, and HEPA air scrubbing at four air changes per hour are all Level 2 features, the tempting over-answers.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Protecting the site","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA ACR 2025 §3.9.2","units":["dvt:coverings"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0181","stem":"Before brushing from outside, the tech finds a louvered wall hood. What does the LintEater manual say to do with the louvers?","options":["Tape them open or pop them out and refit them after","Snap them off and leave them off to improve the flow","Leave them closed so the brush pushes them aside","Cut the hood away and leave an open pipe end instead"],"expl":"Gardus says to tape or clip the damper open, or remove the louvers by bending them slightly without breaking the plastic pins, and to reinstall them when done. Leaving them off is the trap: the 2024 IRC and IMC require a backdraft damper at every dryer termination.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Termination service","tier":0,"ref":"Gardus LintEater manual, Steps 7 and 14","units":["dvt:louvers"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0182","stem":"During service, the tech finds the wall hood fitted with a fine bird screen matted with lint. Under the 2024 IRC and IMC, what is the correct action?","options":["Remove the screen and recommend a damper-type hood","Clean the screen and reinstall it to keep birds out","Replace it with coarser half-inch hardware cloth","Leave it and shorten the service interval"],"expl":"The 2024 IRC (M1502.3) and 2024 IMC (504.4) both say screens shall not be installed at the dryer duct termination; the backdraft damper handles pests, and DEDP notes a restrictive barrier screen can fail a duct. Coarser mesh is still a screen and still catches lint.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Termination service","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.3; IMC 2024 504.4","units":["code:no-screen","code:backdraft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0183","stem":"After cleaning, the dryer runs but the hood flap drags on its hinge and barely lifts, even though the duct itself was brushed clean. What should the tech do?","options":["Free up or replace the damper so it opens fully","Remove the flap so exhaust leaves with no resistance","Prop the flap open with a screw through the hood","Log it as normal back pressure and close out"],"expl":"NFPA's tip sheet says the outdoor vent flap should open when the dryer is operating, and GE tells owners to check that the hood flaps move freely, so a sticking flap needs clearing or a new hood. Removing the flap is the trap, because the IRC requires a backdraft damper at the termination.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Termination service","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA dryer tip sheet; GE owner's manual","units":["dvt:flap-test-running","dvt:fix-sticking-flap"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0184","stem":"A dryer hood holds an active nest with eggs, and the birds are a native songbird protected under the Migratory Bird Treaty Act. What may the tech do without a federal permit?","options":["Wait until it is inactive before removing it","Remove it once the homeowner signs approval","Relocate the eggs to a box mounted nearby","Remove it since blocked vents cause fires"],"expl":"USFWS says destroying an active nest (eggs or chicks present) of an MBTA-protected bird requires a permit, while an inactive nest may be destroyed without one as long as it is not kept. Homeowner approval does not replace a federal permit, which is the deferral trap; state rules can add protections.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Nest removal","tier":0,"ref":"USFWS depredation permit FAQ, Q4","units":["dvt:active-nest"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0185","stem":"Which bird's nest, found with eggs in a dryer termination, is outside Migratory Bird Treaty Act protection?","options":["European starling","American robin","Eastern phoebe","Carolina wren"],"expl":"USFWS lists nonnative species such as European starlings, house sparrows, and rock (feral) pigeons as not protected under the MBTA, while native birds like robins, phoebes, and wrens are protected and their active nests need a permit. Mnemonic: the three imports, Starling, Sparrow, Pigeon; check state law too.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Nest removal","tier":1,"ref":"USFWS depredation permit FAQ, Q2","units":["dvt:unprotected-birds"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0186","stem":"After cleaning the duct, a vent tech with no appliance training sees heavy lint around the drum through the lint-screen slot. What should he recommend?","options":["Have a qualified service technician clean the inside of the cabinet","Remove the rear panel himself and vacuum out the whole cabinet","Blow it out through the lint-screen slot with compressed air","Vacuum the lint-screen slot alone and call the unit fully clean"],"expl":"CPSC advises having a qualified service person clean the interior of the dryer chassis periodically, and GE's owner manual says to have a qualified technician vacuum lint from the dryer once a year. Pulling panels without appliance training is the trap; DEDP also leaves the appliance's cleanliness outside its scope.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Appliance vs vent","tier":0,"ref":"CPSC Pub 5022; GE owner's manual","units":["dvt:cabinet-interior"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0187","stem":"A clean duct passes the DEDP test, but the dryer's own discharge at the hood, measured running empty on no heat, is far weaker than the test device produced. What does the DEDP appendix suggest?","options":["Have a qualified service provider evaluate how the dryer runs","Re-clean the duct and then repeat the DEDP test","Add a booster fan to make up for the shortfall","Replace the hood with a larger louvered wall cap"],"expl":"The DEDP appendix says a significant pressure differential between the appliance and the test device means the dryer should be evaluated for proper operation by a qualified service provider. Re-cleaning a duct that already passed chases the wrong component.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Appliance vs vent","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014, Appendix","units":["dedp:app-appliance-should"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0188","stem":"A system with a secondary lint trap in the laundry room is due for a DEDP performance test after cleaning. What must the tech establish about the trap first?","options":["That it is present and clean before the test","That it is removed so it cannot skew the test","That it is bypassed with a straight duct piece","That the homeowner will clean it after the test"],"expl":"DEDP 1.7 says it shall be determined whether a secondary lint trap is present and clean before the performance test. Removing or bypassing it changes the system being tested, and leaving it for the homeowner is the deferral trap.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Secondary lint traps","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §1.7","units":["dedp:1.7-secondary-trap"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0189","stem":"A customer says the dryer booster fan in the attic has become noisy and vibrates more than it used to. Per Fantech's DBF4XLT instructions, what is the likely cause?","options":["Lint buildup on the fan impeller","A kinked pressure-switch tube","A slipping drum belt in the dryer","A hood flap chattering in the wind"],"expl":"Fantech notes that excessive booster noise or vibration may indicate lint buildup on the impeller, which is why it calls for periodic impeller inspection. The pressure tube matters for starting: Fantech has the tech verify it is connected when the LED does not light, and a tube problem stops the fan rather than making it vibrate.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Booster fan service","tier":0,"ref":"Fantech DBF4XLT instructions, Maintenance","units":["dvt:booster-noise"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0190","stem":"A tech is about to pull the inlet duct off a Fantech DBF4XLT booster fan to clean its impeller. What do Fantech's instructions require first?","options":["Shut off its own power supply at the source","Switch the dryer off at its own control panel","Unplug the dryer so the pressure switch opens","Wait one minute for the fan to coast to a stop"],"expl":"Fantech's maintenance steps begin with disconnecting the incoming power supply at the source, and its safety notes say to unplug or switch off and lock the service panel. The fan has its own 120 V cord and runs on 5 to 10 minutes after the dryer stops, so turning off or unplugging the dryer does not isolate it.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Booster fan service","tier":0,"ref":"Fantech DBF4XLT instructions, Maintenance","units":["dvt:booster-power"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0191","stem":"After the service, the booster fan's indicator LED stays dark while the dryer runs. Besides confirming the fan has 120 V power, what does Fantech's troubleshooting list tell the tech to check?","options":["That the pressure tube is connected","That the dryer's thermal fuse is still intact","That the hood damper is taped fully open","That the secondary lint trap is removed"],"expl":"The DBF4XLT uses a positive-pressure switch fed by clear tubing from the duct; if the LED does not light with the dryer on, Fantech says to check power, check for obstructions between dryer and fan, and verify the tubing is connected. The dryer's thermal fuse governs heat, not the booster's start signal.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Booster fan service","tier":0,"ref":"Fantech DBF4XLT instructions, Troubleshooting","units":["dvt:booster-led"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0192","stem":"What minimum inspection interval do Fantech's DBF4XLT instructions give for checking the booster fan impeller for lint under normal conditions?","options":["Every six months","Every year","Every three months","Every five years"],"expl":"Fantech says the impeller should be inspected a minimum of every six months under normal conditions, more often with heavy use. Every year matches NFPA's once-a-year vent-cleaning interval, three months approximates the LintEater maker's 3 to 4 vent cleanings a year, and five years is Fantech's warranty term.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Booster fan service","tier":1,"ref":"Fantech DBF4XLT instructions, Maintenance","units":["dvt:nfpa-vent-interval","dvt:booster-6-months"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0193","stem":"In a duct that runs through a cold crawlspace, the brush skates over a hard, cement-like crust that direct agitation will not break loose. How does DEDP treat this deposit?","options":["An adhered mass that needs advanced removal procedures","Loose debris that needs a second brushing","Normal residue that needs no more attention","Light film that needs one contact-vacuum pass"],"expl":"The DEDP appendix describes lint combined with water that has solidified into a cementitious mass that cannot be removed with direct agitation and requires advanced procedures, and DEDP 4.5 has the tech record adhered substances found. Calling it debris is the trap, since DEDP defines debris as non-adhered.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Adhered deposits","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014, Appendix; §4.5","units":["dedp:app-cement","dedp:def-adhered"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0194","stem":"A duct through an unheated attic holds standing water and a cementitious lint mass at a low sag. Beyond reporting the deposit, which recommendation addresses the cause DEDP identifies?","options":["Insulate the run and fix its pitch","Add a booster fan to push moisture out","Shorten the cleaning interval to 3 months","Fit a finer screen at the termination"],"expl":"DEDP names non-insulated ducts through cold spaces and incorrect pitch as causes of condensation that combines with lint into cementitious masses; GE likewise calls for insulating ducts in unheated areas and sloping horizontal runs down toward the outdoors about 1/4 inch per foot. A booster fan moves air but does not stop condensation.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Adhered deposits","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014, Introduction and Appendix","units":["dvt:insulate-slope"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0195","stem":"After a full rotary cleaning, airflow at the hood is still weak, and the rods kept stopping at the same point inside a wall. What does the DEDP appendix recommend to find the cause?","options":["Video-scope or dismantle the duct","Brush it again at a higher drill speed","Add a booster fan to overcome the loss","Retest until two readings finally agree"],"expl":"The DEDP appendix lists a duct that may still be blocked or crushed after servicing and recommends a video scope or physically dismantling the ductwork to identify the cause. A booster fan is the tempting shortcut, but it masks a crushed section instead of finding it.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Blocked or crushed","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014, Appendix","units":["dedp:app-still-blocked"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0196","stem":"Per DEDP 1.8, when and how is the actual length of the permanent exhaust duct determined?","options":["During cleaning, using measured rods or air whip tools","Before cleaning, from the dryer maker's length chart","After testing, from the anemometer velocity reading","At the hood, by adding 5 feet for each elbow found"],"expl":"DEDP 1.8 has the tech estimate developed length at inspection, then says the actual length shall be determined during cleaning using measured fiberglass cleaning rods, cable drives, or air whipping tools. Adding 5 feet per hard elbow is how developed length is estimated (1.8.3), not how actual length is measured.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Measuring length","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §1.8","units":["dedp:1.8-actual","dedp:def-actual"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0197","stem":"A tech needed nine 3-foot rods to reach the hood, and the run has two 4-inch radius mitered 90-degree elbows. Using DEDP's definitions, what is the actual length?","options":["27 feet, the nine rods with no elbow allowance","37 feet, the nine rods plus 5 feet for each elbow","32 feet, the nine rods plus 2.5 feet for each elbow","30 feet, the nine rods plus 1.5 feet for each elbow"],"expl":"DEDP defines actual length as the total measured length, here 9 x 3 = 27 feet; developed length adds fitting equivalents, 5 feet per 4-inch radius mitered 90 in the IMC/IRC table, giving 37 feet. Mixing the two is the trap, and 2.5 feet (mitered 45) and 1.5 feet (10-inch smooth 90) are other rows of that table, which belong in developed length, never in actual length.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Measuring length","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014, Definitions; Table 504.6.4.1","units":["dedp:def-actual","dedp:def-developed"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0198","stem":"The customer's dryer was connected with a crushed white vinyl flex duct. What should the tech reconnect it with?","options":["A single UL 2158A transition, 8 feet or less","The same vinyl, stretched to pull out the kinks","UL 2158A duct spliced from two 5-foot lengths","A UL 181 flexible air duct, 8 feet or less"],"expl":"The 2024 IRC (M1502.4.3) and IMC (504.9.3) require a single length listed and labeled to UL 2158A, not over 8 feet and not concealed. Splicing two listed pieces breaks the single-length rule, and UL 181 covers HVAC air ducts and connectors, not dryer transitions.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Reconnecting","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.3; IMC 2024 504.9.3","units":["dvt:plastic-transition"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0199","stem":"The customer wants the dryer pushed nearly flush to the wall, and every round transition tried so far kinks behind it. Which solution fits this problem?","options":["A listed slim periscope or a recessed wall box","A longer foil transition looped behind the dryer","A second foil transition added to ease the bend","An inline booster fan to make up the kinked loss"],"expl":"Slim adjustable periscope-style transitions are sold for low-clearance installs, and a recessed dryer vent box set in the wall cavity lets the dryer sit flush without crushing the hose; whatever is used must still be a single UL 2158A listed transition. A longer looped foil duct is the usual cause of crushing, not the cure.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Pushing dryer back","tier":0,"ref":"Manufacturer literature; IRC 2024 M1502.4.3","units":["dvt:periscope-box"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0200","stem":"When reconnecting a UL-listed foil-type transition duct, how does GE's installation guide say to size it?","options":["Extend it with the dryer in place and trim the excess","Leave the full 8-foot length coiled behind for moving","Cut it to the gap measured with the dryer pulled out","Compress it fully so it takes up the least floor space"],"expl":"GE says to clamp the duct to the dryer outlet, then with the dryer in its permanent position extend the duct to full length, allow about 2 inches of overlap on the exhaust pipe, cut off the excess, and keep it as straight as possible. Extra coiled length and fully compressed flex both trap lint and restrict flow.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Pushing dryer back","tier":0,"ref":"GE installation instructions, Exhausting the Dryer","units":["dvt:transition-trim"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0201","stem":"The tech separated a rigid dryer duct joint to clear a clog. Under the 2024 IRC, how must that joint be put back together?","options":["Sealed with 181B-FX tape and mechanically fastened","Sealed with cloth duct tape, no UL listing required","Sealed with 181A-P foil tape and fastened with long screws","Fastened with long sheet metal screws, not sealed"],"expl":"2024 IRC M1502.4.2 requires dryer duct joints to be sealed per M1601.4.1 and mechanically fastened, and M1601.4.1 requires tape on metallic ducts to comply with UL 181B and be marked 181B-FX. The 181A-P marking is for fibrous glass duct board tape, and unlisted cloth duct tape meets neither (Whirlpool also says do not use duct tape).","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Joint closures","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.2, M1601.4.1","units":["code:joint-seal","code:tape-181b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0202","stem":"While refastening that joint, the tech reaches for 3/4-inch self-drilling screws. What limit does the 2024 IRC put on fasteners inside a dryer duct?","options":["No more than 1/8 inch may protrude into the duct","No more than 1/4 inch may protrude into the duct","No more than 1/2 inch may protrude into the duct","Screws may protrude if the joint is also foil-taped"],"expl":"2024 IRC M1502.4.2 and IMC 504.9.2 bar screws or similar fasteners that protrude more than 1/8 inch into the duct, and GE and Whirlpool instructions go further and say no fasteners should extend inside at all, since the tips catch lint. Taping over a long screw does nothing about the tip inside.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Joint closures","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.2; IMC 2024 504.9.2","units":["dvt:clamps-not-screws"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0203","stem":"Reinstalling a rigid section he removed, which way should the tech point each crimped end?","options":["Downstream, away from the dryer and toward the exterior","Upstream, back toward the dryer connection","Upward on vertical runs, whatever the flow","Either way, if the joint is foil-taped"],"expl":"2024 IRC M1502.4.2 says the insert end extends into the adjoining duct in the direction of airflow, so crimped ends point away from the dryer; the LintEater and GE instructions say the same. Pointing crimps back toward the dryer leaves an edge facing the airflow that snags lint.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Reconnecting","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1502.4.2","units":["code:male-end"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0204","stem":"The DEDP appendix describes measuring discharge velocity at the termination with the dryer in its final installed position, running empty in non-heat mode. How does DEDP classify that measurement?","options":["Supplemental data that is not a requirement of the Standard","A required step before a pass or fail is recorded","Accepted in place of the test device on new dryers","Mandatory whenever a booster fan is in the system"],"expl":"The appendix says the velocity may be measured with the dryer operating empty in non-heat mode as supplemental data to the DEDP test, and that it is not a requirement of the Standard. The test itself shall never be run with the dryer blower (3.0.2), which is why replacing the test device is false.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Verifying airflow","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014, Appendix; §3.0.2","units":["dedp:app-appliance-optional"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0205","stem":"During service the tech finds an unlisted foil transition partly hidden inside the wall cavity. What does DEDP 1.9 require regarding the consumer?","options":["The consumer is told what type of transition fails code","The consumer is told of the transition only if a test fails","The consumer decides if the transition gets recorded","The consumer is referred to the AHJ for a transition ruling"],"expl":"DEDP 1.9 has the tech verify the transition complies with code or the AHJ and says the consumer must be informed what type of transition duct is not in compliance, whatever the test result. Letting the consumer decide whether to record it is the deferral trap; DEDP 4.4 also records the transition used to reconnect.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Non-compliant findings","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014 §1.9, §4.4","units":["dedp:1.9-inform","dedp:1.9-code-check"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0206","stem":"What makes a reverse-nozzle skipper line travel through a dryer duct on its own?","options":["Rear jets blasting air back toward the operator","Forward jets pulling the head toward open air","A spinning brush head threading itself along the wall","Suction at the far end drawing the hose along"],"expl":"Nikro describes its reverse air blast nozzle as blowing air back toward the operator so the lightweight hose propels itself through the duct away from the operator. Because the jets drive loosened lint back toward the entry end, the tech plans collection accordingly.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Air whips and skippers","tier":0,"ref":"Nikro compressed air tools catalog","units":["dvt:reverse-skipper"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0207","stem":"A tech is about to pull a 240-volt electric dryer away from the wall to reach the duct connection. Which step isolates it from power before he moves it?","options":["Unplug the dryer's power cord","Turn the dryer's control knob to off","Open the door so the drum cannot start","Set the dryer to a no-heat fluff cycle"],"expl":"The LintEater manual says to turn the dryer off and unplug it before pulling it out, and GE's instructions say to disconnect the power supply before servicing. A control knob or door switch leaves 240 volts at the cord and terminal block.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Electrical isolation","tier":0,"ref":"Gardus LintEater manual, Step 8; GE installation instructions","units":["dvt:unplug-dryer"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0208","stem":"After a gas dryer is pushed back into place, the tech wants to confirm the gas connections did not loosen. Which check matches GE's installation instructions?","options":["Test each joint with ammonia-free soap; watch for bubbles","Pass a lit lighter slowly around each joint; watch for a flicker","Sniff each fitting for a gas odor with the supply valve open","Spray ammonia glass cleaner on each joint; watch for foaming"],"expl":"GE says never use an open flame to test for gas leaks and to check connections with a soap solution that contains no ammonia, which can damage brass fittings, retightening and retesting if bubbles appear. Whirlpool likewise specifies an approved noncorrosive leak-detection solution.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Gas dryer precautions","tier":0,"ref":"GE installation instructions, Test for Leaks","units":["dvt:gas-leak-test"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0209","stem":"A homeowner disconnected the gas dryer's old flexible connector while repainting and asks the vent tech to screw it back on. What do GE's instructions call for?","options":["A new certified connector fitted by a qualified person","The old connector reused if it shows no visible kinks","The old connector reused with fresh thread sealant applied","The old connector reused after a soap-bubble leak test"],"expl":"GE's gas dryer instructions say to use a new CSA-approved flexible gas supply line and never reuse old flexible connectors, and gas installation is for a qualified installer. A soap test after reusing the old connector checks for leaks but does not cure the reuse prohibition.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Gas dryer precautions","tier":0,"ref":"GE installation instructions, Gas Requirements","units":["dvt:new-gas-connector"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0210","stem":"As the tech slides a gas dryer out, he smells gas strongly in the laundry room. Which response matches the gas-safety steps in GE's dryer manual?","options":["Touch no switches, clear the house, and call from next door","Unplug the dryer at once to remove any spark source","Open the windows and call the utility from the kitchen","Find the leak with soap solution before calling anyone"],"expl":"GE's 'What to do if you smell gas' steps: light nothing and turn on no appliance, touch no electrical switch and use no phone in the building, clear everyone out, and call the gas supplier from a neighbor's phone, or the fire department if the supplier cannot be reached. Unplugging the dryer is the tempting move, but pulling a plug can arc.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Gas dryer precautions","tier":0,"ref":"GE owner's manual, What to Do If You Smell Gas","units":["dvt:gas-smell"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0211","stem":"A gas dryer's vent was completely blocked by a nest, and the homeowner asks how to protect the family from combustion products that could have spilled indoors. What should the tech recommend, per NFPA?","options":["CO alarms outside the sleeping areas and on each level","One CO alarm mounted directly above the dryer itself","A smoke alarm in the laundry room right beside the dryer","A CO alarm in the kitchen right next to the gas range"],"expl":"NFPA says CO alarms belong in a central location outside each sleeping area and on every level of the home, interconnected for best protection, and CPSC does not recommend installing them in kitchens or above fuel-burning appliances. The IMC requires dryer exhaust to carry any products of combustion outdoors, which a blocked vent defeats.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"CO awareness","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA CO safety tips; CPSC CO Q&A","units":["dvt:co-alarms"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0212","stem":"A tech sets an extension ladder against the eave to step onto a low roof and reach a roof dryer termination. Per OSHA, how far must the side rails extend?","options":["At least 3 feet above the upper landing","At least 1 foot above the roof edge","At least 24 inches above the upper landing","At least 4 feet above the upper landing"],"expl":"OSHA 1910.23(c)(11) and 1926.1053(b)(1) require portable ladders used to reach an upper landing to extend at least 3 feet above it. Four is the number in the 4-to-1 setup ratio, the usual mix-up.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Ladder safety","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1910.23(c)(11); 1926.1053(b)(1)","units":["dvt:ladder-3ft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0213","stem":"The extension ladder's working length, from its feet to the top support point, is 16 feet. How far from the wall should the tech set the feet?","options":["4 feet out, one quarter of the working length","2 feet out, an eighth of the working length","3 feet out, the same as the required top extension","8 feet out, one half of the ladder's working length"],"expl":"OSHA 1926.1053(b)(5)(i) and the OSHA ladder QuickCard put the base about one-quarter of the working length from the wall, so 16 feet puts the feet 4 feet out. Two feet is the one-eighth ratio OSHA allows only for job-made wood ladders with spliced rails. Mnemonic: 4 up, 1 out.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Ladder safety","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1926.1053(b)(5)(i)","units":["dvt:ladder-4to1"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0214","stem":"The only way to a soffit termination puts the ladder near an energized overhead power line of 50 kV or less. Which ladder rule applies to an unqualified worker?","options":["Nonconductive side rails, with ten feet of clearance","Aluminum rails, with three feet of clearance","Aluminum rails, grounded to a water pipe first","Nonconductive rails, with three feet of clearance"],"expl":"OSHA 1910.333(c)(7) requires nonconductive side rails where the worker or ladder could contact energized parts, and 1910.333(c)(3) keeps an unqualified worker and any conductive object at least 10 feet from lines of 50 kV or less. Three feet is the ladder-extension figure, not a power-line clearance.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Ladder safety","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1910.333(c)(3), (c)(7)","units":["dvt:powerline-10ft"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0215","stem":"Under OSHA's general industry walking-working surfaces rule, 29 CFR 1910.28, at what height does an unprotected roof edge require fall protection for a vent tech?","options":["4 feet or more above a lower level","6 feet or more above the lower level","10 feet or more above the level below","15 feet or more above the lower level"],"expl":"1910.28(b)(1) requires guardrails, safety nets, or personal fall protection at unprotected sides and edges 4 feet or more above a lower level. Six feet is the construction trigger in 1926.501(b)(1) and (b)(13), and 15 feet is the low-slope roof distance-from-edge threshold in 1910.28(b)(13). Mnemonic: general industry 4, construction 6.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Roof and fall protection","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1910.28(b)(1)","units":["dvt:fall-triggers"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0216","stem":"A tech must clear a large nest caked with bird droppings from a termination. Which practice follows CDC/NIOSH guidance on Histoplasma?","options":["Wet it with a water spray first and wear a respirator","Brush it out dry and fast to limit time spent nearby","Blow it clear with compressed air, standing upwind","Sweep it up dry while wearing a disposable surgical mask"],"expl":"CDC/NIOSH guidance says to spray dusty material to cut aerosolized spores, avoid shoveling or sweeping dry material, and use respirators (half-facepiece or filtering facepiece for medium-risk work, PAPR or full facepiece with 100-series filters for high-risk work) plus goggles. Histoplasmosis spreads by breathing spores, and a surgical mask is not a respirator.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"PPE and droppings","tier":0,"ref":"CDC/NIOSH Histoplasma prevention","units":["dvt:histo-wet-down"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0217","stem":"One tech brushes from the termination while the dryer's own blower pushes the loosened lint out toward him. What PPE does the LintEater manual say he must wear?","options":["Safety eyewear and a respirator","Hearing protection and nitrile gloves","A hard hat and cut-resistant gloves","Knee pads and a high-visibility vest"],"expl":"Gardus states that eye protection and a respirator must be worn when the dryer or a blower pushes loosened lint out toward the operator, and OSHA 1910.133 requires eye protection with side protection against flying particles. Gloves and hearing protection are reasonable extras, but they are not what the manual requires here.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"PPE and droppings","tier":0,"ref":"Gardus LintEater manual, Additional Methods 1-2; OSHA 1910.133","units":["dvt:linteater-ppe"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0218","stem":"A customer asks how often to clean the dryer's lint filter. What does NFPA's clothes dryer safety tip sheet say?","options":["Before or after each load of laundry you run","Once a day on days the dryer is used","Once a week, when the screen slot is also cleaned","Wash it with soap and water each month"],"expl":"NFPA's tip sheet says to clean the lint filter before or after each load of laundry and to remove lint collected around the drum; USFA's flyer says before and after each cycle. Weekly cleaning of the screen slot and periodic washing are the LintEater maker's extra steps, not a substitute for every-load cleaning.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Customer education","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA Clothes Dryer Safety tip sheet","units":["dvt:nfpa-lint-filter"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0219","stem":"A customer mentions starting the dryer on a long cycle right before bed so the laundry is done by morning. What does NFPA advise?","options":["Switch it off when leaving home or going to sleep","Use the low heat setting when drying overnight","Choose a cool-down cycle whenever drying overnight","Run it overnight if the lint filter was just cleaned"],"expl":"NFPA's dryer tip sheet says to turn the dryer off if you leave home or when you go to bed. Low heat and a cool-down period are CPSC's advice for clothes soiled with volatile chemicals, not permission to run the dryer unattended.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Customer education","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA Clothes Dryer Safety tip sheet","units":["dvt:nfpa-off-at-night"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0220","stem":"A customer plans to machine-dry shop rags that were used to wipe up gasoline and paint thinner. What does NFPA's dryer tip sheet recommend?","options":["Lay them outside to air-dry, then wash and dry as usual","Dry them on high heat to drive off the solvent fast","Dry them in a small load with the lint filter out","Wash them once in cold water, then dry them on high"],"expl":"NFPA says clothes that contacted flammable substances like gasoline or paint thinner should be laid outside to dry, then washed and dried as usual; CPSC adds washing more than once and using low heat with a cool-down if a dryer is used. A single cold wash followed by high heat is the tempting half-measure.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Customer education","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA Clothes Dryer Safety tip sheet; CPSC Pub 5022","units":["dvt:nfpa-solvent-clothes"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0221","stem":"A customer asks how often the dryer vent should be cleaned. Which answer correctly quotes NFPA's dryer safety tip sheet?","options":["Once a year, or sooner if drying starts taking longer","Three to four times a year, whatever the drying time is","Every two years, unless lint is seen at the hood","Every six months, or sooner if the hood flap sticks open"],"expl":"NFPA says once a year, or more often if clothes take longer than normal to dry, clean lint out of the vent pipe or have a dryer lint removal service do it; GE's owner manual also says at least once a year. Three to four times a year is the LintEater maker's recommendation, so always attribute an interval to its source.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Service interval","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA Clothes Dryer Safety tip sheet","units":["dvt:nfpa-vent-interval"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0222","stem":"In a tight laundry closet, cardboard boxes, cleaning supplies, and a pile of clothes sit against the dryer. What does NFPA's tip sheet tell the customer?","options":["Keep the area around the dryer clear of things that burn","Store them at least 3 feet from the dryer's front door","Store them on a shelf mounted above the dryer cabinet","Keep them clear of the exhaust hood, since the heat leaves there"],"expl":"NFPA says to keep the area around the dryer clear of things that can burn, like boxes, cleaning supplies, and clothing. The 3-foot figure belongs to the IRC's default termination clearance from building openings, not to storage.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Customer education","tier":0,"ref":"NFPA Clothes Dryer Safety tip sheet","units":["dvt:nfpa-clear-area"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0223","stem":"The tech found a crushed transition behind the dryer, replaced it with a listed one, and cleared a nest from the hood. Which record best documents the job?","options":["Dated before and after photos and the duct type used","A signed customer note saying the vent works","One after photo of the clean hood from the yard","A verbal summary given to the customer at checkout"],"expl":"NADCA's ACR 2025 5.6.3 recommends photo images in post-project documentation, and DEDP 4.4 has the tech record the type of transition duct used to reconnect the dryer. An after-only photo cannot show the condition that justified the replacement.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Documentation","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA ACR 2025 §5.6.3; DEDP 2014 §4.4","units":["dvt:as-found-photos"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0224","stem":"The customer refuses to pay for a new transition, tells the tech to reconnect the old white vinyl duct, and offers to sign a waiver. Given GE's venting rules and the 2024 IRC, what should the tech do?","options":["Decline to reinstall the vinyl and write up the refusal","Reinstall the vinyl once the waiver is signed and on file","Reinstall the vinyl but stretch it out to reduce kinks","Reinstall the vinyl and schedule a recheck in 6 months"],"expl":"GE's instructions prohibit any plastic in the dryer venting, including the transition, and say to discard existing plastic or foil duct. The 2024 IRC requires a UL 2158A listed transition, and CPSC says to replace plastic or foil accordion ducting. A waiver does not make a prohibited material acceptable, which is the deferral trap. DEDP 4.4 only says to record whichever transition is used. It does not make a plastic one acceptable.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Liability","tier":0,"ref":"GE installation instructions; IRC 2024 M1502.4.3","units":["dvt:plastic-transition","dvt:decline-vinyl"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0225","stem":"After the duct is cleaned and passes testing, the customer says the dryer still takes two cycles to dry and asks the vent tech, who has no appliance training, to replace the heating element. What is the correct response?","options":["Refer the dryer to a qualified appliance service provider for evaluation","Replace the heating element since the duct has already passed","Install an inline booster fan to shorten the long drying time","Reclean the duct and retest until the drying time improves"],"expl":"The DEDP appendix says that when the appliance appears to be the problem, the dryer should be evaluated by a qualified service provider, and GE's manual refers servicing beyond user care to qualified service personnel. Recleaning a duct that already passed treats the wrong component.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Liability","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014, Appendix; GE owner's manual","units":["dvt:refer-appliance"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0226","stem":"A vent company owner is building his first written safety and health program on OSHA's Recommended Practices. Which trio names core elements of that framework?","options":["Management leadership, worker participation, and hazard identification and assessment","Container labels, safety data sheets, and employee chemical training","Respirator selection, medical evaluations, and fit-testing procedures","Emergency reporting procedures, evacuation routes, and employee head counts"],"expl":"OSHA's Recommended Practices for Safety and Health Programs are built on seven core elements, beginning with management leadership, worker participation, and hazard identification and assessment. The other trios are real, but each is the required content of a narrower written program: HazCom (1910.1200(e)), respiratory protection (1910.134(c)), and an emergency action plan (1910.38(c)).","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Safety plan and policy","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 3885 Recommended Practices (2016)","units":["dvt:osha-core-elements"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0227","stem":"A vent company adds two staffing-agency temps to its crew for a 200-unit apartment job. Under OSHA's Recommended Practices, what protection should the temps receive?","options":["The same level of protection the company gives its own techs","The level of protection set by the staffing agency's program","The level of protection set by the property owner's site rules","The level of protection written into each temp's assignment"],"expl":"OSHA's communication and coordination element says host employers, contractors, and staffing agencies commit to providing the same level of safety and health protection to all workers. Deferring to the agency's program, the owner's rules, or the assignment paperwork is the trap; those parties coordinate who handles which part of the program, but the level of protection does not drop.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Safety plan and policy","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 3885, Communication and Coordination","units":["dvt:osha-temps-equal"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0228","stem":"After reviewing typical attic, crawlspace, and rooftop dryer vent jobs, an owner picks the PPE each task needs. How must he verify under 1910.132(d) that this hazard assessment was done?","options":["A dated, written certification naming the workplace evaluated and certifier","A written training record with each tech's name, the date, and a signature","A written program covering selection, fit testing, and medical evaluation","A written inventory of hazardous products, cross-referenced to each SDS"],"expl":"1910.132(d)(2) requires a written certification that identifies the workplace evaluated, the person certifying, the date(s), and that the document is a certification of hazard assessment. The name, date, and signature record is the 1926.503(b) fall-training certification, selection, fit testing, and medical evaluation make up a 1910.134 respirator program, and the chemical inventory keyed to SDS is part of a HazCom program.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Safety plan and policy","tier":1,"ref":"OSHA 1910.132(d)(2)","units":["dvt:ppe-hazard-cert"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0229","stem":"A vent company's written fall protection procedure covers harness inspection and anchor choice for steep-roof terminations. What else do OSHA's personal fall protection rules require the employer to provide for?","options":["Prompt rescue of a tech left suspended after a fall","A safety monitor who watches each tech on the roof","A site-specific fall plan written by a qualified person","A warning line set 6 feet back from each roof edge"],"expl":"Both 1910.140(c)(21) and 1926.502(d)(20) require the employer to provide for prompt rescue after a fall, because a worker hanging in a harness is still in danger. Safety monitors and warning lines are options for roofing work on low-slope roofs under 1926.501(b)(10), and a qualified person's fall protection plan (1926.502(k)) is only an alternative where conventional protection is infeasible.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Safety plan and policy","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1910.140(c)(21); 1926.502(d)(20)","units":["dvt:prompt-rescue"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0230","stem":"A tech will scrape dry pigeon droppings out of a roof cap while wearing a half-mask air-purifying respirator. Under 1910.134's selection rules, what must the respirator be equipped with for this hazard?","options":["A NIOSH-certified particulate filter like a P100 or an N95","An organic vapor cartridge replaced on a written change schedule","An organic vapor cartridge with an end-of-service-life indicator","A NIOSH-certified sorbent cartridge rated for the disinfectant's vapors"],"expl":"Droppings dust and fungal spores are particulates, and 1910.134(d)(3)(iv) calls for a filter NIOSH certifies for particulates (N, R, or P series, or HEPA). A change schedule or an ESLI is how 1910.134(d)(3)(iii) manages gas and vapor cartridges, which are not particulate filters. Match the element to the physical form: dust needs a filter, vapor needs a sorbent.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Respiratory protection","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1910.134(d)(3)(iii)-(iv)","units":["dvt:resp-particulate"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0231","stem":"No hazard assessment calls for respirators during routine lint brushing, but one tech asks to wear his own N95 filtering facepiece. If the employer allows it, what does 1910.134 require?","options":["Give him the Appendix D information for voluntary respirator users","Provide a medical evaluation before he first wears the N95 on a job","Fit test him on that N95 model before first use and then annually","Enroll him in the written program, including annual respirator training"],"expl":"Under 1910.134(c)(2), voluntary use requires giving the user the Appendix D advisory information, and employees whose only respirator use is a voluntary filtering facepiece (dust mask) are exempt from the written program. Medical evaluation, annual fit testing, and program training attach to required use; a voluntary elastomeric half-mask would add a medical evaluation plus cleaning and storage procedures.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Respiratory protection","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1910.134(c)(2)","units":["dvt:resp-voluntary"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0232","stem":"A vent company's policy requires P100 half-masks for all nest removal, though no air sampling has ever been done. Does 1910.134's written respiratory protection program apply?","options":["Yes; an employer rule requiring them is enough on its own","No; not until sampling shows an exposure over a legal limit","No; an employer rule without exposure data counts as voluntary","Yes; fit testing can wait until sampling shows an overexposure"],"expl":"1910.134(c)(1) applies wherever respirators are necessary to protect health or whenever the employer requires them, so a company rule by itself triggers the full program: selection, medical evaluation, fit testing, training, and maintenance. Sampling is one way to show necessity, not a precondition for the program or any part of it; fit testing comes before first use under 1910.134(f)(2), not after an overexposure is measured.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Respiratory protection","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1910.134(c)(1)","units":["dvt:resp-program-trigger"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0233","stem":"Wet, moldy lint is caked in a sagging duct section in a crawlspace, and the tech will cut the section out and bag it. Which eye protection does EPA's mold remediation guide accept?","options":["Fitted goggles designed to keep out dust and small particles","Safety glasses with side shields, as used for flying debris","Goggles with open vent holes that keep the lenses from fogging","A half-face HEPA respirator, which also covers the eye area"],"expl":"EPA's Mold Remediation in Schools and Commercial Buildings says to use properly fitted goggles designed to keep out dust and small particles, or a full-face respirator with HEPA filters, and that safety glasses or goggles with open vent holes are not acceptable. Side-shield glasses satisfy 1910.133 for flying particles, not for mold dust, and EPA notes half-face respirators give no eye protection.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"PPE selection","tier":0,"ref":"EPA 402-K-01-001, Personal Protective Equipment","units":["dvt:epa-mold-goggles"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0234","stem":"A tech will clear a mouse nest out of a transition duct and then refit sharp-edged rigid metal duct. Which glove plan fits CDC's rodent cleanup guidance and 1910.138?","options":["Rubber or plastic gloves on the nest, cut-resistant work gloves on the duct","Leather work gloves for the nest, disposable nitrile ones for the duct","Disposable nitrile gloves for both jobs, doubled up for the duct edges","Cut-resistant gloves for both jobs, rinsed in bleach after the nest"],"expl":"CDC says to wear rubber or plastic gloves (rubber, latex, or vinyl for heavy infestations) to clean up rodent droppings and nests, and 1910.138 requires hand protection selected for the hazard, such as cut-resistant gloves against severe cuts from sheet metal edges. Each wrong plan puts one task in the wrong glove: thin disposable nitrile is not cut protection for a metal edge, and leather or knit cut-resistant gloves are not the rubber or plastic gloves CDC names for droppings.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"PPE selection","tier":0,"ref":"CDC rodent clean-up; OSHA 1910.138","units":["dvt:glove-plan","dvt:rodent-heavy"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0235","stem":"A tech finishes clearing a bird nest and caked droppings from a soffit cap while wearing disposable coveralls and shoe covers. What does NIOSH recommend he do with them?","options":["Remove and seal them in heavy-duty plastic bags for disposal","Spray them with bleach, let them soak for 5 minutes, and reuse them","Seal them in a plastic bag and launder them with the shop's rags","Keep them on for the next unit, then change at the end of the workday"],"expl":"NIOSH's Histoplasma guidance says to remove all protective clothing and shoe coverings when the work is done and seal them in heavy-duty plastic bags for disposal, so spores do not ride to the truck, the next unit, or home. The 5-minute bleach soak is CDC's method for rodent droppings, not a way to reuse disposable gear.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Exhaust contaminants","tier":0,"ref":"NIOSH Histoplasmosis PPE guidance","units":["dvt:bag-disposables"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0236","stem":"A long-vacant rental has a heavy mouse infestation, with nests packed into the dryer cabinet base and the transition duct. Which respirator does CDC's special-precautions guidance list for this cleanup?","options":["A half-mask air-purifying respirator fitted with HEPA filters","A disposable N95 filtering facepiece, worn with safety goggles","A half-mask air-purifying respirator with organic vapor cartridges","A powered air-purifying respirator with organic vapor cartridges"],"expl":"For heavy infestations and vacant dwellings with many rodents, CDC's special precautions call for coveralls, rubber boots or shoe covers, rubber, latex, or vinyl gloves, goggles, and a half-mask air-purifying respirator with HEPA filters (a PAPR with HEPA filters also works). Organic vapor cartridges target chemical vapors, not virus-carrying particles, and a disposable N95 is not the HEPA filter CDC names. CDC also says never to sweep or vacuum the droppings.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Exhaust contaminants","tier":0,"ref":"CDC rodent clean-up, Special Precautions","units":["dvt:rodent-heavy"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0237","stem":"Ten days after clearing a nest of dried bird droppings from a wall cap, a tech has a fever, dry cough, headache, and muscle aches. His doctor suspects a bacterial infection carried by birds. Which disease fits?","options":["Psittacosis, caused by Chlamydia psittaci","Histoplasmosis, caused by Histoplasma","Cryptococcosis, caused by Cryptococcus","Legionnaires' disease, caused by Legionella"],"expl":"CDC describes psittacosis as a bacterial infection (Chlamydia psittaci) caught by breathing dust from dried bird secretions or droppings, with fever, chills, headache, muscle aches, and dry cough 5 to 14 days later, treated with antibiotics. Histoplasmosis and cryptococcosis also come from droppings but are fungal, and Legionnaires' disease is bacterial with the same flu-like signs but comes from breathing mist from water systems, not from birds. Mnemonic: psittacosis is the bird disease antibiotics cure.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Exhaust contaminants","tier":0,"ref":"CDC psittacosis; CDC histoplasmosis; CDC cryptococcosis","units":["dvt:fungal-droppings","dvt:psittacosis"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0238","stem":"Which two diseases can a tech catch by breathing fungal spores stirred up from bird droppings in a dryer vent termination?","options":["Histoplasmosis and cryptococcosis","Psittacosis and histoplasmosis","Cryptococcosis and hantavirus","Psittacosis and hantavirus"],"expl":"CDC lists Histoplasma (soil mixed with bird or bat droppings) and Cryptococcus (soil, bird dung, decaying wood) as fungi that infect people through inhaled spores. Psittacosis is bacterial and hantavirus is a virus shed by rodents. Mnemonic: the two fungi in bird dung are H and C.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Exhaust contaminants","tier":0,"ref":"CDC histoplasmosis; CDC cryptococcosis","units":["dvt:fungal-droppings"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0239","stem":"While running a gas dryer to check airflow in a closed laundry closet, a tech develops a headache, dizziness, and nausea. What does NIOSH say to do?","options":["Turn off the dryer, get to fresh air, and seek medical help","Put on an N95, open the door for fresh air, and finish","Rest in the shade, sip cool water, and treat it as heat exhaustion","Switch the dryer to no-heat, open the door, and finish the test"],"expl":"Headache, dizziness, and nausea near a running gas appliance point to carbon monoxide, and NIOSH says to turn off the equipment, get to uncontaminated air, and call 911 or seek medical help. CO is a colorless, odorless gas that particulate filters do not remove, and heat exhaustion shares the same early signs, which is why the flu-like symptoms are easy to misread.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Exhaust contaminants","tier":0,"ref":"NIOSH carbon monoxide hazards at work","units":["dvt:co-symptoms"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0240","stem":"A new tech says the debris he pulls from dryer ducts is only a clogging nuisance. In NFPA's 2014-2018 estimates of home dryer fires, which item first ignited accounted for all of the civilian deaths?","options":["Lint and other dust or fiber","Clothing being dried in the drum","The appliance housing or casing","Electrical wire or cable insulation"],"expl":"NFPA's Home Dryer Fires report (June 2020) shows dust, fiber, or lint as the item first ignited in 27% of home dryer fires and in all 7 average annual civilian deaths. Clothing is a close second in fires (25%) and leads in injuries (30%), which makes it the tempting trap.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Exhaust contaminants","tier":1,"ref":"NFPA Home Dryer Fires (2020), Table 2","units":["dvt:nfpa-lint-deaths"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0241","stem":"A new-hire handout lists OSHA's fall protection trigger heights for unprotected sides and edges. Which pair is correct?","options":["4 feet under 1910.28, 6 feet under 1926.501","6 feet under 1910.28, 4 feet under 1926.501","4 feet under 1910.28, 10 feet under 1926.501","6 feet under both 1910.28 and 1926.501"],"expl":"General industry, 1910.28(b)(1), protects unprotected sides and edges at 4 feet or more above a lower level; construction, 1926.501(b)(1), at 6 feet or more. Ten feet is the construction trigger for workers on scaffolds (1926.451(g)(1)). Mnemonic: 4 on the shop floor, 6 on the construction site.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Fall protection triggers","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1910.28(b)(1); 1926.501(b)(1)","units":["dvt:fall-triggers"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0242","stem":"A roof dryer cap sits 4 feet from a plastic skylight dome on a low-slope roof, with the floor 18 feet below. How do OSHA's fall rules treat the skylight?","options":["As a hole, needing a cover, guardrail, or personal fall arrest system","As an edge, needing a warning line, a safety monitor, or both","As an edge, needing a warning line at least 6 feet from it","As a hole, needing flagging tape, cones, or a posted sign"],"expl":"Both 1910.28(b)(3) and 1926.501(b)(4) name skylights as holes and require covers, guardrails, or personal fall arrest (1910.28 also allows travel restraint). Warning lines and safety monitors are low-slope roofing options for roof edges under 1926.501(b)(10), and tape around a dome does not stop a fall through it.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Rooftop fall hazards","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1910.28(b)(3); 1926.501(b)(4)","units":["dvt:skylight-hole"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0243","stem":"A crew ropes off a work area around a roof dryer cap on a flat commercial roof with a rope warning line. How high must the line's lowest point, including sag, be above the roof surface?","options":["Between 34 and 39 inches","42 inches, plus or minus 3 inches","Between 30 and 38 inches","At least 42 inches"],"expl":"Under both 1910.29(d)(2)(ii) and 1926.502(f)(2)(ii), a warning line's lowest point, sag included, is no lower than 34 inches and no higher than 39 inches. 42 inches plus or minus 3 is the guardrail top-rail height (1910.29(b)(1)), 30 to 38 inches is the stair handrail range (1910.29(f)(1)(i)), and at least 42 inches is the top-rail height for stair rail systems installed on or after January 17, 2017 (1910.29(f)(1)(ii)(B)). Mnemonic: warning line 34 to 39, guardrail 42 plus or minus 3.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Rooftop fall hazards","tier":1,"ref":"OSHA 1910.29(d)(2)(ii); 1926.502(f)(2)(ii)","units":["dvt:warning-line"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0244","stem":"On a steep roof, a tech clips his shock-absorbing lanyard to a body belt left over from older gear. What do OSHA's rules say?","options":["Not acceptable; personal fall arrest calls for a full-body harness","Acceptable if free fall is limited to 6 feet or less","Acceptable when the anchorage is rated for 5,000 pounds","Not acceptable on steep roofs, but allowed on low-slope ones"],"expl":"Body belts have not been acceptable in personal fall arrest systems since January 1, 1998 under 1926.502(d), and 1910.140(d)(3) prohibits them outright; a belt may still be used in a positioning system. The 6-foot free fall limit and 5,000-pound anchorage are real PFAS criteria, but they do not make a belt acceptable, and the rule does not change with roof slope.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Fall arrest systems","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1926.502(d); 1910.140(d)(3)","units":["dvt:no-body-belt"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0245","stem":"A tech's harness and shock-absorbing lanyard arrest a short slide off a roof edge. He is unhurt and wants to finish the job. What must happen to that gear?","options":["Pull it from service until a competent person clears it","Keep using it if his own visual check finds no damage","Keep using it until the next start-of-shift gear inspection","Swap out the lanyard and keep the harness in service"],"expl":"Under 1910.140(c)(17) and 1926.502(d)(19), fall protection gear that has taken an impact load is removed from service immediately and stays out until a competent person inspects it and finds it undamaged and safe. The user's routine check (1910.140(c)(18), 1926.502(d)(21)) is for everyday wear, not for gear that just stopped a fall, and the harness took the same load as the lanyard.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Fall arrest systems","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1910.140(c)(17); 1926.502(d)(19)","units":["dvt:impact-gear"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0246","stem":"Before a steep-roof termination job, a tech installs a temporary roof anchor for his fall arrest lanyard. Unless a qualified person designs the system, what is the minimum load the anchorage must be able to support?","options":["5,000 pounds for each worker attached","3,000 pounds for each worker attached","1,800 pounds for each harness attached","900 pounds for each worker attached"],"expl":"1910.140(c)(13) and 1926.502(d)(15) require fall arrest anchorages to hold at least 5,000 pounds per attached worker, or to be designed by a qualified person with a safety factor of at least two. 3,000 pounds is the positioning-device anchorage minimum, and 1,800 and 900 pounds are the maximum arresting forces on the body with a harness and with a body belt (1926.502(d)(16)). Mnemonic: 5,000 to hold, 1,800 to feel.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Fall arrest systems","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1910.140(c)(13); 1926.502(d)(15)","units":["dvt:anchor-5000"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0247","stem":"At the start of the day, a tech finds a cracked side rail on the crew's aluminum extension ladder. What do OSHA's ladder rules require?","options":["Tag it 'Do Not Use' and remove it from service until it is repaired","Splint the cracked rail with a clamp and limit it to light loads","Use it for low work until the competent person's periodic inspection","Take off the fly section and use the base alone as a straight ladder"],"expl":"1910.23(b)(10) says a ladder with structural defects is immediately tagged 'Dangerous: Do Not Use' or similar and removed from service until repaired or replaced, and 1926.1053(b)(16) allows marking it defective or tagging it 'Do Not Use'. Field splints and light-duty use are how defective ladders hurt people, and the competent person's periodic inspection under 1926.1053(b)(15) does not license using a known-defective ladder meanwhile.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Ladder safety","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1910.23(b)(10); 1926.1053(b)(16)","units":["dvt:ladder-defect"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0248","stem":"A tech needs a drill and a bundle of 3-foot brush rods at a roof cap reached by an extension ladder. Which climbing method follows OSHA's portable ladder guidance?","options":["Climb with both hands free, keeping 3 points of contact","Keep one hand on the rail and carry the rods in his other hand","Tuck the rods under one arm and grip the rail with his free hand","Have a helper foot the ladder so he can climb holding the tool bag"],"expl":"OSHA's Portable Ladder Safety QuickCard says to always keep 3-point contact (two hands and a foot, or two feet and a hand) and face the ladder, and 1910.23(b)(13) and 1926.1053(b)(22) bar carrying any load that could make the climber lose balance. The one-hand minimum in 1910.23(b)(12) is the floor half-trained techs quote; with rods in the other hand, 3-point contact is gone.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Ladder safety","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA Portable Ladder Safety QuickCard; 1910.23(b)(13)","units":["dvt:ladder-3point"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0249","stem":"On a frosty, gusty morning, a tech must set an extension ladder on a smooth concrete patio glazed with ice to reach a wall cap. What does OSHA require?","options":["Lash it or have it held; slip-resistant feet are not enough","Set the base out 1/8 of the working length so the feet grip better","Set its feet on plywood sheeting spread across the icy concrete","Lean it at a flatter angle so more of each rail bears on the patio"],"expl":"1910.23(c)(9) requires portable ladders on slippery surfaces to be secured and stabilized, and 1926.1053(b)(7) says slip-resistant feet are no substitute for care in placing, lashing, or holding a ladder on slick concrete. The 1/8 setback applies only to wood job-made ladders with spliced rails; a standard ladder stays at 1/4, and neither a flatter lean nor a board laid on the ice secures the ladder.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Ladder safety","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1910.23(c)(9); 1926.1053(b)(7)","units":["dvt:ladder-4to1","dvt:ladder-slippery"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0250","stem":"On a humid afternoon, a tech working a roof cap is sweating heavily and has a headache, nausea, and dizziness, but he is alert and speaking clearly. What does NIOSH name this illness, and what first aid does it list?","options":["Heat exhaustion; cool him, give him fluids, and have him checked out at a clinic","Heat stroke; call 911, then cool him fast in a cold water or ice bath","Heat cramps; give him salt tablets and water, then let him keep working","Heat syncope; have him sit in a cool spot and slowly sip a sports drink"],"expl":"NIOSH lists headache, nausea, dizziness, weakness, irritability, thirst, heavy sweating, and decreased urine output as heat exhaustion, with first aid of removing the worker from the heat, giving liquids, cooling him, and taking him to a clinic or emergency room for evaluation. Confusion, slurred speech, or loss of consciousness would make it heat stroke and a 911 call; NIOSH says to avoid salt tablets for cramps.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Heat illness","tier":0,"ref":"NIOSH heat-related illnesses","units":["dvt:heat-exhaustion"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0251","stem":"A new hire with no hot-weather work history joins a crew doing roof terminations in July. What caps on his heat work does NIOSH's acclimatization schedule set?","options":["No more than 20% of a normal workday on day 1, then up to 20% more daily","No more than 50% on day 1, then 60%, 80%, and 100% on days 2 through 4","A full day from the start, with a cup of water every 15 to 20 minutes","No more than 50% on day 1, then up to 20% more on each day after that"],"expl":"NIOSH's schedule for new workers is no more than 20% of the usual duration of heat work on day 1 and no more than a 20% increase on each additional day. The 50-60-80-100 ramp is NIOSH's schedule for workers with previous experience, and a cup every 15 to 20 minutes is NIOSH's hydration advice, not a substitute for acclimatization.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Heat illness","tier":0,"ref":"NIOSH heat stress recommendations","units":["dvt:acclimatize"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0252","stem":"A tech on a roof hears distant thunder, though the sky overhead is still sunny and dry. Under the National Weather Service's lightning safety guidance, what should he do?","options":["Get down and go indoors now; he is likely within striking distance","Crouch low beside the roof cap until the storm has moved past","Keep working until rain arrives, since lightning strikes under the cloud","Lay down the aluminum ladder and rods, since metal attracts lightning"],"expl":"NWS says that if you hear thunder you are likely within striking distance, 'When Thunder Roars, Go Indoors,' and wait 30 minutes after the last lightning or thunder before going back out. NWS lists the outdoor crouch as a myth that makes no one safer, bolts can strike 10 to 15 miles from the storm, and metal conducts lightning but does not attract it.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Lightning safety","tier":0,"ref":"NWS Lightning Safety","units":["dvt:lightning"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0253","stem":"A tech runs a 50-foot extension cord from a garage receptacle that has no GFCI protection to power his vacuum and drill. Under the note to 1910.304(b)(3)(ii)(A) on GFCI cord sets, where should his portable GFCI connect?","options":["At the wall outlet, so it protects the entire cord run","At the far end of the cord, where the two tools plug in","On the vacuum's plug, since the vacuum draws the most current","On the drill's plug, since the drill is the tool held in hand"],"expl":"1910.304(b)(3)(ii) treats the cord connector on an extension cord used for temporary power as a receptacle outlet needing GFCI protection, and its note accepts a GFCI cord set or device plugged into the receptacle closest to the power source, which protects the whole cord. A GFCI at the far end or on one tool leaves the cord, and the other tool, unprotected. 1926.404(b)(1)(ii) likewise requires GFCI protection on temporary 120-volt 15- and 20-amp outlets.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Electrical safety","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 1910.304(b)(3)(ii); 1926.404(b)(1)(ii)","units":["dvt:gfci-at-outlet"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0254","stem":"After cleaning, a gas dryer's 120-volt cord no longer reaches the outlet in its new position, and the homeowner hands the tech an extension cord. What do GE's installation instructions say?","options":["Decline it; the plug goes into a grounded 3-prong outlet","Use it, if it is a three-wire cord rated for hard or extra-hard use","Use it, provided it is plugged into a GFCI-protected receptacle","Use it for now, and note the outlet problem on the invoice"],"expl":"GE's gas dryer electrical warning says to plug into a grounded 3-prong outlet, not remove the ground prong, and not use an adapter or an extension cord; a wrong outlet is for a licensed electrician to replace. Hard-usage three-wire cords and GFCI protection are OSHA rules for the tech's own temporary tool power, not permission to extend an appliance cord.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Electrical safety","tier":0,"ref":"GE Dryer Installation Instructions 49-3000206","units":["dvt:no-extension-cord"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0255","stem":"After a qualified installer reconnects a gas dryer, the soap solution bubbles at one joint. What do GE's installation instructions call for next?","options":["Close the valve, retighten the joint, and repeat the soap test","Keep the gas on, retighten, and test the joint with a lighter flame","Close the valve, retighten, and retest with ammonia cleaner","Keep the gas on, retighten, and recheck with soap in an hour"],"expl":"GE says that if leaks are found, close the valve, retighten the joint, and repeat the soap test; never use an open flame, and the test solution must not contain ammonia, which can damage brass fittings. Each wrong option keeps one right step and adds the shortcut a rushed tech takes.","domain":"Technician Safety","sub":"Gas dryer handling","tier":0,"ref":"GE Dryer Installation Instructions 49-3000206","units":["dvt:gas-leak-test"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0256","stem":"A two-tech crew cleans dryer vents at five or six houses a day and carries a degreaser, a disinfectant, and a roof sealant on the van. Which statement about the safety data sheets matches OSHA 1910.1200?","options":["They may stay at the shop if workers get the data at once in an emergency","They may stay at the shop if the crew gets copies within 15 working days","They must ride in the van, together with the company's written HazCom program","They can be skipped, as long as each product was bought at a retail store"],"expl":"When workers travel between job sites during a shift, OSHA 1910.1200(g)(9) lets the SDSs stay at the primary facility if the crew can get the information immediately in an emergency; 15 working days is the 1910.1020 records-access window, and a retail purchase does not exempt a product used at professional frequency. The written program may stay at the shop too, under (e)(5). NADCA's HVAC standard is stricter on HVAC projects, keeping SDSs on site for the whole job (ACR 2.7.1), so carrying them in the van is the safer habit.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Hazard communication","tier":1,"ref":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.1200(g)(9), (e)(5)","units":["dvt:sds-travel"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0257","stem":"At the start of the day, a tech pours concentrated degreaser into an unmarked spray bottle and leaves it on the van for his helper to use at later houses. What does OSHA 1910.1200 require for that bottle?","options":["A label that names the product and gives its general hazard information","No label, provided the bottle is emptied by the end of the shift","No label, provided the product's SDS rides in the same van","A label giving the date it was mixed and the tech's initials"],"expl":"The portable-container exemption in 1910.1200(f)(8) covers only a container meant for the immediate use of the employee who filled it, so a bottle left for a helper at later jobs needs a label with the product identifier and general hazard information under (f)(6). Emptying it by shift's end is not the test, and an SDS in the van does not replace a container label; the written-materials alternative in (f)(7) is for stationary process containers.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Hazard communication","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.1200(f)(6), (f)(8)","units":["dvt:portable-label"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0258","stem":"A tech knocks over a gallon of concentrated cleaner on the homeowner's garage floor, and it starts spreading toward a floor drain. Which SDS section should he check first for how to contain and clean up the spill?","options":["Section 6, accidental release measures","Section 8, exposure controls/personal protection","Section 13, disposal considerations","Section 7, safe handling and storage"],"expl":"Section 6, accidental release measures, gives the personal precautions and the methods and materials for containing and cleaning up a spill. Section 13 covers disposal of waste residues and packaging afterward, and Section 8 covers PPE for normal use. Mnemonic for the middle block: 6 spill, 7 store, 8 protect.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Hazard communication","tier":0,"ref":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.1200(g)(2), App. D","units":["dvt:sds-section-6"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0259","stem":"In the attic, the tech finds the dryer duct pulled apart at a joint. Lint has drifted over the insulation, and the roof sheathing above the gap is stained dark. What should he tell the homeowner?","options":["Lint and moist exhaust are dumping into the attic and must be piped outside","The stain confirms mold, so the attic must be remediated before any repair","The ridge and soffit vents will carry the lint and moisture out on their own","Dryer exhaust poses little air quality risk, so the gap can wait a season"],"expl":"The code requires dryer exhaust to go outdoors and never into an attic, soffit, or ridge vent, so the owner needs to hear that lint and moisture are loading the attic and the joint must be reconnected (IRC M1501.1, M1502.2). A tech cannot confirm mold from a stain; that is for an IEP or other qualified professional. DEDP's 'little IAQ risk' remark applies only to a dryer that actually discharges outdoors.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Hazard communication","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1501.1, M1502.2; DEDP 2014 Intro","units":["dvt:attic-stain-mold"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0260","stem":"Behind a gas dryer in a finished basement, the transition has fallen off the wall port, and the family has run the dryer that way for weeks. Besides the lint, what hazard should the tech explain before they use it again?","options":["Carbon monoxide in the burner exhaust that is now venting into the living space","Radon drawn up through the slab by the suction of the dryer's exhaust fan","Sewer gas drawn back up through the floor drain by the dryer's exhaust fan","Unburned natural gas leaking from the burner out through the exhaust port"],"expl":"A gas dryer's exhaust carries combustion products including carbon monoxide, and GE's instructions say that exposure is minimized by venting the dryer outdoors. With the duct off, that exhaust stays in the house, so the dryer should not run until it is reconnected. Radon and sewer gas are house-depressurization problems, and a disconnected dryer blows room air back into the room rather than pulling the house negative.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Hazard communication","tier":0,"ref":"GE gas dryer installation 31-16756; CPSC CO fact sheet","units":["dvt:gas-exhaust-co"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0261","stem":"The tech offers to fog a scented deodorizer into the freshly cleaned dryer duct. The homeowner says her son has asthma. What is the best way to handle the product's hazards?","options":["Show her the label and SDS first, and use it only if she agrees","Use it, then leave a copy of the SDS on her counter to read later","Use it, since the duct vents outdoors and will carry the scent away","Use it, since a product sold at retail carries no real hazard"],"expl":"The occupant should see a product's hazards before it is used, and with an asthmatic child she may well decline; NADCA's HVAC standard applies the same principle by requiring SDSs for every chemical on the job and controls that limit occupant exposure to vapors (ACR 2.7.1, 2.7.2). Leaving the SDS afterward informs her too late, fog put into an idle duct is not swept outdoors because the hood damper stays shut until the dryer runs, and a retail product can still carry label hazards.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Hazard communication","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 2.7.1, 2.7.2 (principle)","units":["dvt:product-consent"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0262","stem":"A new salesperson's script says a dryer vent cleaning 'guarantees lower energy bills and a fire-proof dryer.' Which rewrite fits NADCA's position?","options":["Cleaning helps cut drying time and fire risk but is no guarantee of either","Cleaning guarantees lower power bills once the duct passes the NADCA DEDP test","Cleaning guarantees lower power bills as long as the run is under 35 feet long","Cleaning removes the risk of a dryer fire for a full year after service"],"expl":"DEDP explains that lint buildup lengthens drying, raises energy use, and can lead to fire, so removing it helps, but NADCA expressly does not warrant energy savings or improved performance (DEDP disclaimer; ACR G-16). Passing the DEDP test, a run inside the 35 ft code limit, and NFPA's yearly cleaning interval are real benchmarks, but none turns cleaning into a guarantee.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Customer expectations","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 Disclaimer, Introduction","units":["dvt:no-guarantee"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0263","stem":"After a thorough cleaning, a condo dryer still takes about 90 minutes per load. The run works out to roughly 70 feet of developed length, far past the dryer maker's limit. What should the tech tell the owner?","options":["Cleaning cannot make up for that length, so the system needs a design fix","Some lint is likely still hiding, so the run needs a second cleaning","A plain inline duct fan from the hardware store will fix the long run","The run can be upsized to 6-inch duct to cut down the friction loss"],"expl":"DEDP lists a developed length beyond the manufacturer's limit as a condition that cleaning does not cure, so the owner needs a shorter route or another code-approved design change (DEDP Appendix; IRC M1502.4.6). A generic domestic booster fan is prohibited in dryer exhaust, with only a UL 705 dryer exhaust duct power ventilator allowed, and the code fixes the duct at 4 in nominal, so upsizing is out.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Customer expectations","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 Appendix; IRC 2024 M1502.4.1, M1502.4.5","units":["dvt:design-fix"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0264","stem":"A duct the tech just cleaned and confirmed clean fails the DEDP test on low velocity. The customer asks whether the cleaning was botched. What is the best explanation?","options":["A failed test flags problems in the duct itself, like leaks or extra elbows","A failed test means lint still remains, so the cleaning fee will be refunded","A failed test means the dryer's blower is weak, so it needs service","A failed test is normal in an older home, so it calls for no follow-up"],"expl":"DEDP is run only on a duct already confirmed clean, so a fail points to conditions in the duct itself, and the appendix lists causes such as leaking seams and joints, too many elbows, excess length, and a restrictive termination (DEDP 1.1, 4.7, Appendix). The test never uses the dryer's blower, and DEDP 4.8 has the tech offer solutions, so a fail is not simply written off.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Customer expectations","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 3.0.2, 4.7, 4.8, Appendix","units":["dedp:fwd-clean-first","dedp:app-leaks","dedp:app-design"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0265","stem":"The job was booked as a cleaning only. Partway through, the tech finds a crushed transition and a hood with a broken damper that both need replacing. When should the customer learn the added cost?","options":["Before the added work starts, with photos of each damaged part","After the job is done, on the final invoice she signs","After the job is done, once the DEDP test has passed","Before he leaves, once both parts have been replaced"],"expl":"Extra work is priced and approved before it is done, with photos so the customer sees what was found; NADCA's HVAC standard applies the same principle by requiring damage found during cleaning to be documented and reported to the client (ACR 4.24). The invoice, the passed test, and the moment before leaving all come after the work, which is the surprise this module aims to prevent.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Customer expectations","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 4.24 (principle); DEDP 2014 4.8","units":["dvt:price-before-work"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0266","stem":"While booking a job, the office learns the dryer vents through the roof of a two-story house with a steep pitch. What should the customer be told when the visit is scheduled?","options":["Roof access is part of the work, and rain or wind would mean a new date","The roof cap will be skipped, since the duct gets cleaned from the inside","The tech will judge the roof on the day, so no notice is needed now","The roof work will go ahead in any weather so that the date is kept firm"],"expl":"The termination is part of the job, since nests, lint, and restrictive screens collect at the cap (DEDP Introduction and Appendix), so the customer should know roof access is part of the visit and that rain, wind, or ice can force a new date. Skipping the cap leaves a common blockage in place, and holding the date in any weather ignores the weather hazards covered in the safety module.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Customer expectations","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 1.2.3; NADCA DVT Modules 2-3","units":["dvt:roof-scheduling"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0267","stem":"The pre-cleaning camera shows a hard, cement-like mass of lint and water at a sag in an attic run. What should the tech tell the owner before quoting a standard cleaning?","options":["Brushing alone is unlikely to free it, so it likely needs special work","A standard rotary brush can cut it loose, well within the normal job time","A compressed-air whip can blow it loose within the normal job time","Running the dryer on high heat first can dry it out, so the vacuum gets it"],"expl":"DEDP describes lint and water that have set into a cementitious mass that cannot be removed with direct agitation and requires advanced procedures (DEDP Appendix); a rotary brush and an air whip are both direct agitation. Setting that expectation before quoting avoids promising a normal result, and running the dryer on high heat just pushes hot exhaust into a blocked duct.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Customer expectations","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 Appendix, Introduction","units":["dedp:app-cement"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0268","stem":"A tech arrives at a house he has never serviced. Before he brings any equipment inside, what should he do first?","options":["Walk the whole route from the laundry area and duct path out to the cap","Lay drop cloths from the front door back to the laundry area","Run the DEDP velocity test at the cap to set a baseline reading","Hook the vacuum to the dryer end so the cleaning can begin at once"],"expl":"Walking the whole path shows the route, the termination and roof access, pets, occupants, and finishes, and that sets the protection, containment, and equipment plan; NADCA's HVAC standard applies the same principle by having the inspection set the controls, safety measures, and equipment and weigh the effect on occupants (ACR 1.0, 1.2.1). Drop cloths come after the route is known, and the DEDP test runs only on a duct already confirmed clean.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Jobsite assessment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 1.0, 1.2.1 (principle); DEDP 2014 1.1","units":["dvt:walk-route"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0269","stem":"The collection hose must run through the front door. The family dog roams the house, and a cat likes to hide behind the dryer. What should the tech arrange before starting?","options":["The owner confines both pets away from the front door and work areas","The pets stay loose, with the front door propped for the hose","The pets are shut in the laundry room, where he can watch them","The pets are put out in the yard beside the wall termination"],"expl":"Pets belong out of every work area and away from a door held open for the hose, and the owner is the one who can confine them; protecting occupants is a priority on every job, a principle NADCA's HVAC standard states outright (ACR 3.13). The laundry room and the termination are exactly where the lint, noise, and equipment are, so shutting pets in either spot puts them in the work zone.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Jobsite assessment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 3.13 (principle); NADCA DVT Module 3","units":["dvt:pets-confined"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0270","stem":"During the walkthrough, the homeowner says her husband has severe asthma and works in a home office next to the laundry room. Which plan best fits NADCA's duty to consider occupants?","options":["Work under vacuum and keep him away from the work area","Run the home's HVAC fan during the work to filter stray dust","Blow the lint outdoors with compressed air to finish sooner","Finish by fogging a deodorizer to settle any lingering dust"],"expl":"Keeping the duct under vacuum while lint is dislodged and keeping the sensitive occupant out of the work area both limit his exposure; NADCA's HVAC standard applies the same principle by requiring the effect on occupants to be understood and particulate kept from migrating out of the component (ACR 1.2.1, 3.9.1). Running the central fan can carry laundry-room dust through the house, and compressed air pushes lint out of every leak.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Jobsite assessment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 1.2.1, 3.9.1 (principle)","units":["dvt:sensitive-occupant"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0271","stem":"The only route to an upstairs laundry crosses a freshly refinished oak hallway and a carpeted staircase, and the tech's collection vacuum is a large non-HEPA unit. How should he set up the vacuum and hose?","options":["Vacuum left outside, with only the hose run in over floor covering","Vacuum carried upstairs and set down on a towel in the laundry room","Vacuum left outside, with only the hose run in on the bare floor","Vacuum parked in the hallway, with its exhaust aimed at a window"],"expl":"Leaving the vacuum outside keeps its weight, wheels, and exhaust off the new finish, and the hose route still gets covered, since protective coverings extend past the work area wherever flooring needs protecting (ACR 3.9.2, applied as a principle). Running this non-HEPA unit anywhere indoors breaks the rule that such equipment exhausts outdoors (ACR 3.3, 4.1.3), and a bare-floor hose drags grit across the finish.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Jobsite assessment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 3.9.2, 3.3, 4.1.3 (principle)","units":["dvt:vac-outside","dvt:coverings"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0272","stem":"A house has no outdoor outlet, so the tech runs a gasoline generator to power his collection vacuum. Where should the generator sit while it runs?","options":["Outdoors at least 20 feet from the house, exhaust aimed away from openings","Inside the attached garage, with the big overhead door fully raised","Right under the laundry window, so the extension cord stays short","On the covered front porch, so it stays sheltered from any rain during the job"],"expl":"CPSC says to run portable generators outdoors only, at least 20 feet from the home with the exhaust pointed away from windows, doors, and vents, and never in a garage even with the door open. NADCA's HVAC standard applies the same principle by requiring generators and compressors to sit where their emissions cannot enter the building (ACR 3.2).","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Jobsite assessment","tier":0,"ref":"CPSC generator guidance 2026; ACR 2025 3.2","units":["dvt:generator-20ft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0273","stem":"The dryer duct rises into the ceiling, and the owner has no idea where it ends. No wall cap is visible from the yard. What should the tech do before cleaning?","options":["Trace the run to its end, since some stop in the attic or a soffit","Brush from inside and watch outdoors for lint to show where it ends","Blow air in from inside and look for a lint plume to find the end","Clean from the dryer end only, as the termination is not in the job"],"expl":"An end no one can find may be an illegal attic, soffit, or ridge-vent discharge, and brushing or blowing from inside would pack lint into the attic, so the run is traced first (IRC M1501.1, M1502.3). The termination belongs in the job because lint and nests collect there, and DEDP requires access to it before its performance test can be run.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Jobsite assessment","tier":0,"ref":"IRC 2024 M1501.1, M1502.3; DEDP 2014 1.2.3","units":["dvt:trace-unknown-end"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0274","stem":"In a row of townhomes, the wall cap sits about 4 feet from the neighbor's patio furniture. The tech plans to push the lint out from the laundry end. How should he handle the discharge at the cap?","options":["Blow it into a bag or vacuum sealed tight over the wall cap","Blow it out, then hose the lint off the neighbor's patio furniture","Blow it out after asking the neighbor to shut windows and doors","Blow it out while the wind is carrying it away from the patio"],"expl":"NADCA's DVT outline makes protecting both the interior and the exterior from contaminants a key principle, so lint pushed out of the cap is captured at the source with a bag or vacuum over the termination. Hosing the patio afterward, closing the neighbor's windows, or timing the wind all still drop lint on someone else's property.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Lint containment","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DVT handout, Module 3; ACR 2025 3.0","units":["dvt:capture-at-cap"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0275","stem":"A tech runs a compressed-air whip in from the laundry end toward the wall cap. Lint keeps puffing back out of the wall port into the laundry room. What is the best fix?","options":["Seal the vacuum to the port so the duct stays under negative pressure","Raise the compressor pressure so the lint is driven out to the cap faster","Hang a damp towel over the port and keep working the air whip through it","Open the laundry window so the loose lint drifts outside on its own"],"expl":"With the collection vacuum sealed at the port, the duct runs negative, so lint loosened by the whip moves into the vacuum instead of the room; NADCA's HVAC standard applies the same principle by keeping the component under negative pressure during all cleaning to stop particulate migration (ACR 3.9.1). More air pressure makes the blowback worse, and a towel catches only part of it.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Lint containment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 3.9.1, 4.5 (principle)","units":["dvt:seal-vac-port"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0276","stem":"The tech plans to blow the duct out from the laundry end, but first he finds the wall cap packed solid with an old, empty nest. What should he do first?","options":["Clear the nest from the cap outside before any air goes into the duct","Blow from inside at full pressure to force the nest out of the cap","Blow from inside in short bursts to work the nest loose bit by bit","Seal the port around the air hose, then blow the nest out the cap"],"expl":"Blowing into a duct with a blocked outlet pressurizes it, so lint and nest debris come back out wherever they can, including the open port and leaking joints inside walls and ceilings; the outlet is cleared first. Sealing the port only moves that blowback to the joints, and short bursts still pressurize the run.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Lint containment","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DVT Module 3; ACR 2025 3.9.1","units":["dvt:clear-cap-first"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0277","stem":"The tech's rods, brush heads, and hose come out of a nest-choked vent at the first house of the day coated in lint and droppings. Following NADCA's equipment hygiene principle, what should happen before they go into the second house?","options":["They are cleaned and inspected ahead of going into the next work site","They are wiped down inside the second house, over a drop cloth","They are sealed in a bag that is opened later in the laundry room","They are cleaned once at day's end, after the final job is done"],"expl":"NADCA's HVAC standard requires equipment to be cleaned and inspected before it enters a work site, so contaminants from the last job stay out of the next building (ACR 3.1.1), and the principle fits dryer tools. Sealing is the option for moving equipment within or out of a building (ACR 3.1.3), not for bringing dirty tools into a new house. Wiping them down inside the second house releases the droppings in that home, and waiting until day's end carries them into every house in between.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Lint containment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 3.1.1, 3.1.3 (principle)","units":["dvt:clean-tools-between"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0278","stem":"At the end of the job, the drop cloth behind the dryer is covered in lint and dust. How should the tech take it up?","options":["Fold its edges in over the lint, then carry it out to the van","Shake it out into a trash bag in the laundry room, then fold it up","Shake it out on the back lawn, then fold it up for the next job","Drag it by one corner out through the entry door and to the van"],"expl":"Folding the edges inward traps the lint inside the cloth so it leaves the house contained, in line with the principle that contaminated materials are contained before they leave the building (ACR 3.5). Shaking it indoors puts the dust back in the air, shaking it on the lawn moves the mess outdoors, and dragging it spills lint along the route.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Lint containment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 3.5 (principle); NADCA DVT Module 3","units":["dvt:fold-drop-cloth"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0279","stem":"Working at the laundry end, the tech pulls a dry nest caked with droppings out of the wall port. It has to be carried out through the living room. How should he handle it?","options":["Seal it in a bag on the spot, then carry it out of the home","Carry it out in his gloved hands, then bag it up at the van outside","Carry it out in an open bucket, then bag it at the curb","Carry it to the kitchen trash, then tie that bag off at once"],"expl":"Contaminated material is contained where it is removed and before it moves through the building, a principle NADCA's HVAC standard makes mandatory (ACR 3.5), and dry droppings shed dust with every step. Carrying it loose in gloves or an open bucket spreads that dust through the living room, and the kitchen trash leaves it in the home.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Lint containment","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 3.5 (principle)","units":["dvt:bag-nest"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0280","stem":"Before pulling the dryer out, the tech notices a cracked floor tile at its front edge and a dent in its side panel. What should he do?","options":["Photograph both and show them to the owner before anything is moved","Photograph both and file the photos quietly in the job record","Move the dryer with care and bring them up if the owner asks later","Note both on the final invoice once the dryer is back in its place"],"expl":"Existing damage is recorded and shown to the owner before anything moves, so no one later blames the tech; NADCA's HVAC standard applies the same principle by requiring pre-existing damage to be documented and the client notified (ACR 4.24). Photos filed without telling the owner meet only half of that duty, and raising it later or on the invoice looks like an excuse after the fact.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Property care","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 4.24 (principle)","units":["dvt:preexisting-damage"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0281","stem":"The dryer sits on new vinyl plank flooring and must come out about three feet. Which method best protects the floor?","options":["With a helper, slide it out on sheets of cardboard or hardboard","Walk it out by rocking it from one front corner over to the other corner","Pull it out by the console once the leveling legs are screwed fully in","Tilt it back onto its two rear legs and drag it out across the vinyl planks"],"expl":"Whirlpool's dryer instructions have the dryer moved to its final location on cardboard, which is removed once the dryer is in place. Maytag calls for two or more people to move a dryer and says to grasp the body, not the console panel. A sheet under the legs spreads the load so the dryer slides instead of gouging. Rocking or tilting puts the full weight on one or two legs.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Property care","tier":0,"ref":"Maytag dryer installation instructions","units":["dvt:move-on-cardboard"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0282","stem":"A gas dryer is hooked up with a coated flexible gas connector that has little slack. How should the tech pull the dryer out to reach the duct?","options":["Only as far as the work needs, so the connector isn't kinked or strained","Fully out, since a coated connector can bend over and over safely","Fully out, then work the connector's kinks straight again by hand","Only as far as it reaches, then unscrew the connector at the valve"],"expl":"CPSC warns that gas connectors wear out from too much moving and bending, so the dryer comes out only as far as the work needs and the connector is kept from kinking. Unscrewing it is no shortcut, since GE says never to reuse an old flexible connector and calls for a qualified installer, and a coated connector still fatigues when bent back and forth.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Property care","tier":0,"ref":"CPSC Pub. 5135; GE gas dryer installation 31-16756","units":["dvt:new-gas-connector","dvt:connector-slack"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0283","stem":"A steam dryer shares the washer's cold-water faucet through a Y connector. After sliding the dryer back into place, what should the tech check before he leaves?","options":["The hose couplings at the faucet and the Y connector for any leaks","The washer's drain hose where it hooks into the laundry room standpipe","The dryer's condensate drain hose, checked for a kink or a clog","The hot-water faucet, which steam models also draw water from"],"expl":"Moving a steam dryer flexes its water inlet hose, and GE's hookup steps end with checking for leaks around the Y connector, the faucet, and the hose couplings. Steam models draw from the cold supply only, and a condensate drain hose belongs to ventless condensing and heat-pump dryers, not a vented steam dryer.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Property care","tier":1,"ref":"GE gas dryer installation 31-16756, inlet hoses","units":["dvt:condensing-dryer","dvt:steam-dryer-leaks"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0284","stem":"On a sunny 95°F afternoon, the tech must cross an asphalt shingle roof to reach a roof cap. What property risk does the heat create, and how should he reduce it?","options":["Softened shingles scuff easily, so use soft soles or come back when it cools","Brittle shingles crack easily, so wait for the midday sun to warm them up","Softened shingles lose their seal, so hand-seal every tab he steps on","Brittle shingles snap underfoot, so step where the courses overlap"],"expl":"ARMA notes that the asphalt coating softens in hot weather, so shingles scuff and are easily damaged underfoot; soft-soled shoes, careful footing, and waiting until the roof cools reduce that. Cracking and snapping are ARMA's cold-weather warnings, and heat activates the shingle sealant rather than releasing it.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Property care","tier":0,"ref":"ARMA hot- and cold-weather shingle bulletins","units":["dvt:hot-shingles"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0285","stem":"After the dryer is reconnected, a film of fine lint dust covers the washer lid and the floor behind the machines. What is the best way to clean it up?","options":["HEPA-vacuum the dust, then wipe the area with a damp cloth","Sweep it into a dustpan, then run the dryer to clear the air","Blow it off with the compressor, pushing it toward the door","Dust it off with a dry brush and let the air settle before leaving"],"expl":"A HEPA vacuum followed by a damp wipe picks up fine dust without putting it back in the air, the same finish NADCA's HVAC standard requires on surfaces before a containment comes down (ACR 3.10.8). Sweeping, dry brushing, and blowing all launch the finest dust into the room, where it settles again after the tech leaves.","domain":"Containment & Occupants","sub":"Property care","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 3.10.8 (principle); DVT Module 5","units":["dvt:hepa-damp-wipe"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0286","stem":"A mixed-use building has a coin laundry on the ground floor and a household dryer in each apartment. Under the 2024 IFGC definitions, which of these dryers are Type 2?","options":["The coin laundry machines, run as a business for the public","The apartment dryers, since the building counts as multifamily","The gas units, since Type 2 means a fuel-burning dryer","The units exhausting over 200 cfm, since those need makeup air"],"expl":"The IFGC defines a Type 2 dryer as a factory-built unit used in a business where the public uses it directly, not designed for a family living environment; Type 1 is the household unit. The trap is 'multifamily': the class turns on business use by the public, not on building type, fuel, or the 200 cfm makeup-air trigger of IFGC 614.7.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Type 1 vs Type 2","tier":0,"ref":"IFGC 2024 §202 (Clothes dryer)","units":["dvt:type1-type2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0287","stem":"A tech who mostly cleans house vents inspects a laundromat's Type 2 gas stack dryers. Each stack has one 8-inch exhaust collar, and the maker lists about 600 cfm of airflow. What sets the size and length of each exhaust duct?","options":["The limits the maker of that dryer gives in its installation instructions","The 4-inch size and 35 ft length limit in IFGC Section 614.9","The side wall and roof performance tables in the NADCA DEDP","The length limit set by the maker of a UL 705 power ventilator"],"expl":"IFGC 614.10 (and IMC 504.10) sends ducts serving Type 2 dryers to the appliance maker's instructions; Dexter's T-30X2 manual, for example, calls for 8-inch duct and recommends no more than 14 ft with two elbows per cylinder. The 4-inch and 35 ft rules, the DEDP tables and the power-ventilator length method all belong to domestic 4-inch systems.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Type 2 exhaust","tier":0,"ref":"IFGC 2024 §614.10; IMC 2024 §504.10","units":["dvt:type2-dexter-duct"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0288","stem":"Six Type 2 dryers in a laundromat discharge into one exhaust system with a single exhaust fan. Under 2024 IFGC 614.10, where must that fan's motor be located?","options":["Outside of the exhaust airstream","Within 6 ft of the exhaust duct connection","At the base of the shaft, by its cleanout","At least 3 ft from openings into the building"],"expl":"IFGC 614.10 puts exhaust fan motors for Type 2 dryer systems outside the airstream, so hot, lint-laden exhaust never passes over the motor, and in multiple installations the fan runs continuously or is interlocked with the dryers. The shaft-base cleanout belongs to the multistory common-exhaust rules of 614.11, 6 ft is where the domestic length label goes, and 3 ft is the termination clearance.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Type 2 exhaust","tier":0,"ref":"IFGC 2024 §614.10","units":["code:comm-fan"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0289","stem":"A customer cleans the lint screen after every load, yet lint now falls off the screen while it sits in the dryer, and loads take longer. What does Whirlpool's care guidance say to do?","options":["Scrub the screen with a nylon brush and hot detergent water","Rinse the lint off the screen under a tap after each load","Vacuum out the screen slot and reuse the screen just as it is","Have the exhaust duct cleaned, since the screen is working"],"expl":"Whirlpool says detergent and fabric softener residue can clog the screen, that lint falling off it in place is the sign, and that the fix is a scrub with a nylon brush, hot water and liquid detergent every six months or sooner. Rinsing lint off after each load is the trap: Whirlpool says not to wash the screen to remove lint, because wet lint is hard to remove.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Lint screen","tier":0,"ref":"Whirlpool lint screen care guidance","units":["dvt:lint-screen-residue"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0290","stem":"After cleaning the duct, a vent tech with no appliance training wants to remove what lint he can from inside the dryer. Which area can he clean without opening the cabinet?","options":["The housing the lint screen slides into","The heater box that surrounds the element","The burner area around the igniter and valve","The drum's rear felt seal and its bulkhead"],"expl":"Whirlpool tells owners to vacuum lint from the space where the screen sits, and the LintEater kit includes a lint-trap brush for that housing on top-mounted and door-mounted screens. Gardus says to clean the rest of the dryer interior only if you are familiar with taking dryers apart, otherwise call a professional, so the heater box, burner and drum seals are appliance-tech work.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Dryer components","tier":0,"ref":"Whirlpool lint screen care; LintEater manual p.20","units":["dvt:screen-housing"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0291","stem":"A customer's electric dryer hums when she presses Start, but nothing turns and the element stays cold. According to CPSC's description of dryer controls, why does the element not heat?","options":["The heater is powered only once the drive motor is at speed","The heater is powered only once the sensor bars touch wet clothes","The heater is powered only after the thermal fuse warms and closes","The heater is powered only once the operating thermostat opens"],"expl":"CPSC explains that power to the dryer circuits runs through the motor's centrifugal switch, and the electric heat source is energized only when the drive motor is at normal operating speed. That same motor turns the drum and circulates the air, so heat waits for airflow. The thermal fuse is normally closed and opens once on overheating, and the operating thermostat opens to switch heat off.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Dryer components","tier":1,"ref":"CPSC 1999 dryer report, p.2","units":["dvt:heat-waits-for-motor"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0292","stem":"NADCA's DVT outline splits the exhaust system into dryer interior, transition duct, main duct, and termination cover. Which DEDP-defined component matches the main duct?","options":["The permanent exhaust duct, rigid pipe from the wall to outdoors","The duct connection, the joint where the transition meets the wall pipe","The test duct, 4 to 6 ft of semi-rigid pipe at the wall port","The exhaust penetration, where the pipe passes through the wall"],"expl":"DEDP defines the permanent exhaust duct as the network of 4-inch rigid duct that starts at an interior wall, floor or ceiling and ends at an outside exhaust penetration, which is the outline's main duct. The duct connection is only the point where the transition joins the wall pipe, the test duct is only the temporary 4 to 6 ft piece used for the DEDP test, and the penetration is just the point where the duct crosses a wall or ceiling.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Exhaust system parts","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014, Definitions; DVT handout Module 1","units":["dvt:exhaust-four-parts"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0293","stem":"On a pre-assessment, a tech notes four problems and sorts them by the four parts of the exhaust system named in NADCA's DVT outline. Which finding is a main duct problem?","options":["A joint that has slipped apart in the attic run","A foil connector flattened behind the dryer","A hood flap held open by a mat of lint","Lint packed into the lint screen housing"],"expl":"The outline's four parts are the dryer interior, the transition duct, the main duct and the termination cover. A separated joint in the attic run is a main duct defect; the flattened foil connector is the transition duct, the propped-open flap is the termination cover, and lint in the screen housing is the dryer interior. Mnemonic, in airflow order: Inside, Transition, Main, Cap.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Exhaust system parts","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DVT handout, Module 1 key points","units":["dvt:exhaust-four-parts"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0294","stem":"A small laundry room holds two Type 2 gas dryers and a gas water heater that takes its combustion air from the same room. Why does Dexter's manual say to test the other gas appliances with the dryers running and all doors and windows closed?","options":["Dryer exhaust can depressurize the room and backdraft the heater","Dryer exhaust can pressurize the room and push flue gas into dryers","Dryer burners share the gas line and can starve the heater's flame","Dryer lint drawn into the heater's burner can choke its combustion air"],"expl":"Dexter warns that the dryers can affect other gas appliances that take combustion air from the same room, that ventilation must prevent back flow of their gases, and that those appliances are tested with the dryers running and the room closed up. Every cubic foot pushed out the dryer duct must be replaced; IFGC 614.7 requires makeup air above 200 cfm, and one Dexter 30 lb stack moves about 600 cfm.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Exhaust principles","tier":0,"ref":"Dexter T-30X2 manual §4.2.3; IFGC 2024 §614.7","units":["dvt:type2-makeup-backdraft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0295","stem":"A homeowner's dryer duct has built up lint over the years, and she asks what that does besides raise the fire risk. Which effect do the DEDP introduction and Whirlpool's venting guidance describe?","options":["Moist air leaves the dryer more slowly, so drying time and energy use rise","Airflow holds steady, since the blower turns at a fixed speed","The blower speeds up to hold its air volume, so energy use goes up","Exhaust leaves hotter at the hood, so drying time goes down"],"expl":"DEDP says that as the duct obstructs, drying time increases and the dryer can overheat, raising energy use, and Whirlpool says a clogged vent slows moist air from leaving the dryer and extends drying time. A fixed-speed blower still moves less air against more resistance and does not speed up to make up the loss, and CPSC's tests found the exhaust temperature falling, not rising, as the vent clogged.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Exhaust principles","tier":0,"ref":"NADCA DEDP 2014, Introduction; Whirlpool AF guidance","units":["dvt:cpsc-temps","dvt:clog-slows-drying"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0296","stem":"A customer's Whirlpool dryer showed an AF code during the last load, and she asks what it means. According to Whirlpool, what does the code indicate and what does the dryer do?","options":["Restricted airflow, and the dryer keeps on running as the code shows","Restricted airflow, and the dryer shuts down until the vent is cleared","An overheated heater, and the dryer shuts down until a fuse is replaced","An overheated heater, and the dryer keeps running but with no heat"],"expl":"Whirlpool's AF (Restricted Air Flow) or Check Vent alert means the lint screen or vent is clogged, or the vent is crushed, kinked or has too many turns, and the dryer continues to run while it shows; Check Vent models sense airflow at the start of automatic cycles. The shut-down options are the trap: because the dryer keeps working, the code is easy to ignore while drying times stretch.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Exhaust principles","tier":0,"ref":"Whirlpool 'AF or Check Vent' guidance","units":["dvt:af-code"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0297","stem":"In a laundromat, a contractor is joining the 8-inch ducts from several Type 2 dryers into one main discharge duct. How does Dexter's installation manual say each branch must enter the main?","options":["At 45 degrees, angled in the direction of the discharge airflow","At 45 degrees, pointing upstream against the airflow","At 90 degrees, entering through the top of the main duct","At 90 degrees, entering through the side of the main duct"],"expl":"Dexter says each dryer's 8-inch duct should enter the main discharge duct at a 45 degree angle in the direction of discharge airflow, from the sides or bottom, and never at a right angle. The same manual wants an inspection door for periodic cleanout of the main duct and no more than 0.3 in. w.c. static back pressure at each dryer's exhaust outlet.","domain":"Understanding Dryer Vents","sub":"Type 2 exhaust","tier":1,"ref":"Dexter T-30X2 manual §4.2.6","units":["dvt:type2-branch-45"],"reserved":true},{"id":"dvt-0298","stem":"Working from the wall hood, a tech feeds a reverse-spinning skipper line toward the disconnected dryer. Where should he set up the collection for the loosened lint?","options":["At the hood end, since its rear jets push the debris back","At the laundry end, since its jets drive lint ahead of the nozzle","At the laundry end, since negative pressure goes opposite the tool","At the hood and laundry ends, since the jets throw lint both ways"],"expl":"Nikro describes its reverse-spinning skipper as a dryer vent tool that propels itself around elbows and suits cleaning from outside, and its reverse nozzles sweep debris toward the operator; Vent-Vision likewise says the reverse skipper blasts lint back toward the collection system. Forward nozzles are the ones that push debris away from the operator, so match the collection point to the nozzle's direction.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Compressed-air tools","tier":0,"ref":"Nikro air-tool catalog; Vent-Vision air-tool literature","units":["dvt:reverse-skipper"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0299","stem":"A tech feeds a forward-blast air nozzle in from the wall hood while the dryer is still connected and switched off. Where does the loosened lint end up?","options":["Forced into the dryer outlet and blower housing","Pulled back out the hood by the nozzle's rear-facing jets","Stopped by the lint screen before reaching the blower","Carried through to the drum, where the next load picks it up"],"expl":"Nikro's forward nozzles loosen debris and push it away from the operator toward the vacuum, so when fed from the hood they drive lint toward the laundry end. With the dryer still attached and switched off, that lint is forced into the dryer's exhaust outlet and blower housing, and reverse flow reaches the blower before the lint screen (CPSC air path: heat source, drum, lint filter, blower, duct). LintEater's procedure likewise starts by taking the vent off the dryer and hooking a shop vac or blower to it before brushing.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Compressed-air tools","tier":0,"ref":"Nikro air-tool catalog; LintEater manual Step 10","units":["dvt:forward-nozzle"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0300","stem":"Wet weather makes a steep roof cap unsafe to reach today, so the tech must brush and vacuum from the laundry end alone. Which accessory makes that possible?","options":["A Y-fitting, with the vac on one leg and a slit-capped tool port","A dryer adapter, with a built-in guard that keeps the brush out","A lint catcher bag, clipped over the wall port behind the dryer","A blockage removal tool, threaded onto the lead rod ahead of the brush"],"expl":"Nikro's dryer vent 'Y' tool takes the 3-inch vac hose on one port and the 4-inch duct on another. Its third, slit-capped port is a feed-through for brushes or compressed air, so the tech can agitate and vacuum at the same time. The LintEater dryer adapter does the opposite job, because its guard stops a brush from entering the dryer. The LintCatcher bag clips over the exterior hood to catch lint blown out by a blower or the dryer, and it does no vacuuming. The blockage removal tool is for pulling out nests, not for brushing.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Negative pressure setups","tier":0,"ref":"Nikro DV15360 manual, Dryer Vent Cleaning","units":["dvt:y-fitting"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0301","stem":"Halfway through a long run, suction at the hose of a Nikro DV15360 dryer vent vac drops off sharply. What does Nikro's manual say to do?","options":["Switch it off at once and find the full bag or clog","Pull out the impaction prefilter so the HEPA gets more air","Tap the impaction prefilter clean and then put it back in","Switch on all three of its motors to pull through the clog"],"expl":"Nikro says a loss of vacuum power means turning the machine off immediately, because running on can damage the motor and HEPA filter, then checking for a full collection bag or tank, a clogged hose or down tube, or a loaded prefilter, cloth bag or HEPA. The impaction prefilter is never cleaned, only replaced, and the vac is never run without its filters.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Vacuum collection","tier":0,"ref":"Nikro DV15360 manual, Filter Maintenance","units":["dvt:nikro-suction-loss"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0302","stem":"A tech uses positive pressure, with a leaf blower on the laundry end pushing loosened lint out while he brushes in from the wall hood. The hood sits above the customer's flower bed. What does the LintEater system use to keep that lint off the property?","options":["A catcher bag clipped onto the hood that bags lint as it exits","A dryer adapter whose guard holds the lint back at the blower","Tape across the hood louvers that holds the lint inside the hood","A drop cloth spread under the hood to catch lint as it falls"],"expl":"Gardus's LintCatcher clips to the exterior hood so lint pushed out by a shop vac, blower or the dryer collects in the bag while the brush works, and a zippered opening empties it. Under positive pressure the lint always ends up at the termination, so exterior collection is part of site clean-up. The dryer adapter's guard only keeps the brush out of the blower, and hood tape holds the damper open.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Positive pressure setups","tier":0,"ref":"Gardus LintCatcher; LintEater manual Method 2","units":["dvt:linteater-ppe","dvt:capture-at-cap","dvt:positive-pressure-leaks","dvt:lintcatcher"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0303","stem":"A pre-clean camera run showed a slipped joint in the attic, but the tech cleaned anyway, with a leaf blower on the laundry end while he brushed down from the roof cap. Now lint dusts the insulation around that joint. What explains it?","options":["The blower pressurized the entire duct, so the lint escaped through the gap","The duct was under negative pressure, so the joint pushed lint out","The rods pulled the joint apart and forced lint through the opening","The hot attic air made the lint rise and drift out through the joint"],"expl":"A leaf blower or the dryer on the laundry end puts the whole duct under positive pressure, so air and lint leave wherever it is open, including a slipped attic joint. Under negative pressure a gap draws air in instead, which is why NADCA's ACR keeps the section being cleaned negative to its surroundings. The camera had already shown the joint apart, so the rods did not cause it, and the blower, not buoyancy, moves the air.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Positive pressure setups","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.4, §4.1; LintEater manual Method 2","units":["dvt:pre-clean-camera","dvt:positive-pressure-leaks"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0304","stem":"A tech has a small single-motor shop vac sealed to the laundry end and runs a high-flow forward air whip in from the hood. Lint puffs out at the vac connection and the transition collar. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The whip is putting in more air than the vac pulls out","The vac is pulling hard enough to flatten the connector","The whip is set at too low a pressure to move lint","The whip's jets are aimed back at the hood, not the vac"],"expl":"Collection works only while the vac removes more air than the agitation tool adds; when a high-flow whip overpowers a small vac, the duct goes positive and lint escapes at the leaks nearest the vac. NADCA's ACR requires continuous negative pressure in the section being cleaned, and Nikro tells DV15360 users to run all three motors for optimal performance. A collapsing connector means a blockage under suction, not puffing.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Vacuum collection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.4, §4.5; Nikro DV15360 manual","units":["dvt:vent-collapses","dvt:vac-must-win"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0305","stem":"Before brushing a long concealed run, a tech clips a camera to his rods and records a pass from the hood to the wall port. What is the main value of that pre-clean pass?","options":["It reveals crushed or disconnected sections and any nests ahead of cleaning","It serves as the DEDP's primary inspection of the duct's cleanliness","It stands in for the DEDP test on short, straight dryer runs","It replaces the naked-eye check of the duct once cleaning is done"],"expl":"A pre-clean camera pass shows what the tools will meet, such as disconnected or crushed sections, nests and protruding screws, and Rotobrush says video keeps a tech from simply pushing lint deeper while proving results to the customer. DEDP says video is not its primary inspection, it never replaces the performance test even on short straight runs, and cleanliness is confirmed afterward by visual inspection, which DEDP defines as naked-eye examination.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Cameras","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 Foreword, §1.4, Definitions; Rotobrush FAQ","units":["dvt:pre-clean-camera"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0306","stem":"After cleaning, the tech's camera shows a spotless run from the wall port to the hood, yet the duct fails the DEDP velocity minimum. Which condition does DEDP say its test accounts for that a clean-looking video can miss?","options":["Air lost through leaky elbows and snap-lock seams","Loose lint lying along the bottom of the pipe","A bird nest packed into the elbow by the hood","Screws that stick out into the pipe's airstream"],"expl":"DEDP's foreword says the test shows whether the installed duct moves the minimum airflow, compensating for leaky elbows, leaky ducts, broken connections and frictional losses, and its appendix lists leaking snap-lock seams and untaped joints as failure causes. Loose lint, a nest in an elbow and protruding screws are things a camera pass would show, and DEDP's pre-test observations already require the barrier to be visibly free of lint.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Cameras","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 Foreword; Appendix","units":["dedp:fwd-compensates","dedp:app-leaks"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0307","stem":"Post-clean camera footage shows a hard, cement-like crust at a low spot that the silicon carbide brush would not remove. For the DEDP documentation, what must the tech record?","options":["That an adhered substance is present at the low spot, and what it is","That debris is still left in the duct after the cleaning","That the crust needs no record unless the duct fails the test","That the pipe is crushed and deformed at the low spot"],"expl":"DEDP defines an adhered substance as material a silicon carbide bristle brush cannot remove by direct contact, and Section 4.5 has the tech indicate and record what was found when visual observations show one. Calling it debris is the trap: DEDP debris is non-adhered material, the kind the cleaning should already have removed, and the record does not wait for a test result.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Cameras","tier":0,"ref":"DEDP 2014 §4.5, §1.3.2, Definitions","units":["dedp:4.5-adhered","dedp:def-adhered"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0308","stem":"In a finished basement laundry, the collection vac will sit beside the dryer and exhaust into the room. Which setup meets NADCA's rule for vacuum equipment that exhausts indoors?","options":["A HEPA filtered vac, used with its full set of filters in place","A shop vac fitted with a fine paper bag and a pleated cartridge filter","A MERV 16 filtered vac run with its full set of filters in place","A shop vac run while a HEPA air scrubber cleans the room air"],"expl":"NADCA's ACR requires vacuum equipment that exhausts inside the building to use HEPA filtration (99.97% at 0.3 micron), and non-HEPA collection equipment must exhaust outdoors (ACR 3.3, 4.1.3). A MERV 16 filter and a pleated shop-vac cartridge are good filters, but neither is HEPA, and a HEPA air scrubber is only a supplemental control that does not make a non-HEPA vac compliant (ACR 3.6). Keeping the full filter set in place also follows makers such as Nikro, whose dryer vent vac manual says never to run the unit without its filters.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Vacuum collection","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.3, §3.6, §4.1.2-4.1.3; Nikro DV15360 manual","units":["dvt:hepa-indoors"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0309","stem":"A tech sets up the Nikro DV15360 HEPA vac for a lint-heavy dryer vent job. Which change to the filter stack does Nikro's manual allow for dryer vent work?","options":["Leave out the paper bag and let lint fill the poly bag","Leave out the cloth bag and let lint fill the paper bag","Leave out the prefilter and let the cloth bag catch the lint","Leave out the HEPA filter and let the cloth bag catch lint"],"expl":"For dryer vent lint and large particles, Nikro lets the paper collection bag be left out so material loads straight into the 4-mil poly bag. Removing the lower down tube also helps prevent clogs, but every other filter must stay in place. Dropping the paper bag is the only filter change Nikro lists for dryer vents, and the cloth bag, prefilter and HEPA always stay. Mnemonic: for lint, drop the paper, keep the rest.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Vacuum collection","tier":1,"ref":"Nikro DV15360 manual, Filter Options","units":["dvt:nikro-filter-option"],"reserved":false},{"id":"dvt-0310","stem":"With the dryer pulled out, the tech sees a gray mat of lint coating the area behind it and the rear of the cabinet. What does the LintEater procedure have him do about it?","options":["Vacuum it up, and use the lint trap brush to loosen it","Blow it out the door with the leaf blower for the vent","Wipe it up with a damp rag so the lint cannot fly","Leave the lint for an appliance tech, since it is out of scope"],"expl":"LintEater Step 9 has the tech clean the area behind the dryer and the back of the dryer itself with the lint trap brush and a vacuum before hooking up to the vent. The manual's opening tips also recommend cleaning lint from behind, under and around the dryer. A damp rag is not the tool the procedure names, and a leaf blower only spreads the lint through the house. The out-of-scope rule covers the dryer's interior, not loose lint on the floor and panels.","domain":"Cleaning & Verification","sub":"Site clean-up","tier":0,"ref":"LintEater manual Step 9","units":["dvt:clean-behind-dryer"],"reserved":false}],"built":"2026-09-21"});
